{"parentUuid":"cf111059-fedc-43c8-96f2-ead95267d0fc","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac7100d52d01adab3","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffq1nPrui7KPL8Y1aMtb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018sKGuLzMkarE4ghVPyipFJ","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tool-results/bqbv3yx3j.txt"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1037,"cache_read_input_tokens":56160,"output_tokens":123,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1037},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":123,"cache_read_input_tokens":56160,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1037,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1037,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_018sKGuLzMkarE4ghVPyipFJ":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tool-results/bqbv3yx3j.txt"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cffq1n7zRHQJaKSToiVYS","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"4e97935f-641f-42eb-b1fb-59ee7b418dda","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:07:39.498Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"4e97935f-641f-42eb-b1fb-59ee7b418dda","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"725cf014-e8e6-4e21-907d-d54c6c265ee5","agentId":"ac7100d52d01adab3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018sKGuLzMkarE4ghVPyipFJ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tYou are an independent reviewer in Flint's ingest review gate. Another model wrote these entries; your job is to FALSIFY them, not confirm. Read-only on entries and sources. Write only your report file.\n2\t\n3\tStandards: `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\docs\\ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md` (read it first). Every rule there is a check.\n4\t\n5\tFor EVERY entry in scope:\n6\t1. Every number, definition, table value and quote appears at the cited PDF page (and printed page) and means the same thing. Open the pages yourself; do not trust the entry.\n7\t2. Attribution: plain text is the book author's own statement. Anything else must be labelled \"Eric's baseline:\", \"Eric's review:\" or \"Inference:\". Flag any unlabelled claim the author did not make, any \"why it works\" mechanism the author did not give, and any invented example.\n8\t3. Confidence: `proven` only for established, widely replicated physiology or mechanics, so challenge every `proven`. Soviet-era or single-lab findings and prescriptions: `supported` at most. `untested` for anecdotes. `contested` needs a stated reason.\n9\t4. \"When NOT to apply\" is present and substantive.\n10\t5. Duplicates: flag any entry that restates an existing vault entry (grep `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\Knowledge\\library`) without a new angle.\n11\t6. Missing: up to 5 important ideas in the covered pages that no entry captures.\n12\t\n13\tOutput (Markdown): a table with one row per entry: file | verdict (GO / FIX / NO-GO) | problem, quoting the entry and the source with its page | exact correction. For GO rows, list what you checked (\"checked: 2.5x at PDF 23 / p.12\"). Then the missing list. Then an overall verdict: PROMOTE, GO AFTER FIXES (list them), or NO-GO.\n14\t# Atomization brief (shared by every Flint ingest agent)\n15\t\n16\tRead this whole file before writing anything. It holds the rules the Harries review taught (2026-09-26: 25 of 35 files needed fixes). The review gate will check every rule here.\n17\t\n18\t## Schema\n19\t\n20\tFollow `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\.claude\\skills\\ingest-book\\SKILL.md` exactly: `_domain.md`, `_book.md` (map and resume checkpoint, updated after EACH chapter or unit), and entries named `<kind>--<kebab-slug>.md` with kind one of principle, framework, case-study, heuristic, claim. Use the frontmatter keys exactly as the skill gives them. The body has **What it is** / **Why it works** / **When to apply** / **When NOT to apply** (mandatory, non-trivial) / **Example** / **Related**.\n21\t\n22\tStyle reference, read before writing: `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\Knowledge\\library\\strength-and-conditioning\\fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer\\case-study--isometric-sprinter-and-hurdler.md`, plus one principle file in the same folder.\n23\t\n24\t## Voices: label every one\n25\t\n26\t- **The source author's statements are plain text.**\n27\t- **Eric's views** (from `docs/method-baseline.md` or his reviews) are prefixed \"Eric's baseline:\" or \"Eric's review:\".\n28\t- **Your own reasoning, application or synthesis** is prefixed \"Inference:\".\n29\t- **Nothing unlabeled that the author did not say.** The most common failures: a judgment from Eric's review written as if the author said it; a mechanism the author never gave presented as \"why it works\" (write \"The author gives no mechanism. Inference: ...\"); an invented example.\n30\t\n31\t## Lessons from the Supertraining Chapter 1 review (2026-09-26: 30 FIX, 1 NO-GO of 41)\n32\t\n33\t- **Keep the source's internal contradictions.** When a book says X on one page and not-X on another, record both with their pages and set `contested`. Never pick one side silently. The NO-GO entry reversed the book's SJ-CMJ strength-deficit rule because the book states it both ways.\n34\t- **Name the right voice.** In co-authored books, write \"the authors\" unless a passage clearly belongs to one author. A passage that names Verkhoshansky in the third person is Siff writing.\n35\t- **Keep `proven` rare and scoped.** Scope it to the replicated core (for example, \"Hill's force-velocity curve\"), never the whole entry, and mark any disputed sentence inside it. Four of eight `proven` ratings were lowered.\n36\t- **No mechanism the source does not give.** About a dozen \"Why it works\" sections supplied physiology the book never states. Label it \"Inference:\" or leave it out.\n37\t- **Do not add \"well established\" or \"modern research shows\" without a citation.** Name the paper, or delete the phrase.\n38\t\n39\t## Lessons from the 2026-09-27 review wave (Supertraining Ch2-3, Issurin Ch3, Bondarchuk, Evans, Old Bull)\n40\t\n41\t- **Invented Examples are the most common failure.** 34 of 36 Supertraining Ch2-3a Examples were unlabelled inventions. Use the source's own example where it gives one; otherwise start the Example with \"Inference:\".\n42\t- **\"Verified against the page image\" means you opened the image.** Four Issurin Ch3 entries claimed it for figures they had misread (Fig 3.7 inverted, a Table 3.7 row swapped). Sparse slides are not \"bare diagrams\" until you have looked.\n43\t- **Name the population and method the source gives.** Bondarchuk's data are 7,000+ native and foreign athletes, questionnaires, r >= 0.349, steroid-free; entries that said \"Soviet athletes\" were wrong everywhere.\n44\t- **Check what a cited paper actually says.** Issurin 2016's critique targets Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model, not Issurin's A-T-R; Herzog & Leonard 2000 shows force enhancement, against the book's sentence. When the book misreports a study, record both.\n45\t- **No review history in entries.** \"Prior version\", \"corrected from a NO-GO finding\" and similar belong in `staging/fixes_*_applied.md`, never in the entry body.\n46\t- **Fix logs must match the files.** One fixer log claimed fixes that were not made. Fixers run their own grep checks and paste the output.\n47\t- **Section headers use the vault form** `**What it is** —` (with the em dash). Do not add suffixes such as \": mandatory\". Em dashes are banned only in titles and body text. Never wrap a `[[wikilink]]` across lines.\n48\t\n49\t## Evidence\n50\t\n51\t- Every number, definition and quote carries a checkable locator: PDF page (and printed page if it differs), or file plus timestamp. Never write \"confirmed in PDF\" unless you opened that page.\n52\t- Never invent a number. Keep ambiguity visible: mark unclear audio or OCR as unclear, and do not harmonize two conflicting numbers from the source; record both with their locators.\n53\t- OCR sources (`staging/ocr/*.md`): pages listed as low_confidence in the header must be checked against the page image before any number from them is cited. Use the Read tool on the PDF page, or render it.\n54\t- **OCR confidence scores miss the worst errors** (checked 2026-09-27). Zatsiorsky 2e: the OCR interleaves the two columns and the sidebars line by line, so its text is not readable prose; use it only to find content, and read and quote from the page image. *CFTS*: single-column prose that mostly reads in order, but upside-down lines come out as gibberish, some lines are dropped, and figures are scrambled, all at \"0 low-confidence pages\". For any OCR source, quote only after checking the page image, and read every figure and table from the image.\n55\t\n56\t## Confidence\n57\t\n58\t- Practitioner courses and lectures: ceiling `supported`.\n59\t- Books: `proven` only for established, widely replicated physiology or mechanics. Soviet-era or single-lab empirical findings and training prescriptions: `supported` at most. `untested` for single cases and anecdotes. `contested` where another library source or modern evidence disagrees (say which).\n60\t\n61\t## Dedup and converge\n62\t\n63\tBefore each entry, grep `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\Knowledge\\library` and the staging library (`C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\staging\\library`) for the concept. If it is covered, add a new entry only for a genuinely new angle, and link the existing one in Related. List reciprocal-link suggestions in your final report. Never edit files in the vault or another agent's staging folder.\n64\t\n65\t## Baylor context\n66\t\n67\tRead `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\docs\\method-baseline.md` (approved). Use it in When to apply, labelled \"Eric's baseline:\". Department names: Athletic Performance, Athletic Medicine, Applied Performance. Plain style, no em dashes. Use commas, colons and parentheses.\n68\t\n69\t## Scope and output\n70\t\n71\t- Write ONLY into your assigned staging folder. No git, no vault edits.\n72\t- Stop at a chapter or unit boundary. It is better to finish fewer chapters well; the `_book.md` checkpoint lets the next batch resume.\n73\t- Final reply: entries by kind and by confidence; chapters or units done and pending with page ranges; reciprocal-link suggestions; unclear or contradictory spots in the source; anything you could not read.\n74\t# Review: Supertraining Chapter 6d (6.12-6.17), 25 entries\n75\t\n76\tReviewer: Claude Opus (independent, instructed to falsify). Date: 2026-10-03.\n77\tScope: the 25 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6d.txt`, folder `staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`.\n78\tSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx 379-403 (printed 368-392), plus PDF 305, 340, 375 and 378 for cross-references. PDF idx = printed + 11.\n79\t\n80\tMethod: text layer for every quote (all 25 entries' quotes compared against it). Page images opened at 200 dpi: PDF 383, 386, 387, 388, 389, 392, 393, 394, 395, 396, 397, 398, 399, 400. PDF 379-382, 384, 385, 390, 391 and 401-403 were checked by text layer and italic-span scan only; none carries a figure or table value an entry depends on (Table 6.6 at PDF 391 is cited by name only). Fig 6.39 at 300 dpi plus 450-dpi crops of both panels (`_renders/r6d_p395_upper.png`, `_renders/r6d_p395_lower.png`); Fig 6.32(b) at 450 dpi (`_renders/r6d_p383_figb.png`); PDF 392 and 393 (Tables 6.7-6.12) read from the images. Italics checked with a font-span scan (PDF 379, 382, 384, 385, 399, 400, 402: every \"italics in the original\" claim is correct). Every \"Eric's baseline:\" quote checked against `docs/method-baseline.md` lines 30, 32, 33, 34, 74, 75, 89, 101, 103, 105, 106, 115, 116, 117, 181, 182, 183, 185, 186, 187, 188: all quotes, line numbers and tags are correct. The Issurin 2016 brief (`research-papers/issurin-2016-...md`), the Issurin stage entry, and the Evans/Bompa cycle-types entry were opened, and every quote from them matches. All wikilinks in the 25 files resolve. Em dashes appear only in the six section headers of each file.\n81\t\n82\tCounts: **14 GO, 11 FIX, 0 NO-GO.**\n83\t\n84\t## Per-entry verdicts\n85\t\n86\t| File | Verdict | Problem (entry vs source, with page) | Exact correction |\n87\t|---|---|---|---|\n88\t| principle--programme-objective-approach-quantified-goals-dictate-training | GO | None found. | Checked: the three objective components, the \"if-then\" sentence, 6.12.1 \"minimal expenditure of energy\" and \"structural organisation... time management\", and the 6.11 closing \"probability... much smaller\", all at PDF 379 / p.368; *objectives* is italic (span check); baseline line 32 quote and tag. |\n89\t| claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes | FIX | (1) The \"Contested by Issurin\" paragraph sets Issurin against the claim, but the brief it cites says the 2016 review \"repeats the book's critique of traditional planning.\" Issurin agrees that traditional complex training falls short for elite athletes. He disputes the remedy (one target per block versus a minimal number). The brief also records Issurin 2016 saying \"no evidence that amateur athletes have any serious restrictions\" with highly concentrated workloads (brief, p. 11), which contests the book's \"complex for beginners\" limit (PDF 380). (2) \"28-study support\" is given without the brief's caveat that the count is author-curated from a non-systematic narrative review. (3) Minor: the early-studies list puts Matveyev 1970 with Krestovnikov 1951 to Korobkov 1960. At PDF 380, Matveyev 1970 is cited separately, for parallel loading improving functions \"in the necessary balance.\" | Rewrite the contested paragraph as follows. \"Issurin 2016 agrees that traditional complex training falls short for elite athletes (the brief: the review repeats the book's critique of traditional planning). He contests the remedy: his multi-targeted blocks train a minimal number of targets, not one. He also reports no evidence that amateurs are restricted by concentrated workloads, against the book's 'only to beginners or athletes of average qualification' (PDF 380). The 28-study count is Issurin's own curated, non-systematic synthesis (brief, p. 11).\" Move Matveyev 1970 into its own clause. Keep `contested`. |\n90\t| claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving | GO | The contested reason is accurate and correctly scoped. It targets Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model in team sports and quotes brief p. 6 exactly. It does not overreach to block periodisation in general. | Checked: \"up to 2 months\" and \"4-12 weeks\" a paragraph apart, mechanism sentence, block-selection passage (PDF 381 / p.370); citation list, \"most appropriate for highly qualified athletes\", 13-15% (PDF 382 / p.371); 20% \"over a macrocycle\" (PDF 305 / p.294); Issurin brief p. 6 quotes; baseline line 183. Optional: add the brief's p. 11 amateur-athlete line as a second contest of \"most appropriate for highly qualified athletes.\" |\n91\t| case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities | FIX | (1) In When NOT to apply, \"Both groups were qualified track athletes\" contradicts the same sentence's \"the long jumpers' level is not stated\" (PDF 382 says only \"women long jumpers\"). (2) Dedup miss: the 6c entry `principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum` (6.10, PDF 374) cites Antonova 1982 and Levchenko 1982 for the same dip-then-rebound finding and is not in Related. (3) Source note not recorded: the book dates Levchenko as 1980 (PDF 382), 1981 (PDF 381) and 1982 (PDF 374). | Change to \"Levchenko's subjects were 'highly qualified sprinters'; the long jumpers' level is not stated.\" Add the 6c principle to Related, with one line on the new angle (this page adds force-time take-off data, stiffness and the injury statement). Add: \"The book dates Levchenko 1980 here, 1981 at PDF 381 and 1982 at PDF 374.\" Checked otherwise: all quotes at PDF 382 / p.371; *cybernetic periodisation* italic; 6.2.2 quote at PDF 340 / p.329; baseline line 74. |\n92\t| heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block | GO | Duplicate check against 6c `heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window`. The entry names that overlap and adds a genuinely new angle: the learning-then-perfecting split, the daily \"freshness\" rule, and the specific low-intensity means (PDF 383). | Checked: italic \"Although there is a primary emphasis...\" (PDF 382); \"too late were it to commence in the competitive period\" (PDF 382); two stages and the means list (PDF 383 / p.372); Fig 6.32(a) image (block, dotted line, Learning/Perfecting, the STATE curve below zero then highest at right); 6.14.1 technique sentence (PDF 394); baseline line 101 (cosmetic: the quote ends with a doubled quotation mark). |\n93\t| heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks | FIX | (1) The book's other figure is missing. Chapter 5 says gains after 4-8 week blocks \"may occur most commonly after a latency period of 1-3 weeks\" (PDF 305 / p.294). This page says the processes need \"far longer than the 1-2 weeks usually allocated\" (PDF 383). They likely measure different things (latency to the first gains versus time for the adaptive processes to finish; that reading is reviewer inference), but the brief requires both figures to be recorded side by side. The 6c entry `heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next` already draws this latency-versus-duration distinction. (2) Partial duplicate: \"do not fill it with more volume\" is 6c `heuristic--time-technique...` rule 2 (\"The volume of training work, therefore, should not be increased during the period of emergence of the LDTE,\" PDF 375), which is not linked. The new angle (critique of the usual 1-2 week unload, extra volume as a \"barrier\" to restoration) is real, so the entry stays. | Add to Why it works: \"Chapter 5 gives a 1-3 week latency before gains appear (PDF 305 / p.294). Inference: that is the time to the first gains, while this page is about how long the reduced-volume stage must last, so the two are recorded side by side rather than as a contradiction (the same distinction is drawn in [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]).\" Add `[[heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window]]` to Related and name rule 2 as the same instruction. Checked otherwise: all quotes at PDF 383 / p.372; 4-12 weeks and the T2 duration at PDF 375; Bompa 3:1 link resolves. |\n94\t| framework--speed-sport-block-organisation-and-two-glycolytic-peaks | FIX | Fig 6.32(b) is misread. The entry says \"most curves (Vmax, S-E, Speed, STATE, An-Gly) dip or stay low during it.\" At 450 dpi, two curves rise during the shaded block. One climbs steadily and ends at the S-E label. The other peaks mid-block and falls, then rises to a second hump, and ends at An-Gly; that shape matches the text's first glycolytic peak \"timed to end near the conclusion\" of the block (PDF 384). Two curves dip below the time axis during the block and then climb (ending at Vmax and STATE). One curve starts only at the block's end, rises steeply to a mid-panel peak and falls back (ending at Speed), which matches \"Speed work begins only at the beginning of the LDTE.\" | Replace the figure paragraph with: \"Fig 6.32(b), read from the image (no numeric scale): during the shaded block two curves fall below the time axis and then climb (ending at Vmax, highest, and STATE). Two rise: one steadily (ending at S-E) and one to a mid-block peak and back down, then to a second hump (ending at An-Gly), matching the text's two glycolytic peaks. One curve starts only at the block's end, peaks and then falls back (ending at Speed). The right-edge labels are the only certain curve identities; the middle crossings are hard to follow.\" Checked otherwise: all quotes at PDF 384 / p.373; caption at PDF 383; baseline lines 116 and 117 with tags. |\n95\t| framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound | GO | Optional: \"Both variants sit in a chapter whose models all use concentrated loading, which Issurin 2016 questions.\" Variant A is the \"traditional form\" (PDF 385), so the Issurin challenge fits only Variant B. | Checked: italic mesocycle definition, \"3 and 4 week stages\", \"4-6 weeks\" (PDF 385 / p.374); Variant B points and the qualification rule (PDF 385-386); \"usually 3-5 months\" (PDF 387); Fig 6.33(a)-(c) image (A volume peaks mid-cycle and GPP peaks early and declines; B volume peaks early, fitness state sigmoid, shaded GPP grows across the cycle); baseline line 106. |\n96\t| claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation | FIX | The \"Contested inside the book\" paragraph treats aim 1 and the monocyclic recommendation as answers to the same question. They are not clearly the same. Aim 1 asks for \"at least two large mesocycles... even in sports which traditionally have one main competitive period and use monocyclic periodisation\" (PDF 389). So aim 1 itself separates the number of mesocycles from the number of competition peaks. The long-duration model at PDF 396 is \"monocyclic... with one competitive stage\" but also \"within a monocycle... a two-stage organisation of strength and speed training.\" Only PDF 387 equates two large mesocycles with \"this form of bi-cyclic organisation.\" The tension is a terminology slip at PDF 387, not two opposed positions. Fig 6.34 reading is correct. | Rewrite the paragraph as: \"The book uses 'two large mesocycles' and 'bi-cyclic' interchangeably at PDF 387 but separately at PDF 389 (two mesocycles even with one main competitive period) and PDF 396 (two strength stages inside a monocycle). Read with PDF 389 and 396, the two passages answer different questions: how many large mesocycles, and how many competition peaks. The apparent conflict comes from the PDF 387 wording.\" Keep `contested`, and state that its basis is the Issurin disagreement (stages of \"about two months\", four to seven a season, against two mesocycles of 3-5 months; both quotes verified in the vault entry). |\n97\t| case-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength | FIX | Fig 6.35, 6.36(a) bars and 6.37 bars and all figure values verified (aerobic week: VO2 max about 87% to about 107%, crossing 100% at about 36 h; Training effect about 93%, crossing at about 12-24 h, about 101-102% at 72 h; anaerobic week: VO2 max about 113% at about 12 h falling to about 106%; Training effect about 96%, crossing at about 30-36 h, about 102-103% at 72 h). (1) The second \"unclear spot\" is not a conflict. Text: aerobic potential \"rose over a 12 hour span and then diminished\" (PDF 388). The figure shows exactly that, about 113% down to about 106%; \"diminished\" does not mean below the initial level. (2) When to apply: \"expect roughly three days for the stressed quality to come back above baseline\" uses the text's 72 h, but in both panels the stressed quality crosses 100% at about 30-36 h. | Delete the sentence that sets \"rose over a 12 hour span and then diminished\" against the figure, or reword it as \"consistent with the figure.\" Change When to apply to: \"the text gives 72 h for restoration; the figure shows the stressed quality back above its initial level at about 30-36 h and still rising at 72 h.\" Keep the first unclear spot (anaerobic \"after 72 hours\" versus the crossing at about 30-36 h): it is correct. Optional missing point: the authors call the experimental findings on microcycles \"currently scant\" (PDF 387). |\n98\t| heuristic--use-low-intensity-gpp-as-restoration-in-concentrated-loading-models | GO | None found. | Checked: Variant B GPP passage (PDF 386 / p.375); explosive-model GPP passage and sports massage (PDF 394 / p.383); aerobic restoration sentence (PDF 395); Fig 6.33(b)/(c) GPP shapes against the image; the \"active-rest\" mechanism is labelled Inference. |\n99\t| framework--seven-primary-aims-of-programming-training | GO | Aim 6's bold paraphrase \"Lead with SPP\" stands in for the book's \"Transcending the emphasis of SPP.\" The entry declares that it paraphrases and quotes the operative sentence. | Checked: aims 1-7 and the conditional sentence (PDF 389-390 / pp.378-379, image); baseline line 182 quote and tag. |\n100\t| framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models | GO | None found. | Checked: descriptive and prescriptive definitions, qualitative and quantitative, conventions 1-6 (PDF 390-391); \"fairly random deviations\" and the double-negative sentence (PDF 391); Table 6.6 Stepanova et al 1983; Siff-framing line labelled Inference; baseline line 32. |\n101\t| case-study--tabachnik-100m-sprinter-model-characteristics | FIX | All Table 6.8 and 6.9 values match the PDF 392 image. The writer's flagged sums are correct (men 5.70 + 4.45 = 10.15 s; women 6.15 + 5.00 = 11.15 s; Table 6.9's second 50 m of 4.30-4.40 s against Table 6.8's 4.45 s). Missed inconsistency, visible on the same page: Table 6.8 men's \"5m run from low start\" is 1.18 s, while Table 6.9's 15 m for the same 10.0 s goal is 1.16-1.18 s. 15 m cannot take the same time as 5 m from the same start, so the tables time sections differently (or one is misprinted), and the book does not say which. | Add to When NOT to apply: \"Table 6.8's 5 m from a low start (1.18 s, men) equals Table 6.9's 15 m (1.16-1.18 s) for the same 10.0 s goal (PDF 392 / p.381); the two tables cannot use the same start and timing, and the book does not explain.\" Optional: Table 6.9's 15 m step from the 10.2 s goal to the 10.5 s goal (1.18-1.20 to 1.26-1.28) is far larger than the other steps. |\n102\t| framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports | FIX | Fig 6.39 read at 300 dpi and 450-dpi crops. Phases (I to late month 4, II about month 5, III about months 6-8, IV from late month 8), block 1 position, block-2-to-block-1 height of about two thirds, the Starting/Explosive crossing at about months 8-9, and the right-edge order are all correct. Errors: (1) \"all three strength curves... fall during block 1.\" They fall only in the first half: the two upper curves bump at about month 1, bottom out at about month 2 (Maximum strength also at about month 2), and are already rising through month 3 while block 1 is still running. (2) \"rise to a first peak at about months 4-5 (around the phase I/II boundary).\" All three peak at about month 5, inside phase II, just after the first dashed line. (3) \"Technique... falls during block 2\" is wrong for most of block 2. Technique peaks at about month 4 (just after block 1), falls through phase II, bottoms early in block 2 (about month 6-7), then rises through the rest of block 2. It is already above its first peak by the block's end, peaks at about month 10 and declines toward month 11. (4) Block 2 spans from just into month 6 to just past the month 7/8 tick, so \"about months 6-7, ending early in month 8.\" (5) The contested reasoning stops short. The brief line the entry cites (p. 11: CU \"better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality\") describes jumping and throwing, which is this model's own domain. Issurin's challenge (p. 6: team, combat, swimming) applies to the transfer to football, not to the model in its stated domain. The entry does not say so. The GPP/Speed \"cannot be told apart\" call is acceptable under the no-guessing rule. | Replace the upper-panel sentences with: \"Upper panel: the two upper curves bump at about month 1; all three curves are lowest at about month 2 and already rising in the last month of block 1; they peak at about month 5, inside phase II; they dip again during block 2 (lowest about month 7); then they climb through phase IV to their highest values at month 11.\" Replace the Technique sentence with: \"Technique rises inside block 1, peaks at about month 4, falls through phase II to a low early in block 2 (about months 6-7), rises through the rest of block 2, peaks at about month 10 and declines toward month 11.\" Add: \"GPP ends above Speed at the right edge (labels); their middle course is unclear.\" Block 2: \"about months 6-7, ending early in month 8.\" In When NOT to apply, add: \"Issurin's p. 11 remark favours the concentrated model in narrow-quality sports such as jumping, the model's own domain; the challenge applies to transferring it to team sports such as football.\" Keep `contested`, with the reason scoped to the football transfer. |\n103\t| framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports | GO | The Fig 6.46 caption-label mismatch is described correctly (caption: \"A is the block of concentrated strength loading\"; image: \"GPP\" under A, \"Strength Load\" under B). | Checked: 6.14.2 and 6.14.3 quotes (PDF 395-397); Fig 6.40 (block 1 about months 2-4, block 2 about months 6-7, phases) and Fig 6.41 (phase I months 1-6, blocks about months 2-3 and 6-7, aerobic capacity peaking near the start of phase II) against the images, with the month numerals read as month ends; Gilyazova 7-14%; baseline line 115. |\n104\t| claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity | FIX | The Inference \"A football season of games is shorter than the model's 4-5 month competition period\" is doubtful. Late August to a December or January postseason is about 4-4.5 months, comparable to the model's period. | Change to \"A football season (late August to the postseason) is comparable in length to the model's 4-5 month competition period, but football is not an endurance sport and the authors do not extend the rule to team sports.\" Checked otherwise: quotes at PDF 396 / p.385; baseline lines 105, 181 and 187 with tags; PDF 394 second-block sentence. |\n105\t| framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports | FIX | (1) Wrong use of a citation. When to apply calls boxing and swimming \"the... examples the authors cite for tri-cyclic forms (PDF 387).\" At PDF 387 they are sports that \"traditionally employ\" tri-cyclic forms, where the authors say bi-cyclic periodisation \"may also be applied.\" The authors cite them as candidates for moving away from tri-cyclic. (2) Inconsistent confidence. This model's stated domain is \"individual and one-on-one sports,\" and Issurin 2016 names combat sports directly (brief, p. 6). Yet the entry is `supported`, while the explosive-strength model (whose home domain Issurin's p. 11 favours) is `contested`. Contest should follow the stated domain. The entry also uses the concentrated blocks: \"This model... three strength blocks\" (PDF 397). | (1) Rewrite as: \"The authors list boxing and swimming as sports that traditionally use tri-cyclic forms but may use bi-cyclic ones (PDF 387). They name team sports and one-on-one events as cases where tri-cyclic 'may have to be used, as dictated by the competition calendar.'\" (2) Set `confidence: contested` with the reason \"Issurin 2016 judges concentrated-unidirectional blocks under-supported for combat sports, this model's stated domain (brief, p. 6).\" Or keep `supported` and explicitly justify why the explosive model is contested and this one is not. Fig 6.42 reading verified (phases, three blocks of decreasing height, right-edge labels). |\n106\t| heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work | FIX | The Fig 6.45/6.46 caption-label mismatch is described correctly. But \"Which wave is the strength block cannot be settled from the page\" ignores the entry's own quote. PDF 399 says the strength block comes \"following the groundwork laid by general physical preparation (GPP).\" That matches the printed labels (GPP under the earlier small wave A, Strength Load under the large wave B), so the caption is the likelier error. Fig 6.43 and 6.44 readings are correct. | Change to: \"The text's sequence (GPP groundwork, then the strength block, PDF 399) matches the printed labels. Inference: the caption is the likelier error. Still unclear on the page.\" Checked otherwise: PDF 397 \"More Advanced Use\" quote; PDF 398 A-D stage text, \"phase B\" as printed, Matveyev non-discreteness; PDF 399 incompatibility sentence; baseline line 105 with tag. |\n107\t| principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice | GO | None found. | Checked: PDF 399 / p.388 passage; aim 7 (PDF 390); italic \"competitions, of course...\" (PDF 399); PDF 382 \"too late\" quote; baseline line 103; links resolve. |\n108\t| heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates | GO | Suggestion only: link the 6.2.2 entry `heuristic--build-the-annual-plan-backwards-from-competitions`, which covers related calendar-first planning. | Checked: rules 1-4 and the italic recuperation sentence (PDF 400 / p.389); baseline line 185; Issurin \"about two months\" verified in the vault entry. |\n109\t| framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training | GO | Suggestion only: link `heuristic--build-the-annual-plan-backwards-from-competitions` (6.2.2). | Checked: complexity sentence (PDF 400); steps 1-8 and the contingency line (PDF 401 / p.390); baseline line 183. |\n110\t| heuristic--test-monthly-under-constant-conditions-and-compare-with-targets | GO | None found. | Checked: 6.16 guidelines (PDF 402 / p.391); \"largely random and at times unpredictable\" (PDF 378 / p.367, 6.11); baseline lines 30, 34, 74 and 75 with tags. |\n111\t| framework--four-documents-for-programming-and-controlling-training | GO | None found. The line 89 tension is handled well and labelled. | Checked: italic \"keeping of meticulous training records\" and documents 1-2 (PDF 402); documents 3-4 and the subjective-signs passage (PDF 403); baseline lines 30 and 89. |\n112\t| principle--programming-is-an-art-on-principles-not-an-algorithm | GO | None found. | Checked: 6.17 passages (PDF 403 / p.392); 6.12.1 \"creative flexibility\" (PDF 379); baseline lines 183 and 33 with tags. |\n113\t\n114\t## Missing (important ideas in PDF 379-403 that no entry captures)\n115\t\n116\t1. **Table 6.11, javelin model characteristics (Konstantinov 1978, PDF 393 / p.382).** Strength and power norms for international-class throwers: snatch body mass + 15 kg (men) and + 10 kg (women), back squat 2 x and 1.5 x body mass, standing triple jump 10.20 and 7.80 m, vertical jump 95 and 70 cm, standing broad jump 3.20 and 2.80 m, plus throwing-volume norms (170 and 140 average-intensity throws, 35 and 30 above-average). This is the closest thing in the chapter to a power-athlete strength norm table.\n117\t2. **Tables 6.7 and 6.10, middle-distance models (Maximenko 1979, PDF 392-393).** They include flexibility (stride and straddle splits, in degrees) as model characteristics, and a defined special-endurance test: 2 x (4 x 400 m), 2 min between reps, 8-10 min between series.\n118\t3. **Table 6.12, juvenile sprinter norms by age 11-16 (Alabin & Yushkevitch 1978, PDF 393).** It has a source error worth recording: \"Standing quintuple jump (cm)\" with values of 9.25-13.70, which must be metres.\n119\t4. **The authors' own evidence caveat on microcycles (PDF 387).** \"the currently scant experimental findings,\" and \"large unidirectional loading sharply reduces the capability of displaying those qualities and abilities which facilitate the effective and safe execution of training programmes.\" This bears on the confidence of the microcycle case study.\n120\t5. **Long-duration timing (PDF 396).** Because low-intensity work predominates, \"the optimal date for increasing the current adaptation reserves is deferred.\" This is the rationale for the long 6-month preparatory period, and the endurance entry omits it.\n121\t\n122\t## Notes on the special-attention items\n123\t\n124\t- **Fig 6.39 (6.14.1).** Phase boundaries and block positions in the entry hold up. Three curve-timing claims do not (see the FIX row): the strength curves start recovering inside block 1; the first peak is in phase II at about month 5; and Technique rises through most of block 2. For football planning, the image also shows that the model's strength indicators peak at the very end of phase IV (month 11). That fits Eric's November priority window (baseline line 185) better than an early-season peak would. This is my reading of the image, not a statement by the authors.\n125\t- **Contested entries and Issurin 2016.** The brief confirms the critique targets Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model, not block periodisation in general, and none of the four entries overreaches to all block periodisation. The problems are elsewhere: the complex-training entry has the direction of disagreement wrong (Issurin shares the critique of complex training); the explosive-strength entry does not say that Issurin's p. 11 favours the model in its own domain; and the tri-cyclic entry, whose domain Issurin names, is not contested.\n126\t- **Bi-cyclic \"same question\".** Aim 1 (PDF 389) is about the number of large mesocycles and explicitly allows one main competitive period. The monocyclic recommendation (PDF 387, 396) is about the number of competition peaks. Only PDF 387's wording merges the two. Recommend recording it as a terminology tension, with `contested` resting on Issurin.\n127\t- **Tables 6.8 and 6.9, Fig 6.36(b).** Every value is correct. One flagged inconsistency was spurious (12 h rise then \"diminished\") and one was missed (5 m vs 15 m).\n128\t- **Fig 6.45/6.46 caption vs label.** Described correctly in both entries that mention it. The text supports the labels over the caption for Fig 6.45.\n129\t\n130\t## Overall verdict\n131\t\n132\t**GO AFTER FIXES.** Required fixes:\n133\t1. bi-cyclic explosive model: correct the Fig 6.39 curve timing (recovery inside block 1, first peak about month 5 in phase II, Technique rising through most of block 2, block 2 ending early in month 8) and scope the Issurin contest to the football transfer.\n134\t2. tricyclic: correct the boxing/swimming citation and make the confidence consistent with Issurin's combat-sport critique.\n135\t3. two-mesocycles: rewrite the internal-contest paragraph as a PDF 387 terminology tension; base `contested` on Issurin.\n136\t4. complex-training: Issurin agrees with the critique of complex training; the contest is the remedy and the beginners limit; add the brief's caveat on the 28 studies; separate Matveyev 1970.\n137\t5. speed-sport: correct the Fig 6.32(b) reading (S-E and An-Gly rise during the block).\n138\t6. unidirectional microcycles: drop the spurious 12 h \"conflict\"; the restoration time is 72 h in the text and about 30-36 h in the figure.\n139\t7. Tabachnik: add the 5 m vs 15 m inconsistency.\n140\t8. loading-reduction: record PDF 305's 1-3 week latency beside \"far longer than 1-2 weeks\"; link the 6c time-technique entry (rule 2).\n141\t9. Antonova/Levchenko: fix the self-contradictory sentence; link the 6c LDTE principle; note the Levchenko dates.\n142\t10. prolonged-competition: change \"shorter\" to \"comparable.\"\n143\t11. follow-a-concentrated-block: note that the text supports the figure labels over the caption.\n144\t"}]},"uuid":"ecbd1686-a488-424f-b117-f64b83a8748e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:07:39.906Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tool-results/bqbv3yx3j.txt","content":"You are an independent reviewer in Flint's ingest review gate. Another model wrote these entries; your job is to FALSIFY them, not confirm. Read-only on entries and sources. Write only your report file.\n\nStandards: `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\docs\\ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md` (read it first). Every rule there is a check.\n\nFor EVERY entry in scope:\n1. Every number, definition, table value and quote appears at the cited PDF page (and printed page) and means the same thing. Open the pages yourself; do not trust the entry.\n2. Attribution: plain text is the book author's own statement. Anything else must be labelled \"Eric's baseline:\", \"Eric's review:\" or \"Inference:\". Flag any unlabelled claim the author did not make, any \"why it works\" mechanism the author did not give, and any invented example.\n3. Confidence: `proven` only for established, widely replicated physiology or mechanics, so challenge every `proven`. Soviet-era or single-lab findings and prescriptions: `supported` at most. `untested` for anecdotes. `contested` needs a stated reason.\n4. \"When NOT to apply\" is present and substantive.\n5. Duplicates: flag any entry that restates an existing vault entry (grep `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\Knowledge\\library`) without a new angle.\n6. Missing: up to 5 important ideas in the covered pages that no entry captures.\n\nOutput (Markdown): a table with one row per entry: file | verdict (GO / FIX / NO-GO) | problem, quoting the entry and the source with its page | exact correction. For GO rows, list what you checked (\"checked: 2.5x at PDF 23 / p.12\"). Then the missing list. Then an overall verdict: PROMOTE, GO AFTER FIXES (list them), or NO-GO.\n# Atomization brief (shared by every Flint ingest agent)\n\nRead this whole file before writing anything. It holds the rules the Harries review taught (2026-09-26: 25 of 35 files needed fixes). The review gate will check every rule here.\n\n## Schema\n\nFollow `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\.claude\\skills\\ingest-book\\SKILL.md` exactly: `_domain.md`, `_book.md` (map and resume checkpoint, updated after EACH chapter or unit), and entries named `<kind>--<kebab-slug>.md` with kind one of principle, framework, case-study, heuristic, claim. Use the frontmatter keys exactly as the skill gives them. The body has **What it is** / **Why it works** / **When to apply** / **When NOT to apply** (mandatory, non-trivial) / **Example** / **Related**.\n\nStyle reference, read before writing: `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\Knowledge\\library\\strength-and-conditioning\\fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer\\case-study--isometric-sprinter-and-hurdler.md`, plus one principle file in the same folder.\n\n## Voices: label every one\n\n- **The source author's statements are plain text.**\n- **Eric's views** (from `docs/method-baseline.md` or his reviews) are prefixed \"Eric's baseline:\" or \"Eric's review:\".\n- **Your own reasoning, application or synthesis** is prefixed \"Inference:\".\n- **Nothing unlabeled that the author did not say.** The most common failures: a judgment from Eric's review written as if the author said it; a mechanism the author never gave presented as \"why it works\" (write \"The author gives no mechanism. Inference: ...\"); an invented example.\n\n## Lessons from the Supertraining Chapter 1 review (2026-09-26: 30 FIX, 1 NO-GO of 41)\n\n- **Keep the source's internal contradictions.** When a book says X on one page and not-X on another, record both with their pages and set `contested`. Never pick one side silently. The NO-GO entry reversed the book's SJ-CMJ strength-deficit rule because the book states it both ways.\n- **Name the right voice.** In co-authored books, write \"the authors\" unless a passage clearly belongs to one author. A passage that names Verkhoshansky in the third person is Siff writing.\n- **Keep `proven` rare and scoped.** Scope it to the replicated core (for example, \"Hill's force-velocity curve\"), never the whole entry, and mark any disputed sentence inside it. Four of eight `proven` ratings were lowered.\n- **No mechanism the source does not give.** About a dozen \"Why it works\" sections supplied physiology the book never states. Label it \"Inference:\" or leave it out.\n- **Do not add \"well established\" or \"modern research shows\" without a citation.** Name the paper, or delete the phrase.\n\n## Lessons from the 2026-09-27 review wave (Supertraining Ch2-3, Issurin Ch3, Bondarchuk, Evans, Old Bull)\n\n- **Invented Examples are the most common failure.** 34 of 36 Supertraining Ch2-3a Examples were unlabelled inventions. Use the source's own example where it gives one; otherwise start the Example with \"Inference:\".\n- **\"Verified against the page image\" means you opened the image.** Four Issurin Ch3 entries claimed it for figures they had misread (Fig 3.7 inverted, a Table 3.7 row swapped). Sparse slides are not \"bare diagrams\" until you have looked.\n- **Name the population and method the source gives.** Bondarchuk's data are 7,000+ native and foreign athletes, questionnaires, r >= 0.349, steroid-free; entries that said \"Soviet athletes\" were wrong everywhere.\n- **Check what a cited paper actually says.** Issurin 2016's critique targets Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model, not Issurin's A-T-R; Herzog & Leonard 2000 shows force enhancement, against the book's sentence. When the book misreports a study, record both.\n- **No review history in entries.** \"Prior version\", \"corrected from a NO-GO finding\" and similar belong in `staging/fixes_*_applied.md`, never in the entry body.\n- **Fix logs must match the files.** One fixer log claimed fixes that were not made. Fixers run their own grep checks and paste the output.\n- **Section headers use the vault form** `**What it is** —` (with the em dash). Do not add suffixes such as \": mandatory\". Em dashes are banned only in titles and body text. Never wrap a `[[wikilink]]` across lines.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Every number, definition and quote carries a checkable locator: PDF page (and printed page if it differs), or file plus timestamp. Never write \"confirmed in PDF\" unless you opened that page.\n- Never invent a number. Keep ambiguity visible: mark unclear audio or OCR as unclear, and do not harmonize two conflicting numbers from the source; record both with their locators.\n- OCR sources (`staging/ocr/*.md`): pages listed as low_confidence in the header must be checked against the page image before any number from them is cited. Use the Read tool on the PDF page, or render it.\n- **OCR confidence scores miss the worst errors** (checked 2026-09-27). Zatsiorsky 2e: the OCR interleaves the two columns and the sidebars line by line, so its text is not readable prose; use it only to find content, and read and quote from the page image. *CFTS*: single-column prose that mostly reads in order, but upside-down lines come out as gibberish, some lines are dropped, and figures are scrambled, all at \"0 low-confidence pages\". For any OCR source, quote only after checking the page image, and read every figure and table from the image.\n\n## Confidence\n\n- Practitioner courses and lectures: ceiling `supported`.\n- Books: `proven` only for established, widely replicated physiology or mechanics. Soviet-era or single-lab empirical findings and training prescriptions: `supported` at most. `untested` for single cases and anecdotes. `contested` where another library source or modern evidence disagrees (say which).\n\n## Dedup and converge\n\nBefore each entry, grep `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\Knowledge\\library` and the staging library (`C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\staging\\library`) for the concept. If it is covered, add a new entry only for a genuinely new angle, and link the existing one in Related. List reciprocal-link suggestions in your final report. Never edit files in the vault or another agent's staging folder.\n\n## Baylor context\n\nRead `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\docs\\method-baseline.md` (approved). Use it in When to apply, labelled \"Eric's baseline:\". Department names: Athletic Performance, Athletic Medicine, Applied Performance. Plain style, no em dashes. Use commas, colons and parentheses.\n\n## Scope and output\n\n- Write ONLY into your assigned staging folder. No git, no vault edits.\n- Stop at a chapter or unit boundary. It is better to finish fewer chapters well; the `_book.md` checkpoint lets the next batch resume.\n- Final reply: entries by kind and by confidence; chapters or units done and pending with page ranges; reciprocal-link suggestions; unclear or contradictory spots in the source; anything you could not read.\n# Review: Supertraining Chapter 6d (6.12-6.17), 25 entries\n\nReviewer: Claude Opus (independent, instructed to falsify). Date: 2026-10-03.\nScope: the 25 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6d.txt`, folder `staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`.\nSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx 379-403 (printed 368-392), plus PDF 305, 340, 375 and 378 for cross-references. PDF idx = printed + 11.\n\nMethod: text layer for every quote (all 25 entries' quotes compared against it). Page images opened at 200 dpi: PDF 383, 386, 387, 388, 389, 392, 393, 394, 395, 396, 397, 398, 399, 400. PDF 379-382, 384, 385, 390, 391 and 401-403 were checked by text layer and italic-span scan only; none carries a figure or table value an entry depends on (Table 6.6 at PDF 391 is cited by name only). Fig 6.39 at 300 dpi plus 450-dpi crops of both panels (`_renders/r6d_p395_upper.png`, `_renders/r6d_p395_lower.png`); Fig 6.32(b) at 450 dpi (`_renders/r6d_p383_figb.png`); PDF 392 and 393 (Tables 6.7-6.12) read from the images. Italics checked with a font-span scan (PDF 379, 382, 384, 385, 399, 400, 402: every \"italics in the original\" claim is correct). Every \"Eric's baseline:\" quote checked against `docs/method-baseline.md` lines 30, 32, 33, 34, 74, 75, 89, 101, 103, 105, 106, 115, 116, 117, 181, 182, 183, 185, 186, 187, 188: all quotes, line numbers and tags are correct. The Issurin 2016 brief (`research-papers/issurin-2016-...md`), the Issurin stage entry, and the Evans/Bompa cycle-types entry were opened, and every quote from them matches. All wikilinks in the 25 files resolve. Em dashes appear only in the six section headers of each file.\n\nCounts: **14 GO, 11 FIX, 0 NO-GO.**\n\n## Per-entry verdicts\n\n| File | Verdict | Problem (entry vs source, with page) | Exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| principle--programme-objective-approach-quantified-goals-dictate-training | GO | None found. | Checked: the three objective components, the \"if-then\" sentence, 6.12.1 \"minimal expenditure of energy\" and \"structural organisation... time management\", and the 6.11 closing \"probability... much smaller\", all at PDF 379 / p.368; *objectives* is italic (span check); baseline line 32 quote and tag. |\n| claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes | FIX | (1) The \"Contested by Issurin\" paragraph sets Issurin against the claim, but the brief it cites says the 2016 review \"repeats the book's critique of traditional planning.\" Issurin agrees that traditional complex training falls short for elite athletes. He disputes the remedy (one target per block versus a minimal number). The brief also records Issurin 2016 saying \"no evidence that amateur athletes have any serious restrictions\" with highly concentrated workloads (brief, p. 11), which contests the book's \"complex for beginners\" limit (PDF 380). (2) \"28-study support\" is given without the brief's caveat that the count is author-curated from a non-systematic narrative review. (3) Minor: the early-studies list puts Matveyev 1970 with Krestovnikov 1951 to Korobkov 1960. At PDF 380, Matveyev 1970 is cited separately, for parallel loading improving functions \"in the necessary balance.\" | Rewrite the contested paragraph as follows. \"Issurin 2016 agrees that traditional complex training falls short for elite athletes (the brief: the review repeats the book's critique of traditional planning). He contests the remedy: his multi-targeted blocks train a minimal number of targets, not one. He also reports no evidence that amateurs are restricted by concentrated workloads, against the book's 'only to beginners or athletes of average qualification' (PDF 380). The 28-study count is Issurin's own curated, non-systematic synthesis (brief, p. 11).\" Move Matveyev 1970 into its own clause. Keep `contested`. |\n| claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving | GO | The contested reason is accurate and correctly scoped. It targets Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model in team sports and quotes brief p. 6 exactly. It does not overreach to block periodisation in general. | Checked: \"up to 2 months\" and \"4-12 weeks\" a paragraph apart, mechanism sentence, block-selection passage (PDF 381 / p.370); citation list, \"most appropriate for highly qualified athletes\", 13-15% (PDF 382 / p.371); 20% \"over a macrocycle\" (PDF 305 / p.294); Issurin brief p. 6 quotes; baseline line 183. Optional: add the brief's p. 11 amateur-athlete line as a second contest of \"most appropriate for highly qualified athletes.\" |\n| case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities | FIX | (1) In When NOT to apply, \"Both groups were qualified track athletes\" contradicts the same sentence's \"the long jumpers' level is not stated\" (PDF 382 says only \"women long jumpers\"). (2) Dedup miss: the 6c entry `principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum` (6.10, PDF 374) cites Antonova 1982 and Levchenko 1982 for the same dip-then-rebound finding and is not in Related. (3) Source note not recorded: the book dates Levchenko as 1980 (PDF 382), 1981 (PDF 381) and 1982 (PDF 374). | Change to \"Levchenko's subjects were 'highly qualified sprinters'; the long jumpers' level is not stated.\" Add the 6c principle to Related, with one line on the new angle (this page adds force-time take-off data, stiffness and the injury statement). Add: \"The book dates Levchenko 1980 here, 1981 at PDF 381 and 1982 at PDF 374.\" Checked otherwise: all quotes at PDF 382 / p.371; *cybernetic periodisation* italic; 6.2.2 quote at PDF 340 / p.329; baseline line 74. |\n| heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block | GO | Duplicate check against 6c `heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window`. The entry names that overlap and adds a genuinely new angle: the learning-then-perfecting split, the daily \"freshness\" rule, and the specific low-intensity means (PDF 383). | Checked: italic \"Although there is a primary emphasis...\" (PDF 382); \"too late were it to commence in the competitive period\" (PDF 382); two stages and the means list (PDF 383 / p.372); Fig 6.32(a) image (block, dotted line, Learning/Perfecting, the STATE curve below zero then highest at right); 6.14.1 technique sentence (PDF 394); baseline line 101 (cosmetic: the quote ends with a doubled quotation mark). |\n| heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks | FIX | (1) The book's other figure is missing. Chapter 5 says gains after 4-8 week blocks \"may occur most commonly after a latency period of 1-3 weeks\" (PDF 305 / p.294). This page says the processes need \"far longer than the 1-2 weeks usually allocated\" (PDF 383). They likely measure different things (latency to the first gains versus time for the adaptive processes to finish; that reading is reviewer inference), but the brief requires both figures to be recorded side by side. The 6c entry `heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next` already draws this latency-versus-duration distinction. (2) Partial duplicate: \"do not fill it with more volume\" is 6c `heuristic--time-technique...` rule 2 (\"The volume of training work, therefore, should not be increased during the period of emergence of the LDTE,\" PDF 375), which is not linked. The new angle (critique of the usual 1-2 week unload, extra volume as a \"barrier\" to restoration) is real, so the entry stays. | Add to Why it works: \"Chapter 5 gives a 1-3 week latency before gains appear (PDF 305 / p.294). Inference: that is the time to the first gains, while this page is about how long the reduced-volume stage must last, so the two are recorded side by side rather than as a contradiction (the same distinction is drawn in [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]).\" Add `[[heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window]]` to Related and name rule 2 as the same instruction. Checked otherwise: all quotes at PDF 383 / p.372; 4-12 weeks and the T2 duration at PDF 375; Bompa 3:1 link resolves. |\n| framework--speed-sport-block-organisation-and-two-glycolytic-peaks | FIX | Fig 6.32(b) is misread. The entry says \"most curves (Vmax, S-E, Speed, STATE, An-Gly) dip or stay low during it.\" At 450 dpi, two curves rise during the shaded block. One climbs steadily and ends at the S-E label. The other peaks mid-block and falls, then rises to a second hump, and ends at An-Gly; that shape matches the text's first glycolytic peak \"timed to end near the conclusion\" of the block (PDF 384). Two curves dip below the time axis during the block and then climb (ending at Vmax and STATE). One curve starts only at the block's end, rises steeply to a mid-panel peak and falls back (ending at Speed), which matches \"Speed work begins only at the beginning of the LDTE.\" | Replace the figure paragraph with: \"Fig 6.32(b), read from the image (no numeric scale): during the shaded block two curves fall below the time axis and then climb (ending at Vmax, highest, and STATE). Two rise: one steadily (ending at S-E) and one to a mid-block peak and back down, then to a second hump (ending at An-Gly), matching the text's two glycolytic peaks. One curve starts only at the block's end, peaks and then falls back (ending at Speed). The right-edge labels are the only certain curve identities; the middle crossings are hard to follow.\" Checked otherwise: all quotes at PDF 384 / p.373; caption at PDF 383; baseline lines 116 and 117 with tags. |\n| framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound | GO | Optional: \"Both variants sit in a chapter whose models all use concentrated loading, which Issurin 2016 questions.\" Variant A is the \"traditional form\" (PDF 385), so the Issurin challenge fits only Variant B. | Checked: italic mesocycle definition, \"3 and 4 week stages\", \"4-6 weeks\" (PDF 385 / p.374); Variant B points and the qualification rule (PDF 385-386); \"usually 3-5 months\" (PDF 387); Fig 6.33(a)-(c) image (A volume peaks mid-cycle and GPP peaks early and declines; B volume peaks early, fitness state sigmoid, shaded GPP grows across the cycle); baseline line 106. |\n| claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation | FIX | The \"Contested inside the book\" paragraph treats aim 1 and the monocyclic recommendation as answers to the same question. They are not clearly the same. Aim 1 asks for \"at least two large mesocycles... even in sports which traditionally have one main competitive period and use monocyclic periodisation\" (PDF 389). So aim 1 itself separates the number of mesocycles from the number of competition peaks. The long-duration model at PDF 396 is \"monocyclic... with one competitive stage\" but also \"within a monocycle... a two-stage organisation of strength and speed training.\" Only PDF 387 equates two large mesocycles with \"this form of bi-cyclic organisation.\" The tension is a terminology slip at PDF 387, not two opposed positions. Fig 6.34 reading is correct. | Rewrite the paragraph as: \"The book uses 'two large mesocycles' and 'bi-cyclic' interchangeably at PDF 387 but separately at PDF 389 (two mesocycles even with one main competitive period) and PDF 396 (two strength stages inside a monocycle). Read with PDF 389 and 396, the two passages answer different questions: how many large mesocycles, and how many competition peaks. The apparent conflict comes from the PDF 387 wording.\" Keep `contested`, and state that its basis is the Issurin disagreement (stages of \"about two months\", four to seven a season, against two mesocycles of 3-5 months; both quotes verified in the vault entry). |\n| case-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength | FIX | Fig 6.35, 6.36(a) bars and 6.37 bars and all figure values verified (aerobic week: VO2 max about 87% to about 107%, crossing 100% at about 36 h; Training effect about 93%, crossing at about 12-24 h, about 101-102% at 72 h; anaerobic week: VO2 max about 113% at about 12 h falling to about 106%; Training effect about 96%, crossing at about 30-36 h, about 102-103% at 72 h). (1) The second \"unclear spot\" is not a conflict. Text: aerobic potential \"rose over a 12 hour span and then diminished\" (PDF 388). The figure shows exactly that, about 113% down to about 106%; \"diminished\" does not mean below the initial level. (2) When to apply: \"expect roughly three days for the stressed quality to come back above baseline\" uses the text's 72 h, but in both panels the stressed quality crosses 100% at about 30-36 h. | Delete the sentence that sets \"rose over a 12 hour span and then diminished\" against the figure, or reword it as \"consistent with the figure.\" Change When to apply to: \"the text gives 72 h for restoration; the figure shows the stressed quality back above its initial level at about 30-36 h and still rising at 72 h.\" Keep the first unclear spot (anaerobic \"after 72 hours\" versus the crossing at about 30-36 h): it is correct. Optional missing point: the authors call the experimental findings on microcycles \"currently scant\" (PDF 387). |\n| heuristic--use-low-intensity-gpp-as-restoration-in-concentrated-loading-models | GO | None found. | Checked: Variant B GPP passage (PDF 386 / p.375); explosive-model GPP passage and sports massage (PDF 394 / p.383); aerobic restoration sentence (PDF 395); Fig 6.33(b)/(c) GPP shapes against the image; the \"active-rest\" mechanism is labelled Inference. |\n| framework--seven-primary-aims-of-programming-training | GO | Aim 6's bold paraphrase \"Lead with SPP\" stands in for the book's \"Transcending the emphasis of SPP.\" The entry declares that it paraphrases and quotes the operative sentence. | Checked: aims 1-7 and the conditional sentence (PDF 389-390 / pp.378-379, image); baseline line 182 quote and tag. |\n| framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models | GO | None found. | Checked: descriptive and prescriptive definitions, qualitative and quantitative, conventions 1-6 (PDF 390-391); \"fairly random deviations\" and the double-negative sentence (PDF 391); Table 6.6 Stepanova et al 1983; Siff-framing line labelled Inference; baseline line 32. |\n| case-study--tabachnik-100m-sprinter-model-characteristics | FIX | All Table 6.8 and 6.9 values match the PDF 392 image. The writer's flagged sums are correct (men 5.70 + 4.45 = 10.15 s; women 6.15 + 5.00 = 11.15 s; Table 6.9's second 50 m of 4.30-4.40 s against Table 6.8's 4.45 s). Missed inconsistency, visible on the same page: Table 6.8 men's \"5m run from low start\" is 1.18 s, while Table 6.9's 15 m for the same 10.0 s goal is 1.16-1.18 s. 15 m cannot take the same time as 5 m from the same start, so the tables time sections differently (or one is misprinted), and the book does not say which. | Add to When NOT to apply: \"Table 6.8's 5 m from a low start (1.18 s, men) equals Table 6.9's 15 m (1.16-1.18 s) for the same 10.0 s goal (PDF 392 / p.381); the two tables cannot use the same start and timing, and the book does not explain.\" Optional: Table 6.9's 15 m step from the 10.2 s goal to the 10.5 s goal (1.18-1.20 to 1.26-1.28) is far larger than the other steps. |\n| framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports | FIX | Fig 6.39 read at 300 dpi and 450-dpi crops. Phases (I to late month 4, II about month 5, III about months 6-8, IV from late month 8), block 1 position, block-2-to-block-1 height of about two thirds, the Starting/Explosive crossing at about months 8-9, and the right-edge order are all correct. Errors: (1) \"all three strength curves... fall during block 1.\" They fall only in the first half: the two upper curves bump at about month 1, bottom out at about month 2 (Maximum strength also at about month 2), and are already rising through month 3 while block 1 is still running. (2) \"rise to a first peak at about months 4-5 (around the phase I/II boundary).\" All three peak at about month 5, inside phase II, just after the first dashed line. (3) \"Technique... falls during block 2\" is wrong for most of block 2. Technique peaks at about month 4 (just after block 1), falls through phase II, bottoms early in block 2 (about month 6-7), then rises through the rest of block 2. It is already above its first peak by the block's end, peaks at about month 10 and declines toward month 11. (4) Block 2 spans from just into month 6 to just past the month 7/8 tick, so \"about months 6-7, ending early in month 8.\" (5) The contested reasoning stops short. The brief line the entry cites (p. 11: CU \"better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality\") describes jumping and throwing, which is this model's own domain. Issurin's challenge (p. 6: team, combat, swimming) applies to the transfer to football, not to the model in its stated domain. The entry does not say so. The GPP/Speed \"cannot be told apart\" call is acceptable under the no-guessing rule. | Replace the upper-panel sentences with: \"Upper panel: the two upper curves bump at about month 1; all three curves are lowest at about month 2 and already rising in the last month of block 1; they peak at about month 5, inside phase II; they dip again during block 2 (lowest about month 7); then they climb through phase IV to their highest values at month 11.\" Replace the Technique sentence with: \"Technique rises inside block 1, peaks at about month 4, falls through phase II to a low early in block 2 (about months 6-7), rises through the rest of block 2, peaks at about month 10 and declines toward month 11.\" Add: \"GPP ends above Speed at the right edge (labels); their middle course is unclear.\" Block 2: \"about months 6-7, ending early in month 8.\" In When NOT to apply, add: \"Issurin's p. 11 remark favours the concentrated model in narrow-quality sports such as jumping, the model's own domain; the challenge applies to transferring it to team sports such as football.\" Keep `contested`, with the reason scoped to the football transfer. |\n| framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports | GO | The Fig 6.46 caption-label mismatch is described correctly (caption: \"A is the block of concentrated strength loading\"; image: \"GPP\" under A, \"Strength Load\" under B). | Checked: 6.14.2 and 6.14.3 quotes (PDF 395-397); Fig 6.40 (block 1 about months 2-4, block 2 about months 6-7, phases) and Fig 6.41 (phase I months 1-6, blocks about months 2-3 and 6-7, aerobic capacity peaking near the start of phase II) against the images, with the month numerals read as month ends; Gilyazova 7-14%; baseline line 115. |\n| claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity | FIX | The Inference \"A football season of games is shorter than the model's 4-5 month competition period\" is doubtful. Late August to a December or January postseason is about 4-4.5 months, comparable to the model's period. | Change to \"A football season (late August to the postseason) is comparable in length to the model's 4-5 month competition period, but football is not an endurance sport and the authors do not extend the rule to team sports.\" Checked otherwise: quotes at PDF 396 / p.385; baseline lines 105, 181 and 187 with tags; PDF 394 second-block sentence. |\n| framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports | FIX | (1) Wrong use of a citation. When to apply calls boxing and swimming \"the... examples the authors cite for tri-cyclic forms (PDF 387).\" At PDF 387 they are sports that \"traditionally employ\" tri-cyclic forms, where the authors say bi-cyclic periodisation \"may also be applied.\" The authors cite them as candidates for moving away from tri-cyclic. (2) Inconsistent confidence. This model's stated domain is \"individual and one-on-one sports,\" and Issurin 2016 names combat sports directly (brief, p. 6). Yet the entry is `supported`, while the explosive-strength model (whose home domain Issurin's p. 11 favours) is `contested`. Contest should follow the stated domain. The entry also uses the concentrated blocks: \"This model... three strength blocks\" (PDF 397). | (1) Rewrite as: \"The authors list boxing and swimming as sports that traditionally use tri-cyclic forms but may use bi-cyclic ones (PDF 387). They name team sports and one-on-one events as cases where tri-cyclic 'may have to be used, as dictated by the competition calendar.'\" (2) Set `confidence: contested` with the reason \"Issurin 2016 judges concentrated-unidirectional blocks under-supported for combat sports, this model's stated domain (brief, p. 6).\" Or keep `supported` and explicitly justify why the explosive model is contested and this one is not. Fig 6.42 reading verified (phases, three blocks of decreasing height, right-edge labels). |\n| heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work | FIX | The Fig 6.45/6.46 caption-label mismatch is described correctly. But \"Which wave is the strength block cannot be settled from the page\" ignores the entry's own quote. PDF 399 says the strength block comes \"following the groundwork laid by general physical preparation (GPP).\" That matches the printed labels (GPP under the earlier small wave A, Strength Load under the large wave B), so the caption is the likelier error. Fig 6.43 and 6.44 readings are correct. | Change to: \"The text's sequence (GPP groundwork, then the strength block, PDF 399) matches the printed labels. Inference: the caption is the likelier error. Still unclear on the page.\" Checked otherwise: PDF 397 \"More Advanced Use\" quote; PDF 398 A-D stage text, \"phase B\" as printed, Matveyev non-discreteness; PDF 399 incompatibility sentence; baseline line 105 with tag. |\n| principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice | GO | None found. | Checked: PDF 399 / p.388 passage; aim 7 (PDF 390); italic \"competitions, of course...\" (PDF 399); PDF 382 \"too late\" quote; baseline line 103; links resolve. |\n| heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates | GO | Suggestion only: link the 6.2.2 entry `heuristic--build-the-annual-plan-backwards-from-competitions`, which covers related calendar-first planning. | Checked: rules 1-4 and the italic recuperation sentence (PDF 400 / p.389); baseline line 185; Issurin \"about two months\" verified in the vault entry. |\n| framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training | GO | Suggestion only: link `heuristic--build-the-annual-plan-backwards-from-competitions` (6.2.2). | Checked: complexity sentence (PDF 400); steps 1-8 and the contingency line (PDF 401 / p.390); baseline line 183. |\n| heuristic--test-monthly-under-constant-conditions-and-compare-with-targets | GO | None found. | Checked: 6.16 guidelines (PDF 402 / p.391); \"largely random and at times unpredictable\" (PDF 378 / p.367, 6.11); baseline lines 30, 34, 74 and 75 with tags. |\n| framework--four-documents-for-programming-and-controlling-training | GO | None found. The line 89 tension is handled well and labelled. | Checked: italic \"keeping of meticulous training records\" and documents 1-2 (PDF 402); documents 3-4 and the subjective-signs passage (PDF 403); baseline lines 30 and 89. |\n| principle--programming-is-an-art-on-principles-not-an-algorithm | GO | None found. | Checked: 6.17 passages (PDF 403 / p.392); 6.12.1 \"creative flexibility\" (PDF 379); baseline lines 183 and 33 with tags. |\n\n## Missing (important ideas in PDF 379-403 that no entry captures)\n\n1. **Table 6.11, javelin model characteristics (Konstantinov 1978, PDF 393 / p.382).** Strength and power norms for international-class throwers: snatch body mass + 15 kg (men) and + 10 kg (women), back squat 2 x and 1.5 x body mass, standing triple jump 10.20 and 7.80 m, vertical jump 95 and 70 cm, standing broad jump 3.20 and 2.80 m, plus throwing-volume norms (170 and 140 average-intensity throws, 35 and 30 above-average). This is the closest thing in the chapter to a power-athlete strength norm table.\n2. **Tables 6.7 and 6.10, middle-distance models (Maximenko 1979, PDF 392-393).** They include flexibility (stride and straddle splits, in degrees) as model characteristics, and a defined special-endurance test: 2 x (4 x 400 m), 2 min between reps, 8-10 min between series.\n3. **Table 6.12, juvenile sprinter norms by age 11-16 (Alabin & Yushkevitch 1978, PDF 393).** It has a source error worth recording: \"Standing quintuple jump (cm)\" with values of 9.25-13.70, which must be metres.\n4. **The authors' own evidence caveat on microcycles (PDF 387).** \"the currently scant experimental findings,\" and \"large unidirectional loading sharply reduces the capability of displaying those qualities and abilities which facilitate the effective and safe execution of training programmes.\" This bears on the confidence of the microcycle case study.\n5. **Long-duration timing (PDF 396).** Because low-intensity work predominates, \"the optimal date for increasing the current adaptation reserves is deferred.\" This is the rationale for the long 6-month preparatory period, and the endurance entry omits it.\n\n## Notes on the special-attention items\n\n- **Fig 6.39 (6.14.1).** Phase boundaries and block positions in the entry hold up. Three curve-timing claims do not (see the FIX row): the strength curves start recovering inside block 1; the first peak is in phase II at about month 5; and Technique rises through most of block 2. For football planning, the image also shows that the model's strength indicators peak at the very end of phase IV (month 11). That fits Eric's November priority window (baseline line 185) better than an early-season peak would. This is my reading of the image, not a statement by the authors.\n- **Contested entries and Issurin 2016.** The brief confirms the critique targets Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model, not block periodisation in general, and none of the four entries overreaches to all block periodisation. The problems are elsewhere: the complex-training entry has the direction of disagreement wrong (Issurin shares the critique of complex training); the explosive-strength entry does not say that Issurin's p. 11 favours the model in its own domain; and the tri-cyclic entry, whose domain Issurin names, is not contested.\n- **Bi-cyclic \"same question\".** Aim 1 (PDF 389) is about the number of large mesocycles and explicitly allows one main competitive period. The monocyclic recommendation (PDF 387, 396) is about the number of competition peaks. Only PDF 387's wording merges the two. Recommend recording it as a terminology tension, with `contested` resting on Issurin.\n- **Tables 6.8 and 6.9, Fig 6.36(b).** Every value is correct. One flagged inconsistency was spurious (12 h rise then \"diminished\") and one was missed (5 m vs 15 m).\n- **Fig 6.45/6.46 caption vs label.** Described correctly in both entries that mention it. The text supports the labels over the caption for Fig 6.45.\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** Required fixes:\n1. bi-cyclic explosive model: correct the Fig 6.39 curve timing (recovery inside block 1, first peak about month 5 in phase II, Technique rising through most of block 2, block 2 ending early in month 8) and scope the Issurin contest to the football transfer.\n2. tricyclic: correct the boxing/swimming citation and make the confidence consistent with Issurin's combat-sport critique.\n3. two-mesocycles: rewrite the internal-contest paragraph as a PDF 387 terminology tension; base `contested` on Issurin.\n4. complex-training: Issurin agrees with the critique of complex training; the contest is the remedy and the beginners limit; add the brief's caveat on the 28 studies; separate Matveyev 1970.\n5. speed-sport: correct the Fig 6.32(b) reading (S-E and An-Gly rise during the block).\n6. unidirectional microcycles: drop the spurious 12 h \"conflict\"; the restoration time is 72 h in the text and about 30-36 h in the figure.\n7. Tabachnik: add the 5 m vs 15 m inconsistency.\n8. loading-reduction: record PDF 305's 1-3 week latency beside \"far longer than 1-2 weeks\"; link the 6c time-technique entry (rule 2).\n9. Antonova/Levchenko: fix the self-contradictory sentence; link the 6c LDTE principle; note the Levchenko dates.\n10. prolonged-competition: change \"shorter\" to \"comparable.\"\n11. follow-a-concentrated-block: note that the text supports the figure labels over the caption.\n","numLines":144,"startLine":1,"totalLines":144}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4e97935f-641f-42eb-b1fb-59ee7b418dda","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
