{"parentUuid":"fa95c845-229f-4319-919b-34e054fbdeaa","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac7100d52d01adab3","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffq1wCw67V6i2V5jXVWr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Q4iTN3uwhZbFaHsWpk8cma","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging; cat fixes_supertraining_ch6d_applied.md","description":"Read the fix log"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14543,"cache_read_input_tokens":57197,"output_tokens":115,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":14543},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":115,"cache_read_input_tokens":57197,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14543,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":14543,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Q4iTN3uwhZbFaHsWpk8cma":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging; cat fixes_supertraining_ch6d_applied.md","description":"Read the fix log"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cffq1vvKtCVNqV1tsjv32","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"16c6f559-ed12-4aa8-8a22-8bfb757d138a","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:07:41.401Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"16c6f559-ed12-4aa8-8a22-8bfb757d138a","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"725cf014-e8e6-4e21-907d-d54c6c265ee5","agentId":"ac7100d52d01adab3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q4iTN3uwhZbFaHsWpk8cma","type":"tool_result","content":"# Fixes applied: Supertraining 6.12-6.17 (Ch6d), 25 entries\n\nFixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch6d_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES: 11 FIX rows, 5 missing ideas, link suggestions).\nSource checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`. I extracted the text of PDF idx 305, 379-403 and 515 with PyMuPDF (`staging/_tmp/f6d_text.txt`). I rendered and opened: Fig 6.39 (PDF 395) at 200 dpi whole, 400 dpi crops of both panels and 400/500 dpi zooms of the first peak and the month 8/9 crossing (`_renders/f6d_p395_*.png`); Fig 6.32(b) (PDF 383) at 400 dpi and the block region at 600 dpi (`f6d_p383_figb*.png`); Fig 6.36(b) (PDF 388), both panels at 400 dpi (`f6d_p388_b_left.png`, `f6d_p388_b_right.png`); Tables 6.7-6.12 (PDF 392, 393, 200 dpi); and text strips for PDF 305 (latency), 374, 381 and 382 (Antonova and Levchenko dates), 387 (\"currently scant\") and 396 (\"deferred\"). Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.\nAlso read in full: the vault brief `research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a.md`, plus the vault entries `block-periodization--issurin/framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle.md` and `claim--multi-peak-annual-preparation-elite-track-and-field.md`.\nScope: the 25 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6d.txt`. I edited the 11 flagged entries plus 2 GO entries for missing ideas (`framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models`, `framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports`). There are no new entries, so the scope file is unchanged at 25 lines. No renames. I did not edit `_book.md`, any 6b or 6c file, or the vault.\n\n## Per-fix outcome\n\n| # | file | outcome | what changed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports | **Applied, with my own figure readings and a different basis for `contested`** | The Fig 6.39 paragraph is rewritten from my 400 dpi reading (see \"Figure readings\"). Block 2 now \"starts at the month 5/6 tick and ends just past the month 7/8 tick, early in month 8\". Upper panel: the curves are \"lowest at about month 2 and are already rising in month 3, while block 1 is still running\", \"peak at about month 5, just inside phase II\", dip in block 2 and are highest at month 11. Following the curves through the month 8/9 crossing, Starting Strength is the lower of the two upper curves before it (labelled Inference). Lower panel: Technique \"rises through the rest of block 2 to about its first-peak level by the block's end\", then peaks at about month 10. GPP and Speed can be followed in phase IV only; before month 8 they are unclear. When NOT now opens \"**Contested, on two grounds that need to be kept apart.**\" (1) The basis for `contested`: Issurin's book disputes the two-large-mesocycle structure inside the model's own domain (stages of \"about two months\", four to seven a season; world-class jumpers and sprinters peaking 7 to 11 times). (2) Issurin 2016, quoted exactly from the brief: p. 11 \"better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality and as restricted when peak performance requires many interacting abilities\"; p. 6 combat and team sports: \"improved some fitness measures, but ... did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance\", \"insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\". Then: \"The brief does not name jumping or throwing as narrow-target sports, and it does not reject the model for them.\" An Inference scopes the challenge to transferring the model to American football, a team sport, and the brief's non-systematic, author-curated caveat is added. The \"300 dpi\" locator is now \"400 dpi with crops of both panels\". |\n| 2 | framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports | **Applied** | (1) When to apply now reads: \"The authors list boxing and swimming as sports that traditionally use tri-cyclic forms but may use bi-cyclic ones (PDF p. 387 / printed p. 376). They name team sports and one-on-one events as cases where 'tri-cyclic periodisation may have to be used ...'\". (2) `confidence: supported` is now `contested`. The reason is stated as \"Contested by Issurin 2016 inside the model's stated domain (the basis for the `contested` rating)\": the model is \"intended primarily for individual and one-on-one sports\" and runs on three concentrated strength blocks, and brief p. 6 names combat and swimming. The brief's non-systematic caveat is added. The old \"keeps `supported` because the tri-cyclic layout itself is not what Issurin challenges\" is removed. |\n| 3 | claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation | **Applied** | \"Contested inside the book\" is replaced with \"**A terminology tension inside the book, not two opposed positions.**\" The new paragraph: PDF 387 uses \"two large mesocycles\" and \"bi-cyclic\" interchangeably; PDF 389 (two mesocycles \"even in sports which traditionally have one main competitive period\") and PDF 396 (\"one competitive stage\" but \"a two-stage organisation of strength and speed training\") keep them apart. So the passages answer different questions (how many mesocycles, how many competition peaks). When NOT now says \"**Contested by Issurin (the basis for the `contested` rating)**\". It adds: \"The disagreement comes from Issurin's 2008 book; the library's brief of Issurin 2016 does not address how many mesocycles a year should hold.\" It also adds the multi-peak link (7 to 11 peaks a season). |\n| 4 | claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes | **Applied** | Matveyev 1970 is moved into its own clause with the PDF 380 quote (\"Parallel loading ... in the necessary balance for various sports (Matveyev, 1970)\"). When NOT now reads \"**Contested by Issurin on the remedy and on the beginners limit, not on the critique (the basis for the `contested` rating).**\" The new text: Issurin agrees (the brief quote \"repeats the book's critique of traditional planning\", with a note that \"the book\" there is Issurin's own Block Periodization, plus a labelled Inference tying traditional planning to complex organisation). He contests the remedy (a minimal number of targets, not one) and the beginners limit (brief p. 11 \"no evidence that amateur athletes have any serious restrictions\" against PDF 380 \"applicable only to beginners or athletes of average qualification\"). The 28 studies are \"Issurin's own non-systematic, author-curated selection ... not a pooled estimate\". `contested` kept. |\n| 5 | framework--speed-sport-block-organisation-and-two-glycolytic-peaks | **Applied, with one refinement** | The figure paragraph is replaced. Two curves dip below the axis and recover after the block (they end at Vmax and STATE). Two rise during the block: S-E steadily, and An-Gly to a peak \"in the latter part of the block (about two thirds to three quarters of the way through)\", then a fall and a second hump. Speed starts only at the block's end. Right-edge labels are the only certain identities. Identities through the crossings are labelled Inference, and which below-axis curve is Vmax and which is STATE is marked unclear. |\n| 6 | case-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength | **Applied, with my own crossing times** | The spurious 12 h conflict is gone. The sentence now reads \"The text's 'aerobic potential rose over a 12 hour span and then diminished' is consistent with the figure (a peak of about 113% at about 6-10 h, then a fall to about 106%, still above the initial level)\". When to apply: \"The text gives 72 h for restoration; in the figure the stressed quality is back above its initial level at about 35 h (VO2 max, aerobic week) and about 38-40 h ('Training effect,' anaerobic week), and is still rising at 72 h.\" The figure-value line and the unclear-spot line use my readings (anaerobic Training effect crossing about 38-40 h, starting about 94%). The Eric's-baseline Inference now says the text's 72 h matches his window while the figure shows recovery sooner. **Missing idea 4** is added (see below), with the reason the rating stays `supported`. |\n| 7 | case-study--tabachnik-100m-sprinter-model-characteristics | **Applied** | When NOT adds the 5 m (1.18 s) versus 15 m (1.16-1.18 s) inconsistency for the same 10.0 s goal, checked on the PDF 392 image. It also adds the review's optional point: the large 15 m step between the 10.2 s and 10.5 s goals. A pointer to the descriptive-models entry, now holding Tables 6.7, 6.10, 6.11 and 6.12, is added. |\n| 8 | heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks | **Applied** | Why it works adds the PDF 305 / p.294 quote \"4-8 week concentrated blocks ... may occur most commonly after a latency period of 1-3 weeks\" (checked on the image). An Inference records it side by side with this page's figure (time to first gains versus how long the reduction must last), with the link to the 6c allow-restoration entry. When to apply names rule 2 of the 6c time-technique entry as the same instruction and quotes PDF 375. That link is also added to Related. |\n| 9 | case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities | **Applied, extended** | The sentence now reads \"Levchenko's subjects were 'highly qualified sprinters'; the long jumpers' level is not stated (PDF p. 382 says only 'women long jumpers')\". A source note on dates covers Levchenko 1980 (PDF 382), 1981 (PDF 381) and 1982 (PDF 374). It also covers **Antonova** 1982 (PDF 382, 374) and 1980 (PDF 381), a slip the review did not list (all checked on the page images). Related now opens with the 6c LDTE principle and says what this page adds (force-time take-off data, muscle stiffness, the injury statement). |\n| 10 | claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity | **Applied** | The text now reads \"A football season (late August to the postseason) is comparable in length to the model's 4-5 month competition period, but football is not an endurance sport and the authors do not extend the rule to team sports.\" |\n| 11 | heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work | **Applied** | \"cannot be settled\" is replaced. The text's sequence for Fig 6.45 (strength block \"following the groundwork laid by general physical preparation (GPP)\", PDF 399) matches the printed labels. \"Inference: the caption is the likelier error. The page itself does not settle it.\" |\n\n## Figure readings (my own, from the renders)\n\n**Fig 6.39 (PDF 395), months \"about\".** Month numerals sit between tick marks; dashed lines at late month 4 (I/II), the month 5/6 tick (II/III), mid month 8 (III/IV). Block 1: start to late month 3. Block 2: month 5/6 tick to just past the month 7/8 tick, about two thirds of block 1's height.\n- Upper panel. The top two curves bump early in month 1. All three are lowest at about month 2 (Maximum Strength slightly earlier), and they are rising through month 3 inside block 1. Peaks: Maximum Strength and the middle curve just right of the I/II dashed line (early month 5); the top curve is a flat top across phase II. Lows in block 2 at about months 6-7. Highest at month 11. The two upper curves cross just right of the III/IV line, at about the month 8/9 tick. By smooth continuation the steeply rising lower curve becomes the top one, so Starting Strength is the lower of the two through months 1-8 (Inference). Right-edge order: Starting, Explosive, Maximum (certain).\n- Lower panel. Technique peaks at about month 4, is lowest at about month 6 (early block 2), and is back to about its first-peak level at block 2's end. That is level with the first peak, not clearly above it as the review says. It peaks at about month 10 and declines toward month 11. In phase IV: the Speed curve peaks at about months 8-9 and falls, the GPP curve is lowest at about months 8-9 and rises, and they cross at about month 10. Before month 8, GPP and Speed are unclear.\n- Agreement with the review: (1), (3), (4) and the right-edge order hold. On (2), the peaks are at the very start of phase II (about early month 5), consistent with the review's \"inside phase II, just after the first dashed line\"; the old \"months 4-5 (around the phase I/II boundary)\" was close but placed them on the boundary.\n\n**Fig 6.32(b) (PDF 383).** Two curves above the axis leave the origin inside the block. One rises steadily, concave up, to S-E. The other peaks about 70% of the way through the block, falls to a low after it, rises to a second hump and eases, ending at An-Gly. Two curves dip below the axis (one deeper) and cross back above it after the block, ending at Vmax and STATE; which one ends where is unclear. One curve starts at the block's right edge and rises steeply to a mid-panel peak, then falls to Speed. The S-E and Vmax identities depend on continuity through crossings at about two thirds of the panel width (Inference).\n\n**Fig 6.36(b) (PDF 388).**\n- Aerobic week: VO2 max starts about 87%, crosses 100% at about 35 h, about 107% at 72 h. Training effect starts about 93%, crosses at about 15-18 h, about 101.5-102% at 72 h.\n- Anaerobic week: VO2 max starts just under 100%, peaks about 113% at about 6-10 h, about 106% at 72 h. Training effect starts about 94%, crosses at about 38-40 h, about 102.5-103% at 72 h.\n- The review's \"about 30-36 h\" for both crossings is close for the aerobic panel but early for the anaerobic one.\n\n## Confidence across the four Issurin-related entries\n\nOne test, applied to all four: does a library source disagree with what the entry claims, in the domain the entry claims it for (the ATOMIZE brief's definition of `contested`)?\n\n| entry | final | stated reason (in the entry) |\n|---|---|---|\n| explosive-strength bi-cyclic model | `contested` | Issurin's book disputes the two-large-mesocycle structure in the model's own domain (stages of about two months, four to seven a season; world-class jumpers peak 7-11 times). Issurin 2016 does **not** count against the model where the authors place it (p. 11 \"narrow target quality\"). It counts against the transfer to football, a team sport (p. 6). |\n| tri-cyclic model | `contested` (was `supported`) | Issurin 2016 p. 6 names combat sports and swimming, which are inside the model's stated domain (\"individual and one-on-one sports\"), and the model runs on concentrated blocks. |\n| two large mesocycles | `contested` | Issurin's 2008 book (stage length and number, multi-peak). The entry says that the 2016 brief does not address mesocycle count. The internal tension is now recorded as terminology, not as a basis for the rating. |\n| complex training | `contested` | Issurin agrees with the critique. He contests the remedy (a minimal number of targets, not one) and the beginners limit (brief p. 11 on amateurs). The 28 studies are author-curated. |\n\n## Missing ideas: placement\n\n| # | idea | placement |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Table 6.11 javelin norms (PDF 393) | Added to `framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models` (GO entry; its Example already lists the chapter's model tables). Snatch Bmass + 15 / + 10 kg, squat 2 x / 1.5 x Bmass, broad jump, triple jump, vertical jump, 30 m, throw counts (no time period given). The power-norm remark is labelled Inference. |\n| 2 | Tables 6.7 and 6.10 middle distance (PDF 392-393) | Same entry: splits in degrees as model characteristics, and the 2 x (4 x 400 m) test quoted from the Table 6.7 caption. Also recorded as printed: \"Relative mean strength of 5 muscle groups (kg)\" against a caption that describes hip, knee and ankle extensors and flexors \"relative to bodymass\". |\n| 3 | Table 6.12 cm/m unit error (PDF 393) | Same entry: \"Standing quintuple jump (cm)\", 9.25 to 13.70, with \"Inference: the values must be metres\" and the standing long jump row (179.8-261.2 cm) as the check. |\n| 4 | \"currently scant experimental findings\" (PDF 387) | Added to the microcycle case study's When NOT, with the \"sharply reduces the capability\" sentence (checked on the image), and a stated reason that the rating stays `supported`. |\n| 5 | \"optimal date ... is deferred\" (PDF 396) | Added to the endurance entry's long-duration bullet (checked on the image). |\n\nNo new entry was needed, so `_scope_supertraining_ch6d.txt` stays at 25 lines.\n\n## Disagreements with the review\n\n1. **Explosive model: `contested` is kept, but not on the review's basis.** The review says keep `contested` \"with the reason scoped to the football transfer\", and row 32 says \"contest should follow the stated domain\". Those are two different rules: under the second, a football-only contest would not make the entry contested. I applied the domain rule to all four entries. The explosive model is contested inside its domain by Issurin's book (two large mesocycles against stages of about two months and multi-peak seasons in elite track and field), and Issurin 2016 is scoped to the football transfer, as asked.\n2. **The p. 11 wording.** The review (row 29 and notes) and the task paraphrase brief p. 11 as favouring the model in \"jumping and throwing\" or \"jumping-type sports\". The brief says only \"better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality\"; \"jumping\" appears only in its population list (p. 11). The entry quotes the brief's words and states that the brief does not name jumping or throwing. The mapping of explosive events to \"narrow target quality\" is labelled Inference.\n3. **Fig 6.39 Technique at the end of block 2.** The review says Technique is \"already above its first peak by the block's end\". At 400 dpi it is about level with the first peak, so the entry says \"to about its first-peak level\".\n4. **Fig 6.32(b) An-Gly peak.** The review says it \"peaks mid-block\". The 600 dpi crop puts the peak about 70% of the way through the block, so the entry says \"in the latter part of the block\". This also fits the text's \"timed to end near the conclusion\".\n5. **Fig 6.36(b) crossing times.** The review gives about 30-36 h in both panels. I read about 35 h (aerobic week, VO2 max) and about 38-40 h (anaerobic week, Training effect); the entry uses my values. The review's point stands: both are well before the text's 72 h.\n6. **Antonova dates.** The review notes only Levchenko's three dates. PDF 381 also dates Antonova 1980 (1982 at PDF 374 and 382), so the source note records both.\n7. **Complex-training brief quote.** The review's rewrite quotes the brief's \"repeats the book's critique of traditional planning\" without context. In the brief, \"the book\" is Issurin's own Block Periodization, not Supertraining, so the entry says so.\n8. **Optional items applied:** the Tabachnik 15 m step (row 28 optional). **Not applied:** the optional notes on GO rows (mesocycle-variants Issurin line, the concentrated-loading p. 11 amateur line, the 6.2.2 build-backwards link for the two GO planning entries), per the \"flagged files only\" rule. The links are listed below for the coordinator.\n\n## Addendum attribution\n\nNone of the 25 files cites the Addendum (PDF 514-590): the check found 0 matches for \"Addendum\" or \"PDF p. 5xx\" across 25 files. No change needed.\n\n## For the coordinator\n\n- Link targets confirmed to exist at the time of editing: `principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum`, `heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window`, `heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next` (6c), plus `block-periodization--issurin/claim--multi-peak-annual-preparation-elite-track-and-field` (vault).\n- Suggested, not applied (GO rows): link `heuristic--build-the-annual-plan-backwards-from-competitions` (exists) from `heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates` and `framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training`.\n- Reciprocal links (6c, not edited by me): the 6c LDTE principle could link back to [[case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities]]; the 6c time-technique entry could link [[heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks]].\n- Endurance entry (GO, `supported`): it says the models \"carry the same Issurin 2016 challenge\". Brief p. 6 names swimming. If the coordinator wants the domain rule applied there too, swimming would sit inside the medium-duration domain. I did not change that rating; it is outside the flagged set.\n- Confidence tally for 6d: 5 `contested` (complex training, concentrated loading, two mesocycles, explosive model, tri-cyclic), 19 `supported`, 1 `untested`. Tri-cyclic moved from `supported` to `contested`.\n\n## Checks\n\nGrep proof (new wording), run from the book folder:\n\n```\nexplosive: 9:confidence: contested\nexplosive: 23:ends just past the month 7/8 tick, early in month 8\nexplosive: 23:already rising in month 3, while block 1 is still running\nexplosive: 23:They peak at about month 5, just inside phase II\nexplosive: 23:rises through the rest of block 2 to about its first-peak level\nexplosive: 23:GPP ends above Speed at the right edge\nexplosive: 29:does not name jumping or throwing as narrow-target sports, and it does not reject the model for them\nexplosive: 29:it counts against transferring it to American football\nexplosive: 29:for team sports the reviewed studies \"did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance\" (brief, p. 6)\ntricyclic: 9:confidence: contested\ntricyclic: 24:list boxing and swimming as sports that traditionally use tri-cyclic forms but may use bi-cyclic ones\ntricyclic: 26:Contested by Issurin 2016 inside the model's stated domain\ntwo-meso: 22:A terminology tension inside the book, not two opposed positions\ntwo-meso: 28:the basis for the `contested` rating\ntwo-meso: 28:does not address how many mesocycles a year should hold\ntwo-meso: 28:peaking 7 to 11 times\ncomplex: 19:Separately, \"Parallel loading of different emphasis\ncomplex: 29:not on the critique\ncomplex: 29:no evidence that amateur athletes have any serious restrictions\ncomplex: 29:non-systematic, author-curated selection\nspeed: 22:one rises steadily and keeps rising (ending at S-E)\nspeed: 22:latter part of the block\nspeed: 22:which of the two below-axis curves ends at Vmax\nmicro: 20:crosses 100% at about 38-40 h\nmicro: 27:about 35 h (VO2 max, aerobic week)\nmicro: 29:crosses 100% at about 38-40 h\nmicro: 29:is consistent with the figure\nmicro: 29:currently scant experimental findings\ntabachnik: 32:15 m cannot take the same time as 5 m\ntabachnik: 32:much larger than the other steps\nunload: 18:latency period of 1-3 weeks\nunload: 20:rule 2 of [[heuristic--time-technique\nunload: 26:[[heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window]], [[periodization\nantonova: 26:Levchenko's subjects were \"highly qualified sprinters\"\nantonova: 26:Source note on dates\nantonova: 30:[[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]] (6.10\nprolonged: 20:is comparable in length to the model's 4-5 month competition period\nfollow: 27:Inference: the caption is the likelier error\nendurance: 26:the optimal date for increasing the current adaptation reserves is deferred\nendurance: 35:reads the two passages as a terminology tension\ndescriptive: 32:2 series of 4 x 400m\ndescriptive: 33:Bmass + 15kg\ndescriptive: 34:Standing quintuple jump (cm)\n```\n\nOld phrases, count of 6d files still holding each (all 0):\n\n```\nfall during block 1 after a small early bump: 0\nfirst peak at about months 4-5: 0\nfalls during block 2, then rises: 0\ncannot be told apart reliably: 0\nthe boxing and swimming examples the authors cite: 0\nThis entry keeps `supported`: 0\nContested inside the book.: 0\nAlso contested by Issurin: 0\nKorobkov et al, 1960; Matveyev, 1970: 0\nmost curves (Vmax, S-E, Speed, STATE, An-Gly) dip: 0\nexpect roughly three days: 0\nbut the figure shows it still about 6% above initial: 0\nabout 30-36 h: 0\nBoth groups were qualified track athletes: 0\nA football season of games is shorter: 0\nWhich wave is the strength block cannot be settled: 0\nfor this internal conflict: 0\nreported no effect: 0\n```\n\nMechanical checks over the 25 scoped files (`staging/_tmp/f6d_checks.sh`, run under `/bin/bash`):\n\n```\n== files in scope: 25\n== em dashes not in section headers:\n(end)\n== header count per file:\n  25 6\n== wrapped wikilinks:\n(end)\n== Addendum / PDF 5xx citations:\n0\n== unresolved links:\n(end)\n== confidence tally:\n   5 confidence: contested\n  19 confidence: supported\n   1 confidence: untested\n== CR endings:\n0\n```\n\n(The review-history scan flags only the complex-training line quoting the brief's \"the review repeats\", which refers to Issurin's review paper, not to this gate.)\n\nLint, run from the Flint root:\n\n```\n$ ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 132, 133]\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--functional-training-means-sport-specific-and-no-exercise-is-non-functional.md: unresolved [[claim--core-stability-balance-training-has-no-shown-transfer-to-sport]]\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md: unresolved [[heuristic--overcoming-sticking-points-repertoire-of-27-strategies]]\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md: unresolved [[framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set]]\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md: unresolved [[framework--miscellaneous-bodybuilding-set-variations]]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 5\n```\n\nThere are no lint lines for any 6d file. `_book.md` is the known false positive (title and table dashes). The other four are Chapter 7 files that another batch is writing into the same folder right now. An earlier run in this session, before those files appeared, gave TOTAL ISSUES: 1. None of the five is in my scope.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"793ec0bd-c5b3-47b3-8b37-af4dc4633a35","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:07:41.556Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# Fixes applied: Supertraining 6.12-6.17 (Ch6d), 25 entries\n\nFixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch6d_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES: 11 FIX rows, 5 missing ideas, link suggestions).\nSource checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`. I extracted the text of PDF idx 305, 379-403 and 515 with PyMuPDF (`staging/_tmp/f6d_text.txt`). I rendered and opened: Fig 6.39 (PDF 395) at 200 dpi whole, 400 dpi crops of both panels and 400/500 dpi zooms of the first peak and the month 8/9 crossing (`_renders/f6d_p395_*.png`); Fig 6.32(b) (PDF 383) at 400 dpi and the block region at 600 dpi (`f6d_p383_figb*.png`); Fig 6.36(b) (PDF 388), both panels at 400 dpi (`f6d_p388_b_left.png`, `f6d_p388_b_right.png`); Tables 6.7-6.12 (PDF 392, 393, 200 dpi); and text strips for PDF 305 (latency), 374, 381 and 382 (Antonova and Levchenko dates), 387 (\"currently scant\") and 396 (\"deferred\"). Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.\nAlso read in full: the vault brief `research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a.md`, plus the vault entries `block-periodization--issurin/framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle.md` and `claim--multi-peak-annual-preparation-elite-track-and-field.md`.\nScope: the 25 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6d.txt`. I edited the 11 flagged entries plus 2 GO entries for missing ideas (`framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models`, `framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports`). There are no new entries, so the scope file is unchanged at 25 lines. No renames. I did not edit `_book.md`, any 6b or 6c file, or the vault.\n\n## Per-fix outcome\n\n| # | file | outcome | what changed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports | **Applied, with my own figure readings and a different basis for `contested`** | The Fig 6.39 paragraph is rewritten from my 400 dpi reading (see \"Figure readings\"). Block 2 now \"starts at the month 5/6 tick and ends just past the month 7/8 tick, early in month 8\". Upper panel: the curves are \"lowest at about month 2 and are already rising in month 3, while block 1 is still running\", \"peak at about month 5, just inside phase II\", dip in block 2 and are highest at month 11. Following the curves through the month 8/9 crossing, Starting Strength is the lower of the two upper curves before it (labelled Inference). Lower panel: Technique \"rises through the rest of block 2 to about its first-peak level by the block's end\", then peaks at about month 10. GPP and Speed can be followed in phase IV only; before month 8 they are unclear. When NOT now opens \"**Contested, on two grounds that need to be kept apart.**\" (1) The basis for `contested`: Issurin's book disputes the two-large-mesocycle structure inside the model's own domain (stages of \"about two months\", four to seven a season; world-class jumpers and sprinters peaking 7 to 11 times). (2) Issurin 2016, quoted exactly from the brief: p. 11 \"better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality and as restricted when peak performance requires many interacting abilities\"; p. 6 combat and team sports: \"improved some fitness measures, but ... did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance\", \"insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\". Then: \"The brief does not name jumping or throwing as narrow-target sports, and it does not reject the model for them.\" An Inference scopes the challenge to transferring the model to American football, a team sport, and the brief's non-systematic, author-curated caveat is added. The \"300 dpi\" locator is now \"400 dpi with crops of both panels\". |\n| 2 | framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports | **Applied** | (1) When to apply now reads: \"The authors list boxing and swimming as sports that traditionally use tri-cyclic forms but may use bi-cyclic ones (PDF p. 387 / printed p. 376). They name team sports and one-on-one events as cases where 'tri-cyclic periodisation may have to be used ...'\". (2) `confidence: supported` is now `contested`. The reason is stated as \"Contested by Issurin 2016 inside the model's stated domain (the basis for the `contested` rating)\": the model is \"intended primarily for individual and one-on-one sports\" and runs on three concentrated strength blocks, and brief p. 6 names combat and swimming. The brief's non-systematic caveat is added. The old \"keeps `supported` because the tri-cyclic layout itself is not what Issurin challenges\" is removed. |\n| 3 | claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation | **Applied** | \"Contested inside the book\" is replaced with \"**A terminology tension inside the book, not two opposed positions.**\" The new paragraph: PDF 387 uses \"two large mesocycles\" and \"bi-cyclic\" interchangeably; PDF 389 (two mesocycles \"even in sports which traditionally have one main competitive period\") and PDF 396 (\"one competitive stage\" but \"a two-stage organisation of strength and speed training\") keep them apart. So the passages answer different questions (how many mesocycles, how many competition peaks). When NOT now says \"**Contested by Issurin (the basis for the `contested` rating)**\". It adds: \"The disagreement comes from Issurin's 2008 book; the library's brief of Issurin 2016 does not address how many mesocycles a year should hold.\" It also adds the multi-peak link (7 to 11 peaks a season). |\n| 4 | claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes | **Applied** | Matveyev 1970 is moved into its own clause with the PDF 380 quote (\"Parallel loading ... in the necessary balance for various sports (Matveyev, 1970)\"). When NOT now reads \"**Contested by Issurin on the remedy and on the beginners limit, not on the critique (the basis for the `contested` rating).**\" The new text: Issurin agrees (the brief quote \"repeats the book's critique of traditional planning\", with a note that \"the book\" there is Issurin's own Block Periodization, plus a labelled Inference tying traditional planning to complex organisation). He contests the remedy (a minimal number of targets, not one) and the beginners limit (brief p. 11 \"no evidence that amateur athletes have any serious restrictions\" against PDF 380 \"applicable only to beginners or athletes of average qualification\"). The 28 studies are \"Issurin's own non-systematic, author-curated selection ... not a pooled estimate\". `contested` kept. |\n| 5 | framework--speed-sport-block-organisation-and-two-glycolytic-peaks | **Applied, with one refinement** | The figure paragraph is replaced. Two curves dip below the axis and recover after the block (they end at Vmax and STATE). Two rise during the block: S-E steadily, and An-Gly to a peak \"in the latter part of the block (about two thirds to three quarters of the way through)\", then a fall and a second hump. Speed starts only at the block's end. Right-edge labels are the only certain identities. Identities through the crossings are labelled Inference, and which below-axis curve is Vmax and which is STATE is marked unclear. |\n| 6 | case-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength | **Applied, with my own crossing times** | The spurious 12 h conflict is gone. The sentence now reads \"The text's 'aerobic potential rose over a 12 hour span and then diminished' is consistent with the figure (a peak of about 113% at about 6-10 h, then a fall to about 106%, still above the initial level)\". When to apply: \"The text gives 72 h for restoration; in the figure the stressed quality is back above its initial level at about 35 h (VO2 max, aerobic week) and about 38-40 h ('Training effect,' anaerobic week), and is still rising at 72 h.\" The figure-value line and the unclear-spot line use my readings (anaerobic Training effect crossing about 38-40 h, starting about 94%). The Eric's-baseline Inference now says the text's 72 h matches his window while the figure shows recovery sooner. **Missing idea 4** is added (see below), with the reason the rating stays `supported`. |\n| 7 | case-study--tabachnik-100m-sprinter-model-characteristics | **Applied** | When NOT adds the 5 m (1.18 s) versus 15 m (1.16-1.18 s) inconsistency for the same 10.0 s goal, checked on the PDF 392 image. It also adds the review's optional point: the large 15 m step between the 10.2 s and 10.5 s goals. A pointer to the descriptive-models entry, now holding Tables 6.7, 6.10, 6.11 and 6.12, is added. |\n| 8 | heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks | **Applied** | Why it works adds the PDF 305 / p.294 quote \"4-8 week concentrated blocks ... may occur most commonly after a latency period of 1-3 weeks\" (checked on the image). An Inference records it side by side with this page's figure (time to first gains versus how long the reduction must last), with the link to the 6c allow-restoration entry. When to apply names rule 2 of the 6c time-technique entry as the same instruction and quotes PDF 375. That link is also added to Related. |\n| 9 | case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities | **Applied, extended** | The sentence now reads \"Levchenko's subjects were 'highly qualified sprinters'; the long jumpers' level is not stated (PDF p. 382 says only 'women long jumpers')\". A source note on dates covers Levchenko 1980 (PDF 382), 1981 (PDF 381) and 1982 (PDF 374). It also covers **Antonova** 1982 (PDF 382, 374) and 1980 (PDF 381), a slip the review did not list (all checked on the page images). Related now opens with the 6c LDTE principle and says what this page adds (force-time take-off data, muscle stiffness, the injury statement). |\n| 10 | claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity | **Applied** | The text now reads \"A football season (late August to the postseason) is comparable in length to the model's 4-5 month competition period, but football is not an endurance sport and the authors do not extend the rule to team sports.\" |\n| 11 | heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work | **Applied** | \"cannot be settled\" is replaced. The text's sequence for Fig 6.45 (strength block \"following the groundwork laid by general physical preparation (GPP)\", PDF 399) matches the printed labels. \"Inference: the caption is the likelier error. The page itself does not settle it.\" |\n\n## Figure readings (my own, from the renders)\n\n**Fig 6.39 (PDF 395), months \"about\".** Month numerals sit between tick marks; dashed lines at late month 4 (I/II), the month 5/6 tick (II/III), mid month 8 (III/IV). Block 1: start to late month 3. Block 2: month 5/6 tick to just past the month 7/8 tick, about two thirds of block 1's height.\n- Upper panel. The top two curves bump early in month 1. All three are lowest at about month 2 (Maximum Strength slightly earlier), and they are rising through month 3 inside block 1. Peaks: Maximum Strength and the middle curve just right of the I/II dashed line (early month 5); the top curve is a flat top across phase II. Lows in block 2 at about months 6-7. Highest at month 11. The two upper curves cross just right of the III/IV line, at about the month 8/9 tick. By smooth continuation the steeply rising lower curve becomes the top one, so Starting Strength is the lower of the two through months 1-8 (Inference). Right-edge order: Starting, Explosive, Maximum (certain).\n- Lower panel. Technique peaks at about month 4, is lowest at about month 6 (early block 2), and is back to about its first-peak level at block 2's end. That is level with the first peak, not clearly above it as the review says. It peaks at about month 10 and declines toward month 11. In phase IV: the Speed curve peaks at about months 8-9 and falls, the GPP curve is lowest at about months 8-9 and rises, and they cross at about month 10. Before month 8, GPP and Speed are unclear.\n- Agreement with the review: (1), (3), (4) and the right-edge order hold. On (2), the peaks are at the very start of phase II (about early month 5), consistent with the review's \"inside phase II, just after the first dashed line\"; the old \"months 4-5 (around the phase I/II boundary)\" was close but placed them on the boundary.\n\n**Fig 6.32(b) (PDF 383).** Two curves above the axis leave the origin inside the block. One rises steadily, concave up, to S-E. The other peaks about 70% of the way through the block, falls to a low after it, rises to a second hump and eases, ending at An-Gly. Two curves dip below the axis (one deeper) and cross back above it after the block, ending at Vmax and STATE; which one ends where is unclear. One curve starts at the block's right edge and rises steeply to a mid-panel peak, then falls to Speed. The S-E and Vmax identities depend on continuity through crossings at about two thirds of the panel width (Inference).\n\n**Fig 6.36(b) (PDF 388).**\n- Aerobic week: VO2 max starts about 87%, crosses 100% at about 35 h, about 107% at 72 h. Training effect starts about 93%, crosses at about 15-18 h, about 101.5-102% at 72 h.\n- Anaerobic week: VO2 max starts just under 100%, peaks about 113% at about 6-10 h, about 106% at 72 h. Training effect starts about 94%, crosses at about 38-40 h, about 102.5-103% at 72 h.\n- The review's \"about 30-36 h\" for both crossings is close for the aerobic panel but early for the anaerobic one.\n\n## Confidence across the four Issurin-related entries\n\nOne test, applied to all four: does a library source disagree with what the entry claims, in the domain the entry claims it for (the ATOMIZE brief's definition of `contested`)?\n\n| entry | final | stated reason (in the entry) |\n|---|---|---|\n| explosive-strength bi-cyclic model | `contested` | Issurin's book disputes the two-large-mesocycle structure in the model's own domain (stages of about two months, four to seven a season; world-class jumpers peak 7-11 times). Issurin 2016 does **not** count against the model where the authors place it (p. 11 \"narrow target quality\"). It counts against the transfer to football, a team sport (p. 6). |\n| tri-cyclic model | `contested` (was `supported`) | Issurin 2016 p. 6 names combat sports and swimming, which are inside the model's stated domain (\"individual and one-on-one sports\"), and the model runs on concentrated blocks. |\n| two large mesocycles | `contested` | Issurin's 2008 book (stage length and number, multi-peak). The entry says that the 2016 brief does not address mesocycle count. The internal tension is now recorded as terminology, not as a basis for the rating. |\n| complex training | `contested` | Issurin agrees with the critique. He contests the remedy (a minimal number of targets, not one) and the beginners limit (brief p. 11 on amateurs). The 28 studies are author-curated. |\n\n## Missing ideas: placement\n\n| # | idea | placement |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Table 6.11 javelin norms (PDF 393) | Added to `framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models` (GO entry; its Example already lists the chapter's model tables). Snatch Bmass + 15 / + 10 kg, squat 2 x / 1.5 x Bmass, broad jump, triple jump, vertical jump, 30 m, throw counts (no time period given). The power-norm remark is labelled Inference. |\n| 2 | Tables 6.7 and 6.10 middle distance (PDF 392-393) | Same entry: splits in degrees as model characteristics, and the 2 x (4 x 400 m) test quoted from the Table 6.7 caption. Also recorded as printed: \"Relative mean strength of 5 muscle groups (kg)\" against a caption that describes hip, knee and ankle extensors and flexors \"relative to bodymass\". |\n| 3 | Table 6.12 cm/m unit error (PDF 393) | Same entry: \"Standing quintuple jump (cm)\", 9.25 to 13.70, with \"Inference: the values must be metres\" and the standing long jump row (179.8-261.2 cm) as the check. |\n| 4 | \"currently scant experimental findings\" (PDF 387) | Added to the microcycle case study's When NOT, with the \"sharply reduces the capability\" sentence (checked on the image), and a stated reason that the rating stays `supported`. |\n| 5 | \"optimal date ... is deferred\" (PDF 396) | Added to the endurance entry's long-duration bullet (checked on the image). |\n\nNo new entry was needed, so `_scope_supertraining_ch6d.txt` stays at 25 lines.\n\n## Disagreements with the review\n\n1. **Explosive model: `contested` is kept, but not on the review's basis.** The review says keep `contested` \"with the reason scoped to the football transfer\", and row 32 says \"contest should follow the stated domain\". Those are two different rules: under the second, a football-only contest would not make the entry contested. I applied the domain rule to all four entries. The explosive model is contested inside its domain by Issurin's book (two large mesocycles against stages of about two months and multi-peak seasons in elite track and field), and Issurin 2016 is scoped to the football transfer, as asked.\n2. **The p. 11 wording.** The review (row 29 and notes) and the task paraphrase brief p. 11 as favouring the model in \"jumping and throwing\" or \"jumping-type sports\". The brief says only \"better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality\"; \"jumping\" appears only in its population list (p. 11). The entry quotes the brief's words and states that the brief does not name jumping or throwing. The mapping of explosive events to \"narrow target quality\" is labelled Inference.\n3. **Fig 6.39 Technique at the end of block 2.** The review says Technique is \"already above its first peak by the block's end\". At 400 dpi it is about level with the first peak, so the entry says \"to about its first-peak level\".\n4. **Fig 6.32(b) An-Gly peak.** The review says it \"peaks mid-block\". The 600 dpi crop puts the peak about 70% of the way through the block, so the entry says \"in the latter part of the block\". This also fits the text's \"timed to end near the conclusion\".\n5. **Fig 6.36(b) crossing times.** The review gives about 30-36 h in both panels. I read about 35 h (aerobic week, VO2 max) and about 38-40 h (anaerobic week, Training effect); the entry uses my values. The review's point stands: both are well before the text's 72 h.\n6. **Antonova dates.** The review notes only Levchenko's three dates. PDF 381 also dates Antonova 1980 (1982 at PDF 374 and 382), so the source note records both.\n7. **Complex-training brief quote.** The review's rewrite quotes the brief's \"repeats the book's critique of traditional planning\" without context. In the brief, \"the book\" is Issurin's own Block Periodization, not Supertraining, so the entry says so.\n8. **Optional items applied:** the Tabachnik 15 m step (row 28 optional). **Not applied:** the optional notes on GO rows (mesocycle-variants Issurin line, the concentrated-loading p. 11 amateur line, the 6.2.2 build-backwards link for the two GO planning entries), per the \"flagged files only\" rule. The links are listed below for the coordinator.\n\n## Addendum attribution\n\nNone of the 25 files cites the Addendum (PDF 514-590): the check found 0 matches for \"Addendum\" or \"PDF p. 5xx\" across 25 files. No change needed.\n\n## For the coordinator\n\n- Link targets confirmed to exist at the time of editing: `principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum`, `heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window`, `heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next` (6c), plus `block-periodization--issurin/claim--multi-peak-annual-preparation-elite-track-and-field` (vault).\n- Suggested, not applied (GO rows): link `heuristic--build-the-annual-plan-backwards-from-competitions` (exists) from `heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates` and `framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training`.\n- Reciprocal links (6c, not edited by me): the 6c LDTE principle could link back to [[case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities]]; the 6c time-technique entry could link [[heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks]].\n- Endurance entry (GO, `supported`): it says the models \"carry the same Issurin 2016 challenge\". Brief p. 6 names swimming. If the coordinator wants the domain rule applied there too, swimming would sit inside the medium-duration domain. I did not change that rating; it is outside the flagged set.\n- Confidence tally for 6d: 5 `contested` (complex training, concentrated loading, two mesocycles, explosive model, tri-cyclic), 19 `supported`, 1 `untested`. Tri-cyclic moved from `supported` to `contested`.\n\n## Checks\n\nGrep proof (new wording), run from the book folder:\n\n```\nexplosive: 9:confidence: contested\nexplosive: 23:ends just past the month 7/8 tick, early in month 8\nexplosive: 23:already rising in month 3, while block 1 is still running\nexplosive: 23:They peak at about month 5, just inside phase II\nexplosive: 23:rises through the rest of block 2 to about its first-peak level\nexplosive: 23:GPP ends above Speed at the right edge\nexplosive: 29:does not name jumping or throwing as narrow-target sports, and it does not reject the model for them\nexplosive: 29:it counts against transferring it to American football\nexplosive: 29:for team sports the reviewed studies \"did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance\" (brief, p. 6)\ntricyclic: 9:confidence: contested\ntricyclic: 24:list boxing and swimming as sports that traditionally use tri-cyclic forms but may use bi-cyclic ones\ntricyclic: 26:Contested by Issurin 2016 inside the model's stated domain\ntwo-meso: 22:A terminology tension inside the book, not two opposed positions\ntwo-meso: 28:the basis for the `contested` rating\ntwo-meso: 28:does not address how many mesocycles a year should hold\ntwo-meso: 28:peaking 7 to 11 times\ncomplex: 19:Separately, \"Parallel loading of different emphasis\ncomplex: 29:not on the critique\ncomplex: 29:no evidence that amateur athletes have any serious restrictions\ncomplex: 29:non-systematic, author-curated selection\nspeed: 22:one rises steadily and keeps rising (ending at S-E)\nspeed: 22:latter part of the block\nspeed: 22:which of the two below-axis curves ends at Vmax\nmicro: 20:crosses 100% at about 38-40 h\nmicro: 27:about 35 h (VO2 max, aerobic week)\nmicro: 29:crosses 100% at about 38-40 h\nmicro: 29:is consistent with the figure\nmicro: 29:currently scant experimental findings\ntabachnik: 32:15 m cannot take the same time as 5 m\ntabachnik: 32:much larger than the other steps\nunload: 18:latency period of 1-3 weeks\nunload: 20:rule 2 of [[heuristic--time-technique\nunload: 26:[[heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window]], [[periodization\nantonova: 26:Levchenko's subjects were \"highly qualified sprinters\"\nantonova: 26:Source note on dates\nantonova: 30:[[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]] (6.10\nprolonged: 20:is comparable in length to the model's 4-5 month competition period\nfollow: 27:Inference: the caption is the likelier error\nendurance: 26:the optimal date for increasing the current adaptation reserves is deferred\nendurance: 35:reads the two passages as a terminology tension\ndescriptive: 32:2 series of 4 x 400m\ndescriptive: 33:Bmass + 15kg\ndescriptive: 34:Standing quintuple jump (cm)\n```\n\nOld phrases, count of 6d files still holding each (all 0):\n\n```\nfall during block 1 after a small early bump: 0\nfirst peak at about months 4-5: 0\nfalls during block 2, then rises: 0\ncannot be told apart reliably: 0\nthe boxing and swimming examples the authors cite: 0\nThis entry keeps `supported`: 0\nContested inside the book.: 0\nAlso contested by Issurin: 0\nKorobkov et al, 1960; Matveyev, 1970: 0\nmost curves (Vmax, S-E, Speed, STATE, An-Gly) dip: 0\nexpect roughly three days: 0\nbut the figure shows it still about 6% above initial: 0\nabout 30-36 h: 0\nBoth groups were qualified track athletes: 0\nA football season of games is shorter: 0\nWhich wave is the strength block cannot be settled: 0\nfor this internal conflict: 0\nreported no effect: 0\n```\n\nMechanical checks over the 25 scoped files (`staging/_tmp/f6d_checks.sh`, run under `/bin/bash`):\n\n```\n== files in scope: 25\n== em dashes not in section headers:\n(end)\n== header count per file:\n  25 6\n== wrapped wikilinks:\n(end)\n== Addendum / PDF 5xx citations:\n0\n== unresolved links:\n(end)\n== confidence tally:\n   5 confidence: contested\n  19 confidence: supported\n   1 confidence: untested\n== CR endings:\n0\n```\n\n(The review-history scan flags only the complex-training line quoting the brief's \"the review repeats\", which refers to Issurin's review paper, not to this gate.)\n\nLint, run from the Flint root:\n\n```\n$ ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 132, 133]\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--functional-training-means-sport-specific-and-no-exercise-is-non-functional.md: unresolved [[claim--core-stability-balance-training-has-no-shown-transfer-to-sport]]\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md: unresolved [[heuristic--overcoming-sticking-points-repertoire-of-27-strategies]]\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md: unresolved [[framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set]]\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md: unresolved [[framework--miscellaneous-bodybuilding-set-variations]]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 5\n```\n\nThere are no lint lines for any 6d file. `_book.md` is the known false positive (title and table dashes). The other four are Chapter 7 files that another batch is writing into the same folder right now. An earlier run in this session, before those files appeared, gave TOTAL ISSUES: 1. None of the five is in my scope.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"16c6f559-ed12-4aa8-8a22-8bfb757d138a","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
