{"parentUuid":"642adff2-8314-423a-820f-c2e904ebe83c","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac7100d52d01adab3","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffq246vV5VPHSqsXMnQ3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013DJL2GuE82WR4EPRR8Fowg","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/; cat framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports.md","description":"Read explosive-strength model entry"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10070,"cache_read_input_tokens":71740,"output_tokens":172,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":14},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10070},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":172,"cache_read_input_tokens":71740,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10070,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10070,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_013DJL2GuE82WR4EPRR8Fowg":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/; cat framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports.md","description":"Read explosive-strength model entry"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffq23o4u8MkioqT1Kp8C","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"f9788e4f-dd67-42f2-8a77-cdda4c386596","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:07:43.339Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"f9788e4f-dd67-42f2-8a77-cdda4c386596","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"725cf014-e8e6-4e21-907d-d54c6c265ee5","agentId":"ac7100d52d01adab3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013DJL2GuE82WR4EPRR8Fowg","type":"tool_result","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14.1\"\ntopic: explosive strength annual model\ncategory: [periodization, annual-plan, concentrated-loading, explosive-strength, bi-cyclic, speed-strength]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Annual Model for Explosive-Strength Sports: Two Mesocycles, Two Concentrated Strength Blocks, the Second Smaller in Volume and Higher in Intensity\n\n**What it is** — The authors' prescriptive model for sports \"requiring explosive strength\" (PDF p. 394 / printed p. 383; Fig 6.39 on PDF p. 395 / printed p. 384; both pages checked against the page images, the figure also at 400 dpi with crops of both panels). The text gives these rules:\n1. **Bi-cyclic structure.** \"This model involves bi-cyclic periodisation of training and includes two mesocycles aimed at optimally developing the body's current adaptation reserves and achieving the planned level of special-strength preparedness at the most important competitions.\" The fitness model tracks \"absolute, explosive and starting strength indicators, the highest values of which are achieved at the end of each mesocycle\" (PDF p. 394).\n2. **Strength blocks against a background of speed and technique.** \"The organisation of loading provides for exploiting the LDTE of the block of strength work in each mesocycle against a background of the fundamental speed and technical training. The basic principle is block loading with means of different primary emphasis.\" Technique is \"not excluded during the strength loading block. However, such work is included at reduced intensity using the separate elements and their links (see 2.1.2), as well as the rhythmic patterns of individual phases\" (PDF p. 394). LDTE is the book's long-term delayed training effect of concentrated strength loading.\n3. **Second block: less volume, more intensity.** \"The second strength block imposes a smaller volume, although the general loading intensity is higher than in the first block. Since the second block is executed against a background of the adaptation occurring in the first mesocycle, the LDTE will be preserved during the second competitive stage. It is unnecessary at this time to further increase the volume of strength work, which can be used exclusively for preparing the neuromuscular system before the subsequent training that emphasizes technique or competitive readiness\" (PDF p. 394).\n4. **Block contents.** The blocks follow \"the principle of preserving the training potential of the loading by means of the conjugate sequence system (5.3.3)\" (PDF p. 394). The section just before 6.14.1 shows what a speed-strength block holds: \"Jumping > Weights exercises > Plyometrics,\" \"not sharply separated by time intervals, but substituted continuously for one another, ensuring that intensity and functional indicators are monitored carefully to avoid overtraining\" (PDF p. 394; Fig 6.38, Verkhoshansky 1977). In Fig 6.38 the three overlapping volume waves are A jumps (early, small), B weights exercises (middle, the largest) and C plyometric depth jumps (late, small); read from the image, no numeric axis.\n5. **GPP.** \"Within each block, they offer variety and aid restoration after high volume strength loading, while at the LDTE stage, the GPP assists restoration after intense specialised loading... The largest relative volume of GPP means occurs during the competitive periods\" (PDF p. 394). See [[heuristic--use-low-intensity-gpp-as-restoration-in-concentrated-loading-models]].\n\n**Fig 6.39, read from the image (all month values \"about\"; no vertical scale).** The time axis runs 11 months (the 6.14 conventions: \"Each model consists of about 11 months, since, on average, one month is allotted for the final transition period,\" PDF p. 390 / printed p. 379). Phases: I (1st preparation) about months 1-4, II (1st competition) about month 5, III (2nd preparation) about months 6-8, IV (2nd competition) about months 9-11. The phase boundaries and dashed lines do not fall exactly on the month ticks (phase I ends late in month 4; phase III ends during month 8). Block 1 is a tall shaded rectangle over about months 1-3, ending late in month 3. Block 2 is a shorter rectangle over about months 6-7: it starts at the month 5/6 tick and ends just past the month 7/8 tick, early in month 8. It is roughly two thirds of block 1's height, matching the text's \"smaller volume.\" Upper panel: the two upper curves bump at about month 1; all three strength curves are lowest at about month 2 and are already rising in month 3, while block 1 is still running. They peak at about month 5, just inside phase II (the two lower curves just right of the first dashed line; the top curve is nearly flat across most of phase II). They dip again during block 2 (lowest about months 6-7), then climb through phase IV to their highest values at month 11. The two upper curves cross just after the phase III/IV line, at about the month 8/9 tick, so only the right-edge order is certain: Starting Strength ends highest, then Explosive Strength, then Maximum Strength. Inference (following each curve smoothly through the crossing): the curve that ends at Starting Strength is the lower of the two upper curves through months 1-8. Lower panel: Technique rises inside block 1, peaks at about month 4 (just after block 1), falls through phase II to a low early in block 2 (about month 6), rises through the rest of block 2 to about its first-peak level by the block's end, peaks at about month 10 and declines toward month 11 (its course through the crossings in months 4-7 is read by continuity). GPP ends above Speed at the right edge (labels). In phase IV the two can be followed: the curve ending at Speed peaks at about months 8-9 and then falls, the curve ending at GPP is lowest at about months 8-9 and then rises, and they cross at about month 10. Before month 8 their courses cross several times and are unclear; the text is the source for GPP being highest in the competitive periods.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is the LDTE: the concentrated block lowers the athlete's state, and the gain appears afterward, so technique and speed work is timed for the period after the block. For concentrated loading in general they state a mechanism at PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370 (checked against the page image): it \"produces a brief, fairly stable state of incomplete restoration which is associated with the persistent and relatively prolonged disturbance of homeostasis. This triggers extensive functional changes which are prerequisites for subsequent supercompensation.\" The second block can be smaller because it runs \"against a background of the adaptation occurring in the first mesocycle\" (PDF p. 394). No data are shown for this model; Fig 6.39 is a schematic credited to Siff & Verkhoshansky (1999).\n\n**When to apply** — Planning a year for an explosive-strength sport with two clearly separated competition phases: put the large strength block early in the first preparation phase, a smaller and more intense block early in the second preparation phase, keep technique work going at reduced intensity inside each block, and shift to technique, speed and competition readiness as the delayed effect appears. Eric's baseline: \"In football the target is a peak every Saturday, with November and the postseason as the priority window\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 185, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)). Inference: football has one long competition phase, not two, so the model maps onto the football year only partly: a large block in the winter off-season and a smaller, more intense block in the summer before fall camp would follow the model's block sizes, with phase IV standing in for the season. The authors do not discuss football or weekly competition.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested, on two grounds that need to be kept apart.** (1) *The annual structure, inside the model's own domain (the basis for the `contested` rating).* Issurin's Block Periodization builds the year from training stages that \"should be about two months,\" four to seven a season ([[block-periodization--issurin/framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle]], Block Periodization PDF pp. 38-40), and reports world-class track and field athletes, jumpers among them, peaking 7 to 11 times in a season ([[block-periodization--issurin/claim--multi-peak-annual-preparation-elite-track-and-field]]). That is a different structure from this model's two large mesocycles; see [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]]. (2) *Issurin 2016 on the concentrated block, which bears on transferring the model to football, not on the model in its own domain.* Every model in 6.14 uses the concentrated block: \"All models in this chapter use the principle of concentrated special strength loading, which elicits the LDTE\" (PDF p. 390 / printed p. 379, checked against the page image). The library's brief of Issurin 2016 records that the concentrated-unidirectional model \"was presented as better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality and as restricted when peak performance requires many interacting abilities\" (brief, p. 11). It also records that concentrated-unidirectional programs in combat and team sports \"improved some fitness measures, but the reviewed studies did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance,\" and that the author judged that evidence \"insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\" (brief, p. 6); see [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]. The brief does not name jumping or throwing as narrow-target sports, and it does not reject the model for them. Inference: a single explosive event is the clearest case of \"a narrow target quality,\" so the brief does not count against this model where the authors place it; it counts against transferring it to American football, a team sport whose performance rests on many interacting abilities, and for team sports the reviewed studies \"did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance\" (brief, p. 6). The brief also says the review's study selection is non-systematic and author-curated, so this is Issurin's judgment, not pooled evidence. Other limits: the model is drawn for \"highly qualified athletes\" (the concentrated method \"was devised for preparing highly qualified athletes,\" PDF p. 381); the block rectangles are not literal (\"The use of rectangular blocks should not create the impression that the concentrated loads are applied suddenly at peak intensity,\" PDF p. 391 / printed p. 380); and stage boundaries \"can vary somewhat\" with the real calendar (PDF p. 391). Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule\" (line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Inference: that rule fits the model's phase IV, where the strength block is over, but the model gives no in-season strength dose, and the baseline's game week (\"lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game,\" line 186, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)) is a microcycle rule the annual model does not address.\n\n**Example** — The authors give the model, not a worked year (Fig 6.39, PDF p. 395). Inference: for a jumper with indoor and outdoor seasons, block 1 would sit in the first three months of autumn preparation with the jumps, weights and depth-jump sequence inside it, the indoor season would fall about month 5, block 2 would run about months 6-7 at less volume and more intensity, and the main outdoor competitions would fall in months 9-11.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]], [[heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work]], [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]], [[heuristic--use-low-intensity-gpp-as-restoration-in-concentrated-loading-models]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle]], [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types]]","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"01abaccc-b4d4-453b-acdc-a84379b823b8","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:07:43.544Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14.1\"\ntopic: explosive strength annual model\ncategory: [periodization, annual-plan, concentrated-loading, explosive-strength, bi-cyclic, speed-strength]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Annual Model for Explosive-Strength Sports: Two Mesocycles, Two Concentrated Strength Blocks, the Second Smaller in Volume and Higher in Intensity\n\n**What it is** — The authors' prescriptive model for sports \"requiring explosive strength\" (PDF p. 394 / printed p. 383; Fig 6.39 on PDF p. 395 / printed p. 384; both pages checked against the page images, the figure also at 400 dpi with crops of both panels). The text gives these rules:\n1. **Bi-cyclic structure.** \"This model involves bi-cyclic periodisation of training and includes two mesocycles aimed at optimally developing the body's current adaptation reserves and achieving the planned level of special-strength preparedness at the most important competitions.\" The fitness model tracks \"absolute, explosive and starting strength indicators, the highest values of which are achieved at the end of each mesocycle\" (PDF p. 394).\n2. **Strength blocks against a background of speed and technique.** \"The organisation of loading provides for exploiting the LDTE of the block of strength work in each mesocycle against a background of the fundamental speed and technical training. The basic principle is block loading with means of different primary emphasis.\" Technique is \"not excluded during the strength loading block. However, such work is included at reduced intensity using the separate elements and their links (see 2.1.2), as well as the rhythmic patterns of individual phases\" (PDF p. 394). LDTE is the book's long-term delayed training effect of concentrated strength loading.\n3. **Second block: less volume, more intensity.** \"The second strength block imposes a smaller volume, although the general loading intensity is higher than in the first block. Since the second block is executed against a background of the adaptation occurring in the first mesocycle, the LDTE will be preserved during the second competitive stage. It is unnecessary at this time to further increase the volume of strength work, which can be used exclusively for preparing the neuromuscular system before the subsequent training that emphasizes technique or competitive readiness\" (PDF p. 394).\n4. **Block contents.** The blocks follow \"the principle of preserving the training potential of the loading by means of the conjugate sequence system (5.3.3)\" (PDF p. 394). The section just before 6.14.1 shows what a speed-strength block holds: \"Jumping > Weights exercises > Plyometrics,\" \"not sharply separated by time intervals, but substituted continuously for one another, ensuring that intensity and functional indicators are monitored carefully to avoid overtraining\" (PDF p. 394; Fig 6.38, Verkhoshansky 1977). In Fig 6.38 the three overlapping volume waves are A jumps (early, small), B weights exercises (middle, the largest) and C plyometric depth jumps (late, small); read from the image, no numeric axis.\n5. **GPP.** \"Within each block, they offer variety and aid restoration after high volume strength loading, while at the LDTE stage, the GPP assists restoration after intense specialised loading... The largest relative volume of GPP means occurs during the competitive periods\" (PDF p. 394). See [[heuristic--use-low-intensity-gpp-as-restoration-in-concentrated-loading-models]].\n\n**Fig 6.39, read from the image (all month values \"about\"; no vertical scale).** The time axis runs 11 months (the 6.14 conventions: \"Each model consists of about 11 months, since, on average, one month is allotted for the final transition period,\" PDF p. 390 / printed p. 379). Phases: I (1st preparation) about months 1-4, II (1st competition) about month 5, III (2nd preparation) about months 6-8, IV (2nd competition) about months 9-11. The phase boundaries and dashed lines do not fall exactly on the month ticks (phase I ends late in month 4; phase III ends during month 8). Block 1 is a tall shaded rectangle over about months 1-3, ending late in month 3. Block 2 is a shorter rectangle over about months 6-7: it starts at the month 5/6 tick and ends just past the month 7/8 tick, early in month 8. It is roughly two thirds of block 1's height, matching the text's \"smaller volume.\" Upper panel: the two upper curves bump at about month 1; all three strength curves are lowest at about month 2 and are already rising in month 3, while block 1 is still running. They peak at about month 5, just inside phase II (the two lower curves just right of the first dashed line; the top curve is nearly flat across most of phase II). They dip again during block 2 (lowest about months 6-7), then climb through phase IV to their highest values at month 11. The two upper curves cross just after the phase III/IV line, at about the month 8/9 tick, so only the right-edge order is certain: Starting Strength ends highest, then Explosive Strength, then Maximum Strength. Inference (following each curve smoothly through the crossing): the curve that ends at Starting Strength is the lower of the two upper curves through months 1-8. Lower panel: Technique rises inside block 1, peaks at about month 4 (just after block 1), falls through phase II to a low early in block 2 (about month 6), rises through the rest of block 2 to about its first-peak level by the block's end, peaks at about month 10 and declines toward month 11 (its course through the crossings in months 4-7 is read by continuity). GPP ends above Speed at the right edge (labels). In phase IV the two can be followed: the curve ending at Speed peaks at about months 8-9 and then falls, the curve ending at GPP is lowest at about months 8-9 and then rises, and they cross at about month 10. Before month 8 their courses cross several times and are unclear; the text is the source for GPP being highest in the competitive periods.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is the LDTE: the concentrated block lowers the athlete's state, and the gain appears afterward, so technique and speed work is timed for the period after the block. For concentrated loading in general they state a mechanism at PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370 (checked against the page image): it \"produces a brief, fairly stable state of incomplete restoration which is associated with the persistent and relatively prolonged disturbance of homeostasis. This triggers extensive functional changes which are prerequisites for subsequent supercompensation.\" The second block can be smaller because it runs \"against a background of the adaptation occurring in the first mesocycle\" (PDF p. 394). No data are shown for this model; Fig 6.39 is a schematic credited to Siff & Verkhoshansky (1999).\n\n**When to apply** — Planning a year for an explosive-strength sport with two clearly separated competition phases: put the large strength block early in the first preparation phase, a smaller and more intense block early in the second preparation phase, keep technique work going at reduced intensity inside each block, and shift to technique, speed and competition readiness as the delayed effect appears. Eric's baseline: \"In football the target is a peak every Saturday, with November and the postseason as the priority window\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 185, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)). Inference: football has one long competition phase, not two, so the model maps onto the football year only partly: a large block in the winter off-season and a smaller, more intense block in the summer before fall camp would follow the model's block sizes, with phase IV standing in for the season. The authors do not discuss football or weekly competition.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested, on two grounds that need to be kept apart.** (1) *The annual structure, inside the model's own domain (the basis for the `contested` rating).* Issurin's Block Periodization builds the year from training stages that \"should be about two months,\" four to seven a season ([[block-periodization--issurin/framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle]], Block Periodization PDF pp. 38-40), and reports world-class track and field athletes, jumpers among them, peaking 7 to 11 times in a season ([[block-periodization--issurin/claim--multi-peak-annual-preparation-elite-track-and-field]]). That is a different structure from this model's two large mesocycles; see [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]]. (2) *Issurin 2016 on the concentrated block, which bears on transferring the model to football, not on the model in its own domain.* Every model in 6.14 uses the concentrated block: \"All models in this chapter use the principle of concentrated special strength loading, which elicits the LDTE\" (PDF p. 390 / printed p. 379, checked against the page image). The library's brief of Issurin 2016 records that the concentrated-unidirectional model \"was presented as better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality and as restricted when peak performance requires many interacting abilities\" (brief, p. 11). It also records that concentrated-unidirectional programs in combat and team sports \"improved some fitness measures, but the reviewed studies did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance,\" and that the author judged that evidence \"insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\" (brief, p. 6); see [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]. The brief does not name jumping or throwing as narrow-target sports, and it does not reject the model for them. Inference: a single explosive event is the clearest case of \"a narrow target quality,\" so the brief does not count against this model where the authors place it; it counts against transferring it to American football, a team sport whose performance rests on many interacting abilities, and for team sports the reviewed studies \"did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance\" (brief, p. 6). The brief also says the review's study selection is non-systematic and author-curated, so this is Issurin's judgment, not pooled evidence. Other limits: the model is drawn for \"highly qualified athletes\" (the concentrated method \"was devised for preparing highly qualified athletes,\" PDF p. 381); the block rectangles are not literal (\"The use of rectangular blocks should not create the impression that the concentrated loads are applied suddenly at peak intensity,\" PDF p. 391 / printed p. 380); and stage boundaries \"can vary somewhat\" with the real calendar (PDF p. 391). Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule\" (line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Inference: that rule fits the model's phase IV, where the strength block is over, but the model gives no in-season strength dose, and the baseline's game week (\"lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game,\" line 186, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)) is a microcycle rule the annual model does not address.\n\n**Example** — The authors give the model, not a worked year (Fig 6.39, PDF p. 395). Inference: for a jumper with indoor and outdoor seasons, block 1 would sit in the first three months of autumn preparation with the jumps, weights and depth-jump sequence inside it, the indoor season would fall about month 5, block 2 would run about months 6-7 at less volume and more intensity, and the main outdoor competitions would fall in months 9-11.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]], [[heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work]], [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]], [[heuristic--use-low-intensity-gpp-as-restoration-in-concentrated-loading-models]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle]], [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitatio