{"parentUuid":"bb568a4d-2d56-4273-985b-08fb3f829e73","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac7100d52d01adab3","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffq2CaQYZoLnCAQ6bmjD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4512,"cache_read_input_tokens":81810,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4512,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":448,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cffq2CLWZsNZG6tSdTEpe","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"acf716bd-1635-430a-8113-12dbc7fccef8","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:07:44.802Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"acf716bd-1635-430a-8113-12dbc7fccef8","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac7100d52d01adab3","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffq2CaQYZoLnCAQ6bmjD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QHhboMtpNUCzjQWqCf58tk","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging; mkdir -p _renders; ~/.local/bin/uv run --with pymupdf python3 -c \"\nimport pymupdf\nd=pymupdf.open('books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf')\nfor n in [395,383,388]:\n    p=d[n]; pix=p.get_pixmap(dpi=400); pix.save(f'_renders/r2_6d_p{n}.png'); print(n,pix.width,pix.height)\nprint(d[394].get_text()); print('=====395'); print(d[395].get_text())\n\"","description":"Render figure pages and print text of PDF 394-395"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4512,"cache_read_input_tokens":81810,"output_tokens":325,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":38},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4512},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":325,"cache_read_input_tokens":81810,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4512,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4512,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01QHhboMtpNUCzjQWqCf58tk":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging; mkdir -p _renders; ~/.local/bin/uv run --with pymupdf python3 -c \"\nimport pymupdf\nd=pymupdf.open('books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf')\nfor n in [395,383,388]:\n    p=d[n]; pix=p.get_pixmap(dpi=400); pix.save(f'_renders/r2_6d_p{n}.png'); print(n,pix.width,pix.height)\nprint(d[394].get_text()); print('=====395'); print(d[395].get_text())\n\"","description":"Render figure pages and print text of PDF 394-395"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffq2CLWZsNZG6tSdTEpe","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"49edd3c5-7e7e-4f07-8a45-c039b95173a2","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:07:46.431Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"49edd3c5-7e7e-4f07-8a45-c039b95173a2","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"725cf014-e8e6-4e21-907d-d54c6c265ee5","agentId":"ac7100d52d01adab3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QHhboMtpNUCzjQWqCf58tk","type":"tool_result","content":"395 3704 5262\n383 3807 5262\n388 3710 5267\nSUPERTRAINING\nThe Composition of the Concentrated Loading Volume\nAs mentioned earlier, the rectangular blocks used in the diagrams of the training models to depict stages of \nconcentrated loading are not intended to show their precise form or contents. Each block represents concentrated \nloading of a primary emphasis, which does not necessarily utilise only one means of training. For instance, a \nconcentrated block intended to increase speed-strength may consist of several different resistance and jumping \nmeans and methods introduced in a specific conjugate sequence.\nIn Figure 6.38, methods with a successively greater training effect are sequenced within the concentrated \nloading stage, thus: Jumping > Weights exercises > Plyometrics. These exercises are not sharply separated by \ntime intervals, but substituted continuously for one another, ensuring that intensity and functional indicators arc \nmonitored carefully to avoid overtraining.\nFigure 6.38 Structure and contents of exercises within a typical concentrated loading volume for speed-strength sports. A - jumps, B= \nweights exercises, C ■ plyometric depth jumps. (Verkhoshansky, 1977).\nIt is now appropriate to examine principal prescriptive models of the systems of constructing training in the \nannual cycle for a number of sport groups.\n6.14.1 A Model for Sports requiring Explosive Strength\nThis model involves bi-cyclic periodisation of training and includes two mesocycles aimed at optimally \ndeveloping the body's current adaptation reserves and achieving the planned level of special-strength \npreparedness at the most important competitions (Fig 6.39). The model of the dynamics of the athlete’s fitness \nstate (upper diagram) shows the optimal trend in the changes of the absolute, explosive and starting strength \nindicators, the highest values of which are achieved at the end of each mesocycle.\nThe organisation of loading provides for exploiting the LDTE of the block of strength work in each \nmcsocycle against a background of the fundamental speed and technical training. The basic principle is block \nloading with means of different primary emphasis, which creates favourable conditions for in-depth improvement \nof technique and uni-directional preparation for competition. As stated before, technique work is not excluded \nduring the strength loading block. However, such work is included at reduced intensity using the separate \nelements and their links (sec 2.1.2), as well as the rhythmic patterns of individual phases.\nThe second strength block imposes a smaller volume, although the general loading intensity is higher than in \nthe first block. Since the second block is executed against a background of the adaptation occurring in the first \nmesocycle, the LDTE will be preserved during the second competitive stage. It is unnecessary at this time to \nfurther increase the volume of strength work, which can be used exclusively for preparing the neuromuscular \nsystem before the subsequent training that emphasizes technique or competitive readiness. The contents and the \norganisation of the strength loading blocks are based on the principle of preserving the training potential of the \nloading by means of the conjugate sequence system (5.3.3).\nThe means of GPP have a specific purpose. Within each block, they offer variety and aid restoration after \nhigh volume strength loading, while at the LDTE stage, the GPP assists restoration after intense specialised \nloading. It should always be remembered that this procedure constitutes part of pedagogical restoration, which \nfacilitates recovery alongside other methods such as sports massage (see 8.5). The largest relative volume of \nGPP means occurs during the competitive periods.\n383\n\n=====395\nSUPERTRAINING\nFigure 6.39 A model of constructing the training for sports requiring explosive effort. I = the 1st preparation phase, II = the 1st \ncompetition phase. III = the 2nd preparauon phase. IV = the 2nd competition phase. The shaded blocks represent the stages of \nconcentrated special strength loading to promote the subsequent LDTE. (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999).\n6.14.2 A Model for Medium Duration Endurance Sports\nThe model is based on bi-cyclic periodisation and consists of two mesocyclcs designed for optimally developing \nthe current adaptation reserves (Fig 6.40). There are two competition stages in the annual cycle and one need not \ncompete at the fundamental distance during the first stage. Here, it is important to maintain the trend towards \nintensifying the training, in order to increase the current adaptation reserves. One can achieve this by competing \nat other distances in competition, which will simultaneously serve as control estimates of the athlete’s level of \naerobic and anaerobic capacity.\nThe model of the dynamics of the athlete's state promotes twin-peaked changes in aerobic and anaerobic \ncapacity. The dynamics of the special strength preparedness arc expressed in the form of an accelerated increase \nin general strength-endurance and special endurance for repetitive production of explosive force (i.e. explosive \nstrength-endurance) during the second competitive stage, as well as by the planned rise in maximum strength.\nExplosive strength peaks at the beginning of the second competition stage, after which it decreases, together \nwith the rise in the volume of intense (anaerobic-glycolytic) cyclic work. The fundamental functional indicators, \nnamely anaerobic capacity and strength-endurance, reach their highest levels during the most important \ncompetitive stage (10th - 11th months).\nThis organisation of loading combines two strength blocks concurrently with special \naerobic-anaerobic \nwork, where the ratio of aerobic to anaerobic work changes over the annual cycle. There is a predominance of \naerobic loading during the first preparatory stage; during the second stage the work is chiefly at the OBLA (onset \nof blood lactate accumulation) level. There is an increase in the volume of aerobic loading again during the \nsecond competition stage, its role being to promote restoration. Speed work (primarily in the ATP-CP zone) and \nthe development of speed-endurance (chiefly in the anaerobic-glycolytic zone) are timed for the stage where one \nrealises the LDTE of the strength loading, i.e. the development of speed precedes that of speed-endurance.\nThus, the general strategy of loading organisation in the annual cycle is based on the following sequence of \nenhancing special work capacity: General endurance > Speed > Speed-endurance, the orientation of the training \nprocess being towards the systematic development of strength and speed-cndurance during the stage when there \nare important competitions.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"71393b3e-5b90-43c5-88bb-f9322e6c112c","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:07:50.294Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"395 3704 5262\n383 3807 5262\n388 3710 5267\nSUPERTRAINING\nThe Composition of the Concentrated Loading Volume\nAs mentioned earlier, the rectangular blocks used in the diagrams of the training models to depict stages of \nconcentrated loading are not intended to show their precise form or contents. Each block represents concentrated \nloading of a primary emphasis, which does not necessarily utilise only one means of training. For instance, a \nconcentrated block intended to increase speed-strength may consist of several different resistance and jumping \nmeans and methods introduced in a specific conjugate sequence.\nIn Figure 6.38, methods with a successively greater training effect are sequenced within the concentrated \nloading stage, thus: Jumping > Weights exercises > Plyometrics. These exercises are not sharply separated by \ntime intervals, but substituted continuously for one another, ensuring that intensity and functional indicators arc \nmonitored carefully to avoid overtraining.\nFigure 6.38 Structure and contents of exercises within a typical concentrated loading volume for speed-strength sports. A - jumps, B= \nweights exercises, C ■ plyometric depth jumps. (Verkhoshansky, 1977).\nIt is now appropriate to examine principal prescriptive models of the systems of constructing training in the \nannual cycle for a number of sport groups.\n6.14.1 A Model for Sports requiring Explosive Strength\nThis model involves bi-cyclic periodisation of training and includes two mesocycles aimed at optimally \ndeveloping the body's current adaptation reserves and achieving the planned level of special-strength \npreparedness at the most important competitions (Fig 6.39). The model of the dynamics of the athlete’s fitness \nstate (upper diagram) shows the optimal trend in the changes of the absolute, explosive and starting strength \nindicators, the highest values of which are achieved at the end of each mesocycle.\nThe organisation of loading provides for exploiting the LDTE of the block of strength work in each \nmcsocycle against a background of the fundamental speed and technical training. The basic principle is block \nloading with means of different primary emphasis, which creates favourable conditions for in-depth improvement \nof technique and uni-directional preparation for competition. As stated before, technique work is not excluded \nduring the strength loading block. However, such work is included at reduced intensity using the separate \nelements and their links (sec 2.1.2), as well as the rhythmic patterns of individual phases.\nThe second strength block imposes a smaller volume, although the general loading intensity is higher than in \nthe first block. Since the second block is executed against a background of the adaptation occurring in the first \nmesocycle, the LDTE will be preserved during the second competitive stage. It is unnecessary at this time to \nfurther increase the volume of strength work, which can be used exclusively for preparing the neuromuscular \nsystem before the subsequent training that emphasizes technique or competitive readiness. The contents and the \norganisation of the strength loading blocks are based on the principle of preserving the training potential of the \nloading by means of the conjugate sequence system (5.3.3).\nThe means of GPP have a specific purpose. Within each block, they offer variety and aid restoration after \nhigh volume strength loading, while at the LDTE stage, the GPP assists restoration after intense specialised \nloading. It should always be remembered that this procedure constitutes part of pedagogical restoration, which \nfacilitates recovery alongside other methods such as sports massage (see 8.5). The largest relative volume of \nGPP means occurs during the competitive periods.\n383\n\n=====395\nSUPERTRAINING\nFigure 6.39 A model of constructing the training for sports requiring explosive effort. I = the 1st preparation phase, II = the 1st \ncompetition phase. III = the 2nd preparauon phase. IV = the 2nd competition phase. The shaded blocks represent the stages of \nconcentrated special strength loading to promote the subsequent LDTE. (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999).\n6.14.2 A Model for Medium Duration Endurance Sports\nThe model is based on bi-cyclic periodisation and consists of two mesocyclcs designed for optimally developing \nthe current adaptation reserves (Fig 6.40). There are two competition stages in the annual cycle and one need not \ncompete at the fundamental distance during the first stage. Here, it is important to maintain the trend towards \nintensifying the training, in order to increase the current adaptation reserves. One can achieve this by competing \nat other distances in competition, which will simultaneously serve as control estimates of the athlete’s level of \naerobic and anaerobic capacity.\nThe model of the dynamics of the athlete's state promotes twin-peaked changes in aerobic and anaerobic \ncapacity. The dynamics of the special strength preparedness arc expressed in the form of an accelerated increase \nin general strength-endurance and special endurance for repetitive production of explosive force (i.e. explosive \nstrength-endurance) during the second competitive stage, as well as by the planned rise in maximum strength.\nExplosive strength peaks at the beginning of the second competition stage, after which it decreases, together \nwith the rise in the volume of intense (anaerobic-glycolytic) cyclic work. The fundamental functional indicators, \nnamely anaerobic capacity and strength-endurance, reach their highest levels during the most important \ncompetitive stage (10th - 11th months).\nThis organisation of loading combines two strength blocks concurrently with special \naerobic-anaerobic \nwork, where the ratio of aerobic to anaerobic work changes over the annual cycle. There is a predominance of \naerobic loading during the first preparatory stage; during the second stage the work is chiefly at the OBLA (onset \nof blood lactate accumulation) level. There is an increase in the volume of aerobic loading again during the \nsecond competition stage, its role being to promote restoration. Speed work (primarily in the ATP-CP zone) and \nthe development of speed-endurance (chiefly in the anaerobic-glycolytic zone) are timed for the stage where one \nrealises the LDTE of the strength loading, i.e. the development of speed precedes that of speed-endurance.\nThus, the general strategy of loading organisation in the annual cycle is based on the following sequence of \nenhancing special work capacity: General endurance > Speed > Speed-endurance, the orientation of the training \nprocess being towards the systematic development of strength and speed-cndurance during the stage when there \nare important competitions.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--core-stability-balance-training-has-no-shown-transfer-to-sport.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":0,"oldLines":0,"newStart":1,"newLines":26,"lines":["+---","+type: library-entry","+kind: claim","+domain: strength-and-conditioning","+book: supertraining--verkhoshansky","+chapter: \"7.4\"","+topic: core stabilisation training","+category: [core, balance-training, transfer, cueing]","+confidence: untested","+ingest-tier: full-text","+created: 2026-10-03","+---","+","+# Core Stabilisation on Balls and Boards, and Cueing the Transversus Abdominis, Have No Shown Transfer to Sport","+","+**What it is** — The authors write that, \"largely as a consequence of individuals attempting to extrapolate to the general fitness world some of the muscle isolating methods used by physical therapists for injury rehabilitation, it has become fairly fashionable to encounter special programmes to train 'core stabilisation' with balancing tools such as wobble boards and physio balls, augmented by activation of the transversus abdominis (TA) to strengthen the trunk.\" Three objections follow. First, \"stability of the core or trunk cannot be exhibited without the periphery of the body being firmly secured on a solid surface, unless, of course, the athlete has to use the core independently of the floor or any other apparatus, as in diving, trampolining and jumping.\" Second, \"Well meaning instructions to activate TA during dynamic actions such as lifting and wrestling are highly inappropriate, because conscious intervention in any such movements can lead to \"paralysis by analysis\",\" and \"if the athlete is taught the optimal way to carry out any movement, the body naturally activates muscles in the most appropriate way for that given activity. After all, the body \"knows only of movement, not muscles\".\" Third, \"no research has yet shown that isolationist training or ball/board balancing skills transpose to 'functional' sporting action, so that time spent on such pursuits usually can be better spent on more sport specific drills, unless the aim is to resolve some injury.\" (All PDF p. 427 / printed p. 416, checked against the page image.)","+","+**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning rests on two earlier points in the same section: isolation is load-dependent, \"Isolation may occur to a significant extent when the resistance to movement is small, but with increasing load, the relevant stabilisers of the body and joints, as well as assistant movers, come more strongly into play,\" and \"all human movement involves the intricate orchestration of concurrent and sequential contractions of movers and stabilisers\" (PDF p. 427 / printed p. 416). Inference: if stabilisers are recruited by the task and load, a well-learned loaded movement already trains them, and a separate isolation drill adds little; the book states the premises, not this full chain.","+","+**When to apply** — When an athlete's programme spends time on unstable-surface or TA-isolation drills, ask whether that time would be better spent on loaded, sport-specific work, and keep coaching cues external to the movement rather than to a single muscle. The exception the authors grant is rehabilitation: \"unless the aim is to resolve some injury\" (PDF p. 427 / printed p. 416), which in Baylor terms is Athletic Medicine's call. Eric's baseline: \"Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints); single-joint assistance work is kept to a small share of total volume.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 104, Inferred (transcript + slides)).","+","+**When NOT to apply** — \"No research has yet shown\" is a statement about the evidence when the book was written, with no citation, and the book gives no study of its own: confidence `untested`. No entry in the vault or staging library covers core stability or unstable-surface training, so the claim could not be checked against another library source. The authors themselves exempt sports where the core works without a fixed base (\"diving, trampolining and jumping\") and injury rehabilitation. \"Paralysis by analysis\" is asserted, not measured.","+","+**Example** — The authors' own: instructing a lifter or wrestler to brace the transversus abdominis during the action, which they call \"highly inappropriate\" (PDF p. 427 / printed p. 416). Inference: a coach would instead teach the squat or the takedown well and let trunk activation follow the task.","+","+**Related** — [[claim--muscle-isolation-is-load-dependent-mostly-illusory-under-heavy-load]] (4.2.9, cited by the authors as the earlier discussion), [[claim--multiarticular-muscle-action-cannot-be-predicted-from-joints-spanned]] (the \"Limitations of Anatomical Movement Analysis\" section 7.4 asks the reader to bear in mind), [[claim--functional-training-means-sport-specific-and-no-exercise-is-non-functional]], [[claim--trunk-rotation-is-driven-by-the-obliques-not-the-erector-spinae]]"]}],"created":true},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--miscellaneous-bodybuilding-set-variations.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":27,"oldLines":7,"newStart":27,"newLines":7,"lines":[" - **Peak contraction sets:** finish each repetition \"at the point of maximal muscle contraction (see Figs 2.8, 2.9) and not at the end of range when the force has already diminished significantly\", with intense isometric or quasi-isometric effort there."," - **Continuous tension sets** (PDF p. 410): smooth repetitions without bounce, cheating or pause, joints never fully locked; cycling and arm ergometry give continuous tension naturally."," ","-**The PHA claim.** Upper-lower body sets are \"sometimes inappropriately called peripheral heart action (PHA) sets\". The switch to heavy activation of one body part \"tends to promote reflexive relaxation of another distant body part, a process first described by Setchinov in the early 1900s\". Proponents believe PHA \"avoids engorging one muscle part with blood and encourages the development of circulatory fitness\". The authors reply: \"The fact is that all human resisted movement and not just PHA encourages peripheral circulation and PHA offers no superior cardio-circulatory benefits compared with other systems of resistance training\" (PDF p. 412 / printed p. 401). They cite no study for this. In 5.6 the same Setchinov effect is used for restoration between circuit stations rather than relaxation ([[heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing]]); the two descriptions differ in emphasis, not in direction.","+**The PHA claim.** Upper-lower body sets are \"sometimes inappropriately called peripheral heart action (PHA) sets\". The switch to heavy activation of one body part \"tends to promote reflexive relaxation of another distant body part, a process first described by Setchinov in the early 1900s\". Proponents believe PHA \"avoids engorging one muscle part with blood and encourages the development of circulatory fitness\". The authors reply: \"The fact is that all human resisted movement and not just PHA encourages peripheral circulation and PHA offers no superior cardio-circulatory benefits compared with other systems of resistance training\" (PDF p. 412 / printed p. 401). They cite no study for this. In 5.6 the same Setchinov effect is used for restoration between circuit stations rather than relaxation ([[heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing]]). Inference: the two descriptions differ in emphasis, not in direction."," "," **Why it works** — The authors' general mechanism is habituation: a method that \"stimulate[s] the body in an unexpected way\" may break \"habituation and stagnation\" after long training (PDF p. 412). Specific reasons: strict sets build \"neuromuscular patterns of control\" in beginners; muscle priority puts the weakest group where energy is highest; staggered sets use otherwise idle rest; peak contraction sets avoid ending repetitions where force \"has already diminished significantly\" (PDF pp. 412-413). No study is cited for any of them."," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--core-stability-balance-training-has-no-shown-transfer-to-sport.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--miscellaneous-bodybuilding-set-variations.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"49edd3c5-7e7e-4f07-8a45-c039b95173a2","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
