{"parentUuid":"806948a7-4e9e-46c6-a60c-7fda4baf7666","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac7100d52d01adab3","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffq7SgNJXouinb5s1GgB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GcXWwwjMf9WVBfgHyfoxm1","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/block-periodization--issurin/; cat framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle.md; printf '\\n#####\\n'; cat claim--multi-peak-annual-preparation-elite-track-and-field.md","description":"Read two Issurin book entries"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2179,"cache_read_input_tokens":104324,"output_tokens":393,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":202},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2179},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":393,"cache_read_input_tokens":104324,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2179,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2179,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01GcXWwwjMf9WVBfgHyfoxm1":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/block-periodization--issurin/; cat framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle.md; printf '\\n#####\\n'; cat claim--multi-peak-annual-preparation-elite-track-and-field.md","description":"Read two Issurin book entries"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffq7Qm1vGQnZt73uN3rs","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"6670dffb-b87e-46a5-a790-895389bd779a","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:08:58.376Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"6670dffb-b87e-46a5-a790-895389bd779a","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"ce914f6b-f81b-42fd-a40b-4c5550786125","agentId":"ac7100d52d01adab3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GcXWwwjMf9WVBfgHyfoxm1","type":"tool_result","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: block-periodization--issurin\nchapter: \"1.2.3\"\ntopic: training stage structure and multi-peak annual planning\ncategory: [periodization, peaking, annual-plan, programming]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-09-26\n---\n\n# The Training Stage (A-T-R + Competition) Is the Annual Plan's Building Block, Enabling Multiple Peaks\n\n**What it is** — Issurin's annual cycle is built from a sequence of **training stages**,\neach one containing all three mesocycle-blocks (accumulation, transmutation, realization) in\norder, ending in a competition (PDF p. 38, printed p. 27). Each training stage \"gives the\nappearance of an annual cycle in miniature\": a block resembling a preparatory period\n(accumulation), one resembling a competition period (transmutation), and a tapering block\nending in competitive performance (realization) (PDF p. 39, printed p. 28). The duration of\na training stage is set by how long the constituent residual training effects can be\nsuperimposed (Figure 1.8; see\n[[claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]]) and \"should be about two\nmonths,\" though Issurin notes stages can run shorter close to season peaking or longer\nearlier in the season, in which case \"special measures should be used to prolong residual\ntraining effects\" (developed in Chapter 3, now ingested; see\n[[framework--prolonging-residual-effects-with-mini-blocks]] and `_book.md`).\nThe number of training stages in a season \"usually varies from four to seven,\" depending on\nthe sport's competition calendar (PDF p. 40, printed p. 29, Figure 1.9). Issurin lists four\nbenefits of this design over the traditional model at this point in the text (PDF p. 40,\nprinted p. 29): the model allows a reduction in total mileage and time spent training\nwithout substantially changing the total number of workouts; monitoring of trainedness is\nmore purposeful and effective, because the reduced number of targeted abilities allows more\nappropriate tests and an easier dose-effect analysis; psychological traits improve because\nathletes can focus on fewer abilities, supporting mental concentration and motivation; and\nnutritional aspects can be more carefully targeted (a high-protein diet to enhance strength\ntraining's anabolic effect, and carbohydrates \"particularly important in mesocycles for\nspecialized and strength-endurance\" work). Table 1.8 (PDF p. 42, printed p. 31) is a\nseparate table contrasting the traditional and Block Periodization models' main differences,\nnot itself a benefits list. Separately, and also on that page (PDF p. 42, printed p. 31),\nIssurin states the intermediate peaks \"can be planned for mid-season and even for the early\npart of the season,\" not only at a single season-end date. A second, five-item list appears\nlater in the chapter (PDF p. 44, printed p. 33) and differs from the first rather than\nrepeating it: it adds that total training volume \"can be greatly reduced, minimizing the\nincidence of overtraining,\" that the multi-peak design \"facilitates successful participation\nin multiple competitions over the entire season,\" and, as a fifth item, \"a multi-stage annual\nplan creates more favorable conditions for peaking in time for the main competition of the\nseason\"; it keeps the monitoring and diet/restoration points from the first list but omits\nthe psychological benefit.\n\n**Why it works** — Because each training stage runs its own compressed\naccumulation-transmutation-realization cycle and ends in a real competitive test, the athlete\nis brought to a peak (or near-peak) at the end of every stage rather than once per year;\nstacking four to seven such stages across a season is what produces the multi-peak pattern.\nIssurin's stated physiological contrast (Table 1.8) is that the traditional model relies on\nadaptation to concurrent stimuli across a long period, while this model relies on\nsuperimposing sequential residual effects from short, concentrated blocks; the same\nmechanism underlies [[framework--accumulation-transmutation-realization-mesocycles]].\n\n**When to apply** — **Inference:** this is the entry most directly responsive to the strength-and-conditioning\nmiss-log gap (\"peaking phase structure for collegiate football,\" logged 2026-09-03): for a\nfootball program needing camp-readiness, then in-season week-to-week competitive readiness,\nthis framework suggests treating each significant test point (fall camp scrimmage, each\ngame week, or a defined in-season block) as a candidate training-stage boundary rather than\nrelying on a single annual peak, with the caveat below about untested claims. **Eric's\nbaseline** does not currently describe an explicit training-stage or multi-peak model for\nfootball; the closest baseline material is the general macro/meso/microcycle planning\nlanguage and the in-season rule \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" (`method-baseline.md`,\n\"Periodization and programming\" and \"Strength and power,\" citing\n`01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md` pp.17-18, 20-21). This Issurin framework offers a\nmore explicit structural answer to \"how do you actually sequence a season with more than one\npeak\" than anything currently captured in the baseline.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The \"four to seven stages\" and \"about two months\"\nfigures are stated without a derivation the reader can check: Issurin gives no worked\nexample of how stage count or duration is calculated for a specific sport's calendar in\nthis chapter. **Inference:** football's in-season structure (weekly games, not discrete\nmulti-week competition-free blocks) does not map cleanly onto a ~2-month training stage.\nIssurin's model is built from and mainly evidenced by individual/dual sports with discrete\ncompetitions (track and field, canoe-kayak, windsurfing); Chapter 3's microcycle-level\nmaterial (now ingested; see `_book.md`) is a necessary next read before this framework can\nbe translated into a week-by-week in-season football plan. **Caveat:** Issurin's own later\npeer-reviewed review (Issurin, 2016, *Sports Medicine*, \"Benefits and Limitations of Block\nPeriodized Training Approaches to Athletes' Preparation,\" Zotero item `PT9QTQ2I`, full text\ncached in this library at\n`research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a.md`)\nsupports the multi-targeted (A-T-R) block model this entry describes with 28 studies (its\np.11), but that support is a non-systematic, author-curated synthesis, not a pooled\ncomparative estimate (vault brief, Limitations). The team- and combat-sport studies the same\nreview reports without performance or team-practice data (its p.6) tested Verkhoshansky's\nconcentrated-unidirectional model (Table 1, \"based on Verkhoshansky\"), not this book's\ntraining-stage (A-T-R) model. No study in this library tests A-T-R training stages in\nAmerican football. **Inference:**\nBompa & Haff's 5th edition, already in this library, may also bear on the \"does not work\nwell\" claim, but only Bompa Ch1-2 has been ingested so far (`periodization--bompa/_book.md`:\nCh6 Annual Plan and Ch8 Training Cycles are pending), so it cannot ground this contest yet;\ncheck once those chapters are ingested.\n\n**Example** — **Inference:** a five-stage football off-season/camp plan: Stage 1 (general\naccumulation, ~8 weeks, winter conditioning); Stage 2 (strength/power transmutation into\nspring ball, ending in the spring game as a \"competition\"); Stage 3 (summer accumulation);\nStage 4 (fall-camp transmutation, ending in the first scrimmage or game week as\nrealization); Stage 5+ (in-season stages keyed to bye weeks or rivalry-week peaks, pending\nChapter 3's in-season microcycle guidance).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--accumulation-transmutation-realization-mesocycles]],\n[[claim--multi-peak-annual-preparation-elite-track-and-field]] (the empirical motivation for\nneeding a multi-peak model), [[claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]],\n[[principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]].\n\n#####\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: block-periodization--issurin\nchapter: \"1.1.3\"\ntopic: multi-peak competition calendars of elite athletes\ncategory: [periodization, peaking, evidence]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-09-26\n---\n\n# World-Class Track and Field Athletes Compete and Peak Many Times Per Season, Not Once\n\n**What it is** — Table 1.5 (PDF p. 24, printed p. 13, based on Suslov, 2001, with Issurin's\nmodifications) reports three world-class athletes' single-season competition patterns:\n**Marion Jones** (100-200m, long jump; 1998 season) had **10 peaks** (8 in running, 2 in\nlong jump, all at personal-best level) across a **200-day** competitive span, with typical\n19-22 day intervals between peaks. **Sergei Bubka** (pole vault; 1991 season) had **7\npeaks** (all within 3% of his personal best, 595-612 cm) across a **265-day** span,\n23-43 day intervals. **Stefka Kostadinova** (high jump; 1998 season) had **11 peaks** (all\nwithin 3% of her personal best, 200-205 cm), 14-25 day intervals, across winter ~20 days and\nspring/summer 135 days (**Inference:** 155 days combined; the table gives the two figures\nseparately, not a single summed span). Issurin's summary point, stated on the preceding page\n(PDF p. 23, printed p. 12): these athletes' 5-8 month competitive spans \"can not be\nsubdivided into traditional preparatory and competitive periods,\" yet their basic abilities\n(max strength, aerobic regeneration capacity) still had to be maintained across that entire\nspan.\n\n**Why it works: this is observational, not interventional** — These are real competition\nrecords for three specific, historically documented elite athletes (Olympic/World\nchampions), not an experiment; the \"why\" is a report of what elite competition calendars\nactually looked like by 1991/1998, cited to Suslov's (2001) analysis rather than derived\nfrom a controlled study of periodization models.\n\n**When to apply** — **Inference:** as direct evidence for the strength-and-conditioning\nmiss-log's \"peaking phase structure\" gap, this table is the empirical case Issurin uses to argue that a\nsingle annual peak (or even a two-/three-peak model) does not describe how modern elite\nindividual-sport athletes actually compete, which motivates the multi-stage annual model in\n[[framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle]]. Useful for framing the *problem*\n(competition calendars now demand many peaks) to Baylor coaches even before the\n*solution* (Chapter 3's microcycle-level guidance, now ingested; see `_book.md`) is read.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — All three examples are individual, non-contact,\ntechnique/power sports (sprinting/jumping, pole vault, high jump) where \"peak\" can be defined\nby a single measurable performance (time, height, distance) on a given day. **Inference:**\nthis evidence does not directly transfer to football, where \"peak\" is not a single\nmeasurable output and the competitive calendar is fixed by a weekly game schedule rather\nthan a chosen sequence of meets; the multi-peak *problem* generalizes (a football season\nalso demands repeated high-level performance, not one), but the specific 19-43 day\ninter-peak intervals shown here are specific to how often these particular athletes chose to\ncompete, not a transferable spacing rule for football practice or game weeks. Data source is\nsingle (Suslov, 2001, \"with modifications\" by Issurin) with no stated method for how \"peak\"\nwas defined or verified for each performance.\n\n**Example** — **Inference:** when explaining to a Baylor position group why in-season\nprogramming assumes more than one readiness peak per season, this table is a citable,\nreal-world existence proof that elite athletes routinely peak 7-11 times in a season; useful\nfor framing, not for directly copying an inter-peak interval into a football in-season plan.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle]].","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"00849bec-69ac-4e7d-bd4e-2b8756a43356","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:08:58.617Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: block-periodization--issurin\nchapter: \"1.2.3\"\ntopic: training stage structure and multi-peak annual planning\ncategory: [periodization, peaking, annual-plan, programming]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-09-26\n---\n\n# The Training Stage (A-T-R + Competition) Is the Annual Plan's Building Block, Enabling Multiple Peaks\n\n**What it is** — Issurin's annual cycle is built from a sequence of **training stages**,\neach one containing all three mesocycle-blocks (accumulation, transmutation, realization) in\norder, ending in a competition (PDF p. 38, printed p. 27). Each training stage \"gives the\nappearance of an annual cycle in miniature\": a block resembling a preparatory period\n(accumulation), one resembling a competition period (transmutation), and a tapering block\nending in competitive performance (realization) (PDF p. 39, printed p. 28). The duration of\na training stage is set by how long the constituent residual training effects can be\nsuperimposed (Figure 1.8; see\n[[claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]]) and \"should be about two\nmonths,\" though Issurin notes stages can run shorter close to season peaking or longer\nearlier in the season, in which case \"special measures should be used to prolong residual\ntraining effects\" (developed in Chapter 3, now ingested; see\n[[framework--prolonging-residual-effects-with-mini-blocks]] and `_book.md`).\nThe number of training stages in a season \"usually varies from four to seven,\" depending on\nthe sport's competition calendar (PDF p. 40, printed p. 29, Figure 1.9). Issurin lists four\nbenefits of this design over the traditional model at this point in the text (PDF p. 40,\nprinted p. 29): the model allows a reduction in total mileage and time spent training\nwithout substantially changing the total number of workouts; monitoring of trainedness is\nmore purposeful and effective, because the reduced number of targeted abilities allows more\nappropriate tests and an easier dose-effect analysis; psychological traits improve because\nathletes can focus on fewer abilities, supporting mental concentration and motivation; and\nnutritional aspects can be more carefully targeted (a high-protein diet to enhance strength\ntraining's anabolic effect, and carbohydrates \"particularly important in mesocycles for\nspecialized and strength-endurance\" work). Table 1.8 (PDF p. 42, printed p. 31) is a\nseparate table contrasting the traditional and Block Periodization models' main differences,\nnot itself a benefits list. Separately, and also on that page (PDF p. 42, printed p. 31),\nIssurin states the intermediate peaks \"can be planned for mid-season and even for the early\npart of the season,\" not only at a single season-end date. A second, five-item list appears\nlater in the chapter (PDF p. 44, printed p. 33) and differs from the first rather than\nrepeating it: it adds that total training volume \"can be greatly reduced, minimizing the\nincidence of overtraining,\" that the multi-peak design \"facilitates successful participation\nin multiple competitions over the entire season,\" and, as a fifth item, \"a multi-stage annual\nplan creates more favorable conditions for peaking in time for the main competition of the\nseason\"; it keeps the monitoring and diet/restoration points from the first list but omits\nthe psychological benefit.\n\n**Why it works** — Because each training stage runs its own compressed\naccumulation-transmutation-realization cycle and ends in a real competitive test, the athlete\nis brought to a peak (or near-peak) at the end of every stage rather than once per year;\nstacking four to seven such stages across a season is what produces the multi-peak pattern.\nIssurin's stated physiological contrast (Table 1.8) is that the traditional model relies on\nadaptation to concurrent stimuli across a long period, while this model relies on\nsuperimposing sequential residual effects from short, concentrated blocks; the same\nmechanism underlies [[framework--accumulation-transmutation-realization-mesocycles]].\n\n**When to apply** — **Inference:** this is the entry most directly responsive to the strength-and-conditioning\nmiss-log gap (\"peaking phase structure for collegiate football,\" logged 2026-09-03): for a\nfootball program needing camp-readiness, then in-season week-to-week competitive readiness,\nthis framework suggests treating each significant test point (fall camp scrimmage, each\ngame week, or a defined in-season block) as a candidate training-stage boundary rather than\nrelying on a single annual peak, with the caveat below about untested claims. **Eric's\nbaseline** does not currently describe an explicit training-stage or multi-peak model for\nfootball; the closest baseline material is the general macro/meso/microcycle planning\nlanguage and the in-season rule \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" (`method-baseline.md`,\n\"Periodization and programming\" and \"Strength and power,\" citing\n`01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md` pp.17-18, 20-21). This Issurin framework offers a\nmore explicit structural answer to \"how do you actually sequence a season with more than one\npeak\" than anything currently captured in the baseline.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The \"four to seven stages\" and \"about two months\"\nfigures are stated without a derivation the reader can check: Issurin gives no worked\nexample of how stage count or duration is calculated for a specific sport's calendar in\nthis chapter. **Inference:** football's in-season structure (weekly games, not discrete\nmulti-week competition-free blocks) does not map cleanly onto a ~2-month training stage.\nIssurin's model is built from and mainly evidenced by individual/dual sports with discrete\ncompetitions (track and field, canoe-kayak, windsurfing); Chapter 3's microcycle-level\nmaterial (now ingested; see `_book.md`) is a necessary next read before this framework can\nbe translated into a week-by-week in-season football plan. **Caveat:** Issurin's own later\npeer-reviewed review (Issurin, 2016, *Sports Medicine*, \"Benefits and Limitations of Block\nPeriodized Training Approaches to Athletes' Preparation,\" Zotero item `PT9QTQ2I`, full text\ncached in this library at\n`research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a.md`)\nsupports the multi-targeted (A-T-R) block model this entry describes with 28 studies (its\np.11), but that support is a non-systematic, author-curated synthesis, not a pooled\ncomparative estimate (vault brief, Limitations). The team- and combat-sport studies the same\nreview reports without performance or team-practice data (its p.6) tested Verkhoshansky's\nconcentrated-unidirectional model (Table 1, \"based on Verkhoshansky\"), not this book's\ntraining-stage (A-T-R) model. No study in this library tests A-T-R training stages in\nAmerican football. **Inference:**\nBompa & Haff's 5th edition, already in this library, may also bear on the \"does not work\nwell\" claim, but only Bompa Ch1-2 has been ingested so far (`periodization--bompa/_book.md`:\nCh6 Annual Plan and Ch8 Training Cycles are pending), so it cannot ground this contest yet;\ncheck once those chapters are ingested.\n\n**Example** — **Inference:** a five-stage football off-season/camp plan: Stage 1 (general\naccumulation, ~8 weeks, winter conditioning); Stage 2 (strength/power transmutation into\nspring ball, ending in the spring game as a \"competition\"); Stage 3 (summer accumulation);\nStage 4 (fall-camp transmutation, ending in the first scrimmage or game week as\nrealization); Stage 5+ (in-season stages keyed to bye weeks or rivalry-week peaks, pending\nChapter 3's in-season microcycle guidance).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--accumulation-transmutation-realization-mesocycles]],\n[[claim--multi-peak-annual-preparation-elite-track-and-field]] (the empirical motivation for\nneeding a multi-peak model), [[claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]],\n[[principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]].\n\n#####\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: block-periodization--issurin\nchapter: \"1.1.3\"\ntopic: multi-peak competition calendars of elite athletes\ncategory: [periodization, peaking, evidence]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-09-26\n---\n\n# World-Class Track and Field Athletes Compete and Peak Many Times Per Season, Not Once\n\n**What it is** — Table 1.5 (PDF p. 24, printed p. 13, based on Suslov, 2001, with Issurin's\nmodifications) reports three world-class athletes' single-season competition patterns:\n**Marion Jones** (100-200m, long jump; 1998 season) had **10 peaks** (8 in running, 2 in\nlong jump, all at personal-best level) across a **200-day** competitive span, with typical\n19-22 day intervals between peaks. **Sergei Bubka** (pole vault; 1991 season) had **7\npeaks** (all within 3% of his personal best, 595-612 cm) across a **265-day** span,\n23-43 day intervals. **Stefka Kostadinova** (high jump; 1998 season) had **11 peaks** (all\nwithin 3% of her personal best, 200-205 cm), 14-25 day intervals, across winter ~20 days and\nspring/summer 135 days (**Inference:** 155 days combined; the table gives the two figures\nseparately, not a single summed span). Issurin's summary point, stated on the preceding page\n(PDF p. 23, printed p. 12): these athletes' 5-8 month competitive spans \"can not be\nsubdivided into traditional preparatory and competitive periods,\" yet their basic abilities\n(max strength, aerobic regeneration capacity) still had to be maintained across that entire\nspan.\n\n**Why it works: this is observational, not interventional** — These are real competition\nrecords for three specific, historically documented elite athletes (Olympic/World\nchampions), not an experiment; the \"why\" is a report of what elite competition calendars\nactually looked like by 1991/1998, cited to Suslov's (2001) analysis rather than derived\nfrom a controlled study of periodization models.\n\n**When to apply** — **Inference:** as direct evidence for the strength-and-conditioning\nmiss-log's \"peaking phase structure\" gap, this table is the empirical case Issurin uses to argue that a\nsingle annual peak (or even a two-/three-peak model) does not describe how modern elite\nindividual-sport athletes actually compete, which motivates the multi-stage annual model in\n[[framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle]]. Useful for framing the *problem*\n(competition calendars now demand many peaks) to Baylor coaches even before the\n*solution* (Chapter 3's microcycle-level guidance, now ingested; see `_book.md`) is read.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — All three examples are individual, non-contact,\ntechnique/power sports (sprinting/jumping, pole vault, high jump) where \"peak\" can be defined\nby a single measurable performance (time, height, distance) on a given day. **Inference:**\nthis evidence does not directly transfer to football, where \"peak\" is not a single\nmeasurable output and the competitive calendar is fixed by a weekly game schedule rather\nthan a chosen sequence of meets; the multi-peak *problem* generalizes (a football season\nalso demands repeated high-level performance, not one), but the specific 19-43 day\ninter-peak intervals shown here are specific to how often these particular athletes chose to\ncompete, not a transferable spacing rule for football practice or game weeks. Data source is\nsingle (Suslov, 2001, \"with modifications\" by Issurin) with no stated method for how \"peak\"\nwas defined or verified for each performance.\n\n**Example** — **Inference:** when explaining to a Baylor position group why in-season\nprogramming assumes more than one readiness peak per season, this table is a citable,\nreal-world existence proof that elite athletes routinely peak 7-11 times in a season; useful\nfor framing, not for directly copying an inter-peak interval into a football in-season plan.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle]].","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6670dffb-b87e-46a5-a790-895389bd779a","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
