{"parentUuid":"4e0bebf1-48a7-4de2-8e6d-c9ccc06670c0","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac7100d52d01adab3","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffq8dP559YJxmuUwFfWj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SsFF3YYf2kj6rjySbnP5XV","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/; cat framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports.md; printf '\\n####\\n'; ~/.local/bin/uv run --with pymupdf python3 -c \"\nimport pymupdf\nd=pymupdf.open('/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf')\nfor n in [396,397]: print('=====',n); print(d[n].get_text())\n\"","description":"Read endurance entry and PDF 396-397 text"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3464,"cache_read_input_tokens":111116,"output_tokens":954,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":669},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3464},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":954,"cache_read_input_tokens":111116,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3464,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3464,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01SsFF3YYf2kj6rjySbnP5XV":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/; cat framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports.md; printf '\\n####\\n'; ~/.local/bin/uv run --with pymupdf python3 -c \"\nimport pymupdf\nd=pymupdf.open('/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf')\nfor n in [396,397]: print('=====',n); print(d[n].get_text())\n\"","description":"Read endurance entry and PDF 396-397 text"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffq8awUdRuG6CJM1C2mr","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"f82cd58c-78e4-4fdb-8a19-ed8998fb00f2","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:09:19.711Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"f82cd58c-78e4-4fdb-8a19-ed8998fb00f2","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"ce914f6b-f81b-42fd-a40b-4c5550786125","agentId":"ac7100d52d01adab3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SsFF3YYf2kj6rjySbnP5XV","type":"tool_result","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14.2\"\ntopic: endurance annual models\ncategory: [endurance, periodization, annual-plan, concentrated-loading, bi-cyclic, monocyclic]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Annual Models for Endurance Sports: Bi-Cyclic With Two Strength Blocks for Medium Duration, Monocyclic With Two Strength Blocks for Long Duration\n\n**What it is** — Two prescriptive models from 6.14.2 and 6.14.3 (PDF pp. 395-397 / printed pp. 384-386; text and Figs 6.40 and 6.41 checked against the page images; Fig 6.46 on PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389).\n\n**Medium-duration endurance (bi-cyclic, Fig 6.40).**\n- Two mesocycles and two competition stages; in the first \"one need not compete at the fundamental distance,\" but can compete \"at other distances,\" which \"simultaneously serve as control estimates of the athlete's level of aerobic and anaerobic capacity\" (PDF p. 395).\n- \"Explosive strength peaks at the beginning of the second competition stage, after which it decreases, together with the rise in the volume of intense (anaerobic-glycolytic) cyclic work.\" Anaerobic capacity and strength-endurance \"reach their highest levels during the most important competitive stage (10th - 11th months)\" (PDF p. 395).\n- Aerobic loading predominates in the first preparatory stage; \"during the second stage the work is chiefly at the OBLA (onset of blood lactate accumulation) level\"; aerobic volume rises again in the second competition stage \"to promote restoration.\" Speed work (ATP-CP) and speed-endurance (glycolytic) are timed for the LDTE stage, \"i.e. the development of speed precedes that of speed-endurance.\" Overall sequence: \"General endurance > Speed > Speed-endurance\" (PDF p. 395).\n- Strength block contents: \"The first block emphasizes the development of explosive strength and local muscular endurance, while the second emphasizes mainly local muscular endurance\" (PDF p. 396 / printed p. 385).\n- Fig 6.40, read by eye: block 1 over about months 2-4 (taller), block 2 over about months 6-7 (shorter); phases I about months 1-4, II about month 5, III about months 6-8, IV about months 9-11. For a large mesocycle (Fig 6.46, PDF pp. 399-400), aerobic training \"covers the first half of the large mesocycle during which the GPP and concentrated strength loading take place,\" then speed, then specialised endurance, with competitions as a main way to develop it.\n\n**Long-duration endurance (monocyclic, Fig 6.41).**\n- \"Competition in long duration events requires a long preparatory period (up to 6 months) for creating stable adaptation to preserve the special work-capacity for some 4-5 months during the competitive period\" (PDF p. 396). The authors' reason for the long preparation: \"with reference to the predominance of low intensity work (relative to other groups of sports), the optimal date for increasing the current adaptation reserves is deferred, a detail which is taken into account in the model\" (PDF p. 396, checked against the page image).\n- Stable results come \"if the increase in loading during the preparatory period occurs gradually (a mean monthly increase of 7-14%), if there is not a sharp drop in the volume of loading during the competitive period, and if the portion of loading in the zone of anaerobic energy production is not increased (Gilyazova, 1978)\" (PDF p. 396). See [[claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity]].\n- \"Within a monocycle of periodisation, it provides a two-stage organisation of strength and speed training\": \"The first block contains general conditioning strength work, while the second block emphasizes the development of local muscular endurance,\" carrying work capacity \"right up to the important competitions in the 10th and 11th months\" (PDF p. 396).\n- Fig 6.41, read by eye: phase I about months 1-6, phase II about months 6-11; block 1 over about months 2-3, block 2 over about months 6-7. Aerobic capacity peaks around the start of the competition period and then eases, as the text says (\"maximum aerobic productivity at the beginning of the competition period, and some decrease,\" PDF p. 397).\n\n**Why it works** — Both models rest on the LDTE of concentrated strength blocks and on timing speed and speed-endurance after them. The only cited data are Gilyazova (1978) for the monocyclic loading conditions; the figures are schematics (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999).\n\n**When to apply** — Annual planning for middle-distance (medium) or long-distance (long) endurance athletes who use strength blocks. Not a football model; the medium-duration sequence \"General endurance > Speed > Speed-endurance\" is the transferable idea. Eric's baseline: \"A sport cannot maximize all three energy systems at once\" and \"priority has to be picked based on what the competition actually demands\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 115, Explicit; the two phrases are joined by a dash in the baseline). Inference: both models pick one energy-system emphasis per stage in that spirit.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Long-duration sports are given a monocycle, while the 6.13 aims call for \"at least two large mesocycles... even in sports which traditionally... use monocyclic periodisation\" (PDF p. 389); the monocycle here still provides \"a two-stage organisation of strength and speed training\" (PDF p. 396), and [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]] reads the two passages as a terminology tension (number of mesocycles versus number of competition peaks), not opposed positions. The models are for qualified athletes and carry the same Issurin 2016 challenge to concentrated loading as the other 6.14 models ([[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]). Fig 6.46's caption says \"A is the block of concentrated strength loading,\" but the figure prints \"GPP\" under A and \"Strength Load\" under B; which wave is the strength block is unclear.\n\n**Example** — The authors give the schematics only. Inference: an 800 m runner competes at 400 m and 1500 m in the first competition stage as control tests, then runs the main 800 m season in months 10-11 after a smaller second block focused on local muscular endurance.\n\n**Related** — [[claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity]], [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[framework--speed-sport-block-organisation-and-two-glycolytic-peaks]], [[heuristic--use-low-intensity-gpp-as-restoration-in-concentrated-loading-models]], [[principle--local-muscular-endurance-adaptation-is-specific-to-the-trained-muscles]]\n\n####\n===== 396\nSUPERl'RAlNING\nFigure 6.40 A model o f the training for sports which require special endurance (medium duration). Details are discussed in the \naccompanying text. I = the 1st preparation phase. II “ the 1st competition phase. III = the 2nd preparation phase, IV - the 2nd \ncompetition phase. The shaded blocks represent the stages of concentrated special strength loading to promote the subsequent LDTE.\nThe contents of the strength-training blocks are selected by taking into account their conformity to the specific \nwork regime of the sports activity (see 3.2). The first block emphasizes the development of explosive strength \nand local muscular endurance, while the second emphasizes mainly local muscular endurance.\n6.14.3 A Model for Long Duration Endurance Sports\nThis model provides monocyclic pcriodisation with one competitive stage (Fig 6.41). \nPractice shows that \ncompetition in long duration events requires a long preparatory period (up to 6 months) for creating stable \nadaptation to preserve the special work-capacity for some 4-5 months during the competitive period. Therefore, \nin the given case, with reference to the predominance of low intensity work (relative to other groups of sports), \nthe optimal date for increasing the current adaptation reserves is deferred, a detail which is taken into account in \nthe model.\nThe model has yet another distinguishing feature. With monocyclic periodisation, stable sport results are \nachieved in the competitive phase if the increase in loading during the preparatory period occurs gradually (a \nmean monthly increase of 7-14%), if there is not a sharp drop in the volume of loading during the competitive \nperiod, and if the portion of loading in the zone of anaerobic energy production is not increased (Gilyazova,\n1978). It has been established that the prolonged reduction in the volume of loading during the competition \nperiod (with monocyclic periodisation) leads to re-adaptation at a lower level, which is reflected in the drop of \nnon-specific trainability. \nCompetition and special supplementary loading (with limited volumes) cannot \nstimulate further development or even maintain special work-capacity. Thus, it is appropriate to periodically \nincrease the volume of competition and special assistance loading (Baranovsky, 1969; Kryazh, 1969; Muzis, \n1970; Zhikharevich, 1976).\nTherefore, one feature of the given model is that within a monocycle of periodisation, it provides a two-stage \norganisation of strength and speed training. Against a background of the traditional distribution of aerobic, mixed \nand anaerobic (chiefly glycolytic) loading, two blocks of special-strength and speed (chiefly ATP-CP) training \nare included. The first block contains general conditioning strength work, while the second block emphasizes the \ndevelopment of local muscular endurance. \nThis provides for an increase in special work-capacity and its \nstabilisation during the competitive period, right up to the important competitions in the 10th and 11th months.\n385\n\n===== 397\nSUPERTRAINING\nThis model provides for achievement of maximum aerobic productivity at the beginning of the competition \nperiod, and some decrease. There is a gradual rise in special strength-endurance and absolute strength up to the \nstage of important competitions.\nFigure 6.41 A model of the training for long-duration endurance sports. \nI = preparatory phase, II = competitive phase. The shaded \nblocks represent the stages of concentrated special strength loading to promote the subsequent LDTE. (Sifif & Verkhoshansky. 1999).\n6.14.4 A Model for Sports requiring Tricyclic Periodisation\nThe tri-cyclic model is intended primarily for individual and one-on-one sports (Fig 6.42). It comprises three \nmesocycles, with preparatory and competitive micro-stagcs. The first stage is oriented chiefly towards special \nphysical preparation; the second, to perfecting technical mastery, and the third, to readiness for the major \ncompetitions. Depending on the competition calendar and the characteristics of the training objectives in the \nannual cyclc, the length of the third stage can be increased by shortening the duration of the second stage. In \ncertain cases a bi-cyclic model of training can be employed with this group of sports.\nThe model of the athlete's state provides for the achievement of maximum aerobic productivity during the \nsecond stage, \nfollowed by some decrease. Maximum strength rises uniformly, while strength-endurance \naccelerates more during the third stage. Explosive strength reaches a maximum at the beginning of the second and \nat the end of the third stages.\nThe loading includes three strength blocks. The objectives of the first are to develop maximum and explosive \nstrength; the second, to develop strength-endurance; the third, chiefly to increase explosive strength and create \nconditions for exploiting the LDTE of the strength loading during the fundamental competition phase.\nThe first and third blocks of strength work are executed in combination with special work in the mixed \n(aerobic-anaerobic) zone of energy production, while the second is combined with work in the anaerobic (ATP- \nCP and anaerobic-glycolytic) zone. The fundamental volume of speed and technical-tactical preparation is \nexecuted against a background of the LDTE elicited by the blocks of strength work.\nMore Advanced Use of Concentrated Loading\nThe preceding models of bi-cyclic and tri-cyclic pcriodisation involved successive blocks of conccntrated loading \nseparated by several weeks from one another, a scheme which may be modified in certain situations. For \nexample, in speed-strcngth sports, the magnitude of the delayed training effcct (LDTE) may be enhanced further \nif the concentrated loading is followed by specialised work of moderate volume, which includes higher power or \nmore explosive muscle contraction regimes, such as jumps, plyometrics and maximal attempts.\n386","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"e55f4031-9020-44d9-8f30-4ed440b8ab0d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:09:20.444Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14.2\"\ntopic: endurance annual models\ncategory: [endurance, periodization, annual-plan, concentrated-loading, bi-cyclic, monocyclic]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Annual Models for Endurance Sports: Bi-Cyclic With Two Strength Blocks for Medium Duration, Monocyclic With Two Strength Blocks for Long Duration\n\n**What it is** — Two prescriptive models from 6.14.2 and 6.14.3 (PDF pp. 395-397 / printed pp. 384-386; text and Figs 6.40 and 6.41 checked against the page images; Fig 6.46 on PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389).\n\n**Medium-duration endurance (bi-cyclic, Fig 6.40).**\n- Two mesocycles and two competition stages; in the first \"one need not compete at the fundamental distance,\" but can compete \"at other distances,\" which \"simultaneously serve as control estimates of the athlete's level of aerobic and anaerobic capacity\" (PDF p. 395).\n- \"Explosive strength peaks at the beginning of the second competition stage, after which it decreases, together with the rise in the volume of intense (anaerobic-glycolytic) cyclic work.\" Anaerobic capacity and strength-endurance \"reach their highest levels during the most important competitive stage (10th - 11th months)\" (PDF p. 395).\n- Aerobic loading predominates in the first preparatory stage; \"during the second stage the work is chiefly at the OBLA (onset of blood lactate accumulation) level\"; aerobic volume rises again in the second competition stage \"to promote restoration.\" Speed work (ATP-CP) and speed-endurance (glycolytic) are timed for the LDTE stage, \"i.e. the development of speed precedes that of speed-endurance.\" Overall sequence: \"General endurance > Speed > Speed-endurance\" (PDF p. 395).\n- Strength block contents: \"The first block emphasizes the development of explosive strength and local muscular endurance, while the second emphasizes mainly local muscular endurance\" (PDF p. 396 / printed p. 385).\n- Fig 6.40, read by eye: block 1 over about months 2-4 (taller), block 2 over about months 6-7 (shorter); phases I about months 1-4, II about month 5, III about months 6-8, IV about months 9-11. For a large mesocycle (Fig 6.46, PDF pp. 399-400), aerobic training \"covers the first half of the large mesocycle during which the GPP and concentrated strength loading take place,\" then speed, then specialised endurance, with competitions as a main way to develop it.\n\n**Long-duration endurance (monocyclic, Fig 6.41).**\n- \"Competition in long duration events requires a long preparatory period (up to 6 months) for creating stable adaptation to preserve the special work-capacity for some 4-5 months during the competitive period\" (PDF p. 396). The authors' reason for the long preparation: \"with reference to the predominance of low intensity work (relative to other groups of sports), the optimal date for increasing the current adaptation reserves is deferred, a detail which is taken into account in the model\" (PDF p. 396, checked against the page image).\n- Stable results come \"if the increase in loading during the preparatory period occurs gradually (a mean monthly increase of 7-14%), if there is not a sharp drop in the volume of loading during the competitive period, and if the portion of loading in the zone of anaerobic energy production is not increased (Gilyazova, 1978)\" (PDF p. 396). See [[claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity]].\n- \"Within a monocycle of periodisation, it provides a two-stage organisation of strength and speed training\": \"The first block contains general conditioning strength work, while the second block emphasizes the development of local muscular endurance,\" carrying work capacity \"right up to the important competitions in the 10th and 11th months\" (PDF p. 396).\n- Fig 6.41, read by eye: phase I about months 1-6, phase II about months 6-11; block 1 over about months 2-3, block 2 over about months 6-7. Aerobic capacity peaks around the start of the competition period and then eases, as the text says (\"maximum aerobic productivity at the beginning of the competition period, and some decrease,\" PDF p. 397).\n\n**Why it works** — Both models rest on the LDTE of concentrated strength blocks and on timing speed and speed-endurance after them. The only cited data are Gilyazova (1978) for the monocyclic loading conditions; the figures are schematics (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999).\n\n**When to apply** — Annual planning for middle-distance (medium) or long-distance (long) endurance athletes who use strength blocks. Not a football model; the medium-duration sequence \"General endurance > Speed > Speed-endurance\" is the transferable idea. Eric's baseline: \"A sport cannot maximize all three energy systems at once\" and \"priority has to be picked based on what the competition actually demands\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 115, Explicit; the two phrases are joined by a dash in the baseline). Inference: both models pick one energy-system emphasis per stage in that spirit.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Long-duration sports are given a monocycle, while the 6.13 aims call for \"at least two large mesocycles... even in sports which traditionally... use monocyclic periodisation\" (PDF p. 389); the monocycle here still provides \"a two-stage organisation of strength and speed training\" (PDF p. 396), and [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]] reads the two passages as a terminology tension (number of mesocycles versus number of competition peaks), not opposed positions. The models are for qualified athletes and carry the same Issurin 2016 challenge to concentrated loading as the other 6.14 models ([[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]). Fig 6.46's caption says \"A is the block of concentrated strength loading,\" but the figure prints \"GPP\" under A and \"Strength Load\" under B; which wave is the strength block is unclear.\n\n**Example** — The authors give the schematics only. Inference: an 800 m runner competes at 400 m and 1500 m in the first competition stage as control tests, then runs the main 800 m season in months 10-11 after a smaller second block focused on local muscular endurance.\n\n**Related** — [[claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity]], [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[framework--speed-sport-block-organisation-and-two-glycolytic-peaks]], [[heuristic--use-low-intensity-gpp-as-restoration-in-concentrated-loading-models]], [[principle--local-muscular-endurance-adaptation-is-specific-to-the-trained-muscles]]\n\n####\n===== 396\nSUPERl'RAlNING\nFigure 6.40 A model o f the training for sports which require special endurance (medium duration). Details are discussed in the \naccompanying text. I = the 1st preparation phase. II “ the 1st competition phase. III = the 2nd preparation phase, IV - the 2nd \ncompetition phase. The shaded blocks represent the stages of concentrated special strength loading to promote the subsequent LDTE.\nThe contents of the strength-training blocks are selected by taking into account their conformity to the specific \nwork regime of the sports activity (see 3.2). The first block emphasizes the development of explosive strength \nand local muscular endurance, while the second emphasizes mainly local muscular endurance.\n6.14.3 A Model for Long Duration Endurance Sports\nThis model provides monocyclic pcriodisation with one competitive stage (Fig 6.41). \nPractice shows that \ncompetition in long duration events requires a long preparatory period (up to 6 months) for creating stable \nadaptation to preserve the special work-capacity for some 4-5 months during the competitive period. Therefore, \nin the given case, with reference to the predominance of low intensity work (relative to other groups of sports), \nthe optimal date for increasing the current adaptation reserves is deferred, a detail which is taken into account in \nthe model.\nThe model has yet another distinguishing feature. With monocyclic periodisation, stable sport results are \nachieved in the competitive phase if the increase in loading during the preparatory period occurs gradually (a \nmean monthly increase of 7-14%), if there is not a sharp drop in the volume of loading during the competitive \nperiod, and if the portion of loading in the zone of anaerobic energy production is not increased (Gilyazova,\n1978). It has been established that the prolonged reduction in the volume of loading during the competition \nperiod (with monocyclic periodisation) leads to re-adaptation at a lower level, which is reflected in the drop of \nnon-specific trainability. \nCompetition and special supplementary loading (with limited volumes) cannot \nstimulate further development or even maintain special work-capacity. Thus, it is appropriate to periodically \nincrease the volume of competition and special assistance loading (Baranovsky, 1969; Kryazh, 1969; Muzis, \n1970; Zhikharevich, 1976).\nTherefore, one feature of the given model is that within a monocycle of periodisation, it provides a two-stage \norganisation of strength and speed training. Against a background of the traditional distribution of aerobic, mixed \nand anaerobic (chiefly glycolytic) loading, two blocks of special-strength and speed (chiefly ATP-CP) training \nare included. The first block contains general conditioning strength work, while the second block emphasizes the \ndevelopment of local muscular endurance. \nThis provides for an increase in special work-capacity and its \nstabilisation during the competitive period, right up to the important competitions in the 10th and 11th months.\n385\n\n===== 397\nSUPERTRAINING\nThis model provides for achievement of maximum aerobic productivity at the beginning of the competition \nperiod, and some decrease. There is a gradual rise in special strength-endurance and absolute strength up to the \nstage of important competitions.\nFigure 6.41 A model of the training for long-duration endurance sports. \nI = preparatory phase, II = competitive phase. The shaded \nblocks represent the stages of concentrated special strength loading to promote the subsequent LDTE. (Sifif & Verkhoshansky. 1999).\n6.14.4 A Model for Sports requiring Tricyclic Periodisation\nThe tri-cyclic model is intended primarily for individual and one-on-one sports (Fig 6.42). It comprises three \nmesocycles, with preparatory and competitive micro-stagcs. The first stage is oriented chiefly towards special \nphysical preparation; the second, to perfecting technical mastery, and the third, to readiness for the major \ncompetitions. Depending on the competition calendar and the characteristics of the training objectives in the \nannual cyclc, the length of the third stage can be increased by shortening the duration of the second stage. In \ncertain cases a bi-cyclic model of training can be employed with this group of sports.\nThe model of the athlete's state provides for the achievement of maximum aerobic productivity during the \nsecond stage, \nfollowed by some decrease. Maximum strength rises uniformly, while strength-endurance \naccelerates more during the third stage. Explosive strength reaches a maximum at the beginning of the second and \nat the end of the third stages.\nThe loading includes three strength blocks. The objectives of the first are to develop maximum and explosive \nstrength; the second, to develop strength-endurance; the third, chiefly to increase explosive strength and create \nconditions for exploiting the LDTE of the strength loading during the fundamental competition phase.\nThe first and third blocks of strength work are executed in combination with special work in the mixed \n(aerobic-anaerobic) zone of energy production, while the second is combined with work in the anaerobic (ATP- \nCP and anaerobic-glycolytic) zone. The fundamental volume of speed and technical-tactical preparation is \nexecuted against a background of the LDTE elicited by the blocks of strength work.\nMore Advanced Use of Concentrated Loading\nThe preceding models of bi-cyclic and tri-cyclic pcriodisation involved successive blocks of conccntrated loading \nseparated by several weeks from one another, a scheme which may be modified in certain situations. For \nexample, in speed-strcngth sports, the magnitude of the delayed training effcct (LDTE) may be enhanced further \nif the concentrated loading is followed by specialised work of moderate volume, which includes higher power or \nmore explosive muscle contraction regimes, such as jumps, plyometrics and maximal attempts.\n386","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[],"moreFiles":0,"shared":true}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f82cd58c-78e4-4fdb-8a19-ed8998fb00f2","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
