{"parentUuid":"949878c7-081b-4efb-91c3-ad32dab5f00f","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac7100d52d01adab3","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffqAW3cG53KtXrVEi3rq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GdfvCDK8aGEspq2NGziyyj","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/; for f in framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports.md claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation.md claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes.md; do printf '\\n########## %s\\n' $f; sed 1,2d $f | grep -v '^$'; done","description":"Read three Issurin-related FIX entries"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6909,"cache_read_input_tokens":119965,"output_tokens":1782,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1524},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6909},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1782,"cache_read_input_tokens":119965,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6909,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6909,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01GdfvCDK8aGEspq2NGziyyj":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/; for f in framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports.md claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation.md claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes.md; do printf '\\n########## %s\\n' $f; sed 1,2d $f | grep -v '^$'; done","description":"Read three Issurin-related FIX entries"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffqAU7mCuvjYa4J41bAi","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"8ea37069-0aec-4075-a693-4d788d80c59a","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:09:52.615Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"8ea37069-0aec-4075-a693-4d788d80c59a","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"ce914f6b-f81b-42fd-a40b-4c5550786125","agentId":"ac7100d52d01adab3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GdfvCDK8aGEspq2NGziyyj","type":"tool_result","content":"########## framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports.md\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14.4\"\ntopic: tricyclic annual model\ncategory: [periodization, annual-plan, tri-cyclic, combat-sports, concentrated-loading]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n# Tri-Cyclic Model for Individual and One-on-One Sports: Three Mesocycles for SPP, Technical Mastery and Competition Readiness, Each With Its Own Strength Block\n**What it is** — 6.14.4 (PDF p. 397 / printed p. 386, checked against the page image; Fig 6.42 on PDF p. 398 / printed p. 387, checked against the image): \"The tri-cyclic model is intended primarily for individual and one-on-one sports (Fig 6.42). It comprises three mesocycles, with preparatory and competitive micro-stages. The first stage is oriented chiefly towards special physical preparation; the second, to perfecting technical mastery, and the third, to readiness for the major competitions.\" The third stage \"can be increased by shortening the duration of the second stage,\" and \"In certain cases a bi-cyclic model of training can be employed with this group of sports.\"\n- **Athlete-state model:** maximum aerobic productivity in the second stage, then some decrease; \"Maximum strength rises uniformly, while strength-endurance accelerates more during the third stage. Explosive strength reaches a maximum at the beginning of the second and at the end of the third stages.\"\n- **Three strength blocks:** the first \"to develop maximum and explosive strength; the second, to develop strength-endurance; the third, chiefly to increase explosive strength and create conditions for exploiting the LDTE of the strength loading during the fundamental competition phase.\"\n- **Energy-system pairing:** \"The first and third blocks of strength work are executed in combination with special work in the mixed (aerobic-anaerobic) zone of energy production, while the second is combined with work in the anaerobic (ATP-CP and anaerobic-glycolytic) zone. The fundamental volume of speed and technical-tactical preparation is executed against a background of the LDTE elicited by the blocks of strength work.\"\n- **Fig 6.42, read by eye:** phases I about months 1-4, II about months 5-8, III about months 9-11. Block 1 about months 1-3 (tallest), block 2 about months 5-6, block 3 about months 9-10 (shortest). The labels at the right edge of the upper panel read, top to bottom, Explosive strength, Anaerobic capacity, Aerobic capacity, Strength-endurance, with Maximum strength lowest and rising nearly linearly. Shorter mesocycles of this kind \"should not shift achieving the objective of optimal enhancement of the current adaptation reserves (CAR) to the next stage\" (PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389).\n**Why it works** — The authors give the structure, not a mechanism beyond the LDTE of each strength block. No data are shown; the figure is credited to Siff & Verkhoshansky (1999).\n**When to apply** — Individual and combat sports with three main competition periods. The authors list boxing and swimming as sports that traditionally use tri-cyclic forms but may use bi-cyclic ones (PDF p. 387 / printed p. 376). They name team sports and one-on-one events as cases where \"tri-cyclic periodisation may have to be used, as dictated by the competition calendar\" (PDF p. 387). Eric's baseline: \"The head coach's system of play and roster identity (e.g., a \"home run hitters\" lineup vs. a \"steal bases\" lineup) is treated as a direct input into whether a team's programming leans toward maximal strength or toward speed/power emphasis\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 188, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the model's block contents (maximum, endurance, explosive) are the kind of lever that input would set.\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested by Issurin 2016 inside the model's stated domain (the basis for the `contested` rating).** The model is \"intended primarily for individual and one-on-one sports\" and is built on three concentrated strength blocks (PDF p. 397). The library's brief of Issurin 2016 records that concentrated-unidirectional programs in combat and team sports \"improved some fitness measures, but the reviewed studies did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance,\" and that the author judged that evidence \"insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\" (brief, p. 6; [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]). Combat sports and swimming sit inside this model's domain, so the challenge applies to the model itself, not only to a transfer elsewhere; the same challenge to the concentrated blocks is recorded on [[claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving]]. The brief also says the review's study selection is non-systematic and author-curated, so this is Issurin's judgment, not pooled evidence. The model assumes three distinct competition peaks; it is not a template for a weekly-competition season.\n**Example** — The authors give the schematic only. Inference: a wrestler with a winter national championship, a spring continental event and a late-summer world championship runs a maximum-strength block in autumn, a strength-endurance block in late winter with anaerobic work, and a short explosive block before the summer peak.\n**Related** — [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types]], [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]\n\n########## claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation.md\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.3\"\ntopic: bi-cyclic annual organisation\ncategory: [periodization, annual-plan, bi-cyclic, monocyclic, mesocycle]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n# Two Large Mesocycles a Year, the Second Peaking Higher: the Authors Question Monocyclic Periodisation but Keep It for Long-Duration Endurance\n**What it is** — \"Research shows that it is appropriate to use two large mesocycles in the annual cycle to promote supercompensation of the current adaptation reserves (Figure 6.34). In this case, the athlete's state displays two large peaks, with the second one producing the higher special work-capacity (i.e. fitness state). This further corroborates the value of this form of bi-cyclic organisation of annual preparation which was empirically worked out and applied successfully long ago in several sports\" (PDF p. 387 / printed p. 376, checked against the page image). \"Consequently, unquestioning adherence to monocyclic periodisation in a number of sports is not justified, and it is advisable that coaches re-examine this tradition carefully.\" Bi-cyclic periodisation may also suit sports that traditionally use monocyclic forms (skiing, Ogoltsov, 1979; middle-distance running, Turin, 1980; Sirenko, 1980) or tri-cyclic forms (boxing, Nikiforov & Viktorov, 1978; swimming, Vaitsekhovsky, 1981). \"In a number of team sports and one-on-one events, tri-cyclic periodisation may have to be used, as dictated by the competition calendar, whereas in long-duration endurance sports, monocyclic periodisation is usually recommended\" (PDF p. 387).\nThe first primary aim of programming (6.13) repeats it: \"It is appropriate to fit at least two large mesocycles into the annual cycle, even in sports which traditionally have one main competitive period and use monocyclic periodisation\" (PDF p. 389 / printed p. 378, checked against the page image). The practical principles add: \"the optimal period for enhancing the current adaptation reserves is about 20 weeks for the most efficient loading of highly qualified athletes,\" and \"the first mesocycle should always be considered as the foundation for the second\" (PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389, checked against the page image).\nFig 6.34 (PDF p. 387, read from the image, no numeric scale): two shaded strength-loading waves, V1 and V2. The fitness-state curve dips during V1, rises to a peak between the waves, dips again during V2, and then climbs to a level above the first peak at the right edge.\n**A terminology tension inside the book, not two opposed positions.** The book uses \"two large mesocycles\" and \"bi-cyclic\" interchangeably at PDF p. 387 (\"this form of bi-cyclic organisation\"), but keeps them apart at PDF p. 389, which asks for two large mesocycles \"even in sports which traditionally have one main competitive period and use monocyclic periodisation,\" and at PDF p. 396 / printed p. 385, where the monocyclic long-duration model, with \"one competitive stage,\" still provides \"a two-stage organisation of strength and speed training.\" Read with PDF pp. 389 and 396, the passages answer different questions: how many large mesocycles a year, and how many competition peaks. The apparent conflict with \"in long-duration endurance sports, monocyclic periodisation is usually recommended\" (PDF p. 387) comes from the PDF p. 387 wording. See [[framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports]].\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is supercompensation of the \"current adaptation reserves\" and the claim that the second mesocycle builds on the first. They cite no data for the second peak being higher beyond \"research shows\" and long empirical use.\n**When to apply** — Sports with two main competition periods, or one long one where an intermediate peak is useful. Eric's baseline: \"In football the target is a peak every Saturday, with November and the postseason as the priority window\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 185, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)). Inference: if the winter-spring and summer off-season phases are treated as the two large mesocycles, the authors' rule that the second builds on the first and peaks higher fits a fall season with a November priority window; the authors do not discuss football.\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested by Issurin (the basis for the `contested` rating)** on the length and number of training units. The disagreement comes from Issurin's 2008 book; the library's brief of Issurin 2016 does not address how many mesocycles a year should hold. Issurin's training stage \"should be about two months,\" and the number of stages \"usually varies from four to seven\" a season ([[block-periodization--issurin/framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle]], Block Periodization PDF pp. 38-40), against the authors' two large mesocycles of usually 3-5 months (PDF p. 387) and \"about 20 weeks\" to enhance the adaptation reserves (PDF p. 400). Issurin also reports world-class track and field athletes peaking 7 to 11 times in a season ([[block-periodization--issurin/claim--multi-peak-annual-preparation-elite-track-and-field]]). Bompa's cycle types as taught by Evans reserve the bicycle for \"ADVANCED OR ELITE ATHLETES\" and the monocycle for novice and junior athletes ([[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types]]); the authors' models are likewise for qualified athletes. Long-duration endurance sports stay monocyclic in the authors' own scheme.\n**Example** — The authors' illustration is Fig 6.34 and the bi-cyclic models of 6.14.1 and 6.14.2 ([[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]). Inference: a middle-distance runner with an indoor season in winter and the main outdoor championship in late summer runs two large mesocycles of about four to five months each, the first ending at the indoor meets.\n**Related** — [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound]], [[heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates]], [[framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports]], [[framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle]], [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--multi-peak-annual-preparation-elite-track-and-field]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types]]\n\n########## claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes.md\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.2\"\ntopic: complex versus unidirectional training\ncategory: [complex-training, concurrent-training, unidirectional-loading, advanced, programming]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n# Complex (Concurrent) Training Suits Beginners and Average Athletes; for Highly Qualified Athletes the Authors Favour Single-Emphasis Workouts, Microcycles and Stages\n**What it is** — The authors define the two ways of organising training: \"Complex loading involves prescription of multi-faceted training regimes to achieve several different fitness objectives over the same period, whereas the concentrated loading method concentrates for a given period on producing a single major specific fitness quality via the use of a unidirectional regime of training\" (PDF p. 379 / printed p. 368). \"For instance, a complex workout might comprise resistance training, plyometrics and sprinting; a complex microcycle (typically a week) might employ those same training means on different days or during different sessions on the same day.\" Using complex means requires knowing how the means interact through \"the acute and delayed after-effects of each\" (PDF p. 379).\nTheir case against complex training for top athletes (PDF pp. 380-381 / printed pp. 369-370, all pages checked against the images):\n- **The supporting research is old and used weak athletes.** The early studies (Krestovnikov, 1951; Letunov et al., 1954; Zimkin, 1956; Korobkov et al, 1960) found complex loading gave balanced fitness and better strength, speed and endurance than unidirectional work. Separately, \"Parallel loading of different emphasis was shown to simultaneously improve different physiological functions in the necessary balance for various sports (Matveyev, 1970).\" The authors call these concepts \"indisputable in principle,\" but \"the research supporting these ideas was done many years ago and utilised athletes of low qualification... under modern conditions this loading would be applicable only to beginners or athletes of average qualification\" (PDF p. 380).\n- **Verkhoshansky's verdict.** \"According to Verkhoshansky (1977), there generally appears to be little advantage for high-level athletes to utilise the complex system of training, although one should not dismiss their possible value at different stages of training with certain individuals in specific cases\" (PDF p. 380).\n- **Three deficiencies for highly qualified athletes.** (1) Their very high special preparedness needs \"strong and relatively prolonged training influences of appropriate emphasis. Complex training does not achieve this.\" (2) \"Complex training, with its multifaceted influence on the body, cannot create the conditions necessary for producing highly specific physical preparedness.\" (3) \"Extensive complex loading to simultaneously perfect sport technique and special physical preparedness inevitably leads to general fatigue and deterioration of this control\" (PDF p. 380). These deficiencies \"are not as noticeable in athletes of average qualification.\"\n- **Evidence for single-emphasis units.** Single-emphasis workouts make adaptation \"more marked\" (Handelsman & Stetsenko, 1980); sessions devoted solely to technique are \"very effective in improving technical skill\" (Struchkov, 1980; Mironenko, 1981; Petrovsky, 1977); single-ability sessions \"exert a more profound effect on special preparedness\" than multi-objective sessions (five studies cited); single-emphasis microcycles \"powerfully stimulate the trainability of swimmers (Kudelin, 1980)\" and strength-emphasis microcycles \"significantly raise the special physical preparedness of track-and-field jumpers (Mironenko, 1981)\" (PDF p. 381).\n- **The resolution: complexity over time.** \"*The conjugate sequence system does not reject the complex (concurrent) method as the most general principle of training, but only extends it so that it meets the requirements of conditioning highly qualified athletes.* Complexity should be understood in terms of its evolution over time, not its contents and structure at a given instant\" (italics in the original; PDF p. 384 / printed p. 373). Complex preparation \"is limited as a general principle for training all athletes at all stages of development.\"\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are the three deficiencies above: too weak a stimulus in any one direction, too little specificity, and fatigue that degrades technical control. Their stated mechanism for the alternative is \"the summation of positive, accumulated after-effects of preceding loading of different primary emphasis\" (PDF p. 384). No effect sizes are given for any cited study.\n**When to apply** — Deciding whether a well-trained athlete's week should mix every quality in each session or give whole sessions and whole weeks to one emphasis. Eric's baseline: \"Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming: incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 182, Inferred (transcript + slides)). Inference: the baseline and the authors agree that not everything is trained hard at once; the baseline still trains other qualities \"at a retention dose,\" closer to Issurin's multi-targeted blocks than to a single emphasis.\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested by Issurin on the remedy and on the beginners limit, not on the critique (the basis for the `contested` rating).** Issurin agrees that traditional complex training falls short for elite athletes: the library's brief of Issurin 2016 says the review \"repeats the book's critique of traditional planning,\" where \"the book\" is Issurin's own Block Periodization ([[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]], pp. 6-7, 11). Inference: traditional planning there means developing many abilities at once, the same complex organisation this entry's authors criticise. He contests the remedy: his multi-targeted blocks train a minimal number of targets, not one ([[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]]). He also contests who the concentrated approach is for: the brief records \"no evidence that amateur athletes have any serious restrictions\" with highly concentrated block workloads (brief, p. 11), against the book's view that complex loading is \"applicable only to beginners or athletes of average qualification\" (PDF p. 380). The brief reports 28 studies supporting the multi-targeted model, but they are Issurin's own non-systematic, author-curated selection, with varied designs, populations, outcomes and comparators (brief, p. 11), not a pooled estimate. Inside the book there is a caveat a few pages later: a swimming week built from single-emphasis workouts of different kinds still \"typifies the complex (multi-faceted) form of training organisation\" and \"fails to produce a definite increase in speed, aerobic or anaerobic productivity\" (PDF pp. 387-388 / printed pp. 376-377; see [[case-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength]]). So single-emphasis workouts alone are not enough; the authors want the emphasis held across a microcycle or stage. Unidirectional loading \"is effective only if it forms part of a complex of several means of a single primary emphasis involving a variety of methods\" (PDF p. 381). The argument is for highly qualified athletes; for beginners the authors keep complex training.\n**Example** — The authors' own example of a complex unit: a workout of \"resistance training, plyometrics and sprinting,\" or a week using those means on different days (PDF p. 379). Their single-emphasis examples are the swimmer and jumper microcycles of Kudelin (1980) and Mironenko (1981) (PDF p. 381).\n**Related** — [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]], [[claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving]], [[case-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength]], [[claim--strength-and-speed-training-response-shifts-with-training-age]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"62c5e619-13e4-408a-97a7-5c938a728466","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:09:52.898Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"########## framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports.md\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.14.4\"\ntopic: tricyclic annual model\ncategory: [periodization, annual-plan, tri-cyclic, combat-sports, concentrated-loading]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n# Tri-Cyclic Model for Individual and One-on-One Sports: Three Mesocycles for SPP, Technical Mastery and Competition Readiness, Each With Its Own Strength Block\n**What it is** — 6.14.4 (PDF p. 397 / printed p. 386, checked against the page image; Fig 6.42 on PDF p. 398 / printed p. 387, checked against the image): \"The tri-cyclic model is intended primarily for individual and one-on-one sports (Fig 6.42). It comprises three mesocycles, with preparatory and competitive micro-stages. The first stage is oriented chiefly towards special physical preparation; the second, to perfecting technical mastery, and the third, to readiness for the major competitions.\" The third stage \"can be increased by shortening the duration of the second stage,\" and \"In certain cases a bi-cyclic model of training can be employed with this group of sports.\"\n- **Athlete-state model:** maximum aerobic productivity in the second stage, then some decrease; \"Maximum strength rises uniformly, while strength-endurance accelerates more during the third stage. Explosive strength reaches a maximum at the beginning of the second and at the end of the third stages.\"\n- **Three strength blocks:** the first \"to develop maximum and explosive strength; the second, to develop strength-endurance; the third, chiefly to increase explosive strength and create conditions for exploiting the LDTE of the strength loading during the fundamental competition phase.\"\n- **Energy-system pairing:** \"The first and third blocks of strength work are executed in combination with special work in the mixed (aerobic-anaerobic) zone of energy production, while the second is combined with work in the anaerobic (ATP-CP and anaerobic-glycolytic) zone. The fundamental volume of speed and technical-tactical preparation is executed against a background of the LDTE elicited by the blocks of strength work.\"\n- **Fig 6.42, read by eye:** phases I about months 1-4, II about months 5-8, III about months 9-11. Block 1 about months 1-3 (tallest), block 2 about months 5-6, block 3 about months 9-10 (shortest). The labels at the right edge of the upper panel read, top to bottom, Explosive strength, Anaerobic capacity, Aerobic capacity, Strength-endurance, with Maximum strength lowest and rising nearly linearly. Shorter mesocycles of this kind \"should not shift achieving the objective of optimal enhancement of the current adaptation reserves (CAR) to the next stage\" (PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389).\n**Why it works** — The authors give the structure, not a mechanism beyond the LDTE of each strength block. No data are shown; the figure is credited to Siff & Verkhoshansky (1999).\n**When to apply** — Individual and combat sports with three main competition periods. The authors list boxing and swimming as sports that traditionally use tri-cyclic forms but may use bi-cyclic ones (PDF p. 387 / printed p. 376). They name team sports and one-on-one events as cases where \"tri-cyclic periodisation may have to be used, as dictated by the competition calendar\" (PDF p. 387). Eric's baseline: \"The head coach's system of play and roster identity (e.g., a \"home run hitters\" lineup vs. a \"steal bases\" lineup) is treated as a direct input into whether a team's programming leans toward maximal strength or toward speed/power emphasis\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 188, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the model's block contents (maximum, endurance, explosive) are the kind of lever that input would set.\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested by Issurin 2016 inside the model's stated domain (the basis for the `contested` rating).** The model is \"intended primarily for individual and one-on-one sports\" and is built on three concentrated strength blocks (PDF p. 397). The library's brief of Issurin 2016 records that concentrated-unidirectional programs in combat and team sports \"improved some fitness measures, but the reviewed studies did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance,\" and that the author judged that evidence \"insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\" (brief, p. 6; [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]). Combat sports and swimming sit inside this model's domain, so the challenge applies to the model itself, not only to a transfer elsewhere; the same challenge to the concentrated blocks is recorded on [[claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving]]. The brief also says the review's study selection is non-systematic and author-curated, so this is Issurin's judgment, not pooled evidence. The model assumes three distinct competition peaks; it is not a template for a weekly-competition season.\n**Example** — The authors give the schematic only. Inference: a wrestler with a winter national championship, a spring continental event and a late-summer world championship runs a maximum-strength block in autumn, a strength-endurance block in late winter with anaerobic work, and a short explosive block before the summer peak.\n**Related** — [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types]], [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]\n\n########## claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation.md\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.3\"\ntopic: bi-cyclic annual organisation\ncategory: [periodization, annual-plan, bi-cyclic, monocyclic, mesocycle]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n# Two Large Mesocycles a Year, the Second Peaking Higher: the Authors Question Monocyclic Periodisation but Keep It for Long-Duration Endurance\n**What it is** — \"Research shows that it is appropriate to use two large mesocycles in the annual cycle to promote supercompensation of the current adaptation reserves (Figure 6.34). In this case, the athlete's state displays two large peaks, with the second one producing the higher special work-capacity (i.e. fitness state). This further corroborates the value of this form of bi-cyclic organisation of annual preparation which was empirically worked out and applied successfully long ago in several sports\" (PDF p. 387 / printed p. 376, checked against the page image). \"Consequently, unquestioning adherence to monocyclic periodisation in a number of sports is not justified, and it is advisable that coaches re-examine this tradition carefully.\" Bi-cyclic periodisation may also suit sports that traditionally use monocyclic forms (skiing, Ogoltsov, 1979; middle-distance running, Turin, 1980; Sirenko, 1980) or tri-cyclic forms (boxing, Nikiforov & Viktorov, 1978; swimming, Vaitsekhovsky, 1981). \"In a number of team sports and one-on-one events, tri-cyclic periodisation may have to be used, as dictated by the competition calendar, whereas in long-duration endurance sports, monocyclic periodisation is usually recommended\" (PDF p. 387).\nThe first primary aim of programming (6.13) repeats it: \"It is appropriate to fit at least two large mesocycles into the annual cycle, even in sports which traditionally have one main competitive period and use monocyclic periodisation\" (PDF p. 389 / printed p. 378, checked against the page image). The practical principles add: \"the optimal period for enhancing the current adaptation reserves is about 20 weeks for the most efficient loading of highly qualified athletes,\" and \"the first mesocycle should always be considered as the foundation for the second\" (PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389, checked against the page image).\nFig 6.34 (PDF p. 387, read from the image, no numeric scale): two shaded strength-loading waves, V1 and V2. The fitness-state curve dips during V1, rises to a peak between the waves, dips again during V2, and then climbs to a level above the first peak at the right edge.\n**A terminology tension inside the book, not two opposed positions.** The book uses \"two large mesocycles\" and \"bi-cyclic\" interchangeably at PDF p. 387 (\"this form of bi-cyclic organisation\"), but keeps them apart at PDF p. 389, which asks for two large mesocycles \"even in sports which traditionally have one main competitive period and use monocyclic periodisation,\" and at PDF p. 396 / printed p. 385, where the monocyclic long-duration model, with \"one competitive stage,\" still provides \"a two-stage organisation of strength and speed training.\" Read with PDF pp. 389 and 396, the passages answer different questions: how many large mesocycles a year, and how many competition peaks. The apparent conflict with \"in long-duration endurance sports, monocyclic periodisation is usually recommended\" (PDF p. 387) comes from the PDF p. 387 wording. See [[framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports]].\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is supercompensation of the \"current adaptation reserves\" and the claim that the second mesocycle builds on the first. They cite no data for the second peak being higher beyond \"research shows\" and long empirical use.\n**When to apply** — Sports with two main competition periods, or one long one where an intermediate peak is useful. Eric's baseline: \"In football the target is a peak every Saturday, with November and the postseason as the priority window\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 185, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)). Inference: if the winter-spring and summer off-season phases are treated as the two large mesocycles, the authors' rule that the second builds on the first and peaks higher fits a fall season with a November priority window; the authors do not discuss football.\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested by Issurin (the basis for the `contested` rating)** on the length and number of training units. The disagreement comes from Issurin's 2008 book; the library's brief of Issurin 2016 does not address how many mesocycles a year should hold. Issurin's training stage \"should be about two months,\" and the number of stages \"usually varies from four to seven\" a season ([[block-periodization--issurin/framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle]], Block Periodization PDF pp. 38-40), against the authors' two large mesocycles of usually 3-5 months (PDF p. 387) and \"about 20 weeks\" to enhance the adaptation reserves (PDF p. 400). Issurin also reports world-class track and field athletes peaking 7 to 11 times in a season ([[block-periodization--issurin/claim--multi-peak-annual-preparation-elite-track-and-field]]). Bompa's cycle types as taught by Evans reserve the bicycle for \"ADVANCED OR ELITE ATHLETES\" and the monocycle for novice and junior athletes ([[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types]]); the authors' models are likewise for qualified athletes. Long-duration endurance sports stay monocyclic in the authors' own scheme.\n**Example** — The authors' illustration is Fig 6.34 and the bi-cyclic models of 6.14.1 and 6.14.2 ([[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]). Inference: a middle-distance runner with an indoor season in winter and the main outdoor championship in late summer runs two large mesocycles of about four to five months each, the first ending at the indoor meets.\n**Related** — [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound]], [[heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates]], [[framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports]], [[framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle]], [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--multi-peak-annual-preparation-elite-track-and-field]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types]]\n\n########## claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes.md\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.12.2\"\ntopic: complex versus unidirectional training\ncategory: [complex-training, concurrent-training, unidirectional-loading, advanced, programming]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n# Complex (Concurrent) Training Suits Beginners and Average Athletes; for Highly Qualified Athletes the Authors Favour Single-Emphasis Workouts, Microcycles and Stages\n**What it is** — The authors define the two ways of organising training: \"Complex loading involves prescription of multi-faceted training regimes to achieve several different fitness objectives over the same period, whereas the concentrated loading method concentrates for a given period on producing a single major specific fitness quality via the use of a unidirectional regime of training\" (PDF p. 379 / printed p. 368). \"For instance, a complex workout might comprise resistance training, plyometrics and sprinting; a complex microcycle (typically a week) might employ those same training means on different days or during different sessions on the same day.\" Using complex means requires knowing how the means interact through \"the acute and delayed after-effects of each\" (PDF p. 379).\nTheir case against complex training for top athletes (PDF pp. 380-381 / printed pp. 369-370, all pages checked against the images):\n- **The supporting research is old and used weak athletes.** The early studies (Krestovnikov, 1951; Letunov et al., 1954; Zimkin, 1956; Korobkov et al, 1960) found complex loading gave balanced fitness and better strength, speed and endurance than unidirectional work. Separately, \"Parallel loading of different emphasis was shown to simultaneously improve different physiological functions in the necessary balance for various sports (Matveyev, 1970).\" The authors call these concepts \"indisputable in principle,\" but \"the research supporting these ideas was done many years ago and utilised athletes of low qualification... under modern conditions this loading would be applicable only to beginners or athletes of average qualification\" (PDF p. 380).\n- **Verkhoshansky's verdict.** \"According to Verkhoshansky (1977), there generally appears to be little advantage for high-level athletes to utilise the complex system of training, although one should not dismiss their possible value at different stages of training with certain individuals in specific cases\" (PDF p. 380).\n- **Three deficiencies for highly qualified athletes.** (1) Their very high special preparedness needs \"strong and relatively prolonged training influences of appropriate emphasis. Complex training does not achieve this.\" (2) \"Complex training, with its multifaceted influence on the body, cannot create the conditions necessary for producing highly specific physical preparedness.\" (3) \"Extensive complex loading to simultaneously perfect sport technique and special physical preparedness inevitably leads to general fatigue and deterioration of this control\" (PDF p. 380). These deficiencies \"are not as noticeable in athletes of average qualification.\"\n- **Evidence for single-emphasis units.** Single-emphasis workouts make adaptation \"more marked\" (Handelsman & Stetsenko, 1980); sessions devoted solely to technique are \"very effective in improving technical skill\" (Struchkov, 1980; Mironenko, 1981; Petrovsky, 1977); single-ability sessions \"exert a more profound effect on special preparedness\" than multi-objective sessions (five studies cited); single-emphasis microcycles \"powerfully stimulate the trainability of swimmers (Kudelin, 1980)\" and strength-emphasis microcycles \"significantly raise the special physical preparedness of track-and-field jumpers (Mironenko, 1981)\" (PDF p. 381).\n- **The resolution: complexity over time.** \"*The conjugate sequence system does not reject the complex (concurrent) method as the most general principle of training, but only extends it so that it meets the requirements of conditioning highly qualified athletes.* Complexity should be understood in terms of its evolution over time, not its contents and structure at a given instant\" (italics in the original; PDF p. 384 / printed p. 373). Complex preparation \"is limited as a general principle for training all athletes at all stages of development.\"\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are the three deficiencies above: too weak a stimulus in any one direction, too little specificity, and fatigue that degrades technical control. Their stated mechanism for the alternative is \"the summation of positive, accumulated after-effects of preceding loading of different primary emphasis\" (PDF p. 384). No effect sizes are given for any cited study.\n**When to apply** — Deciding whether a well-trained athlete's week should mix every quality in each session or give whole sessions and whole weeks to one emphasis. Eric's baseline: \"Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming: incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 182, Inferred (transcript + slides)). Inference: the baseline and the authors agree that not everything is trained hard at once; the baseline still trains other qualities \"at a retention dose,\" closer to Issurin's multi-targeted blocks than to a single emphasis.\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested by Issurin on the remedy and on the beginners limit, not on the critique (the basis for the `contested` rating).** Issurin agrees that traditional complex training falls short for elite athletes: the library's brief of Issurin 2016 says the review \"repeats the book's critique of traditional planning,\" where \"the book\" is Issurin's own Block Periodization ([[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]], pp. 6-7, 11). Inference: traditional planning there means developing many abilities at once, the same complex organisation this entry's authors criticise. He contests the remedy: his multi-targeted blocks train a minimal number of targets, not one ([[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]]). He also contests who the concentrated approach is for: the brief records \"no evidence that amateur athletes have any serious restrictions\" with highly concentrated block workloads (brief, p. 11), against the book's view that complex loading is \"applicable only to beginners or athletes of average qualification\" (PDF p. 380). The brief reports 28 studies supporting the multi-targeted model, but they are Issurin's own non-systematic, author-curated selection, with varied designs, populations, outcomes and comparators (brief, p. 11), not a pooled estimate. Inside the book there is a caveat a few pages later: a swimming week built from single-emphasis workouts of different kinds still \"typifies the complex (multi-faceted) form of training organisation\" and \"fails to produce a definite increase in speed, aerobic or anaerobic productivity\" (PDF pp. 387-388 / printed pp. 376-377; see [[case-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength]]). So single-emphasis workouts alone are not enough; the authors want the emphasis held across a microcycle or stage. Unidirectional loading \"is effective only if it forms part of a complex of several means of a single primary emphasis involving a variety of methods\" (PDF p. 381). The argument is for highly qualified athletes; for beginners the authors keep complex training.\n**Example** — The authors' own example of a complex unit: a workout of \"resistance training, plyometrics and sprinting,\" or a week using those means on different days (PDF p. 379). Their single-emphasis examples are the swimmer and jumper microcycles of Kudelin (1980) and Mironenko (1981) (PDF p. 381).\n**Related** — [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]], [[claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving]], [[case-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength]], [[claim--strength-and-speed-training-response-shifts-with-training-age]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8ea37069-0aec-4075-a693-4d788d80c59a","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
