{"parentUuid":"39561779-6009-4de1-b230-fbad5d65a339","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac7100d52d01adab3","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffqD6z7fMFPwwDgQYT1o","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01B35avDc5cgB5qwjbXfFsFB","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/; sed -n 14,30p block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads.md; sed -n 55,62p block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads.md; sed -n 15,24p elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types.md","description":"Read context of Issurin and Evans quotes"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2329,"cache_read_input_tokens":136084,"output_tokens":232,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2329},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":232,"cache_read_input_tokens":136084,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2329,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2329,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01B35avDc5cgB5qwjbXfFsFB":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/; sed -n 14,30p block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads.md; sed -n 55,62p block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads.md; sed -n 15,24p elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types.md","description":"Read context of Issurin and Evans quotes"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffqD56kAaRyCZWAjpcM9","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"94e0e716-9fac-4358-960d-1f028531a5eb","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:10:13.990Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"94e0e716-9fac-4358-960d-1f028531a5eb","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"ce914f6b-f81b-42fd-a40b-4c5550786125","agentId":"ac7100d52d01adab3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01B35avDc5cgB5qwjbXfFsFB","type":"tool_result","content":"# Concentration of Training Workloads Is the Cornerstone Principle of Block Periodization\n\n**What it is** — Issurin states the Block Periodization Concept (BPC) rests on one\nfoundational principle, concentration of training workloads, from which two others follow:\n(1) a minimal number of targeted abilities per block, because highly concentrated loads\n\"only provide sufficient training stimuli to improve the targeted ability,\" and (2)\nconsecutive (not simultaneous) development of abilities, because \"the number of decisive\nabilities exceeds the number of abilities that can be developed simultaneously\" (PDF p. 37,\nprinted p. 26, Figure 1.7). The rationale is laid out as a table of contradictions in the\ntraditional, simultaneous-development model for high-level athletes (Table 1.4, PDF p. 20,\nprinted p. 9): energy supply gets spread across too many targets so the main one does not\nget the attention it needs, differing physiological-system restoration times leave athletes\nunder-recovered for all targets at once, workloads interact negatively (energy deficits,\ntechnical complexity, neuromuscular fatigue), mental concentration cannot be split across\nmany targets, and the training stimulus needed for a high-level athlete's progress cannot be\nreached when it is divided among many abilities. **Locator note:** Table 1.4's\nContradictions column literally reads \"There is sufficient energy supply for concurrent\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Issurin's own text (PDF p. 44, printed p. 33) states the\ntraditional, complex (simultaneous multi-ability) model \"has visible benefits for the\npreparation of low- and mid-level athletes\": it is \"more diversified, attractive and\nlively,\" and medium-level concentration is \"sufficient stimulation\" at that level: the\nconcentration principle is explicitly scoped to high-performance athletes, not a universal\nprogramming rule. **Contested (Issurin 2008 vs Issurin 2016):** this book limits the\nconcentration principle to high-level athletes, saying medium-level concentration \"still\n\n**What it is** — Evans's slides (U3 L4 p.25-30, repeated identically in U3 L5 p.3-8), cited\nthroughout to \"Bompa, 2009,\" present three annual-plan structures. Monocycle: \"Only ONE\ncompetition phase and one major peak,\" recommended \"FOR NOVICE AND JUNIOR ATHLETES. LONG\nPREPARATORY PHASE TO ENSURE DEVELOPMENT OF THE TECHNICAL/TACTICAL/PHYSICAL DOMAINS,\" with a\nstated week distribution per 52-week cycle: Preparatory >= 32 weeks, Competitive 10-15 weeks,\nTransition 5 weeks. Bicycle: \"TWO competition phases and two major peaks,\" for \"ADVANCED OR\nELITE ATHLETES WHO QUALIFY FOR NATIONAL CHAMPIONSHIPS. TRY TO KEEP THE PREPARATORY PHASES AS\nLONG AS POSSIBLE TO ENSURE DEVELOPMENT,\" distribution per 26-week cycle: Preparatory 13 weeks,\nCompetitive 5-10 weeks, Transition 3 weeks. Tricycle: \"THREE competition phases and three","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"93a81a35-af6d-428f-bb8b-0d7bf4fa9366","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:10:14.186Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# Concentration of Training Workloads Is the Cornerstone Principle of Block Periodization\n\n**What it is** — Issurin states the Block Periodization Concept (BPC) rests on one\nfoundational principle, concentration of training workloads, from which two others follow:\n(1) a minimal number of targeted abilities per block, because highly concentrated loads\n\"only provide sufficient training stimuli to improve the targeted ability,\" and (2)\nconsecutive (not simultaneous) development of abilities, because \"the number of decisive\nabilities exceeds the number of abilities that can be developed simultaneously\" (PDF p. 37,\nprinted p. 26, Figure 1.7). The rationale is laid out as a table of contradictions in the\ntraditional, simultaneous-development model for high-level athletes (Table 1.4, PDF p. 20,\nprinted p. 9): energy supply gets spread across too many targets so the main one does not\nget the attention it needs, differing physiological-system restoration times leave athletes\nunder-recovered for all targets at once, workloads interact negatively (energy deficits,\ntechnical complexity, neuromuscular fatigue), mental concentration cannot be split across\nmany targets, and the training stimulus needed for a high-level athlete's progress cannot be\nreached when it is divided among many abilities. **Locator note:** Table 1.4's\nContradictions column literally reads \"There is sufficient energy supply for concurrent\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Issurin's own text (PDF p. 44, printed p. 33) states the\ntraditional, complex (simultaneous multi-ability) model \"has visible benefits for the\npreparation of low- and mid-level athletes\": it is \"more diversified, attractive and\nlively,\" and medium-level concentration is \"sufficient stimulation\" at that level: the\nconcentration principle is explicitly scoped to high-performance athletes, not a universal\nprogramming rule. **Contested (Issurin 2008 vs Issurin 2016):** this book limits the\nconcentration principle to high-level athletes, saying medium-level concentration \"still\n\n**What it is** — Evans's slides (U3 L4 p.25-30, repeated identically in U3 L5 p.3-8), cited\nthroughout to \"Bompa, 2009,\" present three annual-plan structures. Monocycle: \"Only ONE\ncompetition phase and one major peak,\" recommended \"FOR NOVICE AND JUNIOR ATHLETES. LONG\nPREPARATORY PHASE TO ENSURE DEVELOPMENT OF THE TECHNICAL/TACTICAL/PHYSICAL DOMAINS,\" with a\nstated week distribution per 52-week cycle: Preparatory >= 32 weeks, Competitive 10-15 weeks,\nTransition 5 weeks. Bicycle: \"TWO competition phases and two major peaks,\" for \"ADVANCED OR\nELITE ATHLETES WHO QUALIFY FOR NATIONAL CHAMPIONSHIPS. TRY TO KEEP THE PREPARATORY PHASES AS\nLONG AS POSSIBLE TO ENSURE DEVELOPMENT,\" distribution per 26-week cycle: Preparatory 13 weeks,\nCompetitive 5-10 weeks, Transition 3 weeks. Tricycle: \"THREE competition phases and three","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"94e0e716-9fac-4358-960d-1f028531a5eb","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
