{"parentUuid":"29628f84-1df1-46dc-a4a1-cc1cbcf6c492","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac7100d52d01adab3","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffqDLH4PNNk1PWSP9mxa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1366,"cache_read_input_tokens":138413,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1366,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":3582,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffqDJX9mVoQfEggJWWNd","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"5f620ce6-5c51-4f16-a24d-95a3ea61af56","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:10:19.443Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"5f620ce6-5c51-4f16-a24d-95a3ea61af56","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac7100d52d01adab3","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffqDLH4PNNk1PWSP9mxa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DiQ459Szi9tcMCabdJGJun","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/; for f in framework--speed-sport-block-organisation-and-two-glycolytic-peaks.md case-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength.md; do printf '\\n########## %s\\n' $f; sed 1,12d $f | grep -v '^$'; grep confidence $f; done","description":"Read speed-sport and microcycle entries"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1366,"cache_read_input_tokens":138413,"output_tokens":717,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":477},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1366},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":717,"cache_read_input_tokens":138413,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1366,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1366,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01DiQ459Szi9tcMCabdJGJun":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/; for f in framework--speed-sport-block-organisation-and-two-glycolytic-peaks.md case-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength.md; do printf '\\n########## %s\\n' $f; sed 1,12d $f | grep -v '^$'; grep confidence $f; done","description":"Read speed-sport and microcycle entries"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffqDJX9mVoQfEggJWWNd","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"925f5f0c-6b18-43d9-9142-3247d2bcb906","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:10:20.923Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"925f5f0c-6b18-43d9-9142-3247d2bcb906","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"ce914f6b-f81b-42fd-a40b-4c5550786125","agentId":"ac7100d52d01adab3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DiQ459Szi9tcMCabdJGJun","type":"tool_result","content":"########## framework--speed-sport-block-organisation-and-two-glycolytic-peaks.md\n# Speed Sports: No Speed Work During the Strength Block, Speed Starts as the Delayed Effect Rises, and Glycolytic Loading Has Two Peaks\n**What it is** — For \"speed sports with a cyclical movement structure\" (Fig 6.32b caption, PDF p. 383 / printed p. 372) the authors separate strength and speed more sharply than in speed-strength sports (PDF p. 384 / printed p. 373; both pages checked against the images):\n1. **Speed out of the block.** \"Speed work is excluded totally from the concentrated strength loading stage. However, it is possible during this stage to perfect the athlete's motor skill, which directly determines high speed of movement.\" That skill is intermuscular coordination with \"no tension in the muscle groups which do not directly take part in executing the motor tasks,\" and \"This type of work is quite compatible with the athlete's decreased functional state, provided it is executed in the optimal range of intensity of effort and frequency of movement.\"\n2. **Speed starts with the LDTE.** \"Speed work begins only at the beginning of the LDTE of strength loading (when the 'State' curve rises above the time axis), together with the necessary gradual rise in the training intensity.\"\n3. **Two anaerobic-glycolytic peaks.** High competition speed \"is determined largely by specific speed-endurance.\" The glycolytic loading \"exhibits two peaks\": the first \"is timed to end near the conclusion of the stage of concentrated strength loading. Specialised strength means are employed and the objective is to develop special strength-endurance which is primarily of a local nature\"; the second \"follows the period during which volume of speed work reaches a peak. Distance methods (in cyclic sports) or specialised means and methods (in one-on-one events and team sports) are employed... this type of work should be done in segments of small volume and combined with speed work.\"\n4. **Protect explosive strength.** \"Along with the rise in strength-endurance, explosive strength tends to decrease when the second peak of the anaerobic-glycolytic loading occurs. Therefore, in those sports where explosive strength is of great importance, it is necessary to include a sufficiently intensive, small volume of maintenance loading.\"\nFig 6.32(b), read from the image (PDF p. 383, no numeric scale; read at 400 and 600 dpi): during the shaded strength block, two curves fall below the time axis and climb back above it only after the block ends (they end at Vmax, highest at the right, and at STATE). Two curves rise during the block: one rises steadily and keeps rising (ending at S-E), and one rises to a peak in the latter part of the block (about two thirds to three quarters of the way through), falls after it, then rises to a second hump before easing at the right (ending at An-Gly). That shape matches the text's two glycolytic peaks, the first \"timed to end near the conclusion\" of the block. One curve starts only at the block's end, rises steeply to a mid-panel peak and then falls back (ending at Speed), matching \"Speed work begins only at the beginning of the LDTE.\" The right-edge labels are the only certain curve identities; the assignments above follow each curve through the crossings after the block (Inference), and which of the two below-axis curves ends at Vmax and which at STATE is unclear.\n**Why it works** — The authors give the reason for allowing coordination work during the block (it is compatible with a lowered functional state) and for the maintenance dose (explosive strength falls during the second glycolytic peak). They do not explain why speed must be excluded entirely rather than reduced. Inference: it follows from their rule that high-intensity work needs a fresh, not a depressed, functional state ([[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]]).\n**When to apply** — Cyclic speed sports such as sprinting, and by the authors' mention of \"team sports\" for the second glycolytic peak, sports where repeated speed matters. Eric's baseline: \"For football specifically, the stated priority stack is: Goal 1, alactic and aerobic capacity/power for fall-camp demands; Goal 2, the same two systems for game demands; glycolytic conditioning is treated as a \"worst case scenario\" to be absorbed incidentally through camp and practice rather than programmed as a primary target\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 116, Explicit, claimed again as Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: the authors program two deliberate glycolytic peaks, which goes further than the baseline; their own caution (small segments combined with speed work, a maintenance dose for explosive strength) is the part that fits the baseline best.\n**When NOT to apply** — Speed-strength and complex-technical sports keep some technique and do not separate speed so sharply ([[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]). Eric's baseline also says \"staff do program it in the off-season\" for glycolytic work (line 117, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q19)), so the conflict with line 116 is about in-season emphasis. No volumes, durations or intensities are given for either glycolytic peak.\n**Example** — The authors give the schematic only. Inference: a 200 m sprinter spends a concentrated strength block doing strength-endurance circuits and relaxed technical drills with no sprinting, starts sprint work as the block ends, and adds short speed-endurance runs only after sprint volume has peaked, with a few explosive lifts kept in each week.\n**Related** — [[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities]], [[framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]\nconfidence: supported\n\n########## case-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength.md\n# Weekly Microcycles for Swimmers and Speed-Strength Athletes: a Mixed Week of Single-Emphasis Workouts Gave Volume but No Clear Supercompensation; Single-Emphasis Weeks Showed 72-Hour Restoration Patterns\n**What it is** — The authors present three weekly microcycles (PDF pp. 387-389 / printed pp. 376-378; all pages and figures checked against the page images). They call the microcycle \"the basic units of training construction\" and say the traditional complex microcycle cannot carry the volume a large mesocycle needs (PDF p. 387).\n1. **A mixed week for highly qualified swimmers (Fig 6.35, PDF p. 388).** The week was built from the restoration time each workout type needs. The bars read: day 1 aerobic, day 2 speed, day 3 complex (lower bar), day 4 anaerobic, day 5 aerobic, day 6 complex (lower bar), day 7 empty. The \"trend of restoration\" curve, which \"combines the separate effects on restoration for the aerobic, anaerobic and speed components,\" wavers around a flat line with its deepest dip around days 2-3. The authors' verdict: \"despite the uni-directional character of individual workouts, the microcycle as the whole typifies the complex (multi-faceted) form of training organisation. The body cannot simultaneously differentiate between and accumulate specific reactions to every type of daily loading... this form of microcycle construction enables one to achieve a large volume of loading, but the absence of any marked supercompensation (as revealed by the trend of restoration curve) shows that it fails to produce a definite increase in speed, aerobic or anaerobic productivity\" (PDF pp. 387-388).\n2. **Single-emphasis swimming weeks (Fig 6.36, PDF p. 388; caption credits \"Kubelin, 1980,\" the text and PDF p. 381 spell it \"Kudelin\").** Each week has three aerobic (or three anaerobic) workouts on days 1, 3 and 6, with speed-strength, complex and speed-endurance workouts on days 2, 4 and 5, and day 7 empty. Text: after the aerobic week \"there was a significant depression of aerobic potential (VO2 max), the restoration of which was observed over a 72 hour period. At the same time, anaerobic potential, assessed by a swimming test of 4 x 50m, restored quickly and exceeded its initial level. In the second case, restoration of anaerobic productivity took longer and exceeded its initial level after 72 hours. It is interesting that, during this time, aerobic potential rose over a 12 hour span and then diminished.\"\n   Figure values (read by eye at 400 dpi, \"about\"): aerobic week, VO2 max starts about 87% of initial, crosses 100% at about 35 h and reaches about 107% by 72 h; the curve labelled \"Training effect\" starts about 93%, crosses 100% at about 15-18 h and sits about 101-102% at 72 h. Anaerobic week, VO2 max jumps to about 113% at about 6-10 h and falls to about 106% by 72 h; \"Training effect\" starts about 94% and crosses 100% at about 38-40 h, reaching about 102-103% at 72 h.\n3. **A speed-strength week (Fig 6.37, PDF p. 389, Mironenko 1979).** Bars: day 1 technique (medium), day 2 speed-strength (medium), day 3 speed-strength (high), day 4 complex (low), day 5 speed-strength (medium), day 6 speed-strength (high), day 7 empty. The text says these microcycles combine \"workouts with large and moderate volumes of strength loading\" with \"workouts with an emphasis on technical and complex training\" (PDF pp. 388-389).\nThe authors close: \"the microcycles used years ago have become the weakest link in the programming of training. Naturally, the preceding examples still do not eliminate those insufficiencies, although they point the way in which scientific research should be directed\" (PDF p. 389).\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason for the mixed week's failure is that the body \"cannot simultaneously differentiate between and accumulate specific reactions to every type of daily loading\" (PDF p. 387). For the single-emphasis weeks they report the restoration curves without explaining them.\n**When to apply** — Designing a loading week where one quality should be pushed: concentrate workouts of one emphasis in the week. The text gives 72 h for restoration; in the figure the stressed quality is back above its initial level at about 35 h (VO2 max, aerobic week) and about 38-40 h (\"Training effect,\" anaerobic week), and is still rising at 72 h. Eric's baseline: \"The in-season game week: Sunday off; Monday heavy lift; Tuesday and Wednesday heavy practice, with a lighter lift Wednesday; lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 186, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)). Inference: the text's 72 h for the swimmers' VO2 max after an aerobic-emphasis week is the same window Eric leaves before kickoff (the figure shows it back above baseline sooner, at about 35 h); this is a coincidence of numbers from a different sport and quality, not support for the football rule.\n**When NOT to apply** — **Unclear spots.** The text says anaerobic productivity \"exceeded its initial level after 72 hours\" in the anaerobic week, but the figure's \"Training effect\" curve crosses 100% at about 38-40 h; the figure also labels that curve \"Training effect,\" not anaerobic potential, so which curve is the 4 x 50 m test is inferred from the text. The text's \"aerobic potential rose over a 12 hour span and then diminished\" is consistent with the figure (a peak of about 113% at about 6-10 h, then a fall to about 106%, still above the initial level). **The authors' own evidence caveat.** They introduce these examples as \"the currently scant experimental findings\" and preface them: \"It has been established that large unidirectional loading sharply reduces the capability of displaying those qualities and abilities which facilitate the effective and safe execution of training programmes\" (PDF p. 387). The rating stays `supported`, the ceiling for Soviet-era findings, because the swimmer data are a reported study with figure values; the \"scant\" caveat and the speed-strength week's lack of outcome data are why it goes no higher. No sample sizes, athlete numbers or units for the \"training load\" bars are given. These are elite swimmers and a Soviet-era report; the speed-strength week has no outcome data at all.\n**Example** — The authors' own example is the Kudelin swimmer data above (PDF p. 388).\n**Related** — [[claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes]], [[framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound]], [[periodization--bompa/heuristic--fatigue-fitness-3to1-ratio]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--microcycle-types-and-load-wave-design]], [[block-periodization--issurin/case-study--popov-aerobic-strength-microcycle]]\nconfidence: supported","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"418274c6-3e77-43e0-a1ed-a99dd98d133b","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:10:21.200Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"########## framework--speed-sport-block-organisation-and-two-glycolytic-peaks.md\n# Speed Sports: No Speed Work During the Strength Block, Speed Starts as the Delayed Effect Rises, and Glycolytic Loading Has Two Peaks\n**What it is** — For \"speed sports with a cyclical movement structure\" (Fig 6.32b caption, PDF p. 383 / printed p. 372) the authors separate strength and speed more sharply than in speed-strength sports (PDF p. 384 / printed p. 373; both pages checked against the images):\n1. **Speed out of the block.** \"Speed work is excluded totally from the concentrated strength loading stage. However, it is possible during this stage to perfect the athlete's motor skill, which directly determines high speed of movement.\" That skill is intermuscular coordination with \"no tension in the muscle groups which do not directly take part in executing the motor tasks,\" and \"This type of work is quite compatible with the athlete's decreased functional state, provided it is executed in the optimal range of intensity of effort and frequency of movement.\"\n2. **Speed starts with the LDTE.** \"Speed work begins only at the beginning of the LDTE of strength loading (when the 'State' curve rises above the time axis), together with the necessary gradual rise in the training intensity.\"\n3. **Two anaerobic-glycolytic peaks.** High competition speed \"is determined largely by specific speed-endurance.\" The glycolytic loading \"exhibits two peaks\": the first \"is timed to end near the conclusion of the stage of concentrated strength loading. Specialised strength means are employed and the objective is to develop special strength-endurance which is primarily of a local nature\"; the second \"follows the period during which volume of speed work reaches a peak. Distance methods (in cyclic sports) or specialised means and methods (in one-on-one events and team sports) are employed... this type of work should be done in segments of small volume and combined with speed work.\"\n4. **Protect explosive strength.** \"Along with the rise in strength-endurance, explosive strength tends to decrease when the second peak of the anaerobic-glycolytic loading occurs. Therefore, in those sports where explosive strength is of great importance, it is necessary to include a sufficiently intensive, small volume of maintenance loading.\"\nFig 6.32(b), read from the image (PDF p. 383, no numeric scale; read at 400 and 600 dpi): during the shaded strength block, two curves fall below the time axis and climb back above it only after the block ends (they end at Vmax, highest at the right, and at STATE). Two curves rise during the block: one rises steadily and keeps rising (ending at S-E), and one rises to a peak in the latter part of the block (about two thirds to three quarters of the way through), falls after it, then rises to a second hump before easing at the right (ending at An-Gly). That shape matches the text's two glycolytic peaks, the first \"timed to end near the conclusion\" of the block. One curve starts only at the block's end, rises steeply to a mid-panel peak and then falls back (ending at Speed), matching \"Speed work begins only at the beginning of the LDTE.\" The right-edge labels are the only certain curve identities; the assignments above follow each curve through the crossings after the block (Inference), and which of the two below-axis curves ends at Vmax and which at STATE is unclear.\n**Why it works** — The authors give the reason for allowing coordination work during the block (it is compatible with a lowered functional state) and for the maintenance dose (explosive strength falls during the second glycolytic peak). They do not explain why speed must be excluded entirely rather than reduced. Inference: it follows from their rule that high-intensity work needs a fresh, not a depressed, functional state ([[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]]).\n**When to apply** — Cyclic speed sports such as sprinting, and by the authors' mention of \"team sports\" for the second glycolytic peak, sports where repeated speed matters. Eric's baseline: \"For football specifically, the stated priority stack is: Goal 1, alactic and aerobic capacity/power for fall-camp demands; Goal 2, the same two systems for game demands; glycolytic conditioning is treated as a \"worst case scenario\" to be absorbed incidentally through camp and practice rather than programmed as a primary target\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 116, Explicit, claimed again as Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: the authors program two deliberate glycolytic peaks, which goes further than the baseline; their own caution (small segments combined with speed work, a maintenance dose for explosive strength) is the part that fits the baseline best.\n**When NOT to apply** — Speed-strength and complex-technical sports keep some technique and do not separate speed so sharply ([[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]). Eric's baseline also says \"staff do program it in the off-season\" for glycolytic work (line 117, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q19)), so the conflict with line 116 is about in-season emphasis. No volumes, durations or intensities are given for either glycolytic peak.\n**Example** — The authors give the schematic only. Inference: a 200 m sprinter spends a concentrated strength block doing strength-endurance circuits and relaxed technical drills with no sprinting, starts sprint work as the block ends, and adds short speed-endurance runs only after sprint volume has peaked, with a few explosive lifts kept in each week.\n**Related** — [[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities]], [[framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]\nconfidence: supported\n\n########## case-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength.md\n# Weekly Microcycles for Swimmers and Speed-Strength Athletes: a Mixed Week of Single-Emphasis Workouts Gave Volume but No Clear Supercompensation; Single-Emphasis Weeks Showed 72-Hour Restoration Patterns\n**What it is** — The authors present three weekly microcycles (PDF pp. 387-389 / printed pp. 376-378; all pages and figures checked against the page images). They call the microcycle \"the basic units of training construction\" and say the traditional complex microcycle cannot carry the volume a large mesocycle needs (PDF p. 387).\n1. **A mixed week for highly qualified swimmers (Fig 6.35, PDF p. 388).** The week was built from the restoration time each workout type needs. The bars read: day 1 aerobic, day 2 speed, day 3 complex (lower bar), day 4 anaerobic, day 5 aerobic, day 6 complex (lower bar), day 7 empty. The \"trend of restoration\" curve, which \"combines the separate effects on restoration for the aerobic, anaerobic and speed components,\" wavers around a flat line with its deepest dip around days 2-3. The authors' verdict: \"despite the uni-directional character of individual workouts, the microcycle as the whole typifies the complex (multi-faceted) form of training organisation. The body cannot simultaneously differentiate between and accumulate specific reactions to every type of daily loading... this form of microcycle construction enables one to achieve a large volume of loading, but the absence of any marked supercompensation (as revealed by the trend of restoration curve) shows that it fails to produce a definite increase in speed, aerobic or anaerobic productivity\" (PDF pp. 387-388).\n2. **Single-emphasis swimming weeks (Fig 6.36, PDF p. 388; caption credits \"Kubelin, 1980,\" the text and PDF p. 381 spell it \"Kudelin\").** Each week has three aerobic (or three anaerobic) workouts on days 1, 3 and 6, with speed-strength, complex and speed-endurance workouts on days 2, 4 and 5, and day 7 empty. Text: after the aerobic week \"there was a significant depression of aerobic potential (VO2 max), the restoration of which was observed over a 72 hour period. At the same time, anaerobic potential, assessed by a swimming test of 4 x 50m, restored quickly and exceeded its initial level. In the second case, restoration of anaerobic productivity took longer and exceeded its initial level after 72 hours. It is interesting that, during this time, aerobic potential rose over a 12 hour span and then diminished.\"\n   Figure values (read by eye at 400 dpi, \"about\"): aerobic week, VO2 max starts about 87% of initial, crosses 100% at about 35 h and reaches about 107% by 72 h; the curve labelled \"Training effect\" starts about 93%, crosses 100% at about 15-18 h and sits about 101-102% at 72 h. Anaerobic week, VO2 max jumps to about 113% at about 6-10 h and falls to about 106% by 72 h; \"Training effect\" starts about 94% and crosses 100% at about 38-40 h, reaching about 102-103% at 72 h.\n3. **A speed-strength week (Fig 6.37, PDF p. 389, Mironenko 1979).** Bars: day 1 technique (medium), day 2 speed-strength (medium), day 3 speed-strength (high), day 4 complex (low), day 5 speed-strength (medium), day 6 speed-strength (high), day 7 empty. The text says these microcycles combine \"workouts with large and moderate volumes of strength loading\" with \"workouts with an emphasis on technical and complex training\" (PDF pp. 388-389).\nThe authors close: \"the microcycles used years ago have become the weakest link in the programming of training. Naturally, the preceding examples still do not eliminate those insufficiencies, although they point the way in which scientific research should be directed\" (PDF p. 389).\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason for the mixed week's failure is that the body \"cannot simultaneously differentiate between and accumulate specific reactions to every type of daily loading\" (PDF p. 387). For the single-emphasis weeks they report the restoration curves without explaining them.\n**When to apply** — Designing a loading week where one quality should be pushed: concentrate workouts of one emphasis in the week. The text gives 72 h for restoration; in the figure the stressed quality is back above its initial level at about 35 h (VO2 max, aerobic week) and about 38-40 h (\"Training effect,\" anaerobic week), and is still rising at 72 h. Eric's baseline: \"The in-season game week: Sunday off; Monday heavy lift; Tuesday and Wednesday heavy practice, with a lighter lift Wednesday; lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 186, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)). Inference: the text's 72 h for the swimmers' VO2 max after an aerobic-emphasis week is the same window Eric leaves before kickoff (the figure shows it back above baseline sooner, at about 35 h); this is a coincidence of numbers from a different sport and quality, not support for the football rule.\n**When NOT to apply** — **Unclear spots.** The text says anaerobic productivity \"exceeded its initial level after 72 hours\" in the anaerobic week, but the figure's \"Training effect\" curve crosses 100% at about 38-40 h; the figure also labels that curve \"Training effect,\" not anaerobic potential, so which curve is the 4 x 50 m test is inferred from the text. The text's \"aerobic potential rose over a 12 hour span and then diminished\" is consistent with the figure (a peak of about 113% at about 6-10 h, then a fall to about 106%, still above the initial level). **The authors' own evidence caveat.** They introduce these examples as \"the currently scant experimental findings\" and preface them: \"It has been established that large unidirectional loading sharply reduces the capability of displaying those qualities and abilities which facilitate the effective and safe execution of training programmes\" (PDF p. 387). The rating stays `supported`, the ceiling for Soviet-era findings, because the swimmer data are a reported study with figure values; the \"scant\" caveat and the speed-strength week's lack of outcome data are why it goes no higher. No sample sizes, athlete numbers or units for the \"training load\" bars are given. These are elite swimmers and a Soviet-era report; the speed-strength week has no outcome data at all.\n**Example** — The authors' own example is the Kudelin swimmer data above (PDF p. 388).\n**Related** — [[claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes]], [[framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound]], [[periodization--bompa/heuristic--fatigue-fitness-3to1-ratio]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--microcycle-types-and-load-wave-design]], [[block-periodization--issurin/case-study--popov-aerobic-strength-microcycle]]\nconfidence: supported","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"925f5f0c-6b18-43d9-9142-3247d2bcb906","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
