{"parentUuid":"9d9d06bd-656c-4a03-a3c6-35e9b402c5f3","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac7100d52d01adab3","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffqFsmhByYZMxkVxXgza","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MX9MDDWezQGPNYP1sjZKuY","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/; for f in case-study--tabachnik-100m-sprinter-model-characteristics.md heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks.md case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities.md; do printf '\\n########## %s\\n' $f; sed 1,12d $f | grep -v '^$'; grep confidence $f; done","description":"Read three more FIX entries"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3605,"cache_read_input_tokens":152444,"output_tokens":1615,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1342},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3605},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1615,"cache_read_input_tokens":152444,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3605,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3605,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01MX9MDDWezQGPNYP1sjZKuY":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/; for f in case-study--tabachnik-100m-sprinter-model-characteristics.md heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks.md case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities.md; do printf '\\n########## %s\\n' $f; sed 1,12d $f | grep -v '^$'; grep confidence $f; done","description":"Read three more FIX entries"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffqFjbaGEvbkP7eLQ4yp","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"1a500472-551a-49d5-9e79-ad065a827b0e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:11:04.733Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"1a500472-551a-49d5-9e79-ad065a827b0e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"ce914f6b-f81b-42fd-a40b-4c5550786125","agentId":"ac7100d52d01adab3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MX9MDDWezQGPNYP1sjZKuY","type":"tool_result","content":"########## case-study--tabachnik-100m-sprinter-model-characteristics.md\n# Tabachnik's Model Characteristics for 10.0 s Men and 11.0 s Women 100 m Sprinters: Speed, Relative Strength and Jump Targets\n**What it is** — Two tables from Tabachnik (1979) give the profile a sprinter should reach to run 10.0 s (men) or 11.0 s (women) (PDF p. 392 / printed p. 381; every value below checked against the page image).\n**Table 6.8** (men 10.0 s / women 11.0 s):\n- General data: initial 100 m time 11.62 / 13.0 s; age of beginning sprinting 14.6 / 14.1 years; years of sprinting 8.4 / 8.3.\n- Speed indicators: simple motor reaction time 0.110 / 0.121; starting reaction time 0.136 / 0.149; total starting time 0.37 / 0.40 s; 5 m from low start 1.18 / 1.32; 30 m from low start 3.80 / 4.25; 50 m from low start 5.70 / 6.15; last 50 m of 100 m 4.45 / 5.00 s.\n- Relative muscular strength (relative to bodymass): trunk extension 2.80 / 2.60; trunk flexion 1.20 / 1.15; knee extension 2.20 / 2.00; knee flexion 1.10 / 1.05; hip extension 3.10 / 2.80; hip flexion 1.20 / 1.05; ankle plantarflexion 3.00 / 2.80.\n- Speed-strength: standing long jump 3.20 / 2.80 m; standing triple jump 10.0 / 8.40 m; ten-step jump from standing start 36.0 / 32.0 m.\n**Table 6.9** (section times by goal, seconds): for a 10.0 s goal, 15 m 1.16-1.18, 30 m 3.70-3.80, first 50 m 5.60-5.70, second 50 m 4.30-4.40, last 10 m 0.900; for 10.9 s, 15 m 1.30-1.32, 30 m 4.20-4.30, first 50 m 6.00-6.10, second 50 m 4.80-4.90, last 10 m 0.990 (goals 10.2, 10.5 and 10.7 s lie between).\nThe tables sit among the authors' examples of descriptive modelling, alongside middle-distance (Tables 6.7, 6.10, Maximenko 1979), javelin (Table 6.11, Konstantinov 1978) and juvenile sprinter norms (Table 6.12, Alabin & Yushkevitch 1978) (PDF pp. 392-393; summarised in [[framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models]]).\n**Why it works** — The book gives no derivation, sample or method for the norms, and does not say how the relative-strength values were measured (the units are \"relative to bodymass\" only). The authors' general claim is that such norms \"enable the coach to identify any weaknesses in the state of the athlete\" (PDF p. 391 / printed p. 380).\n**When to apply** — As a reference for what a world-class sprint profile looked like in this Soviet model, and as a template for building one's own position-specific norm table. Eric's baseline: \"If it doesn't answer a question anyone has, or a direct need, then it doesn't have a place\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 32, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q3)). Inference: a table like this only earns a place if each row answers a programming question for the roster.\n**When NOT to apply** — **Internal inconsistencies, recorded as printed.** In Table 6.8 the men's 50 m from a low start (5.70 s) plus the last 50 m (4.45 s) sum to 10.15 s, not 10.0 s; Table 6.9 gives 4.30-4.40 s for the second 50 m at a 10.0 s goal, faster than Table 6.8's 4.45 s. Women's 6.15 + 5.00 = 11.15 s against an 11.0 s goal. The sources may define the splits differently, but the book does not say. Table 6.8's 5 m from a low start (1.18 s, men) equals Table 6.9's 15 m (1.16-1.18 s) for the same 10.0 s goal (PDF p. 392 / printed p. 381, checked against the page image); 15 m cannot take the same time as 5 m from the same start, so the two tables cannot use the same start and timing, and the book does not explain. In Table 6.9 the 15 m step from the 10.2 s goal (1.18-1.20) to the 10.5 s goal (1.26-1.28) is much larger than the other steps. Hand timing and 1970s testing methods are not described, so the values do not convert directly to modern electronic timing. These are elite sprinters, not football players; the relative-strength values come from an unspecified test and cannot be compared with force-plate or 1RM data.\n**Example** — The authors' own example is the table itself. Inference: a coach reading the standing triple jump target (10.0 m for 10.0 s men) would check whether a sprinter with fast 30 m times but a short triple jump lacks the speed-strength the model expects.\n**Related** — [[framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--model-characteristic-tables-exist-by-qualification]], [[claim--absolute-leg-strength-connects-to-sprint-speed-only-indirectly-via-jump]]\nconfidence: untested\n\n########## heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks.md\n# After Large Volumes, the Delayed Training Effect Needs a Prolonged Reduction in Loading, Far Longer Than the Usual 1-2 Week Unload\n**What it is** — The authors (PDF p. 383 / printed p. 372, checked against the page image): \"A relatively prolonged stage of reduction in the volume of training is necessary to promote the LDTE. However, in practice, coaches usually aim to achieve large volumes of accumulated loading and do not always emphasize the role of restoration. Although they provide recuperation pauses within the microcycle and the so-called overload microcycles to provide some rest, they still do not effectively utilise prolonged reduction of loading after large volumes.\" Such methods are needed \"not only for restoration, but also for the development and optimal use of those physiological processes which underlie adaptation. These processes occur relatively slowly and an extended period is necessary for their completion, which is far longer than the 1-2 weeks usually allocated for reduction of loading.\" They add a warning about extra work: \"By overestimating the loading required for developing special preparedness, athletes frequently not only undertake an excessive volume of work, but also execute this volume when it is clearly contraindicated, namely when restoration is vital. Therefore, additional volumes of loading not only do not offer increased training benefits, but also become a major barrier to stimulating the restoration which is sorely needed by the body.\"\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that the adaptive processes behind the LDTE \"occur relatively slowly.\" They do not name the processes on this page or give a measured duration here. Inference: this matches the book's Chapter 6 figure of 4-12 weeks for the long-term delayed effect, cited in [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]] (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364), but this page itself gives only \"far longer than the 1-2 weeks.\" Chapter 5 gives a different figure: the gains after \"4-8 week concentrated blocks of strength, speed-strength or speed training, may occur most commonly after a latency period of 1-3 weeks\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image). Inference: that is the time to the first gains, while this page is about how long the reduced-volume stage must last, so the two are recorded side by side rather than as a contradiction (the same distinction is drawn in [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]).\n**When to apply** — After a concentrated, high-volume block: plan a stage of reduced volume measured in weeks, not a single deload week, and do not fill it with more volume. The \"no more volume\" part is the same instruction as rule 2 of [[heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window]]: \"The volume of training work, therefore, should not be increased during the period of emergence of the LDTE\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364). This page adds the critique of the usual 1-2 week unload and calls extra volume \"a major barrier\" to restoration. Speed and technique work rise during this stage in the authors' models ([[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]]).\n**When NOT to apply** — The rule is about the period after large accumulated volumes, not routine week-to-week loading. Section 6.10 sets out the delayed effect and its timed volume cut ([[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]]); this 6.12.2 page adds the critique that the usual 1-2 week reduction is too short and that extra volume blocks restoration. Tension with another library source: Bompa's step-loading paradigm uses one unloading week after three loading weeks (3:1) as the standard wave ([[periodization--bompa/framework--step-loading-paradigm]]). The two address different situations (a routine wave versus the aftermath of a concentrated block), so this is not recorded as a contradiction, but a 3:1 deload should not be taken as enough reduction for the LDTE in the authors' sense. The authors themselves report 3- and 4-week stages with a final restoration week as the most common practice and say \"all of them, depending on circumstances, can produce good results\" (PDF p. 385 / printed p. 374). Reduced volume is not reduced intensity: speed work begins at the LDTE stage \"together with the necessary gradual rise in the training intensity\" (PDF p. 384 / printed p. 373).\n**Example** — The authors give no worked case on this page. Inference: after a six-week concentrated strength block, a jumper keeps strength volume low for the following four to six weeks while jump and sprint intensity rises, instead of returning to full volume after one easy week.\n**Related** — [[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear]], [[heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window]], [[periodization--bompa/framework--step-loading-paradigm]], [[heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work]], [[framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound]]\nconfidence: supported\n\n########## case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities.md\n# Antonova (Women Long Jumpers) and Levchenko (Sprinters): Months of Peak Strength Volume Lowered Explosive Qualities, Which Rose Again When Loading Fell\n**What it is** — Under \"Problems with Concentrated Loading\" the authors report two Soviet studies (PDF p. 382 / printed p. 371, checked against the page image). They open with the general cost: concentrated strength loading \"leads to a brief but consistent decrease in speed-strength, which diminishes the athlete's special work-capacity and complicates the task of improving technical skills and speed of movement.\"\n1. **Antonova (1982), women long jumpers.** The study analysed \"the connection between the organisation and the volume of training loads, the changes in the force-time characteristics of the take-off in jumping, the sport results and the explosive strength.\" \"It was found that in the months with the largest training volumes, there was a noticeable decrease in the special-strength preparedness and sporting results. However, these same qualities rose during the subsequent months of reduced loading.\"\n2. **Levchenko (1980), sprinters.** It examined \"the interdependence between the dynamics of the volume of special strength-training means, the elasticity of the muscles and the control results of the standing triple-jump of highly qualified sprinters during the spring-summer stage (15 weeks) of training.\" \"An increase in the volume of special strength-training means results in increased muscle stiffness and decreased explosive force. Unfavourable conditions were thus created for improving technique and running speed and the likelihood of injury increased significantly.\"\nThe authors add that \"voluminous strength loading is detrimental to the technique of the weightlifting movements (Khlystov, 1976; Vorobyev et al, 1978), javelin throwing (Ruvinsky, 1980) and punching speed in boxing (Filimonov, 1979),\" and conclude: \"Rather than negating the value of concentrated loading, these findings stress the importance of knowing when this method may be appropriate to use.\"\n**Why it works** — The book reports correlations between volume and outcomes; it gives no sample sizes, effect sizes or statistics for either study. The only mechanism offered is Levchenko's observed rise in \"muscle stiffness\" with strength volume. The book does not define how stiffness or elasticity was measured.\n**When to apply** — As the evidence behind expecting a temporary drop in jump and sprint qualities during high-volume strength phases, and a rebound when volume falls. The authors' remedy follows on the same page: \"if concentrated loading is used, then *cybernetic periodisation* or programming based on Perceived Ratings of Effort and Ratings of Technique, as well as formal testing, can assist greatly in preventing overtraining (see 6.2.2).\" Section 6.2.2 defines cybernetic periodisation as a scheme in which \"the original preplanned periodisation scheme is regularly modified by subjective and objective feedback\" (PDF p. 340 / printed p. 329, text layer). Eric's baseline: \"Modified reactive strength index (mRSI) from daily CMJ testing was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker in place of subjective wellness questionnaires\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 74, Explicit). Inference: a daily jump measure is the kind of \"formal testing\" that would catch the drop Antonova and Levchenko describe.\n**When NOT to apply** — Levchenko's subjects were \"highly qualified sprinters\"; the long jumpers' level is not stated (PDF p. 382 says only \"women long jumpers\"). **Source note on dates:** the book dates Levchenko 1980 here, 1981 at PDF p. 381 and 1982 at PDF p. 374, and Antonova 1982 here and at PDF p. 374 but 1980 at PDF p. 381 (all three pages, text layer and page images). The injury statement (\"the likelihood of injury increased significantly\") gives no injury counts. The book does not say whether loads were heavy or moderate, only that volume rose. This duplicates in part the Chapter 5 warning in [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]], which cites Kotz, Filinov and Deniskin; the Chapter 6 page adds the Antonova and Levchenko studies and spells the boxing author \"Filimonov, 1979.\"\n**Example** — The authors' own example is the Antonova finding: results fell in the highest-volume months and rose in the reduced-loading months that followed (PDF p. 382).\n**Related** — [[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]] (6.10, PDF p. 374, cites Antonova 1982 and Levchenko 1982 for the same dip-then-rebound finding; this page adds the force-time take-off data, muscle stiffness and the injury statement), [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]], [[claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving]], [[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]], [[heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks]], [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]]\nconfidence: supported","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"892ddd4e-1941-4043-8204-4ec40a0e796a","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:11:05.011Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"########## case-study--tabachnik-100m-sprinter-model-characteristics.md\n# Tabachnik's Model Characteristics for 10.0 s Men and 11.0 s Women 100 m Sprinters: Speed, Relative Strength and Jump Targets\n**What it is** — Two tables from Tabachnik (1979) give the profile a sprinter should reach to run 10.0 s (men) or 11.0 s (women) (PDF p. 392 / printed p. 381; every value below checked against the page image).\n**Table 6.8** (men 10.0 s / women 11.0 s):\n- General data: initial 100 m time 11.62 / 13.0 s; age of beginning sprinting 14.6 / 14.1 years; years of sprinting 8.4 / 8.3.\n- Speed indicators: simple motor reaction time 0.110 / 0.121; starting reaction time 0.136 / 0.149; total starting time 0.37 / 0.40 s; 5 m from low start 1.18 / 1.32; 30 m from low start 3.80 / 4.25; 50 m from low start 5.70 / 6.15; last 50 m of 100 m 4.45 / 5.00 s.\n- Relative muscular strength (relative to bodymass): trunk extension 2.80 / 2.60; trunk flexion 1.20 / 1.15; knee extension 2.20 / 2.00; knee flexion 1.10 / 1.05; hip extension 3.10 / 2.80; hip flexion 1.20 / 1.05; ankle plantarflexion 3.00 / 2.80.\n- Speed-strength: standing long jump 3.20 / 2.80 m; standing triple jump 10.0 / 8.40 m; ten-step jump from standing start 36.0 / 32.0 m.\n**Table 6.9** (section times by goal, seconds): for a 10.0 s goal, 15 m 1.16-1.18, 30 m 3.70-3.80, first 50 m 5.60-5.70, second 50 m 4.30-4.40, last 10 m 0.900; for 10.9 s, 15 m 1.30-1.32, 30 m 4.20-4.30, first 50 m 6.00-6.10, second 50 m 4.80-4.90, last 10 m 0.990 (goals 10.2, 10.5 and 10.7 s lie between).\nThe tables sit among the authors' examples of descriptive modelling, alongside middle-distance (Tables 6.7, 6.10, Maximenko 1979), javelin (Table 6.11, Konstantinov 1978) and juvenile sprinter norms (Table 6.12, Alabin & Yushkevitch 1978) (PDF pp. 392-393; summarised in [[framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models]]).\n**Why it works** — The book gives no derivation, sample or method for the norms, and does not say how the relative-strength values were measured (the units are \"relative to bodymass\" only). The authors' general claim is that such norms \"enable the coach to identify any weaknesses in the state of the athlete\" (PDF p. 391 / printed p. 380).\n**When to apply** — As a reference for what a world-class sprint profile looked like in this Soviet model, and as a template for building one's own position-specific norm table. Eric's baseline: \"If it doesn't answer a question anyone has, or a direct need, then it doesn't have a place\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 32, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q3)). Inference: a table like this only earns a place if each row answers a programming question for the roster.\n**When NOT to apply** — **Internal inconsistencies, recorded as printed.** In Table 6.8 the men's 50 m from a low start (5.70 s) plus the last 50 m (4.45 s) sum to 10.15 s, not 10.0 s; Table 6.9 gives 4.30-4.40 s for the second 50 m at a 10.0 s goal, faster than Table 6.8's 4.45 s. Women's 6.15 + 5.00 = 11.15 s against an 11.0 s goal. The sources may define the splits differently, but the book does not say. Table 6.8's 5 m from a low start (1.18 s, men) equals Table 6.9's 15 m (1.16-1.18 s) for the same 10.0 s goal (PDF p. 392 / printed p. 381, checked against the page image); 15 m cannot take the same time as 5 m from the same start, so the two tables cannot use the same start and timing, and the book does not explain. In Table 6.9 the 15 m step from the 10.2 s goal (1.18-1.20) to the 10.5 s goal (1.26-1.28) is much larger than the other steps. Hand timing and 1970s testing methods are not described, so the values do not convert directly to modern electronic timing. These are elite sprinters, not football players; the relative-strength values come from an unspecified test and cannot be compared with force-plate or 1RM data.\n**Example** — The authors' own example is the table itself. Inference: a coach reading the standing triple jump target (10.0 m for 10.0 s men) would check whether a sprinter with fast 30 m times but a short triple jump lacks the speed-strength the model expects.\n**Related** — [[framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--model-characteristic-tables-exist-by-qualification]], [[claim--absolute-leg-strength-connects-to-sprint-speed-only-indirectly-via-jump]]\nconfidence: untested\n\n########## heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks.md\n# After Large Volumes, the Delayed Training Effect Needs a Prolonged Reduction in Loading, Far Longer Than the Usual 1-2 Week Unload\n**What it is** — The authors (PDF p. 383 / printed p. 372, checked against the page image): \"A relatively prolonged stage of reduction in the volume of training is necessary to promote the LDTE. However, in practice, coaches usually aim to achieve large volumes of accumulated loading and do not always emphasize the role of restoration. Although they provide recuperation pauses within the microcycle and the so-called overload microcycles to provide some rest, they still do not effectively utilise prolonged reduction of loading after large volumes.\" Such methods are needed \"not only for restoration, but also for the development and optimal use of those physiological processes which underlie adaptation. These processes occur relatively slowly and an extended period is necessary for their completion, which is far longer than the 1-2 weeks usually allocated for reduction of loading.\" They add a warning about extra work: \"By overestimating the loading required for developing special preparedness, athletes frequently not only undertake an excessive volume of work, but also execute this volume when it is clearly contraindicated, namely when restoration is vital. Therefore, additional volumes of loading not only do not offer increased training benefits, but also become a major barrier to stimulating the restoration which is sorely needed by the body.\"\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that the adaptive processes behind the LDTE \"occur relatively slowly.\" They do not name the processes on this page or give a measured duration here. Inference: this matches the book's Chapter 6 figure of 4-12 weeks for the long-term delayed effect, cited in [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]] (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364), but this page itself gives only \"far longer than the 1-2 weeks.\" Chapter 5 gives a different figure: the gains after \"4-8 week concentrated blocks of strength, speed-strength or speed training, may occur most commonly after a latency period of 1-3 weeks\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image). Inference: that is the time to the first gains, while this page is about how long the reduced-volume stage must last, so the two are recorded side by side rather than as a contradiction (the same distinction is drawn in [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]).\n**When to apply** — After a concentrated, high-volume block: plan a stage of reduced volume measured in weeks, not a single deload week, and do not fill it with more volume. The \"no more volume\" part is the same instruction as rule 2 of [[heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window]]: \"The volume of training work, therefore, should not be increased during the period of emergence of the LDTE\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364). This page adds the critique of the usual 1-2 week unload and calls extra volume \"a major barrier\" to restoration. Speed and technique work rise during this stage in the authors' models ([[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]]).\n**When NOT to apply** — The rule is about the period after large accumulated volumes, not routine week-to-week loading. Section 6.10 sets out the delayed effect and its timed volume cut ([[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]]); this 6.12.2 page adds the critique that the usual 1-2 week reduction is too short and that extra volume blocks restoration. Tension with another library source: Bompa's step-loading paradigm uses one unloading week after three loading weeks (3:1) as the standard wave ([[periodization--bompa/framework--step-loading-paradigm]]). The two address different situations (a routine wave versus the aftermath of a concentrated block), so this is not recorded as a contradiction, but a 3:1 deload should not be taken as enough reduction for the LDTE in the authors' sense. The authors themselves report 3- and 4-week stages with a final restoration week as the most common practice and say \"all of them, depending on circumstances, can produce good results\" (PDF p. 385 / printed p. 374). Reduced volume is not reduced intensity: speed work begins at the LDTE stage \"together with the necessary gradual rise in the training intensity\" (PDF p. 384 / printed p. 373).\n**Example** — The authors give no worked case on this page. Inference: after a six-week concentrated strength block, a jumper keeps strength volume low for the following four to six weeks while jump and sprint intensity rises, instead of returning to full volume after one easy week.\n**Related** — [[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear]], [[heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window]], [[periodization--bompa/framework--step-loading-paradigm]], [[heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work]], [[framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound]]\nconfidence: supported\n\n########## case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities.md\n# Antonova (Women Long Jumpers) and Levchenko (Sprinters): Months of Peak Strength Volume Lowered Explosive Qualities, Which Rose Again When Loading Fell\n**What it is** — Under \"Problems with Concentrated Loading\" the authors report two Soviet studies (PDF p. 382 / printed p. 371, checked against the page image). They open with the general cost: concentrated strength loading \"leads to a brief but consistent decrease in speed-strength, which diminishes the athlete's special work-capacity and complicates the task of improving technical skills and speed of movement.\"\n1. **Antonova (1982), women long jumpers.** The study analysed \"the connection between the organisation and the volume of training loads, the changes in the force-time characteristics of the take-off in jumping, the sport results and the explosive strength.\" \"It was found that in the months with the largest training volumes, there was a noticeable decrease in the special-strength preparedness and sporting results. However, these same qualities rose during the subsequent months of reduced loading.\"\n2. **Levchenko (1980), sprinters.** It examined \"the interdependence between the dynamics of the volume of special strength-training means, the elasticity of the muscles and the control results of the standing triple-jump of highly qualified sprinters during the spring-summer stage (15 weeks) of training.\" \"An increase in the volume of special strength-training means results in increased muscle stiffness and decreased explosive force. Unfavourable conditions were thus created for improving technique and running speed and the likelihood of injury increased significantly.\"\nThe authors add that \"voluminous strength loading is detrimental to the technique of the weightlifting movements (Khlystov, 1976; Vorobyev et al, 1978), javelin throwing (Ruvinsky, 1980) and punching speed in boxing (Filimonov, 1979),\" and conclude: \"Rather than negating the value of concentrated loading, these findings stress the importance of knowing when this method may be appropriate to use.\"\n**Why it works** — The book reports correlations between volume and outcomes; it gives no sample sizes, effect sizes or statistics for either study. The only mechanism offered is Levchenko's observed rise in \"muscle stiffness\" with strength volume. The book does not define how stiffness or elasticity was measured.\n**When to apply** — As the evidence behind expecting a temporary drop in jump and sprint qualities during high-volume strength phases, and a rebound when volume falls. The authors' remedy follows on the same page: \"if concentrated loading is used, then *cybernetic periodisation* or programming based on Perceived Ratings of Effort and Ratings of Technique, as well as formal testing, can assist greatly in preventing overtraining (see 6.2.2).\" Section 6.2.2 defines cybernetic periodisation as a scheme in which \"the original preplanned periodisation scheme is regularly modified by subjective and objective feedback\" (PDF p. 340 / printed p. 329, text layer). Eric's baseline: \"Modified reactive strength index (mRSI) from daily CMJ testing was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker in place of subjective wellness questionnaires\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 74, Explicit). Inference: a daily jump measure is the kind of \"formal testing\" that would catch the drop Antonova and Levchenko describe.\n**When NOT to apply** — Levchenko's subjects were \"highly qualified sprinters\"; the long jumpers' level is not stated (PDF p. 382 says only \"women long jumpers\"). **Source note on dates:** the book dates Levchenko 1980 here, 1981 at PDF p. 381 and 1982 at PDF p. 374, and Antonova 1982 here and at PDF p. 374 but 1980 at PDF p. 381 (all three pages, text layer and page images). The injury statement (\"the likelihood of injury increased significantly\") gives no injury counts. The book does not say whether loads were heavy or moderate, only that volume rose. This duplicates in part the Chapter 5 warning in [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]], which cites Kotz, Filinov and Deniskin; the Chapter 6 page adds the Antonova and Levchenko studies and spells the boxing author \"Filimonov, 1979.\"\n**Example** — The authors' own example is the Antonova finding: results fell in the highest-volume months and rose in the reduced-loading months that followed (PDF p. 382).\n**Related** — [[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]] (6.10, PDF p. 374, cites Antonova 1982 and Levchenko 1982 for the same dip-then-rebound finding; this page adds the force-time take-off data, muscle stiffness and the injury statement), [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]], [[claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving]], [[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]], [[heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks]], [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]]\nconfidence: supported","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1a500472-551a-49d5-9e79-ad065a827b0e","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
