{"parentUuid":"1f4b0def-3698-4945-835e-320978fe4f1e","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac7100d52d01adab3","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffqLyVdNJDfLcJDrDn4X","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FizJeA16Qn1A56GSU3jiQk","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/; for f in claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity.md heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work.md framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models.md; do printf '\\n########## %s\\n' $f; sed 1,12d $f | grep -v '^$'; grep confidence $f; done","description":"Read remaining edited entries"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1154,"cache_read_input_tokens":173725,"output_tokens":352,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":93},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1154},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":352,"cache_read_input_tokens":173725,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1154,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1154,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01FizJeA16Qn1A56GSU3jiQk":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/; for f in claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity.md heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work.md framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models.md; do printf '\\n########## %s\\n' $f; sed 1,12d $f | grep -v '^$'; grep confidence $f; done","description":"Read remaining edited entries"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffqLt9fPBz4Msgv9xpon","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"136b2624-7e99-4501-b927-ad516218fd81","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:12:01.864Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"136b2624-7e99-4501-b927-ad516218fd81","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"832c731c-797b-4631-bbba-f264eca1936c","agentId":"ac7100d52d01adab3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FizJeA16Qn1A56GSU3jiQk","type":"tool_result","content":"########## claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity.md\n# In a Long Monocyclic Competition Period, a Prolonged Cut in Loading Volume Lowers Fitness; Competition Plus Small Special Loads Cannot Maintain It\n**What it is** — In the long-duration endurance model (PDF p. 396 / printed p. 385, checked against the page image): \"It has been established that the prolonged reduction in the volume of loading during the competition period (with monocyclic periodisation) leads to re-adaptation at a lower level, which is reflected in the drop of non-specific trainability. Competition and special supplementary loading (with limited volumes) cannot stimulate further development or even maintain special work-capacity. Thus, it is appropriate to periodically increase the volume of competition and special assistance loading (Baranovsky, 1969; Kryazh, 1969; Muzis, 1970; Zhikharevich, 1976).\" The paired condition from Gilyazova (1978) is that stable results need \"not a sharp drop in the volume of loading during the competitive period\" and a gradual preparatory increase (\"a mean monthly increase of 7-14%\") (PDF p. 396).\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is \"re-adaptation at a lower level\": the body adapts down to the reduced load. They give no physiological detail and no data beyond the four citations.\n**When to apply** — Long competition periods (the model's is \"some 4-5 months,\" PDF p. 396) in which the athlete would otherwise only compete and do light maintenance: build in periodic increases in special and assistance volume. Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Also: \"In-season programming deliberately waves volume and intensity even when the practice schedule looks repetitive week to week, specifically to avoid training monotony for the athlete\" (line 181, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the authors' warning is against a *prolonged* volume cut in a long season; the baseline's in-season waving, and the bye-week \"speed and strength top-up\" (line 187, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q17)), are the kind of periodic volume increase the authors call for. A football season (late August to the postseason) is comparable in length to the model's 4-5 month competition period, but football is not an endurance sport and the authors do not extend the rule to team sports.\n**When NOT to apply** — The statement is made for long-duration endurance sports under monocyclic periodisation; the authors do not extend it to strength or team sports. It does not mean intensity can be dropped. In the explosive-strength model the second strength block deliberately carries less volume than the first (\"It is unnecessary at this time to further increase the volume of strength work,\" PDF p. 394 / printed p. 383), so the rule applies to special and competition loading in endurance sport, not to every quality in every sport.\n**Example** — The authors give no worked case. Inference: a marathoner in a five-month racing season returns every few weeks to a higher-volume week of race-pace and assistance work instead of only racing and easy running between races.\n**Related** — [[framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports]], [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--in-season-team-sport-mini-block-microcycle]]\nconfidence: supported\n\n########## heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work.md\n# Follow a High-Volume, Low-Intensity Concentrated Block With Moderate-Volume Explosive Work to Enlarge the Delayed Training Effect\n**What it is** — Under the heading \"More Advanced Use of Concentrated Loading\" (PDF p. 397 / printed p. 386, checked against the page image) the authors modify the separated-block models of 6.14: \"in speed-strength sports, the magnitude of the delayed training effect (LDTE) may be enhanced further if the concentrated loading is followed by specialised work of moderate volume, which includes higher power or more explosive muscle contraction regimes, such as jumps, plyometrics and maximal attempts.\" The loads overlap: \"There is an overlapping of the different training volumes, which peak smoothly in each case to ensure optimal increase in the LDTE\" (PDF p. 398 / printed p. 387). In the bi-cyclic version, \"stages A and C refer to concentrated periods of high volume, low intensity work and stages B and D to moderate volumes of specialised, higher intensity speed-strength work. This scheme has been recommended to ensure continuous increase in special strength over a long period (Verkhoshansky, 1978)\" (PDF p. 398).\nThe figures, read from the page images (no numeric axes):\n- **Fig 6.43** (PDF p. 398, monocyclic large mesocycle): a tall wave A in the preparatory phase, a smaller wave B starting as A falls and peaking at about the start of the competition phase. The speed-strength curve sags while A is high, then rises through B and keeps rising into the competition phase.\n- **Fig 6.44** (PDF p. 399 / printed p. 388, bi-cyclic): tall A, small B, tall C, small D in sequence. Speed-strength dips during A, rises during B, flattens or dips slightly during C, and rises again after it to its highest level at the right.\n- **Fig 6.45** (PDF p. 399, a large mesocycle for speed-strength sports): the text says \"the concentrated strength loading block occupies the first half of the large mesocycle, following the groundwork laid by general physical preparation (GPP) and the peak amount of aerobic work... Technique and speed training occupies the second half.\" The figure shows an aerobic-work curve peaking early, a large shaded wave over the first half, a second shaded wave over the second half labelled \"Technique and Speed,\" and a fitness-state curve that dips then rises to its highest point at the right.\n**Why it works** — The authors give a principle rather than a measured mechanism: overlapping loads that \"peak smoothly\" serve the LDTE, and \"the inclusion of regular passive and active restoration periods between stages of greater loading is important to facilitate development of the LDTE\" (PDF p. 398). They also argue for putting the bulk of strength work early: \"Since large volumes of strength and technique training generally are incompatible, the greatest quantity of strength work should be done during the preparatory period, with more emphasis being placed on technique work during the competitive phase when the LDTE rises markedly\" (PDF p. 399). No data are shown; the figures are schematics credited to Siff & Verkhoshansky (1999).\n**When to apply** — Speed-strength athletes coming out of a high-volume strength block: bring in moderate volumes of jumps, plyometrics and maximal attempts as the block tapers, rather than dropping straight to technique and speed alone. The authors say the boundaries are loose: \"stage B does not necessarily begin where shown, but may commence far earlier, while stage A may tail off right near the end of phase B\" (PDF p. 398; \"phase B\" is printed as such). The authors tie this to Matveyev's \"non-discreteness of the preparatory and competitive phases\": \"it is sometimes perfectly appropriate to have elements of each intermingled in various 'doses'\" (PDF p. 398). Eric's baseline: \"Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Inference: the A-then-B wave is one way to stage that shift within one off-season.\n**When NOT to apply** — The scheme is stated for speed-strength sports. It assumes the A stage is \"high volume, low intensity,\" consistent with the low-intensity condition for concentrated loading ([[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]]); an A stage built from heavy, intense lifting is a different situation the authors do not cover. **Unclear figure labels:** the caption of Fig 6.45 says \"A is the block of concentrated strength loading, while B and C refer to the types of training identified in the diagram,\" but in the figure the label \"GPP\" sits under wave A and \"Strength Load\" sits under wave B (PDF p. 399). Fig 6.46 has the same mismatch (PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389). The text's sequence for Fig 6.45 (the strength block comes \"following the groundwork laid by general physical preparation (GPP),\" PDF p. 399) matches the printed labels, GPP under the earlier wave and Strength Load under the later one. Inference: the caption is the likelier error. The page itself does not settle it. No dose for the B stage is given beyond \"moderate volume.\"\n**Example** — The authors give the schematics only. Inference: after a four-week block of high-volume, moderate-load squats, pulls and loaded jumps, a jumper adds two short weekly sessions of bounding, a few depth jumps and one or two near-maximal lifts, with that work peaking about when the first competitions begin.\n**Related** — [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]], [[principle--sequentially-introduce-means-of-greater-training-effect]], [[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]], [[heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks]]\nconfidence: supported\n\n########## framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models.md\n# Sports Modelling: a Descriptive Model of the Sport Feeds a Prescriptive Model of the Athlete's State and the Loading Programme\n**What it is** — The authors split the models needed to design programmes into two classes (PDF p. 390 / printed p. 379, checked against the page image):\n- **The descriptive model** \"furnishes qualitative details of the motor qualities and fitness factors needed for proficiency in a given sport, as well as desirable quantitative kinanthropometric, kinesiological, physiological and biomechanical details which characterise movement at all stages of that sport.\" Examples of its contents: \"movement patterns, velocities and accelerations at different joint angles or stages of an event, reaction time, 'lactate threshold' (OBLA), relative muscle strength, body proportions, agonist/antagonist muscle strength ratios and timing of segments of important movements.\"\n- **The prescriptive model** \"represents a simplified analogue of the actual training process, omitting some details, but preserving the essential information concerning its composition and structure. It describes the dynamics of the athlete's fitness state and the systems of organising annual training.\" Its core is a model of the athlete's state that \"diagramatically expresses the optimal changes in the most important indicators of special work capacity over time\" and \"determines the specific periods when one should plan to achieve peak levels.\" It \"draws strongly on the data furnished by the descriptive model.\"\n- Each can be **qualitative** (the dynamics of the functional indicators, the main training contents and how components connect) or **quantitative** (\"all numerical aspects of the training... such as calculation of volume, intensity, duration of stages and loading distribution in different intensity zones\").\nConventions for the 6.14 annual models (PDF pp. 390-391 / printed pp. 379-380, checked against the page images): months are not named because summer and winter sports are covered; \"Each model consists of about 11 months, since, on average, one month is allotted for the final transition period\"; each model pairs a fitness-state diagram with a loading diagram; \"One can use any method that enables one to measure the values of these indicators\"; \"All models in this chapter use the principle of concentrated special strength loading, which elicits the LDTE\"; the rectangular blocks are schematic, since \"the actual loading follows a concentrated wave pattern which gradually reaches a peak, then subsides in volume\"; and stage boundaries \"can vary somewhat\" with the real calendar.\nOn measurement: \"It is of little value to identify which energy systems, types of fitness or movement patterns are involved in a sport unless accurate numerical determinations are regularly made of each of these factors. Even then, we have to remember that the body is not simply a machine in which input and output are deterministically related, but an organism whose behaviour displays non-linearity and fairly random deviations... Nevertheless, without this information, long-term programming becomes imprecise\" (PDF p. 391, checked against the page image). The descriptive-modelling passage names the \"Multifactorial Fitness Profile (MFP) discussed later... (Ch 7)\" and says \"Many years of Russian research have produced models for every type of sport\" (PDF pp. 390-391). Inference: the passage reads as Siff's framing of the Russian material.\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is control: modelling \"makes it possible to manage the organisational complexity of sports activities and devise a practical scheme for describing and controlling the training process\" (PDF p. 390), and norms \"enable the coach to identify any weaknesses in the state of the athlete and thereby modify the training programme\" (PDF p. 391).\n**When to apply** — Before writing an annual plan: build or borrow a descriptive model of what the sport demands, with numbers, then draw the planned course of the key indicators across the year and the loading that should produce it. Eric's baseline: \"If it doesn't answer a question anyone has, or a direct need, then it doesn't have a place\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 32, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q3)). Inference: the descriptive model is one way to state those questions in advance, so each test in the battery is tied to a factor the model says matters.\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors themselves warn that the body is non-linear and deviates \"fairly random[ly]\" from quantitative prediction, so a model is a guide, not a forecast. Their example norms (Tables 6.6-6.12) come from Soviet track-and-field and weightlifting populations of the 1970s and 1980s and are \"not... all-embracing models\" (PDF p. 391; the sentence is printed with a double negative, \"None of these examples should not be regarded as all-embracing models\"). No football descriptive model is given.\n**Example** — The authors' own examples are the model-characteristic tables, including weightlifters' muscle and fat proportions and PWC170 by weight class (Table 6.6, Stepanova et al, 1983, PDF p. 391) and 100 m sprinter models ([[case-study--tabachnik-100m-sprinter-model-characteristics]]). The other tables on PDF pp. 392-393 / printed pp. 381-382, read from the page images:\n- **Middle distance (Table 6.7, Classes III to I; Table 6.10, CMS and MS 800 m and 1500 m runners; Maximenko, 1979).** Flexibility counts as a model characteristic: \"Stride splits (degrees)\" and \"Straddle splits (degrees)\" (for example, stride splits of 111.5, 116.8 and 131.5 degrees for Classes III, II and I). Special endurance has a defined test, \"2 series of 4 x 400m, with 2 mins rest between each 400m and 8-10 mins rest between each series\" (Table 6.7 caption). The strength row is printed \"Relative mean strength of 5 muscle groups (kg),\" while the caption describes \"the mean of the strength of the extensors and flexors of the hip, knee and ankle joints relative to bodymass\"; the unit and the muscle-group count are recorded as printed.\n- **Javelin (Table 6.11, Konstantinov, 1978), international-class throwers, men / women.** Snatch \"Bmass + 15kg\" / \"Bmass+10kg\"; back squat \"2 x Bmass\" / \"1.5 x Bmass\"; standing broad jump 3.20 / 2.80 m; standing triple jump 10.20 / 7.80 m; vertical jump 95.0 / 70.0 cm; 30 m from a running start 2.90 / 3.20 s; throws of assistance objects or the javelin \"with average intensity\" 170 / 140 times and javelin throws \"with above average intensity\" 35 / 30 times (the table gives no time period for these counts). Inference: this is the chapter's closest thing to a strength and power norm table for a power athlete.\n- **Juvenile sprinters (Table 6.12, Alabin & Yushkevitch, 1978), ages 11-16.** The row \"Standing quintuple jump (cm)\" prints 9.25 at age 11 rising to 13.70 at age 16. Inference: the values must be metres; a quintuple jump of 9-14 cm is impossible, and the standing long jump row of the same table runs 179.8-261.2 cm.\n**Related** — [[case-study--tabachnik-100m-sprinter-model-characteristics]], [[framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--model-characteristic-tables-exist-by-qualification]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--ten-step-process-for-building-an-annual-plan]]\nconfidence: supported","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"6f654c77-7bce-4b10-97b5-8b0cc4e82718","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:12:02.142Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"########## claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity.md\n# In a Long Monocyclic Competition Period, a Prolonged Cut in Loading Volume Lowers Fitness; Competition Plus Small Special Loads Cannot Maintain It\n**What it is** — In the long-duration endurance model (PDF p. 396 / printed p. 385, checked against the page image): \"It has been established that the prolonged reduction in the volume of loading during the competition period (with monocyclic periodisation) leads to re-adaptation at a lower level, which is reflected in the drop of non-specific trainability. Competition and special supplementary loading (with limited volumes) cannot stimulate further development or even maintain special work-capacity. Thus, it is appropriate to periodically increase the volume of competition and special assistance loading (Baranovsky, 1969; Kryazh, 1969; Muzis, 1970; Zhikharevich, 1976).\" The paired condition from Gilyazova (1978) is that stable results need \"not a sharp drop in the volume of loading during the competitive period\" and a gradual preparatory increase (\"a mean monthly increase of 7-14%\") (PDF p. 396).\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is \"re-adaptation at a lower level\": the body adapts down to the reduced load. They give no physiological detail and no data beyond the four citations.\n**When to apply** — Long competition periods (the model's is \"some 4-5 months,\" PDF p. 396) in which the athlete would otherwise only compete and do light maintenance: build in periodic increases in special and assistance volume. Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Also: \"In-season programming deliberately waves volume and intensity even when the practice schedule looks repetitive week to week, specifically to avoid training monotony for the athlete\" (line 181, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the authors' warning is against a *prolonged* volume cut in a long season; the baseline's in-season waving, and the bye-week \"speed and strength top-up\" (line 187, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q17)), are the kind of periodic volume increase the authors call for. A football season (late August to the postseason) is comparable in length to the model's 4-5 month competition period, but football is not an endurance sport and the authors do not extend the rule to team sports.\n**When NOT to apply** — The statement is made for long-duration endurance sports under monocyclic periodisation; the authors do not extend it to strength or team sports. It does not mean intensity can be dropped. In the explosive-strength model the second strength block deliberately carries less volume than the first (\"It is unnecessary at this time to further increase the volume of strength work,\" PDF p. 394 / printed p. 383), so the rule applies to special and competition loading in endurance sport, not to every quality in every sport.\n**Example** — The authors give no worked case. Inference: a marathoner in a five-month racing season returns every few weeks to a higher-volume week of race-pace and assistance work instead of only racing and easy running between races.\n**Related** — [[framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports]], [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--in-season-team-sport-mini-block-microcycle]]\nconfidence: supported\n\n########## heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work.md\n# Follow a High-Volume, Low-Intensity Concentrated Block With Moderate-Volume Explosive Work to Enlarge the Delayed Training Effect\n**What it is** — Under the heading \"More Advanced Use of Concentrated Loading\" (PDF p. 397 / printed p. 386, checked against the page image) the authors modify the separated-block models of 6.14: \"in speed-strength sports, the magnitude of the delayed training effect (LDTE) may be enhanced further if the concentrated loading is followed by specialised work of moderate volume, which includes higher power or more explosive muscle contraction regimes, such as jumps, plyometrics and maximal attempts.\" The loads overlap: \"There is an overlapping of the different training volumes, which peak smoothly in each case to ensure optimal increase in the LDTE\" (PDF p. 398 / printed p. 387). In the bi-cyclic version, \"stages A and C refer to concentrated periods of high volume, low intensity work and stages B and D to moderate volumes of specialised, higher intensity speed-strength work. This scheme has been recommended to ensure continuous increase in special strength over a long period (Verkhoshansky, 1978)\" (PDF p. 398).\nThe figures, read from the page images (no numeric axes):\n- **Fig 6.43** (PDF p. 398, monocyclic large mesocycle): a tall wave A in the preparatory phase, a smaller wave B starting as A falls and peaking at about the start of the competition phase. The speed-strength curve sags while A is high, then rises through B and keeps rising into the competition phase.\n- **Fig 6.44** (PDF p. 399 / printed p. 388, bi-cyclic): tall A, small B, tall C, small D in sequence. Speed-strength dips during A, rises during B, flattens or dips slightly during C, and rises again after it to its highest level at the right.\n- **Fig 6.45** (PDF p. 399, a large mesocycle for speed-strength sports): the text says \"the concentrated strength loading block occupies the first half of the large mesocycle, following the groundwork laid by general physical preparation (GPP) and the peak amount of aerobic work... Technique and speed training occupies the second half.\" The figure shows an aerobic-work curve peaking early, a large shaded wave over the first half, a second shaded wave over the second half labelled \"Technique and Speed,\" and a fitness-state curve that dips then rises to its highest