h-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound.md heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates.md framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training.md; do printf '\\n########## %s\\n' $f; sed 1,12d $f | grep -v '^$'; grep confidence $f; done","description":"Read four GO entries for spot check"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffqMa6VeJ3e5TzHNzb9a","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"d51b10bb-e6ce-45e7-a711-74ed706d0d40","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:12:16.177Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"d51b10bb-e6ce-45e7-a711-74ed706d0d40","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"832c731c-797b-4631-bbba-f264eca1936c","agentId":"ac7100d52d01adab3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01P8Y8gSPyPoBrzCCyBmA5Kc","type":"tool_result","content":"########## claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving.md\n# Concentrated Strength Loading: a Substitutable Block of Up to 2 Months, Claimed Best for Highly Qualified Athletes and to Cut Annual Strength Volume by 13-15%\n**What it is** — Chapter 6 restates the concentrated method and adds claims not in the Chapter 5 entry on its conditions (PDF pp. 381-382 / printed pp. 370-371, both checked against the page images):\n1. **Duration.** \"The main novelty of this method consists of exerting a concentrated training influence on the body with a high volume of unidirectional loading for a limited period (of up to 2 months)\" (PDF p. 381, citing Verkhoshansky, 1977). Earlier on the same page: \"the primary use of unidirectional training for prolonged stages (4-12 weeks) of training can be very effective.\" The two figures sit a paragraph apart; 12 weeks is longer than 2 months, and the book does not reconcile them (the 4-12 weeks refers to \"unidirectional training,\" the 2 months to \"concentrated\" loading).\n2. **The strength block as a planning unit.** \"Concentrated strength loading constitutes a relatively independent part or block of training to create a functional foundation for the subsequent enhancement of technical skill or those motor abilities which chiefly determine success in a given sport.\" The block's means must fit \"the training specifics of that type of sport,\" and \"The strength block should have a specific place in the annual cycle.\" \"To achieve the principal aims of organising loading in the annual cycle, the coach must select a certain strength block or substitute one block for another, depending on the situation and the individual. Obviously, in order to do this, he needs to prepare a detailed selection of appropriate strength blocks in advance\" (PDF p. 381). Concentrated strength loading matters \"not only for raising the athlete's physical fitness, but also for regulating the entire complex of loading over the annual cycle in all sports.\"\n3. **Evidence claimed.** \"The effectiveness of concentrated strength loading has been confirmed by many coaches and special research in a number of speed-strength, cyclic and team sports (Varakin, 1979; Zhdanovich, 1980; Rudokene, 1981; Naraliev, 1981; Ptushkin, 1981; Verkhoshanskaya, 1982; Kabachkova et al, 1982). In particular, it has been established that this system is most appropriate for highly qualified athletes. Moreover, the use of concentrated strength loading enables the general annual volume of strength loading to be reduced (by 13-15%), when compared with the conventional approach (Mironenko, 1981; Antonova, 1982)\" (PDF p. 382).\n4. **Advanced stages.** \"In more advanced stages, the concentrated unidirectional method of loading is the most effective way of further increasing the high level of physical fitness which athletes achieve with multi-year training,\" provided one finds \"regimes of concentrated loading which definitely do not produce negative training effects (Verkhoshansky, 1977)\" (PDF p. 382).\n**Two volume-saving figures.** Chapter 5 says \"the training volume over a macrocycle can be reduced by as much as 20%\" with the conjugate sequence system for advanced athletes (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294; see [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]]). Chapter 6 says the \"general annual volume of strength loading\" falls by 13-15%. These measure different things (all training volume over a macrocycle versus annual strength volume), so they are recorded side by side, not as a contradiction.\n**Why it works** — The authors give a mechanism at PDF p. 381: the concentrated influence \"produces a brief, fairly stable state of incomplete restoration which is associated with the persistent and relatively prolonged disturbance of homeostasis. This triggers extensive functional changes which are prerequisites for subsequent supercompensation and the raising of the body to a higher level of special preparedness.\" The cited studies are named without design, sample size or effect size.\n**When to apply** — Building an annual plan for qualified athletes around one or two strength blocks, each designed for the sport and kept ready to swap in or out. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract: lay out the competition calendar, key non-training dates (holidays, exam weeks), and phase-by-phase volume/intensity waves, then adapt the route as circumstances change while still reaching the same destination\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 183, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the authors' \"detailed selection of appropriate strength blocks in advance\" is a concrete way to make that roadmap adaptable.\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested by Issurin** for team sports. The book says concentrated strength loading was confirmed in \"team sports\" (PDF p. 382); the library's brief of Issurin 2016 records that concentrated-unidirectional programs in combat and team sports \"improved some fitness measures, but the reviewed studies did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance,\" and that Issurin judged the evidence \"insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\" (brief, p. 6). See [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]. The method is stated as most appropriate for highly qualified athletes, not novices. It has costs, set out on the same page: a \"brief but consistent decrease in speed-strength\" ([[case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities]]).\n**Example** — The authors' sports examples are general (speed-strength, cyclic and team sports, PDF p. 382). Inference: a strength coach keeps three prepared strength blocks for a jumper (a general-strength block, a speed-strength block, a reactive block) and chooses which to run in the second preparation phase according to how the first block went.\n**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation]], [[claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]], [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]\nconfidence: contested\n\n########## framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound.md\n# Two Ways to Build a Large Mesocycle: Variant A Rises Smoothly to a Plateau; Variant B Concentrates Load First, Dips, Then Rebounds Higher\n**What it is** — In 6.12.3 (PDF pp. 385-387 / printed pp. 374-376, all checked against the page images) the authors compare two forms of mesocycle organisation (Fig 6.33, PDF p. 386).\n**A terminology warning first.** \"In this chapter, *the term 'mesocycle' is reserved for what in Russian is called 'the large stage' or large mesocycle*, which extends over several months to produce a specific adaptive effect... The large mesocycle, in fact, may comprise a few conventional mesocycles and also be referred to as a phase\" (italics in the original; PDF p. 385). Its duration \"can vary (usually 3-5 months), since its calculation depends on the competition calendar and especially on the dates of the most important competitions\" (PDF p. 387).\n**Common practice, as the authors report it.** \"The literature reveals that three and four week stages are the most frequently used, where the last one week serves as a restoration week. There are combinations of two weeks with heavy loading and two weeks with moderate loading, or an alternation of weeks with high and moderate loading... all of them, depending on circumstances, can produce good results. It is very difficult to establish which of these is the most effective, since the criteria are extremely diverse\" (PDF p. 385). Research has concentrated on the annual cycle and the microcycle, plus the \"pre-competition training stage lasting 4-6 weeks\" (PDF p. 385).\n**The two variants.**\n- **Variant A** \"displays a smooth increase in the functional indicators until it reaches a distinct plateau\" and \"is a traditional form of training organisation\" (PDF p. 385). In Fig 6.33(b) its volume curve rises to a broad peak mid-cycle and falls, GPP peaks early and declines, and the fitness state rises steadily.\n- **Variant B** \"begins with a steady decline which is followed by a sharp increase in the functional indicators up to a level which significantly exceeds the maximum of the first variant\" (PDF p. 385). It has two micro-stages: a first one of concentrated special physical preparation that \"produces a steady decrease in the functional indicators,\" then lower-volume specialised and competition loading \"designed primarily to improve technique or speed so as to stimulate supercompensation.\" \"The volume increases and decreases more steeply than in the traditional scheme, while there is a slight rise in the intensity of loading... one can achieve the necessary volume of work only with a relatively low intensity of loading\" (PDF p. 385). In Fig 6.33(c) volume rises steeply and peaks early, the fitness state stays low then climbs above it, and the shaded GPP area grows across the whole cycle.\n**Who uses which.** \"Variant A is appropriate for athletes of medium qualification, as well as for qualified athletes needing to devote much time to perfecting technical skill. Variant B can be used best by qualified athletes who possess a high special physical work-capacity, can endure voluminous loading and display excellent sports technique\" (PDF p. 386).\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation for Variant B: the concentrated special loading \"cause[s] extensive adaptation,\" and the lower-volume specialised stage that follows stimulates \"supercompensation of energy resources and increase[s] special work-capacity. The stabilisation of the body at a new functional level signifies an increase in adaptive reserves\" (PDF p. 385). Intensity is held low because \"the concentrated volume of intense loading can seriously overstrain the body and disrupt adaptation.\" They also claim Variant B avoids the falling results that come from \"exhausting the motor potential\": \"variant B leads to an increase in the athlete's special work capacity and achievements during the competition phase\" (PDF p. 386). Fig 6.33 is a schematic (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999) with no data.\n**When to apply** — Choosing the shape of a several-month training phase. Variant B for qualified athletes with high work capacity and sound technique who can tolerate a planned dip; Variant A for medium-level athletes and those who still need a lot of technical work. Eric's baseline: \"Undulating/non-linear periodization (Parker/Miller/Panariello), waving volume week to week (e.g., medium-high, medium, high, low over four weeks) is stated as the personally preferred model over strict linear progression\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 106, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: Eric's four-week waves are the \"three and four week stages\" the authors call common practice; the authors' A and B describe the longer phase those waves sit inside, so the two do not conflict.\n**When NOT to apply** — Not for athletes who cannot yet tolerate voluminous loading or whose technique is still unstable (the authors' own limit, PDF p. 386). \"Mesocycle\" here means a phase of 3-5 months, not the looser usage the authors note, \"a period of several weeks or months consisting of a certain number of microcycles\" (PDF p. 385), so the rules do not transfer to a four-week block without translation. The authors give no study for the claim that B exceeds A; the comparison rests on their adaptation theory. Both variants sit in a chapter whose models all use concentrated loading, which Issurin 2016 questions for team sports (see [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]).\n**Example** — The authors give Fig 6.33 only. Inference: a qualified jumper's 16-week autumn phase under Variant B spends the first six or seven weeks on high-volume, moderate-intensity strength and jump work with performance tests dropping, then shifts to lower-volume technique and speed work, with test results expected to climb above their start by the end.\n**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]], [[heuristic--use-low-intensity-gpp-as-restoration-in-concentrated-loading-models]], [[heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks]], [[periodization--bompa/framework--step-loading-paradigm]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-transmutation-realization-mesocycles]]\nconfidence: supported\n\n########## heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates.md\n# Set Mesocycle Boundaries Only by the Main Competition Dates, Treat the First Mesocycle as the Foundation for the Second, and Recuperate After Each Competitive Stage\n**What it is** — The authors' four practical principles for when the calendar and adaptation pull against each other (PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389, checked against the page image). The conflict: \"the dates of the main competitions may change radically, while the dates of traditional competitions remain the same. A worse situation arises if the competition season is extended and the length of the preparatory stages is shortened.\"\n1. \"Organisation of training should provide favourable conditions for promoting functional adaptation of the body within the constraints of the competition calendar. The calendar should be co-ordinated with the periods and duration of the mesocycles.\"\n2. \"The optimal period for enhancing the current adaptation reserves is about 20 weeks for the most efficient loading of highly qualified athletes.\" It \"can be increased or decreased over a small range, which requires an appropriate increase or decrease in the concentration of loading. It is important in this case not to exceed the optimal time limit, since excessive intensification of training can disrupt adaptation.\"\n3. \"To determine the boundaries of the mesocycles, one should be guided only by the dates of the main competitions. This rule should not be broken for any reason, including the desire to demonstrate impressive results at the beginning of the season.\"\n4. \"The loading of the preparatory phase is more specialised and intense during the second mesocycle than the first. Therefore, the first mesocycle should always be considered as the foundation for the second. The interests of intermediate competitions should not alter this objective.\"\nThen, in italics in the original: \"*one should be aware of the detrimental effect that competitive loading has on the athlete's state*, since it leads to serious exhaustion of nervous energy. Therefore, during the transition to the next mesocycle, it is necessary to include a recuperation period, the duration of which is determined individually, depending on the difficulty of the competitive stage\" (PDF p. 400).\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason for rules 2 and 3 is the \"current adaptation reserves\": there is an optimal time over which they can be raised, and stretching or squeezing it changes how concentrated the loading must be. They give no study for the 20-week figure on this page (\"It has already been mentioned,\" referring back to earlier sections outside this batch). The recuperation rule rests on the \"serious exhaustion of nervous energy\" from competition, without data.\n**When to apply** — Drawing an annual plan: place the main competitions first, cut the year into mesocycles from those dates, and do not move the boundaries to chase early-season results or to serve minor meets. Eric's baseline: \"In football the target is a peak every Saturday, with November and the postseason as the priority window\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 185, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)). Inference: under rule 3, November and the postseason would set the boundary of the final mesocycle, and early-season games would be treated as the authors' \"intermediate competitions,\" which \"should not alter this objective.\"\n**When NOT to apply** — Rule 3's \"should not be broken for any reason\" is the authors' strongest wording in the section, but it assumes one or two main competitions; in a sport with a weekly game, every game counts, and the rule does not say how to weight them. The 20-week figure is for \"highly qualified athletes.\" The recuperation period has no stated length. Issurin's competing structure uses stages of \"about two months\" ([[block-periodization--issurin/framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle]]); see [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]].\n**Example** — The authors' own example of the problem is a season that is \"extended\" while \"the length of the preparatory stages is shortened\" (PDF p. 400). Inference: a jumper whose national championship moves three weeks earlier shortens the second mesocycle by concentrating its loading more, rather than shifting the boundary to keep a full block before an early-season invitational.\n**Related** — [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]], [[framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports]], [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--nine-step-annual-program-construction-sequence]]\nconfidence: supported\n\n########## framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training.md\n# Verkhoshansky's Eight-Step Sequence for Programming the Annual Cycle, From Target Results Down to the Microcycles\n**What it is** — The authors recommend \"the following sequence of logical operations, where each calculation is made by taking into account the calculation made in the preceding operation (Verkhoshansky, 1985)\" (PDF p. 401 / printed p. 390, checked against the page image). The eight steps, with their key wording:\n1. **Determine the progress in results and the dates.** \"The calculations rely on a predictive model of the dynamics of the sport results relative to the competition calendar,\" based on \"a multi-faceted objective assessment of the athlete's potential\" and what is \"realistically feasible in the current year at his level of mastery.\"\n2. **Determine the needed changes in special physical preparedness and technical-tactical proficiency,** expressed as \"specific goals, relative to the functional indicators and the characteristics of technical proficiency which must be achieved in the important competitions.\" \"Subjective ratings of perceived effort (RPE) can be very useful in offering further guidance.\"\n3. **Build quantitative models of the fitness state across the year** \"so that these indicators peak in the main competitions.\"\n4. **Select the training means and methods** by \"assessing the training potential of the means and methods.\"\n5. **Calculate the general volume of loading for all means,** based on \"the accumulated effect of the loading in all preceding stages.\"\n6. **Divide the year into mesocycles,** using \"the competition calendar and the dates necessary for optimal increase of the body's current adaptation reserves.\"\n7. **Calculate the distribution of loading over the year,** expressed as \"the quantitative dynamics of the loading imposed by the fundamental training means.\"\n8. **Organise loading over the mesocycle,** as \"a detailed training programme giving the specific distribution of loading during each of the microcycles,\" based on \"the characteristics of the LDTE of concentrated strength loading.\" \"Provision should always be made available for introducing contingency changes in programmes, necessitated by unforeseen incidents such as injuries, changes in competitive timetables and personal disasters.\"\nThe authors frame the difficulty first: \"Its complexity, however, lies not so much in the quantity of calculations as in the insufficient basis for choosing and quantitatively determining the main operations. Therefore in practice, even experienced coaches need to make tentative computations which may have a low probability of producing a correct prognosis\" (PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389, checked against the page image).\n**Why it works** — The sequence is a top-down dependency chain: each step uses the one before. The authors give no evidence that following it improves results; it is a planning procedure.\n**When to apply** — Building or auditing an annual plan. Compare it with two other procedures in the library: Issurin's nine steps start from the competition calendar and end with obligatory correction after review ([[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--nine-step-annual-program-construction-sequence]]); Evans's ten steps start with a sport and position profile ([[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--ten-step-process-for-building-an-annual-plan]]). Verkhoshansky's starts from the predicted result and the fitness changes needed to reach it, and only divides the year into mesocycles at step 6. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 183, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: step 8's contingency provision is the authors' version of that flexibility.\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors concede the operations rest on an \"insufficient basis\" and may have \"a low probability of producing a correct prognosis\" (PDF p. 400). Step 1 assumes a predictive model of performance exists; in team sports there is no single result to predict. The procedure is written for individual athletes and their competitions.\n**Example** — The authors give no worked plan in 6.15. Inference: for a triple jumper, step 1 sets a target of a 40 cm improvement at the national championship in month 10, step 2 converts it to target values for the standing triple jump and approach speed, and steps 6-8 then divide the year into two mesocycles and write the weekly loading.\n**Related** — [[framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models]], [[heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates]], [[framework--four-documents-for-programming-and-controlling-training]], [[principle--programme-objective-approach-quantified-goals-dictate-training]], [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--nine-step-annual-program-construction-sequence]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--ten-step-process-for-building-an-annual-plan]]\nconfidence: supported","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"d93ec437-6bd3-42d6-97da-0cc3ca17bbb3","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:12:16.409Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"########## claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving.md\n# Concentrated Strength Loading: a Substitutable Block of Up to 2 Months, Claimed Best for Highly Qualified Athletes and to Cut Annual Strength Volume by 13-15%\n**What it is** — Chapter 6 restates the concentrated method and adds claims not in the Chapter 5 entry on its conditions (PDF pp. 381-382 / printed pp. 370-371, both checked against the page images):\n1. **Duration.** \"The main novelty of this method consists of exerting a concentrated training influence on the body with a high volume of unidirectional loading for a limited period (of up to 2 months)\" (PDF p. 381, citing Verkhoshansky, 1977). Earlier on the same page: \"the primary use of unidirectional training for prolonged stages (4-12 weeks) of training can be very effective.\" The two figures sit a paragraph apart; 12 weeks is longer than 2 months, and the book does not reconcile them (the 4-12 weeks refers to \"unidirectional training,\" the 2 months to \"concentrated\" loading).\n2. **The strength block as a planning unit.** \"Concentrated strength loading constitutes a relatively independent part or block of training to create a functional foundation for the subsequent enhancement of technical skill or those motor abilities which chiefly determine success in a given sport.\" The block's means must fit \"the training specifics of that type of sport,\" and \"The strength block should have a specific place in the annual cycle.\" \"To achieve the principal aims of organising loading in the annual cycle, the coach must select a certain strength block or substitute one block for another, depending on the situation and the individual. Obviously, in order to do this, he needs to prepare a detailed selection of appropriate strength blocks in advance\" (PDF p. 381). Concentrated strength loading matters \"not only for raising the athlete's physical fitness, but also for regulating the entire complex of loading over the annual cycle in all sports.\"\n3. **Evidence claimed.** \"The effectiveness of concentrated strength loading has been confirmed by many coaches and special research in a number of speed-strength, cyclic and team sports (Varakin, 1979; Zhdanovich, 1980; Rudokene, 1981; Naraliev, 1981; Ptushkin, 1981; Verkhoshanskaya, 1982; Kabachkova et al, 1982). In particular, it has been established that this system is most appropriate for highly qualified athletes. Moreover, the use of concentrated strength loading enables the general annual volume of strength loading to be reduced (by 13-15%), when compared with the conventional approach (Mironenko, 1981; Antonova, 1982)\" (PDF p. 382).\n4. **Advanced stages.** \"In more advanced stages, the concentrated unidirectional method of loading is the most effective way of further increasing the high level of physical fitness which athletes achieve with multi-year training,\" provided one finds \"regimes of concentrated loading which definitely do not produce negative training effects (Verkhoshansky, 1977)\" (PDF p. 382).\n**Two volume-saving figures.** Chapter 5 says \"the training volume over a macrocycle can be reduced by as much as 20%\" with the conjugate sequence system for advanced athletes (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294; see [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]]). Chapter 6 says the \"general annual volume of strength loading\" falls by 13-15%. These measure different things (all training volume over a macrocycle versus annual strength volume), so they are recorded side by side, not as a contradiction.\n**Why it works** — The authors give a mechanism at PDF p. 381: the concentrated influence \"produces a brief, fairly stable state of incomplete restoration which is associated with the persistent and relatively prolonged disturbance of homeostasis. This triggers extensive functional changes which are prerequisites for subsequent supercompensation and the raising of the body to a higher level of special preparedness.\" The cited studies are named without design, sample size or effect size.\n**When to apply** — Building an annual plan for qualified athletes around one or two strength blocks, each designed for the sport and kept ready to swap in or out. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract: lay out the competition calendar, key non-training dates (holidays, exam weeks), and phase-by-phase volume/intensity waves, then adapt the route as circumstances change while still reaching the same destination\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 183, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the authors' \"detailed selection of appropriate strength blocks in advance\" is a concrete way to make that roadmap adaptable.\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested by Issurin** for team sports. The book says concentrated strength loading was confirmed in \"team sports\" (PDF p. 382); the library's brief of Issurin 2016 records that concentrated-unidirectional programs in combat and team sports \"improved some fitness measures, but the reviewed studies did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance,\" and that Issurin judged the evidence \"insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\" (brief, p. 6). See [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]. The method is stated as most appropriate for highly qualified athletes, not novices. It has costs, set out on the same page: a \"brief but consistent decrease in speed-strength\" ([[case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities]]).\n**Example** — The authors' sports examples are general (speed-strength, cyclic and team sports, PDF p. 382). Inference: a strength coach keeps three prepared strength blocks for a jumper (a general-strength block, a speed-strength block, a reactive block) and chooses which to run in the second preparation phase according to how the first block went.\n**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation]], [[claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]], [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]\nconfidence: contested\n\n########## framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound.md\n# Two Ways to Build a Large Mesocycle: Variant A Rises Smoothly to a Plateau; Variant B Concentrates Load First, Dips, Then Rebounds Higher\n**What it is** — In 6.12.3 (PDF pp. 385-387 / printed pp. 374-376, all checked against the page images) the authors compare two forms of mesocycle organisation (Fig 6.33, PDF p. 386).\n**A terminology warning first.** \"In this chapter, *the term 'mesocycle' is reserved for what in Russian is called 'the large stage' or large mesocycle*, which extends over several months to produce a specific adaptive effect... The large mesocycle, in fact, may comprise a few conventional mesocycles and also be referred to as a phase\" (italics in the original; PDF p. 385). Its duration \"can vary (usually 3-5 months), since its calculation depends on the competition calendar and especially on the dates of the most important competitions\" (PDF p. 387).\n**Common practice, as the authors report it.** \"The literature reveals that three and four week stages are the most frequently used, where the last one week serves as a restoration week. There are combinations of two weeks with heavy loading and two weeks with moderate loading, or an alternation of weeks with high and moderate loading... all of them, depending on circumstances, can produce good results. It is very difficult to establish which of these is the most effective, since the criteria are extremely diverse\" (PDF p. 385). Research has concentrated on the annual cycle and the microcycle, plus the \"pre-competition training stage lasting 4-6 weeks\" (PDF p. 385).\n**The two variants.**\n- **Variant A** \"displays a smooth increase in the functional indicators until it reaches a distinct plateau\" and \"is a traditional form of training organisation\" (PDF p. 385). In Fig 6.33(b) its volume curve rises to a broad peak mid-cycle and falls, GPP peaks early and declines, and the fitness state rises steadily.\n- **Variant B** \"begins with a steady decline which is followed by a sharp increase in the functional indicators up to a level which significantly exceeds the maximum of the first variant\" (PDF p. 385). It has two micro-stages: a first one of concentrated special physical preparation that \"produces a steady decrease in the functional indicators,\" then lower-volume specialised and competition loading \"designed primarily to improve technique or speed so as to stimulate supercompensation.\" \"The volume increases and decreases more steeply than in the traditional scheme, while there is a slight rise in the intensity of loading... one can achieve the necessary volume of work only with a relatively low intensity of loading\" (PDF p. 385). In Fig 6.33(c) volume rises steeply and peaks early, the fitness state stays low then climbs above it, and the shaded GPP area grows across the whole cycle.\n**Who uses which.** \"Variant A is appropriate for athletes of medium qualification, as well as for qualified athletes needing to devote much time to perfecting technical skill. Variant B can be used best by qualified athletes who possess a high special physical work-capacity, can endure voluminous loading and display excellent sports technique\" (PDF p. 386).\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation for Variant B: the concentrated special loading \"cause[s] extensive adaptation,\" and the lower-volume specialised stage that follows stimulates \"supercompensation of energy resources and increase[s] special work-capacity. The stabilisation of the body at a new functional level signifies an increase in adaptive reserves\" (PDF p. 385). Intensity is held low because \"the concentrated volume of intense loading can seriously overstrain the body and disrupt adaptation.\" They also claim Variant B avoids the falling results that come from \"exhausting the motor potential\": \"variant B leads to an increase in the athlete's special work capacity and achievements during the competition phase\" (PDF p. 386). Fig 6.33 is a schematic (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999) with no data.\n**When to apply** — Choosing the shape of a several-month training phase. Variant B for qualified athletes with high work capacity and sound technique who can tolerate a planned dip; Variant A for medium-level athletes and those who still need a lot of technical work. Eric's baseline: \"Undulating/non-linear periodization (Parker/Miller/Panariello), waving volume week to week (e.g., medium-high, medium, high, low over four weeks) is stated as the personally preferred model over strict linear progression\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 106, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: Eric's four-week waves are the \"three and four week stages\" the authors call common practice; the authors' A and B describe the longer phase those waves sit inside, so the two do not conflict.\n**When NOT to apply** — Not for athletes who cannot yet tolerate voluminous loading or whose technique is still unstable (the authors' own limit, PDF p. 386). \"Mesocycle\" here means a phase of 3-5 months, not the looser usage the authors note, \"a period of several weeks or months consisting of a certain number of microcycles\" (PDF p. 385), so the rules do not transfer to a four-week block without translation. The authors give no study for the claim that B exceeds A; the comparison rests on their adaptation theory. Both variants sit in a chapter whose models all use concentrated loading, which Issurin 2016 questions for team sports (see [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]).\n**Example** — The authors give Fig 6.33 only. Inference: a qualified jumper's 16-week autumn phase under Variant B spends the first six or seven weeks on high-volume, moderate-intensity strength and jump work with performance tests dropping, then shifts to lower-volume technique and speed work, with test results expected to climb above their start by the end.\n**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]], [[heuristic--use-low-intensity-gpp-as-restoration-in-concentrated-loading-models]], [[heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks]], [[periodization--bompa/framework--step-loading-paradigm]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-transmutation-realization-mesocycles]]\nconfidence: supported\n\n########## heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates.md\n# Set Mesocycle Boundaries Only by the Main Competition Dates, Treat the First Mesocycle as the Foundation for the Second, and Recuperate After Each Competitive Stage\n**What it is** — The authors' four practical principles for when the calendar and adaptation pull against each other (PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389, checked against the page image). The conflict: \"the dates of the main competitions may change radically, while the dates of traditional competitions remain the same. A worse situation arises if the competition season is extended and the length of the preparatory stages is shortened.\"\n1. \"Organisation of training should provide favourable conditions for promoting functional adaptation of the body within the constraints of the competition calendar. The calendar should be co-ordinated with the periods and duration of the mesocycles.\"\n2. \"The optimal period for enhancing the current adaptation reserves is about 20 weeks for the most efficient loading of highly qualified athletes.\" It \"can be increased or decreased over a small range, which requires an appropriate increase or decrease in the concentration of loading. It is important in this case not to exceed the optimal time limit, since excessive intensification of training can disrupt adaptation.\"\n3. \"To determine the boundaries of the mesocycles, one should be guided only by the dates of the main competitions. This rule should not be broken for any reason, including the desire to demonstrate impressive results at the beginning of the season.\"\n4. \"The loading of the preparatory phase is more specialised and intense during the second mesocycle than the first. Therefore, the first mesocycle should always be considered as the foundation for the second. The interests of intermediate competitions should not alter this objective.\"\nThen, in italics in the original: \"*one should be aware of the detrimental effect that competitive loading has on the athlete's state*, since it leads to serious exhaustion of nervous energy. Therefore, during the transition to the next mesocycle, it is necessary to include a recuperation period, the duration of which is determined individually, depending on the difficulty of the competitive stage\" (PDF p. 400).\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason for rules 2 and 3 is the \"current adaptation reserves\": there is an optimal time over which they can be raised, and stretching or squeezing it changes how concentrated the loading must be. They give no study for the 20-week figure on this page (\"It has already been mentioned,\" referring back to earlier sections outside this batch). The recuperation rule rests on the \"serious exhaustion of nervous energy\" from competition, without data.\n**When to apply** — Drawing an annual plan: place the main competitions first, cut the year into mesocycles from those dates, and do not move the boundaries to chase early-season results or to serve minor meets. Eric's baseline: \"In football the target is a peak every Saturday, with November and the postseason as the priority window\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 185, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)). Inference: under rule 3, November and the postseason would set the boundary of the final mesocycle, and early-season games would be treated as the authors' \"intermediate competitions,\" which \"should not alter this objective.\"\n**When NOT to apply** — Rule 3's \"should not be broken for any reason\" is the authors' strongest wording in the section, but it assumes one or two main competitions; in a sport with a weekly game, every game counts, and the rule does not say how to weight them. The 20-week figure is for \"highly qualified athletes.\" The recuperation period has no stated length. Issurin's competing structure uses stages of \"about two months\" ([[block-periodization--issurin/framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle]]); see [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]].\n**Example** — The authors' own example of the problem is a season that is \"extended\" while \"the length of the preparatory stages is shortened\" (PDF p. 400). Inference: a jumper whose national championship moves three weeks earlier shortens the second mesocycle by concentrating its loading more, rather than shifting the boundary to keep a full block before an early-season invitational.\n**Related** — [[claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation]], [[framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training]], [[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]], [[framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports]], [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--nine-step-annual-program-construction-sequence]]\nconfidence: supported\n\n########## framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training.md\n# Verkhoshansky's Eight-Step Sequence for Programming the Annual Cycle, From Target Results Down to the Microcycles\n**What it is** — The authors recommend \"the following sequence of logical operations, where each calculation is made by taking into account the calculation made in the preceding operation (Verkhoshansky, 1985)\" (PDF p. 401 / printed p. 390, checked against the page image). The eight steps, with their key wording:\n1. **Determine the progress in results and the dates.** \"The calculations rely on a predictive model of the dynamics of the sport results relative to the competition calendar,\" based on \"a multi-faceted objective assessment of the athlete's potential\" and what is \"realistically feasible in the current year at his level of mastery.\"\n2. **Determine the needed changes in special physical preparedness and technical-tactical proficiency,** expressed as \"specific goals, relative to the functional indicators and the characteristics of technical proficiency which must be achieved in the important competitions.\" \"Subjective ratings of perceived effort (RPE) can be very useful in offering further guidance.\"\n3. **Build quantitative models of the fitness state across the year** \"so that these indicators peak in the main competitions.\"\n4. **Select the training means and methods** by \"assessing the training potential of the means and methods.\"\n5. **Calculate the general volume of loading for all means,** based on \"the accumulated effect of the loading in all preceding stages.\"\n6. **Divide the year into mesocycles,** using \"the competition calendar and the dates necessary for optimal increase of the body's current adaptation reserves.\"\n7. **Calculate the distribution of loading over the year,** expressed as \"the quantitative dynamics of the loading imposed by the fundamental training means.\"\n8. **Organise loading over the mesocycle,** as \"a detailed training programme giving the specific distribution of loading during each of the microcycles,\" based on \"the characteristics of the LDTE of concentrated strength loading.\" \"Provision should always be made available for introducing contingency changes in programmes, necessitated by unforeseen incidents such as injuries, changes in competitive timetables and personal disasters.\"\nThe authors frame the difficulty first: \"Its complexity, however, lies not so much in the quantity of calculations as in the insufficient basis for choosing and quantitatively determining the main operations. Therefore in practice, even experienced coaches need to make tentative computations which may have a low probability of producing a correct prognosis\" (PDF p. 400 / printed p. 389, checked against the page image).\n**Why it works** — The sequence is a top-down dependency chain: each step uses the one before. The authors give no evidence that following it improves results; it is a planning procedure.\n**When to apply** — Building or auditing an annual plan. Compare it with two other procedures in the library: Issurin's nine steps start from the competition calendar and end with obligatory correction after review ([[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--nine-step-annual-program-construction-sequence]]); Evans's ten steps start with a sport and position profile ([[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--ten-step-process-for-building-an-annual-plan]]). Verkhoshansky's starts from the predicted result and the fitness changes needed to reach it, and only divides the year into mesocycles at step 6. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 183, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: step 8's contingency provision is the authors' version of that flexibility.\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors concede the operations rest on an \"insufficient basis\" and may have \"a low probability of producing a correct prognosis\" (PDF p. 400). Step 1 assumes a predictive model of performance exists; in team sports there is no single result to predict. The procedure is written for individual athletes and their competitions.\n**Example** — The authors give no worked plan in 6.15. Inference: for a triple jumper, step 1 sets a target of a 40 cm improvement at the national championship in month 10, step 2 converts it to target values for the standing triple jump and approach speed, and steps 6-8 then divide the year into two mesocycles and write the weekly loading.\n**Related** — [[framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models]], [[heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates]], [[framework--four-documents-for-programming-and-controlling-training]], [[principle--programme-objective-approach-quantified-goals-dictate-training]], [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--nine-step-annual-program-construction-sequence]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--ten-step-process-for-building-an-annual-plan]]\nconfidence: supported","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d51b10bb-e6ce-45e7-a711-74ed706d0d40","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
