{"parentUuid":"b7f7942e-b508-469c-917f-a30d23317d0b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"25e8ccab-467d-4632-a14a-aba55241bae7","agentId":"ac7100d52d01adab3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PnJQWKce2XNJZofA23ax85","type":"tool_result","content":"14:# After a Concentrated Block, Unload and Wait: Gains Commonly Appear After a 1-3 Week Latency\n16:**What it is** — The authors warn: \"The system is not without its dangers, since concentrated loading is very stressful and, besides increasing the potential for overtraining, it can decrease motor qualities such as speed-strength. After a phase of emphasis on heavy strength training, there may be an increase in muscle stiffness and a loss of explosive strength, sporting skill or speed.\" Their \n20:**When to apply** — After any 4-8 week block that concentrates on one quality, plan a lower-load period before the next concentrated block. Inference: judge the block's result after the 1-3 week latency, not on the last day of loading. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 183, Inferred). Inference: the 1-3 week\n22:**When NOT to apply** — The 1-3 week figure is \"most commonly,\" with no study named. It is a latency to the onset of gains. Chapter 6 gives a different number for a different quantity: the long-term delayed training effect \"was observed to last from 4-12 weeks during the long-term stages of strength preparation,\" its duration \"approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage,\"\n24:**Example** — The authors' own: after a 4-8 week concentrated strength block, the gains \"may occur most commonly after a latency period of 1-3 weeks\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). Inference: an off-season strength block ending in late March would be judged in April, and the speed-emphasis block that follows would open at lower volume rather than at full load.\n26:**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy]] (4.1.2, the delayed training effect), [[case-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means]] (rest weeks between stages), [[framework--fitness-fatigue-m\n18:2. **Do not add volume while the effect emerges.** \"The volume of training work, therefore, should not be increased during the period of emergence of the LDTE\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image).\n16:**What it is** — The authors report \"a steady decrease in speed-strength preparedness during individual training stages of different length (from 5-12 weeks)\" caused by \"voluminous strength loading,\" after which \"speed-strength returns to its init","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"604792f2-9ac0-4847-b48f-b749fe4dad1f","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:12:56.185Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"14:# After a Concentrated Block, Unload and Wait: Gains Commonly Appear After a 1-3 Week Latency\n16:**What it is** — The authors warn: \"The system is not without its dangers, since concentrated loading is very stressful and, besides increasing the potential for overtraining, it can decrease motor qualities such as speed-strength. After a phase of emphasis on heavy strength training, there may be an increase in muscle stiffness and a loss of explosive strength, sporting skill or speed.\" Their \n20:**When to apply** — After any 4-8 week block that concentrates on one quality, plan a lower-load period before the next concentrated block. Inference: judge the block's result after the 1-3 week latency, not on the last day of loading. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 183, Inferred). Inference: the 1-3 week\n22:**When NOT to apply** — The 1-3 week figure is \"most commonly,\" with no study named. It is a latency to the onset of gains. Chapter 6 gives a different number for a different quantity: the long-term delayed training effect \"was observed to last from 4-12 weeks during the long-term stages of strength preparation,\" its duration \"approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage,\"\n24:**Example** — The authors' own: after a 4-8 week concentrated strength block, the gains \"may occur most commonly after a latency period of 1-3 weeks\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). Inference: an off-season strength block ending in late March would be judged in April, and the speed-emphasis block that follows would open at lower volume rather than at full load.\n26:**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy]] (4.1.2, the delayed training effect), [[case-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means]] (rest weeks between stages), [[framework--fitness-fatigue-m\n18:2. **Do not add volume while the effect emerges.** \"The volume of training work, therefore, should not be increased during the period of emergence of the LDTE\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image).\n16:**What it is** — The authors report \"a steady decrease in speed-strength preparedness during individual training stages of different length (from 5-12 weeks)\" caused by \"voluminous strength loading,\" after which \"speed-strength returns to its init","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b7f7942e-b508-469c-917f-a30d23317d0b","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
