{"parentUuid":"aefc1221-8950-412a-a28a-7560dea87359","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac7100d52d01adab3","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffqSsX8d37jKeyZjZS92","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014FRSaYLTfTz8XHuEYrSyFx","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6d_opus_round2.md","content":"# Review round 2: Supertraining Chapter 6d (6.12-6.17), 25 entries\n\nReviewer: Claude Opus (independent round-2 reviewer, instructed to falsify; did not write or fix these entries). Date: 2026-10-03.\nScope: the 25 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6d.txt`, folder `staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/` (Chapter 7 files in the same folder ignored).\nInputs: round-1 review `staging/review_supertraining_ch6d_opus.md`; fix log `staging/fixes_supertraining_ch6d_applied.md`; `docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md`; `docs/method-baseline.md`; the vault brief `research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a.md`; vault entries `block-periodization--issurin/framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle.md`, `claim--multi-peak-annual-preparation-elite-track-and-field.md`, `principle--concentration-of-training-workloads.md`; `elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types.md`.\nSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx = printed + 11.\n\n## Method\n\n- **Page images, opened by me** (all in `staging/_renders/`): Fig 6.39 at 400 dpi with crops of both panels (`r2_6d_p395.png`, `r2_6d_p395_upper.png`, `r2_6d_p395_lower.png`); Fig 6.32(b) at 400 dpi (`r2_6d_p383.png`, crop `r2_6d_p383_figb.png`); Fig 6.36(b) at 400 dpi, both panels (`r2_6d_p388_bleft.png`, `r2_6d_p388_bright.png`); Fig 6.38 (`r2_6d_p394_fig638.png`); Fig 6.33 (`r2_6d_p386_small.png`); Figs 6.44-6.45 (`r2_6d_p399_small.png`). Fig 6.39 positions were measured in pixels against the month ticks of the lower panel (ticks at the 1/2 ... 10/11 boundaries; numerals sit between ticks).\n- **Quote check, all 25 files.** A script extracted every double-quoted string (12+ characters) and matched each fragment (split on ellipses) against the text layer of PDF 290-319 and 330-403, the baseline, the Issurin brief, the Issurin, Evans and Bompa vault entries (whitespace, case, punctuation and line-break hyphens normalised; OCR tolerance by word trigrams). Result per file: Antonova 17/0 missing, endurance 25/0, prolonged 11/0, tricyclic 14/0, technical-mastery 9/0, set-boundaries 17/0, eight-step 18/0, test-monthly 12/0, programming-is-an-art 9/0, Tabachnik 3/0. Every residual \"miss\" in the other 15 files was inspected by hand: all are regex pairing artifacts across nested quotes, the entries' own Inference phrases in quotation marks (for example \"level is harder than timing\"), bracketed edits (\"cause[s]\", \"fairly random[ly]\") or OCR (\"modem\" for \"modern\" at PDF 389). No misquote found.\n- **Tables 6.7-6.12** checked value by value against the PDF 392-393 text layer (Tabachnik and descriptive-model entries).\n- **Dates and durations** checked in the text layer: Antonova 1982 (PDF 374, 382) and 1980 (PDF 381); Levchenko 1982 (PDF 374), 1981 (PDF 381), 1980 (PDF 382); \"4-12 weeks\" (PDF 375, 381); \"usually 3-5 months\" and \"currently scant\" (PDF 387); \"about 20 weeks\" (PDF 400); \"latency period of 1-3 weeks\" (PDF 305).\n- **Baseline line numbers** 32, 101, 105, 115, 116, 117, 181-183, 185-188 checked against `docs/method-baseline.md`: all correct.\n- **Mechanical check** (script over the 25 files): six section headers once each; no em dash outside the six headers or in titles; no line with unbalanced `[[`/`]]` (no wrapped links); every wikilink resolves in staging or the vault; no process or review-history wording (pattern: prior/previous version, corrected from, round, reviewer, review gate, NO-GO, fix log, earlier draft, now reads, fixer, flagged). Output: all 25 files \"OK\".\n\nCounts: **22 GO, 3 FIX, 0 NO-GO.** Of round 1's 11 FIX files, 9 are now GO and 2 need one more pass (complex training, two mesocycles). One round-1 GO file (the endurance models) is now FIX.\n\n## 1. The explosive-strength model entry (framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports): GO\n\n**Fig 6.39, my own reading (PDF 395 / p.384, 400 dpi).** Month ticks in the lower panel fall at about x = 212, 325, 440, 555, 670, 785, 905, 1020, 1135, 1255 (crop coordinates); dashed lines at about 545 (just before the 4/5 tick), 678 (the 5/6 tick) and 968 (mid month 8).\n- Phases I to late month 4, II about month 5, III months 6 to mid 8, IV mid 8 to 11: entry correct, including its note that boundaries do not sit on ticks.\n- Block 1 spans the panel start to x about 420, before the 3/4 tick: \"ending late in month 3\", correct. Block 2 spans x about 688 to 935: starts at the 5/6 tick, ends just past the 7/8 tick, \"early in month 8\", correct. Height ratio 333/510 = 0.65: \"roughly two thirds\", correct.\n- Upper panel. The two upper curves bump in month 1 (x about 118-120); all three are lowest in month 2 (x 250-300; Maximum Strength earliest, x about 250); all are rising through month 3 while block 1 runs to x 420: correct. The middle and bottom curves peak at x about 580-585 (early month 5, just right of the first dashed line) and the top curve is flat across x 550-680: correct. Block-2 lows at x about 790 (Maximum), 810 (middle) and 880 (top), i.e. early to late month 7: the entry's \"lowest about months 6-7\" is slightly early (optional: \"about month 7\"). The two upper curves converge and cross at x about 1040, just past the 8/9 tick and just right of the III/IV line: correct. All three end at their highest values at month 11, right-edge order Starting, Explosive, Maximum: correct. By continuity the steeply rising middle curve ends at Starting Strength; the entry labels this Inference: correct and correctly labelled.\n- Lower panel. Technique: rising inside block 1, peak at x about 490 (month 4), low at x about 750 (month 6, early block 2), back to about y 290 at block 2's end against a first peak of about y 278 (level, not above: the fixer's disagreement 3 is justified), peak at x about 1160 (month 10), declining to 11: all correct. Speed-ending curve peaks at x about 1010 (just before the 8/9 tick) and falls; GPP-ending curve lowest at x about 985 and rises; they cross at x about 1180 (month 10); GPP ends above Speed: all correct. Before month 8 the entry calls the GPP/Speed courses unclear; that is conservative and acceptable.\n- Text check: \"the highest values of which are achieved at the end of each mesocycle\" (PDF 394) matches the figure's peaks at month 5 (end of mesocycle 1) and month 11.\n- Fig 6.38 (PDF 394): A jumps early and small, B weights middle and largest, C depth jumps late and small: correct.\n\n**Quotes.** Every PDF 381, 390, 391 and 394 quote matched the text layer. Issurin quotes verified verbatim: brief p. 11 \"better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality and as restricted when peak performance requires many interacting abilities\"; brief p. 6 \"improved some fitness measures, but the reviewed studies did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance\" and \"insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\"; Issurin book entry \"should be about two months\" and \"usually varies from four to seven\" (Block Periodization PDF pp. 38-40); multi-peak entry: Bubka 7, Jones 10, Kostadinova 11 peaks, so \"7 to 11 times\", with two jumpers among the three: correct. Baseline lines 105, 185 and 186 with tags: correct.\n\n**\"Jumping-type sports\": confirmed absent.** The brief uses \"jumping\" once, in its population list at p. 11: \"The review emphasizes elite, subelite, and well-trained athletes across endurance, team, combat, dual, jumping, and strength/power settings.\" Its only statement on where the concentrated-unidirectional model suits is \"sports dominated by a narrow target quality.\" The entry says \"The brief does not name jumping or throwing as narrow-target sports, and it does not reject the model for them,\" and labels the mapping of a single explosive event to \"a narrow target quality\" as Inference. That is exactly what the brief supports. The fixer's disagreement 2 is justified; round 1's paraphrase overstated the brief.\n\n**Football-transfer scoping.** Correct. The entry keeps two grounds apart: (1) Issurin's book (stage length and count; multi-peak seasons in elite track and field) as the in-domain basis for `contested`; (2) Issurin 2016 as bearing on the transfer to American football, a team sport, with the brief's non-systematic, author-curated caveat. Nothing claims more than the brief says. The football mapping in When to apply is labelled Inference, and the entry says the authors do not discuss football. Fixer disagreement 1 (contest on Issurin's book, 2016 scoped to the transfer) is justified under the domain rule and is more accurate than round 1's proposal.\n\n**Optional (not blocking).**\n- Block-2 lows: \"about month 7\" rather than \"about months 6-7\".\n- \"That is a different structure from this model's two large mesocycles\" is the writer's comparison. The 7-11 peaks are competition peaks, and the model's two competition phases do not exclude several competitions each, so the firmer half of the contrast is the stage length and count. Prefix the comparison with \"Inference:\" and add that clause.\n\n## 2. The other 10 round-1 FIX files\n\n| File | Verdict | Finding (entry vs source, with page) | Exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes | FIX | Round-1 fixes landed: Matveyev 1970 in its own clause with the PDF 380 quote (verified); the 28-study caveat matches brief p. 11; fixer disagreement 7 (\"the book\" in the brief is Issurin's Block Periodization) is correct per the brief's text. **New problem, introduced by the rewrite:** \"He also contests who the concentrated approach is for: the brief records 'no evidence that amateur athletes have any serious restrictions' ... (brief, p. 11), against the book's view that complex loading is 'applicable only to beginners or athletes of average qualification' (PDF p. 380).\" This presents one side of a split inside Issurin. The same brief line says the 2008 book \"(p. 33) says they do fine with medium concentration\", and the vault entry `block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads` records Issurin 2008 saying the traditional complex model \"has visible benefits for the preparation of low- and mid-level athletes\" (Block Periodization PDF p. 44 / printed p. 33) and tags it \"Contested (Issurin 2008 vs Issurin 2016).\" Issurin 2008 agrees with the authors' beginners limit; only Issurin 2016 disputes it. The brief requires the source's internal contradiction to be kept. | After the p. 11 sentence add: \"Issurin's own 2008 book takes the authors' side here: the traditional complex model 'has visible benefits for the preparation of low- and mid-level athletes' (Block Periodization PDF p. 44 / printed p. 33; [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]]), so the contest on the beginners limit rests on the 2016 review only.\" Optional: support \"Issurin agrees that traditional complex training falls short for elite athletes\" with the direct source, Issurin's Table 1.4 of contradictions in the traditional simultaneous model for high-level athletes (Block Periodization PDF p. 20, same vault entry). That lets the sentence stand without the bridging Inference. |\n| claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation | FIX | Round-1 fixes landed: the paragraph now records a terminology tension; quotes at PDF 387, 389, 396 and 400 verified; the `contested` basis is Issurin's book, with the note that the 2016 brief does not address mesocycle count (correct: the brief has nothing on it); Evans/Bompa quotes verified. **Attribution problem (carried in from the round-1 wording, still present):** the bold lead \"A terminology tension inside the book, not two opposed positions,\" \"Read with PDF pp. 389 and 396, the passages answer different questions...\" and \"The apparent conflict ... comes from the PDF p. 387 wording\" are the writer's interpretation, written in plain text (author voice). | Change the bold lead to \"**A terminology tension inside the book (Inference), not two opposed positions.**\" and begin the interpretive sentence \"Inference: read with PDF pp. 389 and 396, the passages answer different questions...\". Keep the descriptive first sentence (what each page says) as plain text. |\n| framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports | GO | Both fixes landed. Boxing and swimming are now correctly described as traditionally tri-cyclic with bi-cyclic possible (PDF 387), and the \"team sports and one-on-one events\" quote is verified. `contested` with the reason stated: the domain is \"intended primarily for individual and one-on-one sports\" (PDF 397), and brief p. 6 names combat and swimming (quotes verbatim). The old \"keeps `supported`\" sentence is gone. | Checked: all 14 quotes; PDF 387, 397, 400; brief p. 6; baseline line 188. |\n| framework--speed-sport-block-organisation-and-two-glycolytic-peaks | GO | Fig 6.32(b) at 400 dpi agrees with the new paragraph. The shaded block runs about x 70-450 (crop). Two curves dip below the axis and recross after the block (x about 490 and 540). One curve peaks at x about 350, 74% through the block (fixer's \"two thirds to three quarters\" is right; disagreement 4 justified), falls to a low at about x 600, then a hump at about x 880, ending at An-Gly. One rises steadily to S-E. One starts at the block's end and peaks at x about 660 before falling to Speed. Vmax/STATE identity is marked unclear and the crossing assignments are labelled Inference: correct. | Optional: When to apply extends the model to team sports through the second glycolytic peak but, unlike every other entry touching team sports, has no Issurin line. Add one sentence: \"For team sports, Issurin 2016 reports no team-practice or competitive-performance effects from concentrated-unidirectional programs (brief, p. 6).\" |\n| case-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength | GO | Fig 6.36(b) at 400 dpi. Aerobic week: VO2 max starts about 87%, crosses 100% at about 35-36 h, about 107% at 72 h; Training effect about 93%, crosses at about 17 h, about 101.7% at 72 h. Anaerobic week: VO2 max starts about 98%, peaks about 113% at about 8 h, about 106% at 72 h; Training effect crosses at about 38 h, about 102.5% at 72 h. All match the entry (fixer's disagreement 5 justified; round 1's 30-36 h was early for the anaerobic panel). The spurious 12 h conflict is gone; the \"currently scant\" and \"sharply reduces\" quotes are verified at PDF 387. | Optional: the anaerobic-week Training effect starts nearer 95% than \"about 94%\" (curve start just above the 95 tick). Optional: \"The rating stays `supported`\" reads as history; write \"The rating is `supported`\". |\n| case-study--tabachnik-100m-sprinter-model-characteristics | GO | Every Table 6.8 and 6.9 value matches the PDF 392 text layer. The 5 m (1.18 s) vs 15 m (1.16-1.18 s) inconsistency and the 10.2-to-10.5 s 15 m step (1.18-1.20 to 1.26-1.28) are correctly added. `untested` is appropriate for an unsourced norm table. | Checked: all values, PDF 391 quote, baseline line 32. |\n| heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks | GO | PDF 305 latency quote verified; the side-by-side note is labelled Inference; the 6c allow-restoration entry does draw the latency-versus-duration distinction (its When NOT); rule 2 quote (\"should not be increased during the period of emergence of the LDTE\", PDF 375) matches the 6c time-technique entry and the page. | Checked: PDF 383, 305, 375, 384, 385 quotes; links resolve. |\n| case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities | GO | Self-contradiction removed; Levchenko and Antonova date notes verified (fixer's disagreement 6 on Antonova 1980 at PDF 381 is correct); 6c LDTE principle linked with the new angle stated, and it does cite Antonova 1982 and Levchenko 1982 at PDF 374. | Checked: all 17 quotes; PDF 340 cybernetic definition; baseline line 74. |\n| claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity | GO | \"Shorter\" replaced with \"comparable in length\", inside the labelled Inference paragraph. | Checked: PDF 394 and 396 quotes; baseline lines 105, 181, 187. |\n| heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work | GO | The caption-vs-label note is fixed and labelled. On the PDF 399 image: \"GPP\" under small wave A, \"Strength Load\" under large wave B, both left of the dashed mid-line, so B occupies the first half as the text says. The labels fit the text; the caption (\"A is the block of concentrated strength loading\") is the likelier error. Fig 6.44 reading (speed-strength dips in A, rises in B, flattens in C, highest at the right) also checked. | Checked: PDF 397-400 quotes; baseline line 105. |\n\nBoth fixer-edited GO entries were also read in full:\n- `framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models`: GO. Tables 6.7, 6.10, 6.11 and 6.12 values (splits 111.5/116.8/131.5; 2 x (4 x 400 m) caption; \"Relative mean strength of 5 muscle groups (kg)\"; javelin snatch Bmass + 15 / + 10 kg, squat 2 x / 1.5 x, broad jump 3.20/2.80, triple jump 10.20/7.80, vertical jump 95.0/70.0 cm, 30 m 2.90/3.20 s, 170/140 and 35/30 throws; quintuple jump 9.25-13.70 \"(cm)\" against long jump 179.8-261.2 cm) all match PDF 392-393. The unit-error and power-norm remarks are labelled Inference.\n- `framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports`: FIX, see section 3.\n\n## 3. Confidence consistency (the \"domain rule\")\n\nThe rule in the fix log: `contested` when a library source disagrees with the entry inside the sport domain the entry claims. That is the ATOMIZE brief's definition, applied by domain. Applied to the six entries in 6d that rest on concentrated blocks and touch Issurin:\n\n| entry | claimed domain | in-domain library disagreement | rating | consistent? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| explosive-strength model | explosive-strength sports (jumpers in the Example) | Issurin's book: stages of about two months, four to seven a season; elite track and field multi-peak | contested | yes |\n| tri-cyclic model | individual and one-on-one sports | brief p. 6: combat and swimming | contested | yes |\n| two large mesocycles | annual structure, all sports | Issurin's book (stage length and count) | contested | yes |\n| complex training | highly qualified vs beginner athletes | Issurin 2016 p. 11 (amateurs), against Issurin 2008 p. 33 (see FIX above) | contested | yes (basis needs the 2008 line) |\n| concentrated strength loading | \"speed-strength, cyclic and team sports\" (PDF 382) | brief p. 6: combat and team sports | contested | yes |\n| **medium- and long-duration endurance models** | middle- and long-distance endurance athletes; \"cyclic sports of medium duration\" (PDF 399) | **brief p. 6: swimming** | **supported** | **no** |\n\n**Call on the endurance entry: it should be `contested`, on brief p. 6.** Reasons:\n1. Swimming sits inside the domain the entry claims. The entry claims \"middle-distance (medium) or long-distance (long) endurance athletes\" and never excludes swimming. The book scopes the medium-duration material to \"cyclic sports of medium duration\" (PDF 399 / p.388, Fig 6.46), and at PDF 374 / p.363 it lists swimming among the cyclic sports where the LDTE of concentrated strength loading was observed (\"middle-distance running (Zhdanovich, 1980), swimming (Rudokenc, 1981) and ice-skating\"). Brief p. 6 has both an elite-swimmer example (\"specific strength and anaerobic capacity improved but swimming performance improved in only two of three athletes\") and the judgment \"insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation.\" That is the same test that moved the tri-cyclic entry to `contested`.\n2. The counter-argument that PDF 387 lists swimming among sports that \"traditionally employ\" tri-cyclic forms does not remove it from the endurance domain. The rule concerns the domain the entry claims, not the book's cycle-type list, and the tri-cyclic entry itself counts swimming in its domain.\n3. **A stale cross-reference introduced by the fix round.** The entry says the models \"carry the same Issurin 2016 challenge to concentrated loading as the other 6.14 models ([[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]).\" The explosive entry now says Issurin 2016 does not count against that model in its own domain. The pointer now contradicts its target. That needs fixing whatever the rating.\n\nCorrection for `framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports`: set `confidence: contested`. Replace the \"carry the same Issurin 2016 challenge...\" sentence with: \"**Contested by Issurin 2016 inside the models' domain (the basis for the `contested` rating).** Both models run on concentrated strength blocks, and the authors count swimming among the cyclic sports where the LDTE of such blocks was observed (PDF p. 374 / printed p. 363). The library's brief of Issurin 2016 reports an elite-swimmer example in which 'specific strength and anaerobic capacity improved but swimming performance improved in only two of three athletes,' and judges the concentrated-unidirectional evidence 'insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation' (brief, p. 6; [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]). The brief also says the review's study selection is non-systematic and author-curated.\" Add the brief to Related. Everything else in the entry checked out: PDF 395-397 and 399-400 quotes, including the added \"optimal date ... is deferred\" (PDF 396), Gilyazova 7-14%, and baseline line 115.\n\nOther entries checked under the same rule and correctly left `supported`: mesocycle variants (no sport domain claimed; its Issurin line says \"for team sports\"), unidirectional microcycles (a microcycle restoration finding the brief does not address), speed-sport, follow-a-concentrated-block and GPP restoration (narrow-target speed and speed-strength domains). After the endurance change the 6d tally is 6 `contested`, 18 `supported`, 1 `untested`.\n\n## 4. Spot checks of round-1 GO entries\n\n| File | Verdict | Checked |\n|---|---|---|\n| claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving | GO | \"up to 2 months\" and \"4-12 weeks\" a paragraph apart (PDF 381); mechanism sentence (PDF 381); citation list, \"most appropriate for highly qualified athletes\", 13-15% (PDF 382); \"reduced by as much as 20%\" over a macrocycle (PDF 305); brief p. 6 quotes verbatim; contested scope (team sports, which the book itself claims at PDF 382) fits the domain rule; baseline line 183. |\n| framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound | GO | Fig 6.33 against the PDF 386 image: (a) A rises smoothly to a plateau, B dips then exceeds it; (b) volume peaks mid-cycle, shaded GPP peaks early and declines, fitness rises steadily; (c) volume peaks early, fitness low then climbing above it, shaded GPP grows across the cycle. All as the entry says. Also checked: \"usually 3-5 months\" (PDF 387), \"4-6 weeks\" and the italic mesocycle definition (PDF 385), the qualification rule (PDF 386), baseline line 106. Round-1 optional note still open: Variant A is the \"traditional form\", so the Issurin line fits Variant B only. |\n| heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates | GO | Rules 1-4, the calendar-conflict sentence, \"about 20 weeks\" and the italic recuperation sentence (PDF 400); Issurin \"about two months\" in the vault entry; baseline line 185. |\n| framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training | GO | The complexity sentence and \"low probability of producing a correct prognosis\" (PDF 400); steps 1-8 and the contingency sentence (PDF 401); Issurin nine-step and Evans ten-step links resolve; baseline line 183. |\n\nEvery quote in the other GO entries (programme-objective, technique-work, GPP restoration, seven aims, technical mastery, test-monthly, four documents, programming-is-an-art) passed the text-layer quote check; their content beyond quotes was not re-audited in this round.\n\n## 5. Mechanical checks (25 files)\n\n```\nprinciple--programme-objective-approach-quantified-goals-dictate-training.md OK\nclaim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes.md OK\nclaim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving.md OK\ncase-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities.md OK\nheuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block.md OK\nheuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks.md OK\nframework--speed-sport-block-organisation-and-two-glycolytic-peaks.md OK\nframework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound.md OK\nclaim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation.md OK\ncase-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength.md OK\nheuristic--use-low-intensity-gpp-as-restoration-in-concentrated-loading-models.md OK\nframework--seven-primary-aims-of-programming-training.md OK\nframework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models.md OK\ncase-study--tabachnik-100m-sprinter-model-characteristics.md OK\nframework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports.md OK\nframework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports.md OK\nclaim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity.md OK\nframework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports.md OK\nheuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work.md OK\nprinciple--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice.md OK\nheuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates.md OK\nframework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training.md OK\nheuristic--test-monthly-under-constant-conditions-and-compare-with-targets.md OK\nframework--four-documents-for-programming-and-controlling-training.md OK\nprinciple--programming-is-an-art-on-principles-not-an-algorithm.md OK\n```\n\n(Each \"OK\" means: six headers once each, no em dash outside headers or in the title, no wrapped wikilinks, every wikilink resolves in staging or the vault, no process wording.) The fix log's grep evidence matches the files: every new phrase it lists is present, and the old phrases I spot-checked are gone.\n\n## Fixer disagreements: rulings\n\n1. Explosive `contested` basis (Issurin's book in-domain; 2016 scoped to the transfer): **justified.**\n2. Brief p. 11 does not say \"jumping\": **justified** (quoted above).\n3. Technique level, not above, at block 2's end: **justified** (y about 290 vs first peak about 278).\n4. An-Gly peak late in the block: **justified** (74% through).\n5. Fig 6.36(b) crossing times about 35 h and 38-40 h: **justified.**\n6. Antonova 1980 at PDF 381: **justified.**\n7. \"The book\" in the brief is Issurin's Block Periodization: **justified.** But the same brief line also carries the Issurin 2008 agreement on lower-level athletes, which the entry omits (FIX above).\n8. Optional GO-row items deferred: acceptable. The 6.2.2 build-backwards link remains a coordinator suggestion.\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** 22 GO, 3 FIX, 0 NO-GO. Required fixes, each a sentence or a label:\n1. `framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports`: `confidence: contested` on brief p. 6 (swimming inside the cyclic-endurance domain, PDF 374 and 399); replace the stale \"same Issurin 2016 challenge as [[explosive]]\" pointer with the in-domain reason; link the brief.\n2. `claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes`: add Issurin 2008's agreement on low- and mid-level athletes (Block Periodization PDF p. 44 / printed p. 33), so the beginners-limit contest is shown as resting on the 2016 review only.\n3. `claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation`: label the terminology-tension interpretation \"Inference:\".\n\nOptional, not blocking: explosive block-2 lows \"about month 7\" and an Inference label on the structure comparison; speed-sport team-sport Issurin line; microcycle \"stays\" to \"is\" and about 95% start; mesocycle-variants Issurin line scoped to Variant B.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8586,"cache_read_input_tokens":409587,"output_tokens":16217,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":4896},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1767},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4870,"cache_read_input_tokens":203910,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1767,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1767,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"},{"input_tokens":212439,"output_tokens":7624,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"advisor_message","model":"claude-fable-5-1"},{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":11347,"cache_read_input_tokens":205677,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6819,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6819,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_014FRSaYLTfTz8XHuEYrSyFx":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6d_opus_round2.md","content":"# Review round 2: Supertraining Chapter 6d (6.12-6.17), 25 entries\n\nReviewer: Claude Opus (independent round-2 reviewer, instructed to falsify; did not write or fix these entries). Date: 2026-10-03.\nScope: the 25 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6d.txt`, folder `staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/` (Chapter 7 files in the same folder ignored).\nInputs: round-1 review `staging/review_supertraining_ch6d_opus.md`; fix log `staging/fixes_supertraining_ch6d_applied.md`; `docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md`; `docs/method-baseline.md`; the vault brief `research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a.md`; vault entries `block-periodization--issurin/framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle.md`, `claim--multi-peak-annual-preparation-elite-track-and-field.md`, `principle--concentration-of-training-workloads.md`; `elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types.md`.\nSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx = printed + 11.\n\n## Method\n\n- **Page images, opened by me** (all in `staging/_renders/`): Fig 6.39 at 400 dpi with crops of both panels (`r2_6d_p395.png`, `r2_6d_p395_upper.png`, `r2_6d_p395_lower.png`); Fig 6.32(b) at 400 dpi (`r2_6d_p383.png`, crop `r2_6d_p383_figb.png`); Fig 6.36(b) at 400 dpi, both panels (`r2_6d_p388_bleft.png`, `r2_6d_p388_bright.png`); Fig 6.38 (`r2_6d_p394_fig638.png`); Fig 6.33 (`r2_6d_p386_small.png`); Figs 6.44-6.45 (`r2_6d_p399_small.png`). Fig 6.39 positions were measured in pixels against the month ticks of the lower panel (ticks at the 1/2 ... 10/11 boundaries; numerals sit between ticks).\n- **Quote check, all 25 files.** A script extracted every double-quoted string (12+ characters) and matched each fragment (split on ellipses) against the text layer of PDF 290-319 and 330-403, the baseline, the Issurin brief, the Issurin, Evans and Bompa vault entries (whitespace, case, punctuation and line-break hyphens normalised; OCR tolerance by word trigrams). Result per file: Antonova 17/0 missing, endurance 25/0, prolonged 11/0, tricyclic 14/0, technical-mastery 9/0, set-boundaries 17/0, eight-step 18/0, test-monthly 12/0, programming-is-an-art 9/0, Tabachnik 3/0. Every residual \"miss\" in the other 15 files was inspected by hand: all are regex pairing artifacts across nested quotes, the entries' own Inference phrases in quotation marks (for example \"level is harder than timing\"), bracketed edits (\"cause[s]\", \"fairly random[ly]\") or OCR (\"modem\" for \"modern\" at PDF 389). No misquote found.\n- **Tables 6.7-6.12** checked value by value against the PDF 392-393 text layer (Tabachnik and descriptive-model entries).\n- **Dates and durations** checked in the text layer: Antonova 1982 (PDF 374, 382) and 1980 (PDF 381); Levchenko 1982 (PDF 374), 1981 (PDF 381), 1980 (PDF 382); \"4-12 weeks\" (PDF 375, 381); \"usually 3-5 months\" and \"currently scant\" (PDF 387); \"about 20 weeks\" (PDF 400); \"latency period of 1-3 weeks\" (PDF 305).\n- **Baseline line numbers** 32, 101, 105, 115, 116, 117, 181-183, 185-188 checked against `docs/method-baseline.md`: all correct.\n- **Mechanical check** (script over the 25 files): six section headers once each; no em dash outside the six headers or in titles; no line with unbalanced `[[`/`]]` (no wrapped links); every wikilink resolves in staging or the vault; no process or review-history wording (pattern: prior/previous version, corrected from, round, reviewer, review gate, NO-GO, fix log, earlier draft, now reads, fixer, flagged). Output: all 25 files \"OK\".\n\nCounts: **22 GO, 3 FIX, 0 NO-GO.** Of round 1's 11 FIX files, 9 are now GO and 2 need one more pass (complex training, two mesocycles). One round-1 GO file (the endurance models) is now FIX.\n\n## 1. The explosive-strength model entry (framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports): GO\n\n**Fig 6.39, my own reading (PDF 395 / p.384, 400 dpi).** Month ticks in the lower panel fall at about x = 212, 325, 440, 555, 670, 785, 905, 1020, 1135, 1255 (crop coordinates); dashed lines at about 545 (just before the 4/5 tick), 678 (the 5/6 tick) and 968 (mid month 8).\n- Phases I to late month 4, II about month 5, III months 6 to mid 8, IV mid 8 to 11: entry correct, including its note that boundaries do not sit on ticks.\n- Block 1 spans the panel start to x about 420, before the 3/4 tick: \"ending late in month 3\", correct. Block 2 spans x about 688 to 935: starts at the 5/6 tick, ends just past the 7/8 tick, \"early in month 8\", correct. Height ratio 333/510 = 0.65: \"roughly two thirds\", correct.\n- Upper panel. The two upper curves bump in month 1 (x about 118-120); all three are lowest in month 2 (x 250-300; Maximum Strength earliest, x about 250); all are rising through month 3 while block 1 runs to x 420: correct. The middle and bottom curves peak at x about 580-585 (early month 5, just right of the first dashed line) and the top curve is flat across x 550-680: correct. Block-2 lows at x about 790 (Maximum), 810 (middle) and 880 (top), i.e. early to late month 7: the entry's \"lowest about months 6-7\" is slightly early (optional: \"about month 7\"). The two upper curves converge and cross at x about 1040, just past the 8/9 tick and just right of the III/IV line: correct. All three end at their highest values at month 11, right-edge order Starting, Explosive, Maximum: correct. By continuity the steeply rising middle curve ends at Starting Strength; the entry labels this Inference: correct and correctly labelled.\n- Lower panel. Technique: rising inside block 1, peak at x about 490 (month 4), low at x about 750 (month 6, early block 2), back to about y 290 at block 2's end against a first peak of about y 278 (level, not above: the fixer's disagreement 3 is justified), peak at x about 1160 (month 10), declining to 11: all correct. Speed-ending curve peaks at x about 1010 (just before the 8/9 tick) and falls; GPP-ending curve lowest at x about 985 and rises; they cross at x about 1180 (month 10); GPP ends above Speed: all correct. Before month 8 the entry calls the GPP/Speed courses unclear; that is conservative and acceptable.\n- Text check: \"the highest values of which are achieved at the end of each mesocycle\" (PDF 394) matches the figure's peaks at month 5 (end of mesocycle 1) and month 11.\n- Fig 6.38 (PDF 394): A jumps early and small, B weights middle and largest, C depth jumps late and small: correct.\n\n**Quotes.** Every PDF 381, 390, 391 and 394 quote matched the text layer. Issurin quotes verified verbatim: brief p. 11 \"better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality and as restricted when peak performance requires many interacting abilities\"; brief p. 6 \"improved some fitness measures, but the reviewed studies did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance\" and \"insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\"; Issurin book entry \"should be about two months\" and \"usually varies from four to seven\" (Block Periodization PDF pp. 38-40); multi-peak entry: Bubka 7, Jones 10, Kostadinova 11 peaks, so \"7 to 11 times\", with two jumpers among the three: correct. Baseline lines 105, 185 and 186 with tags: correct.\n\n**\"Jumping-type sports\": confirmed absent.** The brief uses \"jumping\" once, in its population list at p. 11: \"The review emphasizes elite, subelite, and well-trained athletes across endurance, team, combat, dual, jumping, and strength/power settings.\" Its only statement on where the concentrated-unidirectional model suits is \"sports dominated by a narrow target quality.\" The entry says \"The brief does not name jumping or throwing as narrow-target sports, and it does not reject the model for them,\" and labels the mapping of a single explosive event to \"a narrow target quality\" as Inference. That is exactly what the brief supports. The fixer's disagreement 2 is justified; round 1's paraphrase overstated the brief.\n\n**Football-transfer scoping.** Correct. The entry keeps two grounds apart: (1) Issurin's book (stage length and count; multi-peak seasons in elite track and field) as the in-domain basis for `contested`; (2) Issurin 2016 as bearing on the transfer to American football, a team sport, with the brief's non-systematic, author-curated caveat. Nothing claims more than the brief says. The football mapping in When to apply is labelled Inference, and the entry says the authors do not discuss football. Fixer disagreement 1 (contest on Issurin's book, 2016 scoped to the transfer) is justified under the domain rule and is more accurate than round 1's proposal.\n\n**Optional (not blocking).**\n- Block-2 lows: \"about month 7\" rather than \"about months 6-7\".\n- \"That is a different structure from this model's two large mesocycles\" is the writer's comparison. The 7-11 peaks are competition peaks, and the model's two competition phases do not exclude several competitions each, so the firmer half of the contrast is the stage length and count. Prefix the comparison with \"Inference:\" and add that clause.\n\n## 2. The other 10 round-1 FIX files\n\n| File | Verdict | Finding (entry vs source, with page) | Exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes | FIX | Round-1 fixes landed: Matveyev 1970 in its own clause with the PDF 380 quote (verified); the 28-study caveat matches brief p. 11; fixer disagreement 7 (\"the book\" in the brief is Issurin's Block Periodization) is correct per the brief's text. **New problem, introduced by the rewrite:** \"He also contests who the concentrated approach is for: the brief records 'no evidence that amateur athletes have any serious restrictions' ... (brief, p. 11), against the book's view that complex loading is 'applicable only to beginners or athletes of average qualification' (PDF p. 380).\" This presents one side of a split inside Issurin. The same brief line says the 2008 book \"(p. 33) says they do fine with medium concentration\", and the vault entry `block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads` records Issurin 2008 saying the traditional complex model \"has visible benefits for the preparation of low- and mid-level athletes\" (Block Periodization PDF p. 44 / printed p. 33) and tags it \"Contested (Issurin 2008 vs Issurin 2016).\" Issurin 2008 agrees with the authors' beginners limit; only Issurin 2016 disputes it. The brief requires the source's internal contradiction to be kept. | After the p. 11 sentence add: \"Issurin's own 2008 book takes the authors' side here: the traditional complex model 'has visible benefits for the preparation of low- and mid-level athletes' (Block Periodization PDF p. 44 / printed p. 33; [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]]), so the contest on the beginners limit rests on the 2016 review only.\" Optional: support \"Issurin agrees that traditional complex training falls short for elite athletes\" with the direct source, Issurin's Table 1.4 of contradictions in the traditional simultaneous model for high-level athletes (Block Periodization PDF p. 20, same vault entry). That lets the sentence stand without the bridging Inference. |\n| claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation | FIX | Round-1 fixes landed: the paragraph now records a terminology tension; quotes at PDF 387, 389, 396 and 400 verified; the `contested` basis is Issurin's book, with the note that the 2016 brief does not address mesocycle count (correct: the brief has nothing on it); Evans/Bompa quotes verified. **Attribution problem (carried in from the round-1 wording, still present):** the bold lead \"A terminology tension inside the book, not two opposed positions,\" \"Read with PDF pp. 389 and 396, the passages answer different questions...\" and \"The apparent conflict ... comes from the PDF p. 387 wording\" are the writer's interpretation, written in plain text (author voice). | Change the bold lead to \"**A terminology tension inside the book (Inference), not two opposed positions.**\" and begin the interpretive sentence \"Inference: read with PDF pp. 389 and 396, the passages answer different questions...\". Keep the descriptive first sentence (what each page says) as plain text. |\n| framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports | GO | Both fixes landed. Boxing and swimming are now correctly described as traditionally tri-cyclic with bi-cyclic possible (PDF 387), and the \"team sports and one-on-one events\" quote is verified. `contested` with the reason stated: the domain is \"intended primarily for individual and one-on-one sports\" (PDF 397), and brief p. 6 names combat and swimming (quotes verbatim). The old \"keeps `supported`\" sentence is gone. | Checked: all 14 quotes; PDF 387, 397, 400; brief p. 6; baseline line 188. |\n| framework--speed-sport-block-organisation-and-two-glycolytic-peaks | GO | Fig 6.32(b) at 400 dpi agrees with the new paragraph. The shaded block runs about x 70-450 (crop). Two curves dip below the axis and recross after the block (x about 490 and 540). One curve peaks at x about 350, 74% through the block (fixer's \"two thirds to three quarters\" is right; disagreement 4 justified), falls to a low at about x 600, then a hump at about x 880, ending at An-Gly. One rises steadily to S-E. One starts at the block's end and peaks at x about 660 before falling to Speed. Vmax/STATE identity is marked unclear and the crossing assignments are labelled Inference: correct. | Optional: When to apply extends the model to team sports through the second glycolytic peak but, unlike every other entry touching team sports, has no Issurin line. Add one sentence: \"For team sports, Issurin 2016 reports no team-practice or competitive-performance effects from concentrated-unidirectional programs (brief, p. 6).\" |\n| case-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength | GO | Fig 6.36(b) at 400 dpi. Aerobic week: VO2 max starts about 87%, crosses 100% at about 35-36 h, about 107% at 72 h; Training effect about 93%, crosses at about 17 h, about 101.7% at 72 h. Anaerobic week: VO2 max starts about 98%, peaks about 113% at about 8 h, about 106% at 72 h; Training effect crosses at about 38 h, about 102.5% at 72 h. All match the entry (fixer's disagreement 5 justified; round 1's 30-36 h was early for the anaerobic panel). The spurious 12 h conflict is gone; the \"currently scant\" and \"sharply reduces\" quotes are verified at PDF 387. | Optional: the anaerobic-week Training effect starts nearer 95% than \"about 94%\" (curve start just above the 95 tick). Optional: \"The rating stays `supported`\" reads as history; write \"The rating is `supported`\". |\n| case-study--tabachnik-100m-sprinter-model-characteristics | GO | Every Table 6.8 and 6.9 value matches the PDF 392 text layer. The 5 m (1.18 s) vs 15 m (1.16-1.18 s) inconsistency and the 10.2-to-10.5 s 15 m step (1.18-1.20 to 1.26-1.28) are correctly added. `untested` is appropriate for an unsourced norm table. | Checked: all values, PDF 391 quote, baseline line 32. |\n| heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks | GO | PDF 305 latency quote verified; the side-by-side note is labelled Inference; the 6c allow-restoration entry does draw the latency-versus-duration distinction (its When NOT); rule 2 quote (\"should not be increased during the period of emergence of the LDTE\", PDF 375) matches the 6c time-technique entry and the page. | Checked: PDF 383, 305, 375, 384, 385 quotes; links resolve. |\n| case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities | GO | Self-contradiction removed; Levchenko and Antonova date notes verified (fixer's disagreement 6 on Antonova 1980 at PDF 381 is correct); 6c LDTE principle linked with the new angle stated, and it does cite Antonova 1982 and Levchenko 1982 at PDF 374. | Checked: all 17 quotes; PDF 340 cybernetic definition; baseline line 74. |\n| claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity | GO | \"Shorter\" replaced with \"comparable in length\", inside the labelled Inference paragraph. | Checked: PDF 394 and 396 quotes; baseline lines 105, 181, 187. |\n| heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work | GO | The caption-vs-label note is fixed and labelled. On the PDF 399 image: \"GPP\" under small wave A, \"Strength Load\" under large wave B, both left of the dashed mid-line, so B occupies the first half as the text says. The labels fit the text; the caption (\"A is the block of concentrated strength loading\") is the likelier error. Fig 6.44 reading (speed-strength dips in A, rises in B, flattens in C, highest at the right) also checked. | Checked: PDF 397-400 quotes; baseline line 105. |\n\nBoth fixer-edited GO entries were also read in full:\n- `framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models`: GO. Tables 6.7, 6.10, 6.11 and 6.12 values (splits 111.5/116.8/131.5; 2 x (4 x 400 m) caption; \"Relative mean strength of 5 muscle groups (kg)\"; javelin snatch Bmass + 15 / + 10 kg, squat 2 x / 1.5 x, broad jump 3.20/2.80, triple jump 10.20/7.80, vertical jump 95.0/70.0 cm, 30 m 2.90/3.20 s, 170/140 and 35/30 throws; quintuple jump 9.25-13.70 \"(cm)\" against long jump 179.8-261.2 cm) all match PDF 392-393. The unit-error and power-norm remarks are labelled Inference.\n- `framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports`: FIX, see section 3.\n\n## 3. Confidence consistency (the \"domain rule\")\n\nThe rule in the fix log: `contested` when a library source disagrees with the entry inside the sport domain the entry claims. That is the ATOMIZE brief's definition, applied by domain. Applied to the six entries in 6d that rest on concentrated blocks and touch Issurin:\n\n| entry | claimed domain | in-domain library disagreement | rating | consistent? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| explosive-strength model | explosive-strength sports (jumpers in the Example) | Issurin's book: stages of about two months, four to seven a season; elite track and field multi-peak | contested | yes |\n| tri-cyclic model | individual and one-on-one sports | brief p. 6: combat and swimming | contested | yes |\n| two large mesocycles | annual structure, all sports | Issurin's book (stage length and count) | contested | yes |\n| complex training | highly qualified vs beginner athletes | Issurin 2016 p. 11 (amateurs), against Issurin 2008 p. 33 (see FIX above) | contested | yes (basis needs the 2008 line) |\n| concentrated strength loading | \"speed-strength, cyclic and team sports\" (PDF 382) | brief p. 6: combat and team sports | contested | yes |\n| **medium- and long-duration endurance models** | middle- and long-distance endurance athletes; \"cyclic sports of medium duration\" (PDF 399) | **brief p. 6: swimming** | **supported** | **no** |\n\n**Call on the endurance entry: it should be `contested`, on brief p. 6.** Reasons:\n1. Swimming sits inside the domain the entry claims. The entry claims \"middle-distance (medium) or long-distance (long) endurance athletes\" and never excludes swimming. The book scopes the medium-duration material to \"cyclic sports of medium duration\" (PDF 399 / p.388, Fig 6.46), and at PDF 374 / p.363 it lists swimming among the cyclic sports where the LDTE of concentrated strength loading was observed (\"middle-distance running (Zhdanovich, 1980), swimming (Rudokenc, 1981) and ice-skating\"). Brief p. 6 has both an elite-swimmer example (\"specific strength and anaerobic capacity improved but swimming performance improved in only two of three athletes\") and the judgment \"insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation.\" That is the same test that moved the tri-cyclic entry to `contested`.\n2. The counter-argument that PDF 387 lists swimming among sports that \"traditionally employ\" tri-cyclic forms does not remove it from the endurance domain. The rule concerns the domain the entry claims, not the book's cycle-type list, and the tri-cyclic entry itself counts swimming in its domain.\n3. **A stale cross-reference introduced by the fix round.** The entry says the models \"carry the same Issurin 2016 challenge to concentrated loading as the other 6.14 models ([[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]).\" The explosive entry now says Issurin 2016 does not count against that model in its own domain. The pointer now contradicts its target. That needs fixing whatever the rating.\n\nCorrection for `framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports`: set `confidence: contested`. Replace the \"carry the same Issurin 2016 challenge...\" sentence with: \"**Contested by Issurin 2016 inside the models' domain (the basis for the `contested` rating).** Both models run on concentrated strength blocks, and the authors count swimming among the cyclic sports where the LDTE of such blocks was observed (PDF p. 374 / printed p. 363). The library's brief of Issurin 2016 reports an elite-swimmer example in which 'specific strength and anaerobic capacity improved but swimming performance improved in only two of three athletes,' and judges the concentrated-unidirectional evidence 'insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation' (brief, p. 6; [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]). The brief also says the review's study selection is non-systematic and author-curated.\" Add the brief to Related. Everything else in the entry checked out: PDF 395-397 and 399-400 quotes, including the added \"optimal date ... is deferred\" (PDF 396), Gilyazova 7-14%, and baseline line 115.\n\nOther entries checked under the same rule and correctly left `supported`: mesocycle variants (no sport domain claimed; its Issurin line says \"for team sports\"), unidirectional microcycles (a microcycle restoration finding the brief does not address), speed-sport, follow-a-concentrated-block and GPP restoration (narrow-target speed and speed-strength domains). After the endurance change the 6d tally is 6 `contested`, 18 `supported`, 1 `untested`.\n\n## 4. Spot checks of round-1 GO entries\n\n| File | Verdict | Checked |\n|---|---|---|\n| claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving | GO | \"up to 2 months\" and \"4-12 weeks\" a paragraph apart (PDF 381); mechanism sentence (PDF 381); citation list, \"most appropriate for highly qualified athletes\", 13-15% (PDF 382); \"reduced by as much as 20%\" over a macrocycle (PDF 305); brief p. 6 quotes verbatim; contested scope (team sports, which the book itself claims at PDF 382) fits the domain rule; baseline line 183. |\n| framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound | GO | Fig 6.33 against the PDF 386 image: (a) A rises smoothly to a plateau, B dips then exceeds it; (b) volume peaks mid-cycle, shaded GPP peaks early and declines, fitness rises steadily; (c) volume peaks early, fitness low then climbing above it, shaded GPP grows across the cycle. All as the entry says. Also checked: \"usually 3-5 months\" (PDF 387), \"4-6 weeks\" and the italic mesocycle definition (PDF 385), the qualification rule (PDF 386), baseline line 106. Round-1 optional note still open: Variant A is the \"traditional form\", so the Issurin line fits Variant B only. |\n| heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates | GO | Rules 1-4, the calendar-conflict sentence, \"about 20 weeks\" and the italic recuperation sentence (PDF 400); Issurin \"about two months\" in the vault entry; baseline line 185. |\n| framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training | GO | The complexity sentence and \"low probability of producing a correct prognosis\" (PDF 400); steps 1-8 and the contingency sentence (PDF 401); Issurin nine-step and Evans ten-step links resolve; baseline line 183. |\n\nEvery quote in the other GO entries (programme-objective, technique-work, GPP restoration, seven aims, technical mastery, test-monthly, four documents, programming-is-an-art) passed the text-layer quote check; their content beyond quotes was not re-audited in this round.\n\n## 5. Mechanical checks (25 files)\n\n```\nprinciple--programme-objective-approach-quantified-goals-dictate-training.md OK\nclaim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes.md OK\nclaim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving.md OK\ncase-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities.md OK\nheuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block.md OK\nheuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks.md OK\nframework--speed-sport-block-organisation-and-two-glycolytic-peaks.md OK\nframework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound.md OK\nclaim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation.md OK\ncase-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength.md OK\nheuristic--use-low-intensity-gpp-as-restoration-in-concentrated-loading-models.md OK\nframework--seven-primary-aims-of-programming-training.md OK\nframework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models.md OK\ncase-study--tabachnik-100m-sprinter-model-characteristics.md OK\nframework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports.md OK\nframework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports.md OK\nclaim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity.md OK\nframework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports.md OK\nheuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work.md OK\nprinciple--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice.md OK\nheuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates.md OK\nframework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training.md OK\nheuristic--test-monthly-under-constant-conditions-and-compare-with-targets.md OK\nframework--four-documents-for-programming-and-controlling-training.md OK\nprinciple--programming-is-an-art-on-principles-not-an-algorithm.md OK\n```\n\n(Each \"OK\" means: six headers once each, no em dash outside headers or in the title, no wrapped wikilinks, every wikilink resolves in staging or the vault, no process wording.) The fix log's grep evidence matches the files: every new phrase it lists is present, and the old phrases I spot-checked are gone.\n\n## Fixer disagreements: rulings\n\n1. Explosive `contested` basis (Issurin's book in-domain; 2016 scoped to the transfer): **justified.**\n2. Brief p. 11 does not say \"jumping\": **justified** (quoted above).\n3. Technique level, not above, at block 2's end: **justified** (y about 290 vs first peak about 278).\n4. An-Gly peak late in the block: **justified** (74% through).\n5. Fig 6.36(b) crossing times about 35 h and 38-40 h: **justified.**\n6. Antonova 1980 at PDF 381: **justified.**\n7. \"The book\" in the brief is Issurin's Block Periodization: **justified.** But the same brief line also carries the Issurin 2008 agreement on lower-level athletes, which the entry omits (FIX above).\n8. Optional GO-row items deferred: acceptable. The 6.2.2 build-backwards link remains a coordinator suggestion.\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** 22 GO, 3 FIX, 0 NO-GO. Required fixes, each a sentence or a label:\n1. `framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports`: `confidence: contested` on brief p. 6 (swimming inside the cyclic-endurance domain, PDF 374 and 399); replace the stale \"same Issurin 2016 challenge as [[explosive]]\" pointer with the in-domain reason; link the brief.\n2. `claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes`: add Issurin 2008's agreement on low- and mid-level athletes (Block Periodization PDF p. 44 / printed p. 33), so the beginners-limit contest is shown as resting on the 2016 review only.\n3. `claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation`: label the terminology-tension interpretation \"Inference:\".\n\nOptional, not blocking: explosive block-2 lows \"about month 7\" and an Inference label on the structure comparison; speed-sport team-sport Issurin line; microcycle \"stays\" to \"is\" and about 95% start; mesocycle-variants Issurin line scoped to Variant B.\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":4,"requestId":"req_011CffqSmXDyWfTeivUErXFK","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"dd02f5cc-df68-4d2b-b49d-283107c666bc","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:17:21.185Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"dd02f5cc-df68-4d2b-b49d-283107c666bc","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"25e8ccab-467d-4632-a14a-aba55241bae7","agentId":"ac7100d52d01adab3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014FRSaYLTfTz8XHuEYrSyFx","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6d_opus_round2.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"ed0f4852-957a-4072-aa13-8d5e9958b44c","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:17:21.345Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6d_opus_round2.md","content":"# Review round 2: Supertraining Chapter 6d (6.12-6.17), 25 entries\n\nReviewer: Claude Opus (independent round-2 reviewer, instructed to falsify; did not write or fix these entries). Date: 2026-10-03.\nScope: the 25 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6d.txt`, folder `staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/` (Chapter 7 files in the same folder ignored).\nInputs: round-1 review `staging/review_supertraining_ch6d_opus.md`; fix log `staging/fixes_supertraining_ch6d_applied.md`; `docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md`; `docs/method-baseline.md`; the vault brief `research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a.md`; vault entries `block-periodization--issurin/framework--training-stage-and-multi-peak-annual-cycle.md`, `claim--multi-peak-annual-preparation-elite-track-and-field.md`, `principle--concentration-of-training-workloads.md`; `elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--bompa-annual-plan-cycle-types.md`.\nSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx = printed + 11.\n\n## Method\n\n- **Page images, opened by me** (all in `staging/_renders/`): Fig 6.39 at 400 dpi with crops of both panels (`r2_6d_p395.png`, `r2_6d_p395_upper.png`, `r2_6d_p395_lower.png`); Fig 6.32(b) at 400 dpi (`r2_6d_p383.png`, crop `r2_6d_p383_figb.png`); Fig 6.36(b) at 400 dpi, both panels (`r2_6d_p388_bleft.png`, `r2_6d_p388_bright.png`); Fig 6.38 (`r2_6d_p394_fig638.png`); Fig 6.33 (`r2_6d_p386_small.png`); Figs 6.44-6.45 (`r2_6d_p399_small.png`). Fig 6.39 positions were measured in pixels against the month ticks of the lower panel (ticks at the 1/2 ... 10/11 boundaries; numerals sit between ticks).\n- **Quote check, all 25 files.** A script extracted every double-quoted string (12+ characters) and matched each fragment (split on ellipses) against the text layer of PDF 290-319 and 330-403, the baseline, the Issurin brief, the Issurin, Evans and Bompa vault entries (whitespace, case, punctuation and line-break hyphens normalised; OCR tolerance by word trigrams). Result per file: Antonova 17/0 missing, endurance 25/0, prolonged 11/0, tricyclic 14/0, technical-mastery 9/0, set-boundaries 17/0, eight-step 18/0, test-monthly 12/0, programming-is-an-art 9/0, Tabachnik 3/0. Every residual \"miss\" in the other 15 files was inspected by hand: all are regex pairing artifacts across nested quotes, the entries' own Inference phrases in quotation marks (for example \"level is harder than timing\"), bracketed edits (\"cause[s]\", \"fairly random[ly]\") or OCR (\"modem\" for \"modern\" at PDF 389). No misquote found.\n- **Tables 6.7-6.12** checked value by value against the PDF 392-393 text layer (Tabachnik and descriptive-model entries).\n- **Dates and durations** checked in the text layer: Antonova 1982 (PDF 374, 382) and 1980 (PDF 381); Levchenko 1982 (PDF 374), 1981 (PDF 381), 1980 (PDF 382); \"4-12 weeks\" (PDF 375, 381); \"usually 3-5 months\" and \"currently scant\" (PDF 387); \"about 20 weeks\" (PDF 400); \"latency period of 1-3 weeks\" (PDF 305).\n- **Baseline line numbers** 32, 101, 105, 115, 116, 117, 181-183, 185-188 checked against `docs/method-baseline.md`: all correct.\n- **Mechanical check** (script over the 25 files): six section headers once each; no em dash outside the six headers or in titles; no line with unbalanced `[[`/`]]` (no wrapped links); every wikilink resolves in staging or the vault; no process or review-history wording (pattern: prior/previous version, corrected from, round, reviewer, review gate, NO-GO, fix log, earlier draft, now reads, fixer, flagged). Output: all 25 files \"OK\".\n\nCounts: **22 GO, 3 FIX, 0 NO-GO.** Of round 1's 11 FIX files, 9 are now GO and 2 need one more pass (complex training, two mesocycles). One round-1 GO file (the endurance models) is now FIX.\n\n## 1. The explosive-strength model entry (framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports): GO\n\n**Fig 6.39, my own reading (PDF 395 / p.384, 400 dpi).** Month ticks in the lower panel fall at about x = 212, 325, 440, 555, 670, 785, 905, 1020, 1135, 1255 (crop coordinates); dashed lines at about 545 (just before the 4/5 tick), 678 (the 5/6 tick) and 968 (mid month 8).\n- Phases I to late month 4, II about month 5, III months 6 to mid 8, IV mid 8 to 11: entry correct, including its note that boundaries do not sit on ticks.\n- Block 1 spans the panel start to x about 420, before the 3/4 tick: \"ending late in month 3\", correct. Block 2 spans x about 688 to 935: starts at the 5/6 tick, ends just past the 7/8 tick, \"early in month 8\", correct. Height ratio 333/510 = 0.65: \"roughly two thirds\", correct.\n- Upper panel. The two upper curves bump in month 1 (x about 118-120); all three are lowest in month 2 (x 250-300; Maximum Strength earliest, x about 250); all are rising through month 3 while block 1 runs to x 420: correct. The middle and bottom curves peak at x about 580-585 (early month 5, just right of the first dashed line) and the top curve is flat across x 550-680: correct. Block-2 lows at x about 790 (Maximum), 810 (middle) and 880 (top), i.e. early to late month 7: the entry's \"lowest about months 6-7\" is slightly early (optional: \"about month 7\"). The two upper curves converge and cross at x about 1040, just past the 8/9 tick and just right of the III/IV line: correct. All three end at their highest values at month 11, right-edge order Starting, Explosive, Maximum: correct. By continuity the steeply rising middle curve ends at Starting Strength; the entry labels this Inference: correct and correctly labelled.\n- Lower panel. Technique: rising inside block 1, peak at x about 490 (month 4), low at x about 750 (month 6, early block 2), back to about y 290 at block 2's end against a first peak of about y 278 (level, not above: the fixer's disagreement 3 is justified), peak at x about 1160 (month 10), declining to 11: all correct. Speed-ending curve peaks at x about 1010 (just before the 8/9 tick) and falls; GPP-ending curve lowest at x about 985 and rises; they cross at x about 1180 (month 10); GPP ends above Speed: all correct. Before month 8 the entry calls the GPP/Speed courses unclear; that is conservative and acceptable.\n- Text check: \"the highest values of which are achieved at the end of each mesocycle\" (PDF 394) matches the figure's peaks at month 5 (end of mesocycle 1) and month 11.\n- Fig 6.38 (PDF 394): A jumps early and small, B weights middle and largest, C depth jumps late and small: correct.\n\n**Quotes.** Every PDF 381, 390, 391 and 394 quote matched the text layer. Issurin quotes verified verbatim: brief p. 11 \"better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality and as restricted when peak performance requires many interacting abilities\"; brief p. 6 \"improved some fitness measures, but the reviewed studies did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance\" and \"insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\"; Issurin book entry \"should be about two months\" and \"usually varies from four to seven\" (Block Periodization PDF pp. 38-40); multi-peak entry: Bubka 7, Jones 10, Kostadinova 11 peaks, so \"7 to 11 times\", with two jumpers among the three: correct. Baseline lines 105, 185 and 186 with tags: correct.\n\n**\"Jumping-type sports\": confirmed absent.** The brief uses \"jumping\" once, in its population list at p. 11: \"The review emphasizes elite, subelite, and well-trained athletes across endurance, team, combat, dual, jumping, and strength/power settings.\" Its only statement on where the concentrated-unidirectional model suits is \"sports dominated by a narrow target quality.\" The entry says \"The brief does not name jumping or throwing as narrow-target sports, and it does not reject the model for them,\" and labels the mapping of a single explosive event to \"a narrow target quality\" as Inference. That is exactly what the brief supports. The fixer's disagreement 2 is justified; round 1's paraphrase overstated the brief.\n\n**Football-transfer scoping.** Correct. The entry keeps two grounds apart: (1) Issurin's book (stage length and count; multi-peak seasons in elite track and field) as the in-domain basis for `contested`; (2) Issurin 2016 as bearing on the transfer to American football, a team sport, with the brief's non-systematic, author-curated caveat. Nothing claims more than the brief says. The football mapping in When to apply is labelled Inference, and the entry says the authors do not discuss football. Fixer disagreement 1 (contest on Issurin's book, 2016 scoped to the transfer) is justified under the domain rule and is more accurate than round 1's proposal.\n\n**Optional (not blocking).**\n- Block-2 lows: \"about month 7\" rather than \"about months 6-7\".\n- \"That is a different structure from this model's two large mesocycles\" is the writer's comparison. The 7-11 peaks are competition peaks, and the model's two competition phases do not exclude several competitions each, so the firmer half of the contrast is the stage length and count. Prefix the comparison with \"Inference:\" and add that clause.\n\n## 2. The other 10 round-1 FIX files\n\n| File | Verdict | Finding (entry vs source, with page) | Exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes | FIX | Round-1 fixes landed: Matveyev 1970 in its own clause with the PDF 380 quote (verified); the 28-study caveat matches brief p. 11; fixer disagreement 7 (\"the book\" in the brief is Issurin's Block Periodization) is correct per the brief's text. **New problem, introduced by the rewrite:** \"He also contests who the concentrated approach is for: the brief records 'no evidence that amateur athletes have any serious restrictions' ... (brief, p. 11), against the book's view that complex loading is 'applicable only to beginners or athletes of average qualification' (PDF p. 380).\" This presents one side of a split inside Issurin. The same brief line says the 2008 book \"(p. 33) says they do fine with medium concentration\", and the vault entry `block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads` records Issurin 2008 saying the traditional complex model \"has visible benefits for the preparation of low- and mid-level athletes\" (Block Periodization PDF p. 44 / printed p. 33) and tags it \"Contested (Issurin 2008 vs Issurin 2016).\" Issurin 2008 agrees with the authors' beginners limit; only Issurin 2016 disputes it. The brief requires the source's internal contradiction to be kept. | After the p. 11 sentence add: \"Issurin's own 2008 book takes the authors' side here: the traditional complex model 'has visible benefits for the preparation of low- and mid-level athletes' (Block Periodization PDF p. 44 / printed p. 33; [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]]), so the contest on the beginners limit rests on the 2016 review only.\" Optional: support \"Issurin agrees that traditional complex training falls short for elite athletes\" with the direct source, Issurin's Table 1.4 of contradictions in the traditional simultaneous model for high-level athletes (Block Periodization PDF p. 20, same vault entry). That lets the sentence stand without the bridging Inference. |\n| claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation | FIX | Round-1 fixes landed: the paragraph now records a terminology tension; quotes at PDF 387, 389, 396 and 400 verified; the `contested` basis is Issurin's book, with the note that the 2016 brief does not address mesocycle count (correct: the brief has nothing on it); Evans/Bompa quotes verified. **Attribution problem (carried in from the round-1 wording, still present):** the bold lead \"A terminology tension inside the book, not two opposed positions,\" \"Read with PDF pp. 389 and 396, the passages answer different questions...\" and \"The apparent conflict ... comes from the PDF p. 387 wording\" are the writer's interpretation, written in plain text (author voice). | Change the bold lead to \"**A terminology tension inside the book (Inference), not two opposed positions.**\" and begin the interpretive sentence \"Inference: read with PDF pp. 389 and 396, the passages answer different questions...\". Keep the descriptive first sentence (what each page says) as plain text. |\n| framework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports | GO | Both fixes landed. Boxing and swimming are now correctly described as traditionally tri-cyclic with bi-cyclic possible (PDF 387), and the \"team sports and one-on-one events\" quote is verified. `contested` with the reason stated: the domain is \"intended primarily for individual and one-on-one sports\" (PDF 397), and brief p. 6 names combat and swimming (quotes verbatim). The old \"keeps `supported`\" sentence is gone. | Checked: all 14 quotes; PDF 387, 397, 400; brief p. 6; baseline line 188. |\n| framework--speed-sport-block-organisation-and-two-glycolytic-peaks | GO | Fig 6.32(b) at 400 dpi agrees with the new paragraph. The shaded block runs about x 70-450 (crop). Two curves dip below the axis and recross after the block (x about 490 and 540). One curve peaks at x about 350, 74% through the block (fixer's \"two thirds to three quarters\" is right; disagreement 4 justified), falls to a low at about x 600, then a hump at about x 880, ending at An-Gly. One rises steadily to S-E. One starts at the block's end and peaks at x about 660 before falling to Speed. Vmax/STATE identity is marked unclear and the crossing assignments are labelled Inference: correct. | Optional: When to apply extends the model to team sports through the second glycolytic peak but, unlike every other entry touching team sports, has no Issurin line. Add one sentence: \"For team sports, Issurin 2016 reports no team-practice or competitive-performance effects from concentrated-unidirectional programs (brief, p. 6).\" |\n| case-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength | GO | Fig 6.36(b) at 400 dpi. Aerobic week: VO2 max starts about 87%, crosses 100% at about 35-36 h, about 107% at 72 h; Training effect about 93%, crosses at about 17 h, about 101.7% at 72 h. Anaerobic week: VO2 max starts about 98%, peaks about 113% at about 8 h, about 106% at 72 h; Training effect crosses at about 38 h, about 102.5% at 72 h. All match the entry (fixer's disagreement 5 justified; round 1's 30-36 h was early for the anaerobic panel). The spurious 12 h conflict is gone; the \"currently scant\" and \"sharply reduces\" quotes are verified at PDF 387. | Optional: the anaerobic-week Training effect starts nearer 95% than \"about 94%\" (curve start just above the 95 tick). Optional: \"The rating stays `supported`\" reads as history; write \"The rating is `supported`\". |\n| case-study--tabachnik-100m-sprinter-model-characteristics | GO | Every Table 6.8 and 6.9 value matches the PDF 392 text layer. The 5 m (1.18 s) vs 15 m (1.16-1.18 s) inconsistency and the 10.2-to-10.5 s 15 m step (1.18-1.20 to 1.26-1.28) are correctly added. `untested` is appropriate for an unsourced norm table. | Checked: all values, PDF 391 quote, baseline line 32. |\n| heuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks | GO | PDF 305 latency quote verified; the side-by-side note is labelled Inference; the 6c allow-restoration entry does draw the latency-versus-duration distinction (its When NOT); rule 2 quote (\"should not be increased during the period of emergence of the LDTE\", PDF 375) matches the 6c time-technique entry and the page. | Checked: PDF 383, 305, 375, 384, 385 quotes; links resolve. |\n| case-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities | GO | Self-contradiction removed; Levchenko and Antonova date notes verified (fixer's disagreement 6 on Antonova 1980 at PDF 381 is correct); 6c LDTE principle linked with the new angle stated, and it does cite Antonova 1982 and Levchenko 1982 at PDF 374. | Checked: all 17 quotes; PDF 340 cybernetic definition; baseline line 74. |\n| claim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity | GO | \"Shorter\" replaced with \"comparable in length\", inside the labelled Inference paragraph. | Checked: PDF 394 and 396 quotes; baseline lines 105, 181, 187. |\n| heuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work | GO | The caption-vs-label note is fixed and labelled. On the PDF 399 image: \"GPP\" under small wave A, \"Strength Load\" under large wave B, both left of the dashed mid-line, so B occupies the first half as the text says. The labels fit the text; the caption (\"A is the block of concentrated strength loading\") is the likelier error. Fig 6.44 reading (speed-strength dips in A, rises in B, flattens in C, highest at the right) also checked. | Checked: PDF 397-400 quotes; baseline line 105. |\n\nBoth fixer-edited GO entries were also read in full:\n- `framework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models`: GO. Tables 6.7, 6.10, 6.11 and 6.12 values (splits 111.5/116.8/131.5; 2 x (4 x 400 m) caption; \"Relative mean strength of 5 muscle groups (kg)\"; javelin snatch Bmass + 15 / + 10 kg, squat 2 x / 1.5 x, broad jump 3.20/2.80, triple jump 10.20/7.80, vertical jump 95.0/70.0 cm, 30 m 2.90/3.20 s, 170/140 and 35/30 throws; quintuple jump 9.25-13.70 \"(cm)\" against long jump 179.8-261.2 cm) all match PDF 392-393. The unit-error and power-norm remarks are labelled Inference.\n- `framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports`: FIX, see section 3.\n\n## 3. Confidence consistency (the \"domain rule\")\n\nThe rule in the fix log: `contested` when a library source disagrees with the entry inside the sport domain the entry claims. That is the ATOMIZE brief's definition, applied by domain. Applied to the six entries in 6d that rest on concentrated blocks and touch Issurin:\n\n| entry | claimed domain | in-domain library disagreement | rating | consistent? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| explosive-strength model | explosive-strength sports (jumpers in the Example) | Issurin's book: stages of about two months, four to seven a season; elite track and field multi-peak | contested | yes |\n| tri-cyclic model | individual and one-on-one sports | brief p. 6: combat and swimming | contested | yes |\n| two large mesocycles | annual structure, all sports | Issurin's book (stage length and count) | contested | yes |\n| complex training | highly qualified vs beginner athletes | Issurin 2016 p. 11 (amateurs), against Issurin 2008 p. 33 (see FIX above) | contested | yes (basis needs the 2008 line) |\n| concentrated strength loading | \"speed-strength, cyclic and team sports\" (PDF 382) | brief p. 6: combat and team sports | contested | yes |\n| **medium- and long-duration endurance models** | middle- and long-distance endurance athletes; \"cyclic sports of medium duration\" (PDF 399) | **brief p. 6: swimming** | **supported** | **no** |\n\n**Call on the endurance entry: it should be `contested`, on brief p. 6.** Reasons:\n1. Swimming sits inside the domain the entry claims. The entry claims \"middle-distance (medium) or long-distance (long) endurance athletes\" and never excludes swimming. The book scopes the medium-duration material to \"cyclic sports of medium duration\" (PDF 399 / p.388, Fig 6.46), and at PDF 374 / p.363 it lists swimming among the cyclic sports where the LDTE of concentrated strength loading was observed (\"middle-distance running (Zhdanovich, 1980), swimming (Rudokenc, 1981) and ice-skating\"). Brief p. 6 has both an elite-swimmer example (\"specific strength and anaerobic capacity improved but swimming performance improved in only two of three athletes\") and the judgment \"insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation.\" That is the same test that moved the tri-cyclic entry to `contested`.\n2. The counter-argument that PDF 387 lists swimming among sports that \"traditionally employ\" tri-cyclic forms does not remove it from the endurance domain. The rule concerns the domain the entry claims, not the book's cycle-type list, and the tri-cyclic entry itself counts swimming in its domain.\n3. **A stale cross-reference introduced by the fix round.** The entry says the models \"carry the same Issurin 2016 challenge to concentrated loading as the other 6.14 models ([[framework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports]]).\" The explosive entry now says Issurin 2016 does not count against that model in its own domain. The pointer now contradicts its target. That needs fixing whatever the rating.\n\nCorrection for `framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports`: set `confidence: contested`. Replace the \"carry the same Issurin 2016 challenge...\" sentence with: \"**Contested by Issurin 2016 inside the models' domain (the basis for the `contested` rating).** Both models run on concentrated strength blocks, and the authors count swimming among the cyclic sports where the LDTE of such blocks was observed (PDF p. 374 / printed p. 363). The library's brief of Issurin 2016 reports an elite-swimmer example in which 'specific strength and anaerobic capacity improved but swimming performance improved in only two of three athletes,' and judges the concentrated-unidirectional evidence 'insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation' (brief, p. 6; [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]). The brief also says the review's study selection is non-systematic and author-curated.\" Add the brief to Related. Everything else in the entry checked out: PDF 395-397 and 399-400 quotes, including the added \"optimal date ... is deferred\" (PDF 396), Gilyazova 7-14%, and baseline line 115.\n\nOther entries checked under the same rule and correctly left `supported`: mesocycle variants (no sport domain claimed; its Issurin line says \"for team sports\"), unidirectional microcycles (a microcycle restoration finding the brief does not address), speed-sport, follow-a-concentrated-block and GPP restoration (narrow-target speed and speed-strength domains). After the endurance change the 6d tally is 6 `contested`, 18 `supported`, 1 `untested`.\n\n## 4. Spot checks of round-1 GO entries\n\n| File | Verdict | Checked |\n|---|---|---|\n| claim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving | GO | \"up to 2 months\" and \"4-12 weeks\" a paragraph apart (PDF 381); mechanism sentence (PDF 381); citation list, \"most appropriate for highly qualified athletes\", 13-15% (PDF 382); \"reduced by as much as 20%\" over a macrocycle (PDF 305); brief p. 6 quotes verbatim; contested scope (team sports, which the book itself claims at PDF 382) fits the domain rule; baseline line 183. |\n| framework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound | GO | Fig 6.33 against the PDF 386 image: (a) A rises smoothly to a plateau, B dips then exceeds it; (b) volume peaks mid-cycle, shaded GPP peaks early and declines, fitness rises steadily; (c) volume peaks early, fitness low then climbing above it, shaded GPP grows across the cycle. All as the entry says. Also checked: \"usually 3-5 months\" (PDF 387), \"4-6 weeks\" and the italic mesocycle definition (PDF 385), the qualification rule (PDF 386), baseline line 106. Round-1 optional note still open: Variant A is the \"traditional form\", so the Issurin line fits Variant B only. |\n| heuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates | GO | Rules 1-4, the calendar-conflict sentence, \"about 20 weeks\" and the italic recuperation sentence (PDF 400); Issurin \"about two months\" in the vault entry; baseline line 185. |\n| framework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training | GO | The complexity sentence and \"low probability of producing a correct prognosis\" (PDF 400); steps 1-8 and the contingency sentence (PDF 401); Issurin nine-step and Evans ten-step links resolve; baseline line 183. |\n\nEvery quote in the other GO entries (programme-objective, technique-work, GPP restoration, seven aims, technical mastery, test-monthly, four documents, programming-is-an-art) passed the text-layer quote check; their content beyond quotes was not re-audited in this round.\n\n## 5. Mechanical checks (25 files)\n\n```\nprinciple--programme-objective-approach-quantified-goals-dictate-training.md OK\nclaim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes.md OK\nclaim--concentrated-strength-loading-limited-period-qualified-athletes-and-volume-saving.md OK\ncase-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities.md OK\nheuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block.md OK\nheuristic--loading-reduction-for-the-delayed-effect-must-outlast-1-2-weeks.md OK\nframework--speed-sport-block-organisation-and-two-glycolytic-peaks.md OK\nframework--mesocycle-variants-smooth-rise-versus-concentrated-dip-and-rebound.md OK\nclaim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation.md OK\ncase-study--unidirectional-weekly-microcycles-swimming-and-speed-strength.md OK\nheuristic--use-low-intensity-gpp-as-restoration-in-concentrated-loading-models.md OK\nframework--seven-primary-aims-of-programming-training.md OK\nframework--descriptive-and-prescriptive-training-models.md OK\ncase-study--tabachnik-100m-sprinter-model-characteristics.md OK\nframework--bi-cyclic-annual-model-for-explosive-strength-sports.md OK\nframework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports.md OK\nclaim--prolonged-competition-period-volume-cuts-lower-special-work-capacity.md OK\nframework--tricyclic-annual-model-for-individual-and-one-on-one-sports.md OK\nheuristic--follow-a-concentrated-block-with-moderate-volume-explosive-work.md OK\nprinciple--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice.md OK\nheuristic--set-mesocycle-boundaries-by-main-competition-dates.md OK\nframework--eight-step-sequence-for-programming-annual-training.md OK\nheuristic--test-monthly-under-constant-conditions-and-compare-with-targets.md OK\nframework--four-documents-for-programming-and-controlling-training.md OK\nprinciple--programming-is-an-art-on-principles-not-an-algorithm.md OK\n```\n\n(Each \"OK\" means: six headers once each, no em dash outside headers or in the title, no wrapped wikilinks, every wikilink resolves in staging or the vault, no process wording.) The fix log's grep evidence matches the files: every new phrase it lists is present, and the old phrases I spot-checked are gone.\n\n## Fixer disagreements: rulings\n\n1. Explosive `contested` basis (Issurin's book in-domain; 2016 scoped to the transfer): **justified.**\n2. Brief p. 11 does not say \"jumping\": **justified** (quoted above).\n3. Technique level, not above, at block 2's end: **justified** (y about 290 vs first peak about 278).\n4. An-Gly peak late in the block: **justified** (74% through).\n5. Fig 6.36(b) crossing times about 35 h and 38-40 h: **justified.**\n6. Antonova 1980 at PDF 381: **justified.**\n7. \"The book\" in the brief is Issurin's Block Periodization: **justified.** But the same brief line also carries the Issurin 2008 agreement on lower-level athletes, which the entry omits (FIX above).\n8. Optional GO-row items deferred: acceptable. The 6.2.2 build-backwards link remains a coordinator suggestion.\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** 22 GO, 3 FIX, 0 NO-GO. Required fixes, each a sentence or a label:\n1. `framework--annual-models-for-medium-and-long-duration-endurance-sports`: `confidence: contested` on brief p. 6 (swimming inside the cyclic-endurance domain, PDF 374 and 399); replace the stale \"same Issurin 2016 challenge as [[explosive]]\" pointer with the in-domain reason; link the brief.\n2. `claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes`: add Issurin 2008's agreement on low- and mid-level athletes (Block Periodization PDF p. 44 / printed p. 33), so the beginners-limit contest is shown as resting on the 2016 review only.\n3. `claim--two-large-mesocycles-a-year-bi-cyclic-organisation`: label the terminology-tension interpretation \"Inference:\".\n\nOptional, not blocking: explosive block-2 lows \"about month 7\" and an Inference label on the structure comparison; speed-sport team-sport Issurin line; microcycle \"stays\" to \"is\" and about 95% start; mesocycle-variants Issurin line scoped to Variant B.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"dd02f5cc-df68-4d2b-b49d-283107c666bc","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
